  where kind = 'note') = $expected_content$[{"itemId":"1fd509a0-0dc5-51f1-88c3-e42cd80de884","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"topic-note:1","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"title":"Core Theoretical Framework - Descriptive vs Inferential Statistics","body":"Statistics is the science of collecting, organizing, presenting, analyzing, and interpreting data to make informed business decisions. The field is divided into descriptive statistics (summarizing data) and inferential statistics (making predictions about populations based on samples).","noteType":"topic_note","keyPoints":["Descriptive Statistics: Methods for organizing and summarizing information (e.g., mean, charts, frequency distributions).","Inferential Statistics: Using sample data to make predictions or draw conclusions about a larger population.","Population: The complete set of all individuals or items of interest.","Sample: A subset of the population selected for study.","Parameter: A descriptive measure calculated from a population (e.g., population mean μ).","Statistic: A descriptive measure calculated from a sample (e.g., sample mean x̄)."],"examTip":"Always distinguish between population (parameter) and sample (statistic). This is frequently tested in MCQs."}},{"itemId":"6175c4aa-6405-5db9-be54-5784696733c9","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"topic-note:10","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"title":"Sampling Methods and Techniques","body":"Sampling involves selecting a subset of a population for study. Different sampling methods suit different research objectives and resource constraints.","noteType":"topic_note","keyPoints":["Census: Studying every member of the population. Accurate but expensive and time-consuming.","Sampling: Studying a subset. More practical and cost-effective.","Simple Random Sampling: Every member has equal chance of selection.","Systematic Sampling: Selecting every kth member from a list (e.g., every 10th person).","Stratified Sampling: Divide population into strata (subgroups), then sample from each stratum.","Cluster Sampling: Divide population into clusters, randomly select clusters, study all members in selected clusters.","Convenience Sampling: Non-probabilistic. Select easily accessible members.","Quota Sampling: Non-probabilistic. Set quotas for different subgroups.","Sampling Error: Natural variation that occurs because we study a sample, not the entire population."],"examTip":"Stratified sampling ensures all subgroups are represented. Quota sampling is non-probabilistic."}},{"itemId":"e92041fa-d4e7-5033-8471-f5d122c0356e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"topic-note:11","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"title":"Sampling Distributions and Central Limit Theorem","body":"The sampling distribution of the mean describes how sample means vary across different samples from the same population. The Central Limit Theorem is fundamental to inferential statistics.","noteType":"topic_note","keyPoints":["Sampling Distribution: The probability distribution of a sample statistic (e.g., sample mean).","Standard Error: The standard deviation of a sampling distribution. SE = σ / √n.","Property: As sample size (n) increases, standard error decreases.","Central Limit Theorem (CLT): As sample size gets large (typically n ≥ 30), the sampling distribution of the mean approaches a normal distribution, regardless of the population's shape.","Importance: CLT allows us to use normal distribution methods for inference even when population distribution is unknown.","Point Estimate: A single numerical value used to estimate a population parameter (e.g., using sample mean to estimate population mean)."],"examTip":"CLT is one of the most important theorems in statistics. Sample size ≥ 30 is the general rule for CLT to apply."}},{"itemId":"6a91fe26-f07e-5618-a772-babb501e8536","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"topic-note:2","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"title":"Levels of Data Measurement - Nominal, Ordinal, Interval, Ratio","body":"Understanding the four levels of measurement is crucial for selecting appropriate statistical methods. Each level has specific characteristics and determines which operations (addition, ranking, etc.) can be performed.","noteType":"topic_note","keyPoints":["Nominal: Labels or categories with no inherent order (e.g., gender, color, marital status). Only counting and mode are appropriate.","Ordinal: Data with a meaningful order but unequal intervals (e.g., ranking, education level, satisfaction rating).","Interval: Numeric data with equal intervals but no true zero (e.g., temperature in Celsius, IQ scores).","Ratio: Numeric data with equal intervals AND a true zero point allowing ratios (e.g., height, weight, income, age).","True Zero: A value of zero means \"none\" or \"absence\" - only found in ratio scales."],"examTip":"Remember the hierarchy: Ratio > Interval > Ordinal > Nominal. Ratio is the highest level with all properties."}},{"itemId":"1fcddbd3-bd14-5091-8c43-faa5bfb9ab4e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"topic-note:3","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"title":"Data Sources and Variables","body":"Data can be classified by source (primary vs secondary) and by type (qualitative vs quantitative). Understanding variable types determines appropriate analysis methods.","noteType":"topic_note","keyPoints":["Primary Data: Collected firsthand specifically for the current research purpose (e.g., surveys, experiments, interviews).","Secondary Data: Already existing data collected by others for different purposes (e.g., journals, government reports).","Qualitative Variables: Non-numeric categories or labels (e.g., gender, nationality).","Quantitative Variables: Numeric measurements that can be counted or measured.","Discrete Quantitative: Countable values with gaps (e.g., number of children, students in a class).","Continuous Quantitative: Measurable values on a continuum (e.g., weight, height, temperature)."],"examTip":"Number of children = discrete. Weight = continuous. This distinction appears frequently in exams."}},{"itemId":"368c510d-3ca9-5341-87d5-62879803b7c7","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"topic-note:4","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"title":"Data Presentation - Frequency Distributions and Histograms","body":"Organizing large datasets into frequency distributions makes patterns visible. Understanding class intervals, boundaries, and midpoints is essential for constructing and interpreting frequency tables and histograms.","noteType":"topic_note","keyPoints":["Range: Maximum value - Minimum value. First step in creating grouped data.","Class Width (Interval): The difference between upper and lower class boundaries.","Class Midpoint: (Upper Boundary + Lower Boundary) / 2. Represents the center of each class.","Histogram: A bar graph with adjacent bars representing continuous data.","Ogive: A cumulative frequency graph used to find medians and quartiles graphically.","Frequency Density: Used when class intervals are unequal. Calculated as Frequency / Class Width.","Relative Frequency: Frequency / Total Frequency. The sum of all relative frequencies = 1.0.","Pie Chart: Best for showing proportions or percentages of a whole."],"examTip":"Class Midpoint formula is heavily tested. Always add boundaries, never subtract when finding midpoint."}},{"itemId":"87327e34-e88a-5b33-baa1-06e4d66016c1","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"topic-note:5","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"title":"Measures of Central Tendency - Mean, Median, Mode","body":"Central tendency measures locate the \"center\" of a dataset. Each measure has strengths and weaknesses depending on data characteristics and the presence of outliers.","noteType":"topic_note","keyPoints":["Arithmetic Mean: The average. x̄ = Σx / n for ungrouped data; x̄ = Σfx / Σf for grouped data.","Median: The middle value when data is ordered. Also Q2, D5, P50. Best for skewed data with outliers.","Mode: The most frequently occurring value. Can be used for qualitative data.","Symmetrical Distribution: Mean = Median = Mode.","Positively Skewed (Right Tail): Mean > Median > Mode.","Negatively Skewed (Left Tail): Mean < Median < Mode.","Weighted Mean: Used when some values have more importance. x̄w = Σ(w × x) / Σw.","Geometric Mean: Used for growth rates and ratios. GM = ⁿ√(x1 × x2 × ... × xn).","Harmonic Mean: Used for rates (e.g., speed). HM = n / Σ(1/x).","Property: Sum of deviations from the mean always equals zero: Σ(x - x̄) = 0."],"examTip":"For skewed distributions, Median is the best measure. Mean is most affected by outliers."}},{"itemId":"f93cba49-b589-5d73-9e0b-edd341a7064e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"topic-note:6","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"title":"Partition Values - Quartiles, Deciles, Percentiles","body":"Partition values divide ordered datasets into equal parts. They are essential for understanding data distribution and identifying position within a dataset.","noteType":"topic_note","keyPoints":["Quartiles (Q): Divide data into 4 equal parts. Q1 = 25th percentile, Q2 = Median = 50th percentile, Q3 = 75th percentile.","Deciles (D): Divide data into 10 equal parts. D1 to D9. D5 = Median.","Percentiles (P): Divide data into 100 equal parts. P1 to P99. P50 = Median.","Relationships: D7 = P70; Q1 = P25; Q3 = P75.","For Grouped Data: Use the formula with Lower Boundary (XL), cumulative frequency (f0), frequency of median class (fD5), and class width (c)."],"examTip":"Know the equivalencies: Q2 = D5 = P50 = Median. These appear in multiple forms on exams."}},{"itemId":"db0ed8dc-d660-53e4-8b27-44fe2249810e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"topic-note:7","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"title":"Measures of Dispersion - Range, Variance, Standard Deviation","body":"Dispersion measures quantify the spread or variability in a dataset. They complement central tendency measures by describing how data points differ from the center.","noteType":"topic_note","keyPoints":["Range: Maximum - Minimum. Simplest measure but highly affected by outliers.","Variance: Average of squared deviations from the mean. σ² = Σ(x - x̄)² / N.","Standard Deviation: Square root of variance. σ = √(Variance). Expressed in same units as data.","Interquartile Range (IQR): Q3 - Q1. Resistant to outliers.","Quartile Deviation (Semi-IQR): (Q3 - Q1) / 2. Also called semi-interquartile range.","Coefficient of Variation (CV): (Standard Deviation / Mean) × 100. Unitless, used to compare variability across different datasets.","Mean Deviation: Is minimized when calculated from the Median.","Property: If variance = 0, all values are identical.","Property: Standard Deviation is always positive or zero."],"examTip":"If SD = 4, then Variance = 16. CV allows comparison between datasets with different units or scales."}},{"itemId":"2bc84b85-e191-5fb3-acee-2fc48c259769","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"topic-note:8","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"title":"Skewness and Kurtosis","body":"Skewness measures asymmetry in a distribution, while kurtosis measures the peakedness. These shape characteristics affect which statistical methods are appropriate.","noteType":"topic_note","keyPoints":["Skewness: Measures the direction and degree of asymmetry in a distribution.","Positive Skew (Right Skew): Long tail to the right. Mean > Median.","Negative Skew (Left Skew): Long tail to the left. Mean < Median.","Kurtosis: Measures the flatness or peakedness of a distribution relative to the normal distribution.","Leptokurtic: Peaked distribution (tall and thin).","Mesokurtic: Normal distribution (bell-shaped curve).","Platykurtic: Flat distribution (short and wide)."],"examTip":"Right skew = positive skew = tail points right. Mesokurtic = normal distribution."}},{"itemId":"8ca95079-6045-5666-81dd-ebc20a37b065","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"topic-note:9","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"title":"Probability Foundations - Sample Space and Events","body":"Probability quantifies the likelihood of events. Understanding basic probability rules is essential for inferential statistics and business decision-making under uncertainty.","noteType":"topic_note","keyPoints":["Sample Space: The set of all possible outcomes of a random experiment.","Event: A subset of the sample space (one or more outcomes).","Probability Range: 0 ≤ P(E) ≤ 1. Impossible event = 0, Certain event = 1.","Complement Rule: P(not A) = 1 - P(A).","Mutually Exclusive Events: Cannot occur simultaneously. P(A and B) = 0.","Addition Rule (Mutually Exclusive): P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B).","Independent Events: Occurrence of one does not affect the other.","Multiplication Rule (Independent): P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B).","Conditional Probability: P(A|B) = Probability of A given that B has occurred.","Classical Probability: Based on equally likely outcomes. P(E) = (Number of favorable outcomes) / (Total outcomes)."],"examTip":"For mutually exclusive events, use addition. For independent events, use multiplication."}}]$expected_content$::jsonb,
  'business-statistics: note published payload and identity match source');
select is((select count(*)::int from public.read_published_managed_content('40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003')
  where kind = 'cbt_question'), 0,
  'business-statistics: CBT questions are withheld from the student content read');
reset role;
select ok((select coalesce(jsonb_agg(jsonb_build_object(
  'itemId', item_id, 'courseId', course_id, 'kind', kind,
  'questionId', question_id, 'sourceKey', source_key,
  'parentItemId', parent_item_id, 'revision', revision,
  'payload', payload) order by source_key collate "C"), '[]'::jsonb)
  from (select i.id item_id, i.course_id, i.kind, i.question_id, i.source_key, i.parent_item_id,
    i.published_revision revision, r.payload from public.managed_content_items i
    join public.managed_content_revisions r on r.item_id = i.id and r.revision = i.published_revision
    where i.course_id = '40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003' and i.published_revision is not null) published
  where kind = 'cbt_question') = $expected_content$[{"itemId":"b5ad2d4a-ea93-5f72-8016-bceaefdf1db9","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"bd815bac-0f5c-4910-9b04-063e3ec28066","sourceKey":"quiz:1","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is the best definition of Statistics?","options":["The study of mathematical numbers and equations.","The science of collecting, organizing, presenting, analyzing, and interpreting data.","The process of guessing business outcomes based on intuition.","A branch of accounting that deals with audit trials."],"correctOption":1,"section":"Basic Concepts & Data Collection"}},{"itemId":"99279dc5-2f55-52c6-b237-8a095c79d54f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d1fc9509-2855-4678-b0cd-b4f9af85c516","sourceKey":"quiz:10","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A descriptive measure calculated from a sample is called a:","options":["Statistic","Parameter","Universe","Constant"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Basic Concepts & Data Collection"}},{"itemId":"d6feeded-12f6-5efd-893f-df684680fb93","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e72a3015-8b79-4a6b-88b5-a49521b911e9","sourceKey":"quiz:100","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Statistics is essential in business for:","options":["Quality control","Market research","Financial forecasting","All of the above"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Advanced Interpretation"}},{"itemId":"971de73c-becb-5d2d-b08e-5e38624007c9","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3db6dc54-2634-4891-8ad4-33e8ffcc77d2","sourceKey":"quiz:101","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In statistical terminology, a 'parameter' is best described as:","options":["A numerical summary computed from a sample","A numerical characteristic of a population","Any variable used in a regression model","A non-numerical attribute of data"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Statistical Terminology"}},{"itemId":"b9b28683-9cdb-5d85-804f-ffb176741631","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7b6ce1ed-0e9e-4373-9803-ea1c1741142c","sourceKey":"quiz:102","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which branch of statistics is concerned with drawing conclusions about a population based on sample data?","options":["Descriptive statistics","Inferential statistics","Exploratory data analysis","Applied mathematics"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Statistical Terminology"}},{"itemId":"77ecb5bf-5584-5dd2-8fee-8e166e2a15ed","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"217cf903-d57c-46db-a614-1e91f297cba7","sourceKey":"quiz:103","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An ordinal scale of measurement:","options":["Has a true zero point and equal intervals","Allows only the classification of data into named categories","Allows ranking but does not guarantee equal intervals between ranks","Has all properties of a ratio scale"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Statistical Terminology"}},{"itemId":"37605e91-ba5b-55c6-a8ab-576c79a25fed","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f896aed5-9ac0-4162-b43c-7e95b0b3da6a","sourceKey":"quiz:104","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The process by which a researcher ensures that every member of the population has an equal chance of being selected is called:","options":["Stratified sampling","Systematic sampling","Simple random sampling","Cluster sampling"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Sampling & Estimation"}},{"itemId":"f799816d-78fb-5d8b-aa25-a66d05f4e5d3","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0bb48f73-2811-4804-8b8a-a695403853b0","sourceKey":"quiz:105","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"'Respondent fatigue' in survey research refers to:","options":["The physical tiredness of field enumerators","A reduction in data quality due to declining respondent attention during lengthy surveys","The refusal rate among selected respondents","The tendency of respondents to give socially desirable answers"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Basic Concepts & Data Collection"}},{"itemId":"3d478c33-3636-5e6e-967a-50ef356b5c4c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"49e7e9a0-6863-47c6-8ccb-2f50d2e811be","sourceKey":"quiz:106","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A variable whose values arise from a counting process (e.g., number of customers per day) is called:","options":["Continuous","Qualitative","Discrete","Interval-scaled"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Statistical Terminology"}},{"itemId":"45ffb001-2ce2-533b-9263-cd97b6ab5325","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"25cae864-d528-4827-8b0a-668347b38c52","sourceKey":"quiz:107","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In a stratified random sample, the population is first divided into:","options":["Clusters of convenience","Mutually exclusive, homogeneous sub-groups called strata","Equal-sized intervals along a sampling frame","Random blocks selected by computer"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Sampling & Estimation"}},{"itemId":"19302529-22f4-5309-adc6-c9e4f8e607e0","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"cb3dcf74-91a8-4e46-83a1-36846e2c4871","sourceKey":"quiz:108","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The sampling frame is best defined as:","options":["The formula used to determine sample size","The physical list or device from which the sample is actually drawn","The entire target population","The questionnaire used to collect data"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Sampling & Estimation"}},{"itemId":"e7bfd1bf-633d-53e3-9dcd-90329846dcc7","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"11f4a012-156e-4754-a2a2-4383c1b3806f","sourceKey":"quiz:109","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following data collection errors arises when the selected sampling unit cannot be contacted?","options":["Response error","Interviewer bias","Non-response error","Coverage error"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Basic Concepts & Data Collection"}},{"itemId":"62ac9f3c-4a0d-527e-b866-16b1bb2974a2","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"db572a63-7b9a-45e3-a379-554b2623532c","sourceKey":"quiz:11","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"What is the first step in constructing a frequency distribution for a large data set?","options":["Find the mean.","Determine the range of the data.","Draw a histogram.","Calculate the standard deviation."],"correctOption":1,"section":"Data Presentation & Frequency Distributions"}},{"itemId":"69a76b4e-7e58-5a84-8923-ddb53415670f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c1499326-b3b7-4087-b716-5621cf9a115d","sourceKey":"quiz:110","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The distinction between 'data' and 'information' in statistics is that:","options":["Data is always numerical whereas information is qualitative","Information is raw and unprocessed while data is processed","Data refers to raw, unprocessed facts while information is data that has been processed into a meaningful form","Both terms are interchangeable in modern usage"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Statistical Terminology"}},{"itemId":"80d7eb43-d5f1-50b9-9f8f-6a612f4ce9ef","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"39fddb98-39a0-46df-b2de-be1a2f8d5e13","sourceKey":"quiz:111","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A frequency distribution's class boundaries differ from class limits in that:","options":["Class boundaries are always whole numbers","Class boundaries eliminate gaps between classes by extending each limit by 0.5 units","Class boundaries are the midpoints of each class","Class boundaries equal the class limits for continuous data"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Graphical Representation"}},{"itemId":"f7fdec52-649a-517c-8e57-2b82e62a44ef","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3c55d507-83c6-4e46-ace8-b36a4e5887c6","sourceKey":"quiz:112","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An ogive is constructed by plotting:","options":["Class midpoints against frequencies","Cumulative frequencies (or cumulative relative frequencies) against upper class boundaries","Lower class limits against class frequencies","Class widths against relative frequencies"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Graphical Representation"}},{"itemId":"dbb50d7a-6fc7-578b-b987-58b684263bc6","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"065044ae-da73-4366-8010-bd79bf8bd341","sourceKey":"quiz:113","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following graphical displays is MOST suitable for showing the relationship between two continuous variables?","options":["Pie chart","Scatter diagram","Frequency polygon","Bar chart"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Graphical Representation"}},{"itemId":"9a2aa111-408f-58e6-9975-0cfda3cf4f91","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"32f095af-fc60-4671-9bff-2c92fa5367b0","sourceKey":"quiz:114","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The 'less than' ogive for the following data at the upper boundary of the class 30–39 (given f=12, cumulative f up to 29=11) gives cumulative frequency of:","options":["11","12","23","35"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Data Presentation & Frequency Distributions"}},{"itemId":"2f59c493-2dc2-5358-8603-477cfbb7d136","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3b2f683e-ae31-4489-a0e9-6a48628ff5cf","sourceKey":"quiz:115","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In constructing a histogram, the area of each bar represents:","options":["The class midpoint","The class width","The class frequency (or relative frequency)","The cumulative frequency up to that class"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Graphical Representation"}},{"itemId":"9adec2b2-c481-577e-ac8a-a53390408d81","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"68da1029-5071-4d3f-8955-25fe72f4fe19","sourceKey":"quiz:116","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A Pareto chart combines which two graphical elements?","options":["A pie chart and a line graph","A bar chart (ordered by frequency) and a cumulative frequency line","A scatter plot and a histogram","A stem-and-leaf plot and a box plot"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Graphical Representation"}},{"itemId":"e6037368-084f-5f8b-9c61-9373adaf66a1","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3e7fc4ff-cf34-43fa-b489-18436e1e67ae","sourceKey":"quiz:117","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A time-series graph shows a consistent upward movement over years. This pattern is called a:","options":["Seasonal variation","Cyclical variation","Secular trend","Random fluctuation"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Graphical Representation"}},{"itemId":"832fa2f3-78e8-5238-83ed-3aa95b2518c7","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c658e497-7a27-41f3-9691-ae907d466371","sourceKey":"quiz:118","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"For a distribution, if the histogram is bell-shaped and symmetric, which of the following is TRUE?","options":["Mean > Median > Mode","Mode > Median > Mean","Mean = Median = Mode","Mean < Median = Mode"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Measures of Central Tendency"}},{"itemId":"e971139d-840b-5e14-b620-a84252c326c4","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a5e0c692-8169-4ee5-ae0c-73aea3e0a1c9","sourceKey":"quiz:119","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"False-baseline graphs are considered misleading because they:","options":["Use too many colours","Truncate the vertical axis, visually exaggerating differences between values","Combine too many categories in a pie chart","Present data in three dimensions unnecessarily"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Graphical Representation"}},{"itemId":"5e6252cc-4cd5-58b1-9834-cc1b75755f12","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a41249f5-775e-46ff-8e7f-efd1bdfc080c","sourceKey":"quiz:12","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The difference between the upper class boundary and the lower class boundary is the:","options":["Class Midpoint","Class Mark","Class Width (Interval)","Frequency"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Data Presentation & Frequency Distributions"}},{"itemId":"3886a29c-dca3-57e3-bb1a-8416a3eb5b4d","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e572bf76-2927-4730-ae6a-04859efd16ff","sourceKey":"quiz:120","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Data collected over successive time periods on the SAME variable are called:","options":["Cross-sectional data","Time-series data","Panel data","Longitudinal data"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Basic Concepts & Data Collection"}},{"itemId":"a2314e13-9466-5acf-a1a5-007d85d94cfb","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"507a07d9-e955-4a03-9412-dee8b02f6db8","sourceKey":"quiz:121","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"For the dataset {4, 7, 7, 9, 12, 15, 15, 15, 20}, the MODE is:","options":["7","9","15","12"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Measures of Central Tendency"}},{"itemId":"9abc1052-ba36-564f-9042-12ce53bcf098","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2d82939f-7531-4b87-9b23-ad2d5838fdc7","sourceKey":"quiz:122","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"For the dataset {3, 6, 8, 10, 13}, the MEDIAN is:","options":["6","8","9","10"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Measures of Central Tendency"}},{"itemId":"5e261d79-90e1-5da8-b604-a03720eeb6e6","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"18ed7b9b-44f8-4e7e-85f5-eaff3d9d7439","sourceKey":"quiz:123","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The MEAN of the values 12, 15, 18, 21, 24 is:","options":["18","19","20","21"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Measures of Central Tendency"}},{"itemId":"7adf00b5-becc-58eb-bf6d-a6fa76560c42","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6892e1f6-dbbf-4611-be07-e10b192c6d05","sourceKey":"quiz:124","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In a frequency distribution, the MODAL CLASS is the class with:","options":["The smallest class width","The highest frequency","The largest class boundary","The midpoint closest to the mean"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Measures of Central Tendency"}},{"itemId":"15085c3c-20bd-5329-bbbe-c5c0d806540a","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"bb44e01e-c84d-426c-95ee-bfa6bc18c228","sourceKey":"quiz:125","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The GEOMETRIC MEAN is most appropriate for averaging:","options":["Income levels of employees","Annual growth rates or index numbers over time","Examination scores","Temperature readings"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Measures of Central Tendency"}},{"itemId":"84bfd045-ec64-5d82-8db0-b09f02f965ea","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a81ddc67-1dc2-42e5-abbd-0624a4820f59","sourceKey":"quiz:126","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"For the dataset {5, 8, 10, 12, 15}, the MEAN ABSOLUTE DEVIATION (MAD) is:","options":["2.8","3.0","3.2","3.6"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Measures of Dispersion"}},{"itemId":"e2987745-3c14-5129-9caf-8d27b6476580","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1919cceb-2d2e-46a9-8628-94e18d87ac80","sourceKey":"quiz:127","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"If Σ(x − x̄)² = 240 and n = 15, the population STANDARD DEVIATION is:","options":["4","16","√16 = 4","√15"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Measures of Dispersion"}},{"itemId":"48e6992b-9edb-5b2d-9023-a06c5ddcf00c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"06dbdf19-c6a3-4302-8d6d-939586099eff","sourceKey":"quiz:128","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The COEFFICIENT OF VARIATION is useful for comparing dispersion across two datasets because it:","options":["Eliminates the effect of sample size differences","Expresses standard deviation as a percentage of the mean, removing the unit of measurement","Converts all values to z-scores before comparison","Uses the range instead of the standard deviation"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Measures of Dispersion"}},{"itemId":"ac3adcc7-a860-51c6-a2e0-5f91d7f62a9f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7705b618-bb1d-4646-bc6b-fa23ba0ab5a6","sourceKey":"quiz:129","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"For a dataset with mean = 50, median = 55, and standard deviation = 10, Pearson's first coefficient of skewness is:","options":["-1.5","-0.5","0.5","1.5"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Skewness & Kurtosis"}},{"itemId":"46b44b3d-a92d-5bc9-844e-ac51c0c6b627","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3e22930d-656b-458d-a9c3-23ec7e1b1fdb","sourceKey":"quiz:13","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A bar graph where the bars are adjacent (touching) and represent continuous data is a:","options":["Pie Chart","Pictogram","Histogram","Bar Chart"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Data Presentation & Frequency Distributions"}},{"itemId":"0cb8fe25-5e20-54df-80a6-49656fab7d71","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"867cb729-55ff-455a-81f9-14968b58bb42","sourceKey":"quiz:130","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A distribution is described as LEPTOKURTIC when its excess kurtosis (Kurt − 3) is:","options":["Equal to zero","Less than zero","Greater than zero","Equal to one"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Skewness & Kurtosis"}},{"itemId":"28491ed0-5db9-5a58-9a35-ff33e2f7d302","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ff160bf2-f71b-4532-87d7-98330042c1de","sourceKey":"quiz:131","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The SEMI-INTERQUARTILE RANGE (SIQR) is calculated as:","options":["Q3 − Q1","(Q3 − Q1) ÷ 2","(Q3 + Q1) ÷ 2","Q2 − Q1"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Measures of Dispersion"}},{"itemId":"7b344b00-f99c-5921-b00a-01a2e286e3f5","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"847e76f6-4626-4d38-8be4-a22d66548c4d","sourceKey":"quiz:132","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"For grouped data, the MEDIAN is estimated using the formula L + [(n/2 − F)/f] × h. In this formula, F represents:","options":["The frequency of the median class","The total number of observations","The cumulative frequency of all classes BEFORE the median class","The class width of the median class"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Measures of Central Tendency"}},{"itemId":"e82c0494-af21-54b1-91a2-fc3c90c6683a","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"cdb8142f-efd6-4f20-a76e-5619a55c7c71","sourceKey":"quiz:133","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which measure of location is MOST appropriate when data contains extreme outliers?","options":["Arithmetic mean","Weighted mean","Median","Mode"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Measures of Central Tendency"}},{"itemId":"7dd75690-0e44-5bd8-87d3-01e36395ed02","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ca9117de-4203-49b3-ba84-f1528dc3b912","sourceKey":"quiz:134","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"KURTOSIS as a descriptive measure captures:","options":["The direction and degree of asymmetry of a distribution","The peakedness (tail-heaviness) of a distribution relative to a normal distribution","The spread of data about the median","The proportion of data within two standard deviations of the mean"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Skewness & Kurtosis"}},{"itemId":"68c52b12-fece-5115-93de-385b19341450","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ae5dedb4-d3ce-4d7c-a59d-d53f09b3d017","sourceKey":"quiz:135","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"For a symmetric distribution with mean = 40 and standard deviation = 8, approximately what percentage of data lies between 24 and 56 (using the empirical rule)?","options":["68%","95%","99.7%","75%"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Measures of Dispersion"}},{"itemId":"1732ff71-31f7-5cec-bcec-0924aa8f5322","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"647d670e-342d-4ed2-b8df-3ee9e1c3b559","sourceKey":"quiz:136","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Chebyshev's theorem states that for ANY distribution, the proportion of values lying within k standard deviations of the mean is at least:","options":["1 − 1/k","1 − 1/k²","1/k²","k/(k+1)"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Measures of Dispersion"}},{"itemId":"07facdbf-14ec-5797-8485-13f42dfede22","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9cc8145a-06ee-49c2-b66f-802636b83e13","sourceKey":"quiz:137","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A bag contains 4 red, 5 blue, and 3 green balls. The probability of drawing a red ball at random is:","options":["1/3","1/4","1/5","4/12 = 1/3"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Probability Foundations"}},{"itemId":"ded71325-d01a-5541-902f-df204099817d","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a7502971-2c9b-47ad-8423-1fe77b07ebcd","sourceKey":"quiz:138","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Events A and B are such that P(A) = 0.6, P(B) = 0.5, and P(A ∩ B) = 0.3. P(A ∪ B) is:","options":["0.6","0.7","0.8","1.1"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Probability Foundations"}},{"itemId":"7a544a01-20f7-569c-9e86-939e0a99307d","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"767446a0-d715-4c2e-8669-1c3820a803f9","sourceKey":"quiz:139","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Using P(A) = 0.6, P(B) = 0.5, and P(A ∩ B) = 0.3, P(A | B) (probability of A given B) is:","options":["0.3","0.5","0.6","0.7"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Probability Foundations"}},{"itemId":"2fd033d0-2939-5222-8984-9423c81ace4a","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"402bc12b-0dfe-4957-bc91-459462ec352a","sourceKey":"quiz:14","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The sum of relative frequencies in any distribution should always equal:","options":["0","100","1.0","The total frequency"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Data Presentation & Frequency Distributions"}},{"itemId":"5c83fda2-a867-5fc4-b711-434446e08893","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c7f70129-4fa3-4a51-99d8-b8a57bfed675","sourceKey":"quiz:140","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"P(A) = 0.4, P(B) = 0.5. If A and B are INDEPENDENT, then P(A ∩ B) is:","options":["0.10","0.20","0.30","0.45"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Probability Foundations"}},{"itemId":"60e76914-5fc2-5219-b4a1-1685d0a06d37","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId"