  where kind = 'note') = $expected_content$[{"itemId":"77fd5219-370d-5f83-bad9-d039b15eb2f3","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"calculator-trick:1","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"title":"Price Allocation (IFRS 15 Bundled Sales)","body":"When allocating transaction prices to multiple performance obligations, use the percentage method to avoid rounding errors.","noteType":"calculator_trick","example":"Bundle sells for ₦700,000. Individual items worth ₦500,000 and ₦300,000 (total ₦800,000).\n\nQuick method:\n1. Calculate: 500 ÷ 800 = 0.625 (leave in calculator)\n2. Multiply: 0.625 × 700 = 437.5k for Item 1\n3. Calculate: 300 ÷ 800 = 0.375\n4. Multiply: 0.375 × 700 = 262.5k for Item 2\n\nResult: Item 1 = ₦437,500, Item 2 = ₦262,500 (Total = ₦700k ✓)","formula":"Allocated Price = (Standalone Price ÷ Total Standalone Prices) × Transaction Price"}},{"itemId":"3692d3ac-9ff7-5c9e-8b63-828c55dfa4cd","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"calculator-trick:10","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"title":"Inventory Valuation - Lower of Cost and NRV","body":"Always do the comparison before recording.","noteType":"calculator_trick","example":"Item A: Cost ₦50k, NRV ₦45k → Record ₦45k\nItem B: Cost ₦30k, NRV ₦35k → Record ₦30k\nItem C: Cost ₦80k, NRV ₦80k → Record ₦80k\n\nTotal Inventory = 45k + 30k + 80k = ₦155k\n\nWrite-down loss = (50-45) = ₦5k (expense)","formula":"Inventory Value = LOWER of (Cost, NRV)"}},{"itemId":"dc5f215f-7522-579d-9908-cacfb2540f2f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"calculator-trick:2","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"title":"Net Realizable Value (NRV) Calculation","body":"Always calculate NRV first, then compare with cost. Pick the LOWER value.","noteType":"calculator_trick","example":"Cost: ₦100,000\nSales Value: ₦220,000\nSelling costs: Advertisement ₦10,000 + Transport ₦50,000 + Rent ₦44,000 = ₦104,000\n\nStep 1: NRV = 220,000 - 104,000 = ₦116,000\nStep 2: Compare: Cost (100k) vs NRV (116k)\nStep 3: Pick LOWER = ₦100,000 ✓\n\nInventory recorded at ₦100,000","formula":"NRV = Estimated Sales Value - Estimated Costs to Sell"}},{"itemId":"e425d10e-a58e-57a8-9863-1242807c85c1","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"calculator-trick:3","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"title":"Reducing Balance Depreciation Rate","body":"Use the nth root function on your calculator to find the depreciation rate.","noteType":"calculator_trick","example":"Asset: ₦500,000, Scrap: ₦163,840, Life: 5 years\n\nCalculator steps:\n1. Type: 163840 ÷ 500000 = 0.32768\n2. Find 5th root: 0.32768 ^ (1 ÷ 5) = 0.8\n3. Subtract from 1: 1 - 0.8 = 0.2\n4. Convert to %: 0.2 × 100 = 20%\n\nDepreciation rate = 20% per year","formula":"Rate = 1 - ⁿ√(Scrap Value ÷ Cost)"}},{"itemId":"be235e41-144e-5be6-83c2-fa0e9a378eb3","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"calculator-trick:4","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"title":"Straight-Line Depreciation","body":"Quick method for consistent annual depreciation.","noteType":"calculator_trick","example":"Machine: ₦150,000, Scrap: ₦30,000, Life: 4 years\n\nQuick calculation:\n(150,000 - 30,000) ÷ 4 = 120,000 ÷ 4 = ₦30,000 per year\n\nYear 1: ₦30,000\nYear 2: ₦30,000\nYear 3: ₦30,000\nYear 4: ₦30,000","formula":"Annual Depreciation = (Cost - Scrap Value) ÷ Useful Life"}},{"itemId":"d0c2497d-21bd-57c3-82ae-9f899f677b2f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"calculator-trick:5","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"title":"Weighted Average Interest Rate (IAS 23)","body":"For general borrowings, calculate one unified rate instead of tracking each loan separately.","noteType":"calculator_trick","example":"Loan 1: ₦50m @ 3% = ₦1.5m interest\nLoan 2: ₦20m @ 6% = ₦1.2m interest\n\nStep 1: Total interest = 1.5m + 1.2m = ₦2.7m\nStep 2: Total principal = 50m + 20m = ₦70m\nStep 3: Rate = (2.7 ÷ 70) × 100 = 3.857%\n\nUse 3.857% on all construction expenditures","formula":"Capitalization Rate = (Total Interest on All Loans ÷ Total Principal) × 100"}},{"itemId":"7950ed60-9916-5392-98d9-4172fe590c7e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"calculator-trick:6","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"title":"Break-Even Point Calculation","body":"Quick two-step method for finding break-even units.","noteType":"calculator_trick","example":"Fixed Costs: ₦600,000\nSelling Price: ₦1,500\nVariable Cost: ₦900\n\nStep 1: Contribution = 1,500 - 900 = ₦600\nStep 2: BEP = 600,000 ÷ 600 = 1,000 units\n\nMust sell 1,000 units to break even","formula":"BEP (units) = Fixed Costs ÷ Contribution per Unit"}},{"itemId":"53f5f0b3-10e3-50e1-9817-eae79a21f18e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"calculator-trick:7","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"title":"Margin of Safety Quick Check","body":"Calculate how much cushion you have above break-even.","noteType":"calculator_trick","example":"Budgeted Sales: 2,000 units\nBreak-Even: 1,000 units\n\nMargin of Safety = 2,000 - 1,000 = 1,000 units\n\nAs %: (1,000 ÷ 2,000) × 100 = 50%\n\nSales can drop by 50% before making a loss","formula":"Margin of Safety = Budgeted Sales - Break-Even Sales"}},{"itemId":"c00b960b-5f71-5609-b0cb-f12b64b56f8c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"calculator-trick:8","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"title":"Government Grant - Capital Approach","body":"When deducting grant from asset cost, calculate new depreciable amount.","noteType":"calculator_trick","example":"Machine: ₦100m, Grant: ₦10m, Scrap: ₦0, Life: 10 years\n\nCapital Approach:\nNet Cost = 100m - 10m = ₦90m\nAnnual Depreciation = 90m ÷ 10 = ₦9m per year\n\n(Compare to ₦10m without grant)","formula":"Depreciable Amount = (Cost - Grant) - Scrap Value"}},{"itemId":"25370e19-306d-5a4a-b772-fda88e6fd959","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"calculator-trick:9","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"title":"Borrowing Cost Capitalization","body":"Only capitalize interest during ACTIVE construction months.","noteType":"calculator_trick","example":"Loan: ₦50m @ 12% per year\nConstruction: Jan-Aug (8 active months)\nStrike: Sep-Dec (4 idle months)\n\nCapitalize: 50m × 0.12 × (8 ÷ 12) = ₦4m\nExpense: 50m × 0.12 × (4 ÷ 12) = ₦2m\n\nTotal interest: ₦6m (split ₦4m asset, ₦2m expense)","formula":"Capitalized Interest = Loan Amount × Rate × (Active Months ÷ 12)"}},{"itemId":"5eb40a2c-bcd4-5574-a16f-24e35c1b47d5","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"topic-note:1","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"title":"IASB Framework for the Preparation and Presentation of Financial Statements","body":"The Conceptual Framework is not an accounting standard itself, but it provides the foundational rules, concepts, and definitions that the International Accounting Standards Board (IASB) uses to develop IFRS. Its main objective is to ensure financial statements provide transparent, high-quality, and useful information to investors and creditors.","noteType":"topic_note","keyPoints":["Fundamental Qualitative Characteristics: Information must be Relevant (capable of influencing user decisions) and a Faithful Representation (complete, neutral, and free from error).","Enhancing Qualitative Characteristics: Comparability, Verifiability, Timeliness, and Understandability.","Definition of an Asset: A resource controlled by the entity as a result of past events from which future economic benefits are expected to flow."],"examTip":"Know the difference between fundamental (Relevance and Faithful Representation) and enhancing qualitative characteristics."}},{"itemId":"06c82082-6f32-58ed-8584-985f733f7871","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"topic-note:10","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"title":"Cost and Management Accounting Fundamentals","body":"Cost accounting involves classifying, recording, and allocating costs to determine product costs and support management decisions. Key concepts include prime cost, conversion cost, and break-even analysis.","noteType":"topic_note","keyPoints":["Prime Cost: Direct Material + Direct Labour + Direct Expenses","Conversion Cost: Cost of converting raw materials into finished products (Direct Labour + Factory Overheads).","Sunk Cost: A cost that has already been incurred and cannot be recovered.","Marginal Costing: Fixed production overheads treated as period costs and written off to Profit or Loss.","Absorption Costing: Fixed production overheads charged to cost units (included in product cost).","Break-even Point (units): Fixed Cost ÷ Contribution per unit","Margin of Safety: Budgeted Sales - Break-even Sales","Economic Order Quantity (EOQ): Considers Annual Demand, Ordering Cost, Holding Cost.","Variable Cost: Varies directly in proportion to activity level.","Fixed Cost: Remains constant in total regardless of output volume (within relevant range).","Contribution: Sales Revenue - Variable Cost"],"examTip":"Know the formulas for Break-even Point and Margin of Safety. Understand the difference between Marginal and Absorption Costing."}},{"itemId":"30197d9b-d3db-5a45-b0b4-2a07215aade0","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"topic-note:2","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"title":"Introduction to IFRS 15 - Revenue from Contracts with Customers","body":"IFRS 15 establishes a single, comprehensive framework for determining when to recognize revenue and how much to recognize. The core principle is that revenue is recognized when control over goods or services is transferred to the customer.","noteType":"topic_note","keyPoints":["The 5-Step Model: (1) Identify the contract, (2) Identify performance obligations, (3) Determine the transaction price, (4) Allocate the transaction price, (5) Recognize revenue when control passes.","A contract only exists if collection of consideration is probable. If a customer has severe liquidity issues, you cannot recognize a contract.","Performance obligations are distinct promises to transfer goods/services (e.g., software license and technical support are separate).","Transaction price includes fixed and variable consideration (like volume discounts).","Control passes either at a point in time (goods) or over time (services).","Formula: Allocated Price = (Standalone Price ÷ Total Standalone Prices) × Transaction Price","🧮 CALCULATOR TIP: For bundle price allocation - Calculate percentage first (500 ÷ 800 = 0.625), leave in calculator, then multiply by bundle price (0.625 × 700 = 437.5). This avoids rounding errors."],"examTip":"Memorize all 5 steps - this is almost guaranteed to appear. Know when revenue is recognized \"at a point in time\" vs \"over time\"."}},{"itemId":"bd294dd5-b14f-5b96-ac88-e0b616959c46","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"topic-note:3","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"title":"IAS 1 - Presentation of Financial Statements","body":"IAS 1 sets the overall requirements, structure, and minimum content for general-purpose financial statements to ensure comparability with past periods and other entities.","noteType":"topic_note","keyPoints":["Complete Set of Financial Statements: (1) Statement of Financial Position, (2) Statement of Profit or Loss and OCI, (3) Statement of Changes in Equity, (4) Statement of Cash Flows, (5) Notes.","Current vs. Non-Current: An item is \"current\" if expected to be realized/settled within 12 months after the reporting period, held primarily for trading, or part of the normal operating cycle.","Expenses Classification: Expenses can be presented by Nature (depreciation, raw materials, wages) or by Function/Cost of Sales (cost of sales, administrative, distribution)."],"examTip":"Know all 5 components and the strict current vs. non-current classification rules."}},{"itemId":"4fac2aba-ca43-567a-b2a8-3585165894bd","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"topic-note:4","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"title":"IAS 2 - Accounting for Inventory","body":"IAS 2 dictates how to value inventories (raw materials, work in progress, and finished goods). The fundamental principle is that inventory must always be valued at the lower of cost and Net Realizable Value (NRV).","noteType":"topic_note","keyPoints":["Valuation Rule: Inventory = Lower of Cost and NRV.","NRV Formula: Estimated Sales Value - Estimated Costs to Sell.","Cost Components: Purchase price (less trade discounts), direct taxes, delivery/carriage inwards, and conversion costs (direct labor + factory overheads).","Strict Exclusions: Abnormal waste, storage costs (unless necessary for production), administrative overheads, selling costs, and interest.","Prohibited Methods: FIFO and Weighted Average are permitted; LIFO is strictly prohibited.","Write-down: If NRV falls below cost, write down to NRV and recognize the loss as an expense immediately.","🧮 CALCULATOR TIP: For NRV - If Cost = ₦100,000, Sales Value = ₦220,000, Selling Costs = ₦104,000. First calculate NRV (220,000 - 104,000 = ₦116,000). Then pick LOWER value between Cost (100k) and NRV (116k). Answer: ₦100,000."],"examTip":"Lower of cost and NRV is THE rule. LIFO is NEVER allowed. Know what to exclude (abnormal waste, admin costs, selling costs)."}},{"itemId":"fbd5318d-3689-51c0-bcd9-e988ec5fd8d5","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"topic-note:5","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"title":"Introduction to IAS 8 - Accounting Policies, Changes in Estimates and Errors","body":"IAS 8 prescribes the criteria for selecting accounting policies and the accounting treatment for changes in those policies, changes in estimates, and corrections of prior period errors.","noteType":"topic_note","keyPoints":["Change in Accounting Policy: (e.g., switching from cost model to fair value model). Applied RETROSPECTIVELY - adjust opening balances and past comparative amounts as if the new policy had always been applied.","Change in Accounting Estimate: (e.g., changing the useful life of an asset, or changing from straight-line to reducing balance depreciation). Applied PROSPECTIVELY - only affects current and future years.","Errors: Prior period errors are also corrected retrospectively."],"examTip":"Key distinction: Policy changes = retrospective. Estimate changes = prospective. This is very common in exams."}},{"itemId":"7a831529-9b24-5e62-a49c-4239e9394c64","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"topic-note:6","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"title":"IAS 16 - Property, Plant, and Equipment (PPE)","body":"IAS 16 deals with the recognition, initial measurement, and depreciation of tangible non-current assets held for use in production, rental, or administration for more than one period.","noteType":"topic_note","keyPoints":["Initial Cost: Purchase price + non-refundable taxes + directly attributable costs (site preparation, installation, delivery, testing, professional fees). Exclude general admin costs, training, and regular repair costs.","Depreciation: The systematic allocation of the depreciable amount (Cost - Scrap Value) over its useful life, obeying the matching concept.","Straight-Line Method: Depreciation = (Cost - Scrap Value) / Useful Life","Reducing Balance Method Rate: R = 1 - ⁿ√(Scrap Value / Cost)","Disposal: Profit/Loss on Disposal = Sales Proceeds - Carrying Amount (NBV)","🧮 CALCULATOR TIP (Reducing Balance): For 5-year life, scrap ₦163,840, cost ₦500,000. Type: (163840 ÷ 500000) ^ (1 ÷ 5) = 0.8. Then: 1 - 0.8 = 0.2 = 20% rate.","🧮 CALCULATOR TIP (Straight-Line): Example - Cost ₦150,000, Scrap ₦30,000, Life 4 years. Calculation: (150,000 - 30,000) ÷ 4 = ₦30,000 annual depreciation."],"examTip":"Know how to calculate both Straight-line and Reducing Balance depreciation. Practice the formulas with examples."}},{"itemId":"506f7eb2-b2f8-5240-966c-cc555e9e962a","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"topic-note:7","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"title":"IAS 20 - Government Grants","body":"IAS 20 handles the accounting for government assistance and transfers of resources to an entity tied to compliance with operating conditions.","noteType":"topic_note","keyPoints":["Recognition: Do not recognize a grant until there is reasonable assurance that the entity will comply with conditions AND the grant will be received.","Grants Related to Assets (Two Methods): (1) Capital Approach: Deduct the grant from the asset cost, lowering annual depreciation. (2) Income Approach: Keep asset at full cost, treat grant as Deferred Income and release to profit/loss over the asset's life to offset depreciation.","Grants Related to Income: Recognized in the income statement systematically over periods in which related costs/expenses are incurred."],"examTip":"Remember the two approaches for asset-related grants: deduct from cost OR treat as deferred income."}},{"itemId":"46259144-f5d2-5bb3-9325-5c706cd0dc07","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"topic-note:8","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"title":"IAS 23 - Borrowing Costs","body":"IAS 23 determines whether interest, finance charges, and amortization of discounts on debt should be expensed immediately or capitalized (added to the cost of an asset).","noteType":"topic_note","keyPoints":["Capitalization Rule: You MUST capitalize borrowing costs that are directly attributable to the acquisition or construction of a Qualifying Asset.","Qualifying Asset: An asset that takes a substantial period of time (like 2-5 years) to get ready for its intended use or sale.","Idle vs. Active Periods: Only capitalize interest during the active construction period. If construction is halted (e.g., strike) or hasn't started, expense the interest.","General Borrowings: If a pool of general loans is used, calculate a weighted average interest rate.","Capitalized Interest Formula (Specific Loan): Total Loan Principal × Interest Rate × (Active Construction Months / 12)","Weighted Average Rate: (Total Interest on all loans ÷ Total Principal on all loans) × 100","🧮 CALCULATOR TIP (Weighted Average): Add ALL loan interest first (₦1.5m + ₦1.2m = ₦2.7m). Then divide by total principal (₦70m). Result: 2.7 ÷ 70 = 3.86% rate. Apply this rate to construction expenditures."],"examTip":"Key question: \"Is this a qualifying asset?\" If yes = capitalize interest. If no = expense. Know when to stop capitalizing (when active construction stops)."}},{"itemId":"7055f2ab-06ae-53de-9cdc-f1841358d1df","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"topic-note:9","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"title":"Bookkeeping Fundamentals and Errors","body":"Bookkeeping involves recording all financial transactions systematically. Understanding different types of errors and how to correct them is crucial for maintaining accurate financial records.","noteType":"topic_note","keyPoints":["Accounting Equation: Assets = Capital + Liabilities","Error of Principle: Transaction entered in wrong class of account (e.g., motor expenses debited to motor vehicles).","Error of Transposition: Digits wrongly swapped (e.g., ₦4,466 written as ₦4,664).","Suspense Account: Temporary account opened when trial balance totals do not agree.","Unpresented Cheques: Cheques drawn but not yet presented to the bank for payment.","Bank Reconciliation: Bank charges not in cash book are deducted from cash book balance.","Imprest System: Fixed petty cash float maintained and exactly reimbursed.","Books of Original Entry: Purchases Daybook (credit purchases), Journal (non-standard entries)."],"examTip":"Know the different types of errors. Error of Principle is when you put a transaction in the wrong CLASS of account."}}]$expected_content$::jsonb,
  'financial-accounting-1: note published payload and identity match source');
select is((select count(*)::int from public.read_published_managed_content('40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007')
  where kind = 'cbt_question'), 0,
  'financial-accounting-1: CBT questions are withheld from the student content read');
reset role;
select ok((select coalesce(jsonb_agg(jsonb_build_object(
  'itemId', item_id, 'courseId', course_id, 'kind', kind,
  'questionId', question_id, 'sourceKey', source_key,
  'parentItemId', parent_item_id, 'revision', revision,
  'payload', payload) order by source_key collate "C"), '[]'::jsonb)
  from (select i.id item_id, i.course_id, i.kind, i.question_id, i.source_key, i.parent_item_id,
    i.published_revision revision, r.payload from public.managed_content_items i
    join public.managed_content_revisions r on r.item_id = i.id and r.revision = i.published_revision
    where i.course_id = '40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007' and i.published_revision is not null) published
  where kind = 'cbt_question') = $expected_content$[{"itemId":"2af88251-3d94-5d47-969b-c2e1abfee719","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1e787723-9d50-5078-abd3-d441ddb035c6","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0001","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers was issued in ______","options":["September 2007","January 2018","March 2018","May 2014"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"IFRS 15 was issued by the IASB in May 2014, replacing older revenue standards such as IAS 18 and IAS 11. Option B is the date the standard became effective, not when it was issued. Option C is when the revised Conceptual Framework was published, and option A relates to the revised IAS 1."}},{"itemId":"6a3900c4-f72a-5b98-bdd1-8a4bfdb1f305","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"daf74483-0e8f-5ff6-a97a-5fb07f689a92","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0002","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Revenue is to be recognised by a vendor when ______ over the goods or services is transferred to the customer.","options":["risk only","legal title","control","physical possession only"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"Under IFRS 15, revenue is recognised when (or as) control of the promised goods or services passes to the customer, meaning the customer can direct their use and obtain substantially all the remaining benefits. Options B and D are only indicators that control may have passed; they are not the test itself. Option A reflects the old risks-and-rewards idea, which IFRS 15 replaced with the control model."}},{"itemId":"939a3753-2e43-5b60-ae8d-df1a3f1653a9","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ff23a94f-331f-5699-97d7-f40f0b7139a3","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0003","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"______ is an agreement between two or more parties that creates enforceable rights and obligations.","options":["A contract","A performance obligation","A customer","A transaction price"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"IFRS 15 defines a contract as an agreement between two or more parties that creates enforceable rights and obligations, and identifying it is step one of the five-step model. Option B is a promise inside a contract to transfer a good or service. Option D is the consideration expected, and option C is one of the parties, not the agreement itself."}},{"itemId":"cafc0e0a-8bab-5504-98df-d5ed126d281e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"16862a7a-e798-5bf5-b186-7a22c70e9e72","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0004","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A party that has contracted with an entity to obtain goods or services that are an output of the entity's ordinary activities in exchange for consideration is generally referred to as ______","options":["a customer","a creditor","a supplier","a lessee"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"This is the IFRS 15 definition of a customer: someone who contracts to obtain the entity's ordinary outputs in exchange for consideration. Option C provides goods to the entity rather than buying from it. Option B is someone the entity owes money to, and option D obtains the right to use an asset under a lease, which falls under a different standard."}},{"itemId":"22ad4914-cdb2-59c6-8992-b248b9e81f8b","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"55449526-6ffb-5148-8e10-dc277551669e","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0005","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A software company sells a bundle that includes both a software licence and a 12-month support service for a total price of N1,300,000. If sold separately, the software licence would sell for N1,100,000 and the support service N600,000. Allocating the transaction price on the basis of relative stand-alone selling prices, how much is allocated to the software licence and the support service?","options":["Licence N650,000; Support N650,000","Licence N1,100,000; Support N200,000","Licence N841,176; Support N458,824","Licence N700,000; Support N600,000"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"The total stand-alone selling price is N1,100,000 + N600,000 = N1,700,000, so the licence gets 1,100,000/1,700,000 x N1,300,000 = N841,176 and the support gets 600,000/1,700,000 x N1,300,000 = N458,824 (together N1,300,000). Option B puts the whole N400,000 discount on the support service, and option D puts it all on the licence. Option A splits the price equally, ignoring the stand-alone selling prices."}},{"itemId":"8aed3469-1183-5631-9149-99c13dcbd09a","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"786ebfef-9701-5f54-825e-a46faa107267","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0006","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"There are two broad approaches to accounting for government grants related to assets: ______, which permits the grant to be recognised outside profit or loss, and the income approach, under which a grant is recognised in profit or loss over one or more periods.","options":["the deferred income approach","the cash approach","the accrual approach","the capital approach"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 20 Government Grants","explanation":"The capital approach treats a grant as a form of financing, so it is credited outside profit or loss, directly to equity. Option A is actually one way of applying the income approach, where the grant is spread to profit or loss. Options C and B are general bases of accounting, not grant approaches."}},{"itemId":"353e857a-8a2c-5f51-af0a-7173720bcccb","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f4595dc1-43eb-5193-932e-7a486033d332","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0007","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"There are two broad approaches to accounting for government grants related to assets: the capital approach, which permits the grant to be recognised outside profit or loss, and ______, under which a grant is recognised in profit or loss over one or more periods.","options":["the revaluation approach","the income approach","the cost approach","the equity approach"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 20 Government Grants","explanation":"Under the income approach, a grant is recognised in profit or loss over one or more periods, matching it against the costs it is meant to compensate; this is the approach IAS 20 requires. Option D describes taking the grant to equity, which is really the capital approach. Options C and A are measurement ideas for assets, not grant approaches."}},{"itemId":"6aff10b9-01d1-5c26-9bf0-0704a217036b","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"830ce150-2a4d-51d6-a232-3d67c71aabda","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0009","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Government grants shall be recognised in profit or loss on a ______ over the periods in which the entity recognises as expenses the related costs for which the grants are intended to compensate.","options":["systematic basis","straight-line basis only","once-off basis in the year of receipt","cash received basis"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 20 Government Grants","explanation":"IAS 20 requires grants to be recognised in profit or loss on a systematic basis, matched with the costs they are meant to cover. Options D and C would put the grant into profit when the money arrives, which breaks that matching. Option B is too narrow, because the pattern follows the related costs, which need not be straight-line."}},{"itemId":"6b356184-004e-5a76-b014-9d56b5c7faf1","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f617b9cf-98b7-5842-8b7d-74efbbcea1c4","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0010","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The effective date for IFRS 15 was ______","options":["1 January 2018","1 January 2020","1 January 2009","28 May 2014"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"IFRS 15 applies to annual periods beginning on or after 1 January 2018. Option D is when the standard was issued, not when it took effect. Option C is linked to the revised IAS 1, and option B is linked to the 2018 Conceptual Framework amendments."}},{"itemId":"4bd555d4-99a2-5d3b-bdbf-29bfc0ab0bac","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"86cf3e4f-87cf-59c8-a829-f65ec8d950b3","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0011","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"______ are grants given to businesses for any purpose other than to support the acquisition of non-current assets.","options":["Grants related to assets","Government assistance","Forgivable loans","Grants related to income"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 20 Government Grants","explanation":"Under IAS 20, any grant that is not a grant related to assets is a grant related to income, for example a grant to cover wages or running costs. Option A is the opposite category, given to buy or build non-current assets. Option B is the wider term that includes grants and non-monetary help, and option C is just one possible form a grant can take."}},{"itemId":"87363a5d-7a16-5ba9-8e5b-53e5b3d589b4","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"649426f4-22be-566c-9527-32819097320c","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0012","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In accordance with IAS 20, grants should only be recognised when there is reasonable assurance that ______","options":["the entity will comply with the conditions attaching to them and the grants will be received","the grants have been received in cash, even if conditions are not yet met","the related asset has been fully depreciated","the entity has made a profit for the year and the grant is material"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 20 Government Grants","explanation":"IAS 20 sets two conditions that must both hold: reasonable assurance that the entity will comply with the grant's conditions, and that the grant will actually be received. Option B is wrong because receiving cash alone is not enough. Options C and D are not recognition conditions at all."}},{"itemId":"75750800-7758-54cb-8d3b-f24fe527490a","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"59bb1c3b-028b-5ac6-aec7-c3068aaaf9cb","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0013","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The capital approach to accounting for grants permits the grant to be recognised as ______","options":["a deduction from the related expense","income in profit or loss over the useful life of the asset","a credit directly to shareholders' interests (equity), outside profit or loss","a current liability"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 20 Government Grants","explanation":"The capital approach treats a grant as a form of financing, not earned income, so it is credited directly to shareholders' interests in equity and never passes through profit or loss. Option B describes the income approach. Option A is a presentation choice for income-related grants under the income approach, and option D is not how either approach treats the grant."}},{"itemId":"ba1264af-b5d8-57ce-b628-69efa7da97e1","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"83f0ac2d-516c-5350-aef6-266f9492dc3a","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0014","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A ______ is a promise in a contract to transfer to the customer a good or service that is distinct from other goods or services in the contract.","options":["performance obligation","transaction price","stand-alone selling price","contract asset"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"A performance obligation is a promise to transfer a distinct good or service, and identifying these is step two of the IFRS 15 five-step model. Option B is the consideration the entity expects to receive. Option C is what an item would sell for on its own, used to allocate the price, and option D is a right to consideration for work already done."}},{"itemId":"f4543155-2062-5ee3-a8dc-130ae9537fa3","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"dc84bcc1-47ad-57ea-99b9-5c116898d075","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0015","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"During the year ended 31 December 2015, a company changes its accounting policies. In line with IAS 8, how should the accountant treat the change (assuming it is not covered by specific transitional provisions)?","options":["Recognise the full cumulative effect in current year profit or loss","Only disclose the change, without adjusting any figures","Apply the new policy retrospectively, adjusting the opening balance of equity for the earliest period presented and restating comparative figures","Apply the new policy prospectively from the date of change only, with no restatement"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 8 Accounting Policies, Estimates and Errors","explanation":"A voluntary change in accounting policy is applied retrospectively under IAS 8, as if the new policy had always been used, so opening equity for the earliest period shown is adjusted and comparatives are restated. Option D is the treatment for a change in accounting estimate, not a policy. Option A would distort the current year, and option B leaves the figures uncomparable."}},{"itemId":"6ed8891c-4af8-5f0f-9cb9-27637cfa5402","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1f01ceb2-a294-5242-8e73-66509caf8ac5","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0016","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Accounting information must have some characteristics that will make it useful for the intended users. The characteristic that states that there should be no change in the basis for the preparation of the accounting information from period to period is ______","options":["relevance","consistency","timeliness","understandability"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"Consistency means using the same accounting methods and bases from one period to the next, so users can compare results over time. Strictly, the Conceptual Framework treats consistency as something that supports comparability rather than listing it as a separate characteristic, but it is the only option that describes keeping the same basis each period. Option A is about information being able to influence decisions. Option C is about getting information to users in time to act on it, and option D is about presenting it clearly."}},{"itemId":"9b89d4f6-a5cd-5639-86af-b1801361b1b4","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"be43fb95-7d37-523a-b190-09919b84118c","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0017","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"According to IAS 8, how should a material error in the previous financial reporting period be accounted for in the current period?","options":["By applying the correction prospectively from the current period","By retrospective restatement: restating the comparative amounts for the prior period in which the error occurred","By correcting it in current year profit or loss as an exceptional item","By disclosing it without changing any amounts"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 8 Accounting Policies, Estimates and Errors","explanation":"IAS 8 requires material prior period errors to be corrected retrospectively, by restating the comparative figures for the period where the error happened (or the opening balances of the earliest period presented if it happened earlier). Option C would wrongly mix last year's mistake into this year's results. Option A is the treatment for changes in estimates, and option D leaves the error in the figures."}},{"itemId":"e4f71fec-1299-5366-99e5-59fe7153a444","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"815f667d-c8dd-50f5-883b-dcb9d32a8708","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0019","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is an objective of IAS 8?","options":["To set the rules for depreciating property, plant and equipment","To prescribe the criteria for selecting and changing accounting policies, so as to enhance the relevance, reliability and comparability of financial statements","To prescribe how government grants should be recognised","To prescribe how borrowing costs are capitalised on qualifying assets"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 8 Accounting Policies, Estimates and Errors","explanation":"IAS 8 aims to prescribe how accounting policies are selected and changed, and how changes in policies, estimates and errors are treated and disclosed, which makes financial statements more relevant, reliable and comparable. Option D belongs to IAS 23. Option C is IAS 20, and option A is IAS 16."}},{"itemId":"c4d1142c-af01-5b87-b7e0-5300304c728e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"11ce6461-cc66-58f5-ab61-63cdce96c689","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0020","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"IAS 23 focuses on ______","options":["accounting for borrowing costs","accounting for government grants","accounting for revenue from contracts with customers","accounting for inventories"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 23 Borrowing Costs","explanation":"IAS 23 Borrowing Costs sets out when borrowing costs are capitalised as part of a qualifying asset and when they are expensed. Option B is IAS 20. Option D is IAS 2, and option C is IFRS 15."}},{"itemId":"09caab0d-37e5-5893-bf3e-44ecbfb37cb4","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0babc214-a2f9-5191-b831-145ff1f25cd0","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0021","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The initial cost of an inventory is N10,000 and its net realisable value is N8,000. What is the written-down value of the inventory?","options":["N18,000","N10,000","N2,000","N8,000"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 2 Inventories","explanation":"IAS 2 measures inventory at the lower of cost and net realisable value. Here NRV of N8,000 is below cost of N10,000, so the inventory is written down to N8,000 and a N2,000 loss is expensed. Option B ignores the fall in value, option C is the size of the write-down rather than the carrying value, and option A adds the two figures."}},{"itemId":"b2d3b6d3-56e6-577d-af7d-a3887d128d4a","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"eb2cf9b4-7847-5496-8b58-64caef0d263e","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0022","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When is a building treated as inventory?","options":["When its cost exceeds N10,000,000","When it is used by the entity as its head office","When it is held for sale in the ordinary course of business, for example by a property developer","When it is held to earn rental income"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 2 Inventories","explanation":"Inventories are assets held for sale in the ordinary course of business, so a building built or bought by a property developer to sell is inventory under IAS 2. Option B describes owner-occupied property, which is property, plant and equipment under IAS 16. Option D is investment property, and option A is irrelevant because classification depends on purpose, not size."}},{"itemId":"d3f360c6-0f91-5506-b261-d67868c79228","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"dd450b40-c0ba-5439-9deb-83e32d771be6","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0023","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Borrowing costs that are directly attributable to the acquisition or construction of a \"qualifying asset\" must be ______ according to IAS 23 on borrowing costs.","options":["deducted from equity","expensed in profit or loss as incurred","treated as deferred income","capitalised as part of the cost of that asset"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 23 Borrowing Costs","explanation":"IAS 23 requires borrowing costs that are directly attributable to a qualifying asset to be capitalised as part of the asset's cost. Option B is how other borrowing costs are treated, and IAS 23 does not allow it for qualifying assets. Options A and C are not treatments of borrowing costs at all."}},{"itemId":"9ea64e43-5635-54a2-9fe5-b2a873eec6f0","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d8f2115e-87dc-5ac7-964c-6105262f75d6","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0024","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"______ and ______ are components of borrowing costs.","options":["Interest expense calculated using the effective interest method, and finance charges on leases","Dividends paid on ordinary shares, and share issue costs","Depreciation of the qualifying asset, and repairs","Income tax expense, and audit fees"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 23 Borrowing Costs","explanation":"IAS 23 describes borrowing costs as interest and other costs of borrowing funds, including interest expense under the effective interest method and interest on lease liabilities. Option B relates to equity, not borrowing. Options C and D are ordinary operating or tax costs with nothing to do with borrowing."}},{"itemId":"8f01c685-ef65-599e-8f0b-dcc2452b4df9","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7d5f4314-f528-5185-ac6d-6a467a37c757","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0025","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Ezechukwu Nigeria Limited bought an asset for N2,500,000 in January 2023. The company depreciates assets on the reducing balance method. The estimated residual (scrap) value of the asset is N420,175 and the expected useful life is 5 years. Using R = (1 - n-th root of (S/C)) x 100, what is the depreciation rate and the depreciation charge for year 1?","options":["20%; N500,000","16.8%; N420,000","30%; N623,948","30%; N750,000"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment","explanation":"S/C = 420,175 / 2,500,000 = 0.16807, and the fifth root of 0.16807 is 0.7, so R = (1 - 0.7) x 100 = 30%. Year 1 depreciation is 30% x N2,500,000 = N750,000. Option C applies the 30% rate to cost less scrap value (N2,079,825), but reducing balance applies the rate to the full opening carrying amount, with no deduction for scrap value. Option A is a straight-line rate (100%/5), and option B confuses the S/C ratio with the rate."}},{"itemId":"4fcc6320-33a2-5eab-84f6-7a00a72b1e41","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"debb9235-0952-595f-8137-652a395780f5","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0025-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Ezechukwu Nigeria Limited bought an asset for N2,500,000 in January 2023 and depreciates it on the reducing balance method at 30% per annum (residual value N420,175, useful life 5 years). What is the carrying amount of the asset at the end of year 3?","options":["N600,250","N250,000","N857,500","N1,225,000"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment","explanation":"Each year the 30% rate is applied to the opening carrying amount: year 1 depreciation N750,000 leaves N1,750,000, year 2 N525,000 leaves N1,225,000, and year 3 N367,500 leaves N857,500 (accumulated depreciation N1,642,500). Option D is the carrying amount after only two years. Option B wrongly charges 30% of the original cost every year, and option A is the carrying amount after four years."}},{"itemId":"5d86384b-3fd6-5b57-a0db-454ffa376d8a","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4223df42-41bf-5665-8335-f35f3038fe30","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0026","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Jugunnu bought a motor vehicle for N1,150,000 in January 2022. She depreciated it at 20% per annum on a straight line basis on its depreciable amount (cost less estimated scrap value of N150,000). The vehicle was disposed of in December 2024 for N720,000 after incurring N25,000 to put it in good sellable condition. What was the profit or loss on disposal?","options":["Loss of N30,000","Profit of N170,000","Loss of N55,000","Profit of N145,000"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment","explanation":"Annual depreciation is 20% x (N1,150,000 - N150,000) = N200,000, so after three years (2022 to 2024) accumulated depreciation is N600,000 and the carrying amount is N550,000. Net disposal proceeds are N720,000 - N25,000 = N695,000, giving a profit of N695,000 - N550,000 = N145,000. Option B forgets to deduct the N25,000 cost of getting it ready for sale. Options C and A charge only two years of depreciation."}},{"itemId":"b61c5d04-b6d0-5276-97b1-8913a44094e8","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"83f7dec0-a24b-5129-917d-af79ac0f7fe9","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0027","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"IAS 1 requires the reporting entity to prepare and present a complete set of financial statements. Which of the following is NOT a component of a complete set of financial statements under IAS 1?","options":["Statement of changes in equity","Directors' report","Statement of cash flows","Notes, comprising material accounting policy information and other explanatory information"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"Under IAS 1, a complete set comprises the statement of financial position, the statement of profit or loss and other comprehensive income, the statement of changes in equity, the statement of cash flows, and the notes, with comparative information. A directors' report may be required by company law, but it is outside the IAS 1 set. Options C, A and D are all required components."}},{"itemId":"2a329204-9fcf-598d-9c22-f86f74f44192","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ec5e42d4-33f8-5e16-b0d7-3b5519ab73a7","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0028","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Gbojunbe Limited's trial balance at 31 December 2024 shows (N'000): Inventory at 1 January 2024 1,300; Purchases 9,062; Sales 13,040. Closing inventory at 31 December 2024 is valued at N1,600,000. Using the cost of sales method, what is the gross profit for the year?","options":["N3,978,000","N2,678,000","N5,578,000","N4,278,000"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"Cost of sales = opening inventory N1,300,000 + purchases N9,062,000 - closing inventory N1,600,000 = N8,762,000, so gross profit = N13,040,000 - N8,762,000 = N4,278,000. Option B forgets to deduct closing inventory, and option C leaves out opening inventory. Option A is simply sales less purchases, ignoring both inventory figures."}},{"itemId":"73439d47-a7b3-552e-9d6c-92bb6dd0b788","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2050f4aa-8537-580d-a910-75242bac9d87","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0028-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Gbojunbe Limited's trial balance at 31 December 2024 includes (N'000): Inventory at 1 January 2024 1,300; Purchases 9,062; Sales 13,040; Furniture and fittings at cost 300; Irrecoverable debts 60; Insurance 69; Office expenses 115; Rates 21; General expenses 225; Rent received 119; Tax 982. Closing inventory is valued at N1,600,000 and depreciation on furniture and fittings is 10% per annum on cost. Taking rent received as other income and the tax figure as the tax charge for the year, what is the profit before tax?","options":["N3,877,000","N3,758,000","N3,907,000","N2,895,000"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"Gross profit is N13,040,000 - (N1,300,000 + N9,062,000 - N1,600,000) = N4,278,000; add rent received of N119,000 and deduct expenses of N60,000 + N69,000 + N115,000 + N21,000 + N225,000 + depreciation N30,000 = N520,000, giving N3,877,000. Option D is profit after deducting the N982,000 tax. Option B leaves out the rent received, and option C forgets the N30,000 depreciation."}},{"itemId":"00e3c08b-ea1c-5c21-b35f-8ee02b015a95","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7a04fcb1-416b-5801-9280-eb0c7d352042","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0029","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Power Holding Nigeria Limited's inventory records for the year ended December 2024: January opening balance 2,000 units at N2.00; February purchases 1,500 units at N2.50; March purchases 2,500 units at N3.00; April issues 2,500 units; May issues 1,000 units; June purchases 1,200 units at N3.50; July issues 1,500 units; August issues 1,200 units. The company uses FIFO. The closing inventory has an estimated sales value of N5,500 with costs to sell of N1,000. At what amount should closing inventory be measured under IAS 2?","options":["N5,500","N3,500","N4,500","N3,000"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 2 Inventories","explanation":"Under FIFO the oldest units go out first, so the 1,000 units left are all from the June batch at N3.50, a cost of N3,500. NRV is N5,500 - N1,000 = N4,500, and IAS 2 takes the lower of cost and NRV, which is N3,500. Option C is the NRV, which is higher than cost here. Option A ignores the costs to sell, and option D prices the units at the March cost of N3.00."}},{"itemId":"d14c7ae3-03ed-50d7-a927-460587cd5963","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"39d5689f-540b-5c9e-88ae-5d06eab68903","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0029-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Power Holding Nigeria Limited's inventory records for the year ended December 2024: January opening balance 2,000 units at N2.00; February purchases 1,500 units at N2.50; March purchases 2,500 units at N3.00; April issues 2,500 units; May issues 1,000 units; June purchases 1,200 units at N3.50; July issues 1,500 units; August issues 1,200 units. The company uses FIFO. The closing inventory has an estimated sales value of N5,500 with costs to sell of N1,000. Total revenue is N50,000 and operating expenses are N7,000. What is the profit before tax?","options":["N26,050","N20,558","N28,050","N27,050"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 2 Inventories","explanation":"Purchases total N3,750 + N7,500 + N4,200 = N15,450, so cost of sales = opening N4,000 + N15,450 - closing N3,500 (FIFO cost, below NRV of N4,500) = N15,950. Gross profit is N50,000 - N15,950 = N34,050, and after operating expenses of N7,000 profit before tax is N27,050. Option B is profit after 24% tax (N6,492). Option C values closing inventory at NRV of N4,500, and option A uses a closing inventory of N2,500."}},{"itemId":"e5294b46-3787-5929-ad8f-e875fcffcaff","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ba95e20a-6017-5926-984d-e4dc3080abff","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0030","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following correctly distinguishes accounting policies from accounting estimates under IAS 8?","options":["Accounting policies are monetary amounts that involve judgement, while accounting estimates are the rules used to prepare financial statements","A change in accounting estimate is applied retrospectively, while a change in accounting policy is applied prospectively","Accounting policies and accounting estimates mean the same thing and are treated the same way","Accounting policies are the principles, bases, conventions, rules and practices applied in preparing financial statements, while accounting estimates are monetary amounts subject to measurement uncertainty, such as useful lives or allowances for doubtful debts"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 8 Accounting Policies, Estimates and Errors","explanation":"Accounting policies are the specific principles, bases and rules an entity uses, while accounting estimates are the uncertain monetary amounts produced when applying those policies, such as a useful life, residual value or doubtful debt allowance. Option A swaps the two definitions. Option B reverses the treatments, since policy changes are retrospective and estimate changes are prospective. Option C is wrong because IAS 8 treats them differently."}},{"itemId":"ca31c416-fc3b-52cc-aa87-fcc4a603d84f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"57fb9e5a-70b1-5bcc-abee-7796d23d07ab","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0031","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Platinum Nigeria Limited (year ended 31 December 2024) has the following general borrowings, all outstanding for the whole year: 10% Bank Loan N10,000,000; 5% Debenture Loan N20,000,000; 20% Bank Loan N50,000,000. It used these general borrowings to finance construction of its tech city, a qualifying asset. Construction started on 1 April 2024, when N10,000,000 was spent, and a further N40,000,000 was spent on 1 June 2024. Under IAS 23, what capitalisation rate should be applied to the expenditure?","options":["5%, the lowest rate on the borrowings","11.67%, the simple average of 10%, 5% and 20%","15%, the weighted average rate on the general borrowings (N12,000,000 / N80,000,000)","20%, the highest rate on the borrowings"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 23 Borrowing Costs","explanation":"For general borrowings, IAS 23 uses a capitalisation rate equal to the weighted average of the borrowing costs on those loans. Total interest is N1,000,000 + N1,000,000 + N10,000,000 = N12,000,000 on borrowings of N80,000,000, so the rate is 15%. Option B ignores the size of each loan, while options D and A pick a single loan's rate with no basis."}},{"itemId":"bf79ad01-db21-55f0-b18f-c681e36a27de","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"45ee0e23-d0fe-5f95-b08b-1ccd03f626a0","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0031-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Platinum Nigeria Limited (year ended 31 December 2024) has the following general borrowings, all outstanding for the whole year: 10% Bank Loan N10,000,000; 5% Debenture Loan N20,000,000; 20% Bank Loan N50,000,000. It used these general borrowings to finance construction of its tech city, a qualifying asset. Construction started on 1 April 2024, when N10,000,000 was spent, and a further N40,000,000 was spent on 1 June 2024. Using a capitalisation rate of 15%, how much borrowing cost should be capitalised for 2024?","options":["N7,500,000","N4,625,000","N5,625,000","N7,375,000"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 23 Borrowing Costs","explanation":"Capitalisation starts when each amount is spent: N10,000,000 x 15% x 9/12 = N1,125,000 (April to December) plus N40,000,000 x 15% x 7/12 = N3,500,000 (June to December), a total of N4,625,000. The rest of the N12,000,000 total interest, N7,375,000, is expensed, which is option D. Option A charges a full year on the N50,000,000, and option C charges the N40,000,000 for nine months instead of seven."}},{"itemId":"146496a3-310a-5f09-964a-9ffd151febb7","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4806904a-b74f-5a0a-a87e-ade5ec4ce7de","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0032","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"IAS 8 sets criteria that must be met before an entity changes an accounting policy. Under IAS 8, an entity shall change an accounting policy only if the change ______","options":["is needed because the entity revised the useful life of an asset","increases reported profit for the year","is approved by the majority of shareholders at the AGM","is required by an IFRS, or results in financial statements providing reliable and more relevant information"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 8 Accounting Policies, Estimates and Errors","explanation":"IAS 8 allows a change in accounting policy only when an IFRS requires it, or when the change gives reliable and more relevant information about transactions and events, for example adopting a new standard. Option B is not a valid reason, because policies cannot be changed just to improve results. Option C is not an IAS 8 criterion, and option A is a change in accounting estimate, not a policy change."}},{"itemId":"9f8a34df-c0d0-55e0-8a62-cab3f97724e8","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"022c0754-b811-5981-a588-b6f1ff6fe82f","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0033","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The primary objective of the IASB Conceptual Framework is to:","options":["Detect fraud in financial statements","Provide guidance for developing IFRS Standards","Provide a basis for taxation","Replace all accounting standards"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"The Conceptual Framework exists mainly to help the IASB develop IFRS Standards built on consistent concepts, and to help preparers when no standard applies. Option A is wrong because fraud detection is an audit matter. Option C is wrong because tax rules are set by tax law, and option D is wrong because the Framework is not a standard and does not override any standard."}},{"itemId":"c7354ca4-7567-58b6-8393-239c9141df0f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0148a106-72b1-517f-b4b8-d00e16128527","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0034","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"According to the Conceptual Framework, the objective of general-purpose financial reporting is to ______","options":["Provide information useful to investors, lenders, and other creditors","Provide information for tax authorities","Show management performance only","Provide information for government planning"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"General-purpose financial reporting aims to give useful information to existing and potential investors, lenders and other creditors for their decisions about providing resources to the entity. Options B and D name users the Framework does not treat as primary users. Option C is too narrow because reports cover the entity's resources, claims and performance, not just management."}},{"itemId":"41f42ecc-b404-5bd6-93d3-4aa371f86998","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"752e8e14-3ed6-5ca6-bab7-7afc05762e9a","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0035","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The fundamental qualitative characteristics of useful financial information are ______ and ______","options":["Comparability and Verifiability","Timeliness and Understandability","Neutrality and Prudence","Relevance and Faithful Representation"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"Relevance and faithful representation are the two fundamental qualitative characteristics; information must have both to be useful. Options A and B list enhancing characteristics (comparability, verifiability, timeliness, understandability), which add to usefulness but are not fundamental. Option C names neutrality, which is part of faithful representation, and prudence, which supports neutrality."}},{"itemId":"9302ef54-4659-5feb-9509-9fffcfc63a2a","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"fdf34278-c01f-5db1-a54d-cdceb01c7dd2","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0036","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT an enhancing qualitative characteristic of useful financial information?","options":["Comparability","Materiality","Verifiability","Timeliness"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"Materiality is an entity-specific aspect of relevance, not an enhancing qualitative characteristic. The four enhancing characteristics are comparability, verifiability, timeliness and understandability, so options A, C and D are all on the list."}},{"itemId":"249e7727-50a5-51da-a7e0-b78bf18963e2","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"255f1c27-a52e-53da-adbc-6ace5ee341ea","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0037","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Information is considered relevant if it ______","options":["has predictive or confirmatory value","is free from error","is comparable across companies","is easy to understand"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"Relevant information can make a difference to users' decisions because it has predictive value, confirmatory value or both. Option B describes part of faithful representation. Option C describes comparability, and option D describes understandability, which are enhancing characteristics."}},{"itemId":"52669db7-84e5-5748-ab98-acb22787f0fb","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d01d4f35-6ef0-5d1a-8334-bfa553630ed1","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0038","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Faithful representation requires information to be ______, ______, and ______","options":["Comparable, timely, and understandable","Relevant and material","Complete, neutral, and free from error","Verifiable and consistent"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"To be a faithful representation, information should be complete, neutral and free from error, showing the substance of what it claims to show. Option A lists enhancing characteristics. Option B mixes relevance with materiality, and option D mixes verifiability with consistency, none of which are the three components of faithful representation."}},{"itemId":"1b3755df-361d-5e18-bad9-e704324b3636","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"52bdaaab-eaa6-5916-b6eb-6b1dfe2045e0","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0039","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An asset is defined as ______ according to the Conceptual Framework","options":["Any item owned by a company for which cash is paid not bought on credit","Cash held in the bank","Property and equipment only","A resource controlled by the entity as a result of past events from which future economic benefits are expected"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"An asset is a resource controlled by the entity as a result of past events, from which future economic benefits are expected; control, past events and economic benefits are the main ideas. Option A is wrong because assets bought on credit are still assets. Options B and C are too narrow, because receivables, inventory and intangibles are also assets."}},{"itemId":"f2fde8b3-14a3-5beb-94d9-2adcdee39ed6","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ecf59fba-dd1e-5f04-98c1-edb27610ae40","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0040","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"According to the IASB framework, a liability is defined as ______","options":["Any future expense from which liability arises","A future planned payment arising from credit transactions","Owner's equity","A present obligation arising from past events"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"A liability is a present obligation of the entity arising from past events, which is expected to lead to an outflow of economic resources. Options A and B describe future plans or expenses, which create no present obligation. Option C is wrong because owner's equity is the residual interest after deducting liabilities, not a liability."}},{"itemId":"2a5251a0-591d-5c67-a92f-830cb17b2485","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4e3c347a-1681-59bf-845b-29cc3a2d3cf6","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0041","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"According to the IASB framework, Equity is defined as ______","options":["Share capital contributed originally by the owners","Total liabilities arising from past and future credit transactions","Total assets both bought for cash and on credit","The residual interest in the assets after deducting liabilities"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"Under the IASB Conceptual Framework, equity is the residual interest in the assets of the entity after deducting all its liabilities, so it is simply what is left for the owners. Option A is too narrow because equity also includes retained earnings and reserves, not just the share capital first contributed. Options B and C describe liabilities and assets, which are the two elements used to work out equity, not equity itself."}},{"itemId":"33583f39-a878-51f8-9a8b-6955cddc5144","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"41ff95ae-c98e-52d8-91db-2c0d6af4d2e5","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0042","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is a measurement basis under the Conceptual Framework?","options":["Historical cost","Market value only","Replacement cost only","Tax value"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"Historical cost is one of the measurement bases recognised by the Conceptual Framework, alongside current value bases such as fair value, value in use and current cost. Options B and C are wrong because of the word \"only\" - the Framework does not restrict measurement to a single market or replacement figure. Option D, tax value, is a figure used for tax purposes and is not a measurement basis in the Framework."}},{"itemId":"767f453a-2074-5f7c-a0e8-725ba1ac852f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"47a1735b-3b24-5066-8b04-5d5c347049e9","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0044","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Accounting policies are ______","options":["Broad basic assumptions which underlie the preparation of financial statements","Monetary amounts in the financial statements that are subject to measurement uncertainty","Financial accounting standards to be followed.","The specific principles, bases, conventions, rules and practices applied by an entity in preparing and presenting financial statements."],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 8 Accounting Policies, Estimates and Errors","explanation":"Accounting policies are the specific principles, bases, conventions, rules and practices that an entity applies when preparing and presenting its financial statements, which is option D. Option B describes accounting estimates, such as useful lives or allowances for doubtful debts, which are the amounts produced when a policy is applied. Option A describes underlying assumptions such as going concern, and option C describes the standards themselves rather than the entity's own chosen policies."}},{"itemId":"80ee539f-90fa-5511-bf42-6249cfb05ecd","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b847f46b-7ab3-5440-9942-cb6f0113c572","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0045","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT correct regarding the provisions of IAS 8 on accounting changes and error corrections?","options":["A change in accounting estimate is reflected in the current and future periods.","A change in depreciation method (such as from an accelerated method to the straight-line method) is classified as a change in estimate.","A change in depreciation method (such as from accelerated method to the straight-line method) is classified as a change in accounting policy.","IAS 8 generally requires restating prior results to improve the comparability of financial statements."],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 8 Accounting Policies, Estimates and Errors","explanation":"Under IAS 16 and IAS 8, a change in depreciation method (for example from an accelerated method to straight-line) reflects a change in the expected pattern of using up the asset, so it is a change in accounting estimate, not a change in accounting policy - that makes option C the incorrect statement. Option B says the same thing correctly, and option A is also correct because estimate changes are applied prospectively to the current and future periods. Option D is correct too, since policy changes and prior period errors are generally applied retrospectively so that periods stay comparable."}},{"itemId":"a974b8a1-629e-55d1-b0c8-3bd22e0b902b","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a5feda02-4535-5cee-a8cb-b6cf5b6b2817","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0048","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which term best describes applying a new accounting policy to transactions as if that policy had always been applied?","options":["Retrospective application","Retrospective restatement","Prospective application","Prospective restatement"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 8 Accounting Policies, Estimates and Errors","explanation":"Retrospective application means applying a new accounting policy to transactions, other events and conditions as if that policy had always been applied. Option B, retrospective restatement, is the term for correcting prior period errors as if they had never occurred. Option C, prospective application, applies the new policy only from the date of change, and option D is not a term used in IAS 8."}},{"itemId":"649385b2-5d90-59d0-bf6b-a6517033d5d2","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e7b3e421-486f-5db0-a544-ec37b2686760","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0049","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"All of the following should be treated as a change in accounting policy, except ______","options":["A new accounting policy of capitalizing development cost as a project has become eligible for capitalization for the first time.","A new policy resulting from the requirement of a new IFRS.","To provide more relevant information, items of property, plant and equipment are now being measured at fair value, whereas they had previously been measured at cost.","All of these qualify as change in accounting policy."],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 8 Accounting Policies, Estimates and Errors","explanation":"Applying a policy to transactions that differ in substance from earlier ones, or that occur for the first time, is not a change in accounting policy - so capitalising development costs once a project first becomes eligible is just applying the existing policy. Option B is a change in policy because a new IFRS requires it, and option C is a change in policy because moving from the cost model to the revaluation model changes the measurement basis. Option D is wrong because option A does not qualify."}},{"itemId":"cfc5df30-49c6-599a-885c-a45a5c73d749","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"476db845-7e68-51a3-9741-294438240be5","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0050","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"On March 25, 2018, the entity discovered that depreciation expense for 2017 was overstated. The 2017 financial statements were authorized for issue on April 1, 2018. What must the entity do?","options":["Correct the 2017 financial statements before issuing them.","Reduce depreciation for 2018.","Restate the depreciation expense reported for 2017 in the comparative figures of the 2018 financial statements.","Do nothing"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 8 Accounting Policies, Estimates and Errors","explanation":"The error was found on March 25, 2018, before the 2017 financial statements were authorised for issue on April 1, 2018, so the entity simply corrects the 2017 statements before issuing them. Option C would apply only if the statements had already been issued. Option B wrongly pushes the correction into 2018, and option D ignores a known error."}},{"itemId":"fdc2ac78-af4d-524e-b4e6-f43b983fe646","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f0fa146f-35fe-5be4-8c74-a0bd6e93b97b","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0051","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"On March 25, 2018, the entity discovered that depreciation expense for 2017 was overstated. The 2017 financial statements were authorized for issue on March 1, 2018. What must the entity do?","options":["Reissue the 2017 financial statements with the correct depreciation expense.","Reduce depreciation for 2018.","Restate the depreciation expense reported for 2017 in the comparative figures of the 2018 financial statements.","Do nothing"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 8 Accounting Policies, Estimates and Errors","explanation":"Here the 2017 financial statements were already authorised for issue on March 1, 2018 when the error was found, so IAS 8 requires retrospective restatement: the 2017 depreciation is corrected in the comparative figures presented in the 2018 financial statements. Option A is wrong because IAS 8 corrects errors through the comparatives, not by reissuing old statements. Option B treats the error like a change in estimate, and option D ignores it."}},{"itemId":"86b255c0-4079-5064-9ff4-fae97fc4a8ff","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a763ce98-44a0-5667-8a38-5bda47dccf63","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0052","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"How should a change in accounting estimate be accounted for?","options":["As a non-adjusting event per IAS 10","Retrospectively with restated comparative financial statements","Retrospectively with an adjustment to opening retained earnings","Prospectively"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 8 Accounting Policies, Estimates and Errors","explanation":"A change in accounting estimate, such as a revised useful life, is accounted for prospectively - it affects the current period and future periods only, and earlier figures are not restated. Options B and C describe retrospective treatment, which is used for changes in accounting policy and correction of errors. Option A is wrong because IAS 10 deals with events after the reporting period, not estimate changes."}},{"itemId":"7bc02520-c285-5dda-b12d-11c2e7b941c7","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3c3b4702-9df9-577d-97a1-697527ea7079","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0053","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"IAS 2 focuses on...","options":["Borrowing Costs","Operational Lease","Inventory Cost","Business combination"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 2 Inventories","explanation":"IAS 2 is the standard on inventories, setting out how the cost of inventory is measured and when it is written down to net realisable value. Option A is IAS 23 (borrowing costs), option B relates to leases (IFRS 16, formerly IAS 17) and option D is IFRS 3 (business combinations)."}},{"itemId":"f904c62c-2145-5356-9cc8-7e8bcc8641ed","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3a9db66a-eb65-54d2-8380-390d9f9f34f3","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0055","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In recognising the cost of inventory to be recognised in the income statement, one of the following inventory valuation methods is not allowed by IAS 2","options":["LIFO","FIFO","Weighted average method.","A and B"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 2 Inventories","explanation":"IAS 2 does not allow the LIFO (last-in, first-out) cost formula; inventory cost must be assigned using FIFO or the weighted average cost formula (or specific identification for items that are not interchangeable). Options B and C are both permitted methods, so they are wrong. Option D is wrong because FIFO is allowed."}},{"itemId":"a3ed3034-9aa3-5701-9f93-0c7c5e6a6a36","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"bd846b0d-5370-5785-a568-60dd7ab158ef","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0056","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The inclusion of management fee and service charges are what types of borrowing costs?","options":["Interest","Ancillary costs","Finance charge","Inflation"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 23 Borrowing Costs","explanation":"Management fees and service charges paid to arrange a loan are ancillary costs - extra costs incurred in connection with arranging the borrowing, on top of the interest itself. Option A is wrong because interest is the charge for using the money, not the fees for setting up the loan. Option D, inflation, is not a borrowing cost at all."}},{"itemId":"e944ef02-776b-5cc8-ae66-6a75fa11730c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"34785f74-ff72-523b-b4d8-9efa98fc36be","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0058","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"For the purpose of capitalising borrowing costs under IAS 23, borrowings are divided into how many types?","options":["1","2","3","4"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 23 Borrowing Costs","explanation":"For capitalising borrowing costs, IAS 23 distinguishes two types of borrowing: specific borrowings taken out to finance a particular qualifying asset, and general borrowings used for several purposes. The other options do not match these two categories."}},{"itemId":"835c7756-23d8-51a7-9f3d-415d36281a47","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"dc3a33c8-c7e4-5ad0-a0fd-4132aae6cbe0","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0059","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When loans are taken from more than one source with different rates, the method recommended by IAS in computing the interest rate is....","options":["Effective rate method","Weighted average method","Mark-up method","Initial borrowing rate method"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 23 Borrowing Costs","explanation":"When an entity has general borrowings from several lenders at different rates, IAS 23 requires a capitalisation rate equal to the weighted average of the borrowing costs on those general borrowings. Option A, the effective interest method, is how interest on each individual loan is measured, not how the rate across several loans is combined. Options C and D are not methods used under IAS 23."}},{"itemId":"f990f9f3-fd93-59ee-b72c-b3ff5952e0f0","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5a7b4e38-a691-5c19-afae-45f1f91ca817","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0060","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"IAS 8 __________ disclosure of an impending change in accounting policy when an entity has yet to implement a new IFRS that has been issued but not yet come into effect.","options":["prohibits","requires","encourages","Allows"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 8 Accounting Policies, Estimates and Errors","explanation":"When a new IFRS has been issued but is not yet effective and the entity has not applied it, IAS 8 requires the entity to disclose that fact together with known or reasonably estimable information about the possible impact on its financial statements. Options C and D are too weak because the disclosure is compulsory, not just encouraged or allowed. Option A is the opposite of the rule."}},{"itemId":"aaa7cf6b-9428-53ab-81eb-9a11c76998fd","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"40157fc1-7b85-548c-84d1-bde96f834dd0","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0062","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which, if any, of the following statements is correct? Statement 1 - An entity cannot change from applying straight line depreciation to reducing balance depreciation, or Statement 2 - A change in where depreciation is charged in the statement of profit or loss is a change in accounting estimate?","options":["Statement 2 only is correct","Neither statement is correct","Statement 1 only is correct","Both statements are correct"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 8 Accounting Policies, Estimates and Errors","explanation":"Neither statement is correct. Statement 1 is false because an entity can change from straight-line to reducing balance depreciation when the pattern of using the asset changes - this is a change in accounting estimate. Statement 2 is false because changing where depreciation is presented in the statement of profit or loss is a change in presentation, which is a change in accounting policy, not an estimate. So options A, C and D are all wrong."}},{"itemId":"76d0ef23-2aad-57d0-a9ac-6d30f093ff96","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7ecafd5b-04ee-5388-adba-a2e9b628a83e","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0064","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"It is __________ to make, or leave uncorrected, immaterial departures from IFRSs to achieve a particular presentation of an entity's financial position, financial performance or cash flows.","options":["required","inappropriate","permissible","advisable"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 8 Accounting Policies, Estimates and Errors","explanation":"IAS 8 states that it is inappropriate to make, or leave uncorrected, even immaterial departures from IFRSs in order to achieve a particular presentation, because deliberately using small departures to shape the picture defeats faithful presentation. Options A, C and D are wrong because such departures are never required, acceptable or advisable."}},{"itemId":"c1254c06-ee93-5db4-b366-52cb31a3ba96","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"027f0294-8483-5fab-9d13-0b262b3a7c6e","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0069","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A non-current asset was purchased for N200,000 at the start of year 1, when its expected economic life was ten years and its expected residual value was nil. The asset is depreciated by the straight-line method. A review at the start of year 2 (the current year) revealed that, due to technological change, the useful life of the asset is only six years in total. The revised estimate is applied from the beginning of year 2. What is the annual depreciation charge for year 2 and future years?","options":["N20,000","N33,333","N36,000","N45,000"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 8 Accounting Policies, Estimates and Errors","explanation":"A change in useful life is a change in estimate, so it is applied prospectively from the current year: the carrying amount at the start of year 2 is N200,000 - N20,000 = N180,000, and with five years left (years 2 to 6) the charge is N180,000 / 5 = N36,000 a year. Option A is the old charge of N200,000 / 10, which ignores the revision. Option B spreads the original cost over six years as if the new life had applied from the start, which is a retrospective approach, and option D divides N180,000 by four years, forgetting that year 2 itself still needs a charge."}},{"itemId":"1ce8c8b1-627d-513d-bdcd-85a204df6647","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9265f6d2-b516-5f20-9f98-b054413c7f54","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0070","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A business is a going concern with no intentions to reduce or curtail its operations. Which would be the most relevant valuation for its machinery - the net realisable value of the machinery or its depreciated cost?","options":["Net realisable value, because the machinery could be sold at any time","Depreciated cost, because the business will continue to use the machinery rather than sell it","Scrap value, because it is the most prudent figure","Original cost with no depreciation, because the machinery is still in use"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"For a going concern the machinery is expected to be used in the business, not sold, so its depreciated cost is the most relevant value. Option A would only be more relevant if the business were not a going concern and the assets were likely to be sold. Option C undervalues an asset that is still earning money, and option D ignores the fact that the machinery wears out over time."}},{"itemId":"9563eff8-08fb-58bd-9410-c5f621d6f274","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f1c67702-d481-577b-bdb5-872113ac84d0","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0071","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Coy ABC has a customer, Coy XYZ, which is undergoing restructuring due to issues relating to liquidity. Coy ABC has decided not to do any further business with Coy XYZ. In response, Coy XYZ informed Coy ABC that it would get a letter of credit from a nationalised bank against which Coy ABC can dispatch goods worth $15,000. Coy ABC has already manufactured the goods exclusively for Coy XYZ, but the letter of credit has not yet been finalised. When should Coy ABC recognise the revenue?","options":["Not until the letter of credit is finalised (so payment becomes probable) and the goods are transferred to Coy XYZ","Immediately, because the goods have already been manufactured exclusively for Coy XYZ","As soon as Coy XYZ says it will obtain a letter of credit","Never, because Coy XYZ is undergoing restructuring"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"Under IFRS 15 a contract only exists for revenue purposes if it is probable that the entity will collect the consideration, so Coy ABC must wait until the letter of credit is finalised and then recognise revenue when control of the goods passes to Coy XYZ. Option B is wrong because making the goods does not transfer control or prove collectability. Option C is wrong because a promise to get a letter of credit is not yet security for payment, and option D is wrong because the contract can still qualify later once the criteria are met."}},{"itemId":"c9883880-787e-5a86-af3c-7ea2f540a81e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a62eaee4-f0bf-5e24-a643-ac962c188098","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0072","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"On 1 April 2024, BigBoss Ltd agrees to sell 1,000 Endoscopy Machines to SmallBoss Ltd, which are produced using a pre-fabricated tool supplied by another vendor. The payment terms between BigBoss Ltd and SmallBoss Ltd have not been decided as they are dependent on the price of the pre-fabricated tool, which is yet to be finalised. BigBoss Ltd was paid a non-refundable amount of $4,500. How should BigBoss Ltd account for this transaction under IFRS 15 if BigBoss was the principal in this transaction?","options":["Recognise the $4,500 as revenue immediately because it is non-refundable","Recognise revenue for all 1,000 machines on 1 April 2024","Record the $4,500 as a liability until the IFRS 15 contract criteria are met","Credit the $4,500 directly to equity"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"Because the payment terms have not been identified, the arrangement does not yet meet the IFRS 15 criteria for a contract with a customer, so the $4,500 received is held as a liability until those criteria are met. Option A is tempting because the money cannot be refunded, but non-refundable cash alone does not make it revenue while the contract is incomplete. Option B is wrong because no machines have been transferred, and option D is wrong because a customer payment is never equity."}},{"itemId":"4b73bf1e-77f0-5372-bd2d-6234fdb4f38d","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1afac196-43d9-53ac-82c2-583f7f7f0c83","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0073","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"ABC Limited enters a contract with a customer to supply a licence for a standard software product. ABC Limited will also install the software, provide updates to the software and technical support for five years. ABC Limited sees the licence and technical support separately; the software will continue to operate without the software updates, and ABC Limited sub-contracts the installation of the software to several approved installers throughout the country. Identify the distinct goods or services (performance obligations).","options":["One: the whole bundle is a single performance obligation","Two: the software licence and technical support only","Three: the software licence, software updates and technical support only","Four: the software licence, installation service, software updates and technical support"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"Each promise is distinct because the customer can benefit from it on its own and it is separately identifiable: the licence works without updates, support is seen separately, and installation can be done by other installers, so there are four performance obligations. Option A treats the bundle as one item even though the parts are not dependent on each other. Option C leaves out installation, but the fact that approved installers elsewhere can do it shows it is a distinct service, and option B also leaves out the updates."}},{"itemId":"7f5d7234-e60d-56b1-8d2b-5063a6b33359","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"cb5d4f5f-c436-5167-b3eb-36d0f1cf3070","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0074","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"ABC Limited enters into a contract with XYZ to sell its product for N200 per unit for the year 01.01.17 - 31.12.17. If XYZ purchases more than 1,200 units in the year, the price will decrease to N150 per unit (for all units). At the start of the contract ABC Limited did not expect XYZ to purchase more than 1,200 units. On 30.06.17, XYZ bought 600 units and another 500 units on 01.10.17, after which ABC Limited believed XYZ would meet the target. XYZ then bought a further 200 units on 1 December 2017. What is the total revenue ABC Limited should recognise from XYZ for the year ended 31.12.17?","options":["N260,000","N225,000","N180,000","N195,000"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"Once the 1,200-unit target is expected to be met, all units are priced at N150, so total revenue is 1,300 units x N150 = N195,000 (N120,000 + N75,000 + N30,000 - N30,000 adjustment on the first 600 units). Option A prices all 1,300 units at N200 and ignores the volume discount. Option B forgets to reduce the first 600 units from N200 to N150, and option C only counts 1,200 units."}},{"itemId":"2299c9a8-af80-547d-9089-eb2b1e4beb36","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6bc72f4e-2741-5f48-bb9d-94e4a1b416c4","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0074-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"ABC Limited enters into a contract with XYZ to sell its product for N200 per unit for the year 01.01.17 - 31.12.17. If XYZ purchases more than 1,200 units in the year, the price will decrease to N150 per unit (for all units). At the start of the contract ABC Limited did not expect XYZ to purchase more than 1,200 units. On 30.06.17, XYZ bought 600 units and another 500 units on 01.10.17, after which ABC Limited believed XYZ would meet the target. XYZ then bought a further 200 units on 1 December 2017. When ABC Limited revises its expectation on 01.10.17, what adjustment must it make to the revenue already recognised on the first 600 units?","options":["Reduce revenue by N30,000","Increase revenue by N30,000","Reduce revenue by N25,000","No adjustment is needed"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"The first 600 units were recorded at N200 each (N120,000), but once the discount is expected to apply they are worth only N150 each (N90,000), so revenue is reduced by 600 x N50 = N30,000 (Dr Revenue, Cr Trade receivables). Option B moves revenue in the wrong direction. Option C applies the N50 difference to the 500 units of the second sale instead, and option D ignores that variable consideration must be updated when expectations change."}},{"itemId":"8e39c057-cb20-552d-bb90-66a0aabec50a","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"441e2f7a-144c-52be-b9dc-202258660df2","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0075","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A software company sells a bundle that includes both a software license and a 12-month support service for a total price of N1,200,000. If sold separately, the software license would cost N1,000,000 and the support service N300,000. How would you allocate the transaction price (to the nearest naira)?","options":["Software license N1,000,000; support service N200,000","Software license N923,077; support service N276,923","Software license N900,000; support service N300,000","Software license N600,000; support service N600,000"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"The price is allocated in proportion to stand-alone selling prices: total N1,300,000, so the licence gets N1,000,000 / N1,300,000 x N1,200,000 = N923,077 and support gets N300,000 / N1,300,000 x N1,200,000 = N276,923. Option A puts the whole N100,000 discount on the support service, and option C puts it all on the licence, but a bundle discount is shared across all obligations unless there is evidence it belongs to one. Option D simply splits the price equally."}},{"itemId":"484a9e14-984e-574b-acb8-b9ac4531da8f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"89ab5c61-4d8b-5aa7-89e5-0e371485dd96","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0076","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A retailer sells a customer a package consisting of a computer and a printer for N700,000. The retailer realises that there are two separate performance obligations involved. It regularly sells the computer for N500,000 and the printer for N300,000. How will the retailer allocate the total transaction price between the computer and the printer?","options":["Computer N437,500; printer N262,500","Computer N400,000; printer N300,000","Computer N500,000; printer N200,000","Computer N350,000; printer N350,000"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"Stand-alone prices total N800,000, so the computer gets N500,000 / N800,000 x N700,000 = N437,500 and the printer gets N300,000 / N800,000 x N700,000 = N262,500, sharing the N100,000 discount in proportion. Options B and C load the whole discount onto one item, which IFRS 15 does not allow without evidence that the discount relates only to that item. Option D splits the price equally and ignores the stand-alone prices."}},{"itemId":"d9fb99e3-453f-5721-b243-b34fa2a5de7c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e85f419f-3179-56d4-ab51-ce3ffd840da1","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0077","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"From the information provided, determine the cost to be capitalised in the statement of financial position. Motor car N4,500,000; Registration number N150,000; Road license fees N25,000; Delivery charge for the car N35,000; Cost of testing the car N100,000; Purchase of office premises N8,000,000; Legal fees associated with purchase of office N800,000; Driver's wages for first year of operation N1,200,000. What is the cost of the motor vehicle to be capitalised?","options":["N4,785,000","N4,810,000","N4,685,000","N5,985,000"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment","explanation":"Under IAS 16 the cost includes the purchase price plus costs directly needed to get the asset ready for use: N4,500,000 + N150,000 registration + N35,000 delivery + N100,000 testing = N4,785,000. Option B adds the N25,000 road licence, which is a recurring running cost and is expensed. Option C leaves out the testing cost, which IAS 16 specifically allows, and option D adds the driver's wages, which are an operating expense."}},{"itemId":"7f3699ec-d83a-5515-a89e-bed340726fb0","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8faa37d2-1b4b-5ba9-b1c5-ce8f816c8f5e","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0077-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"From the information provided, determine the cost to be capitalised in the statement of financial position. Motor car N4,500,000; Registration number N150,000; Road license fees N25,000; Delivery charge for the car N35,000; Cost of testing the car N100,000; Purchase of office premises N8,000,000; Legal fees associated with purchase of office N800,000; Driver's wages for first year of operation N1,200,000. What is the cost of the office premises (land and building) to be capitalised?","options":["N8,000,000","N8,800,000","N7,200,000","N10,000,000"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment","explanation":"Legal fees on buying property are directly attributable costs of acquiring it, so the capitalised cost is N8,000,000 + N800,000 = N8,800,000. Option A leaves out the legal fees and option C wrongly deducts them. Option D adds the driver's wages of N1,200,000, which relate to running the business, not to the building."}},{"itemId":"9e4badf6-258b-5523-aca1-7b27dc056044","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6d50fc1e-ae38-5d3c-8333-539917101a42","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0078","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An asset (Motor Car) was bought for N2,500,000 with an expected residual value of N250,000. If the useful life of the asset is 5 years, determine the annual depreciation using the straight-line method of depreciation","options":["N450,000","N500,000","N550,000","N225,000"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment","explanation":"Straight-line depreciation is (cost - residual value) / useful life = (N2,500,000 - N250,000) / 5 = N2,250,000 / 5 = N450,000 a year. Option B forgets to deduct the residual value, and option C adds the residual value instead of subtracting it. Option D divides by 10 instead of the 5-year useful life."}},{"itemId":"ea246d27-10d2-5b21-a764-5aadab9a8b12","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6d799b7d-7e43-5551-9b46-a2d1c5f68ce3","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0079","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"ABC bought a motor vehicle for N500,000 with an expected useful life of 5 years. The expected residual value of the asset is N163,840. The reducing balance method of depreciation is used. What is the annual depreciation rate?","options":["20%","13.45%","32.77%","80%"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment","explanation":"The reducing balance rate is r = 1 - (residual / cost)^(1/n) = 1 - (163,840 / 500,000)^(1/5) = 1 - 0.8 = 0.2, or 20%. Option B is what you get by treating it like straight-line ((500,000 - 163,840) / 5 as a percentage of cost). Option C is just the ratio 163,840 / 500,000, and option D is the fraction of the balance kept each year (0.8), not the rate charged."}},{"itemId":"1355db23-8eb4-5652-b6ee-3a5ab62ee38e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"89d9c7c5-38dd-586c-9294-be621481cad2","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0079-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"ABC bought a motor vehicle for N500,000 with an expected useful life of 5 years. The expected residual value of the asset is N163,840. The reducing balance method of depreciation is used. What is the depreciation charge for year 3?","options":["N64,000","N67,232","N80,000","N100,000"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment","explanation":"At a 20% rate the carrying amount falls from N500,000 to N400,000 in year 1 and N320,000 in year 2, so the year 3 charge is 20% x N320,000 = N64,000. Option B is the straight-line charge (N500,000 - N163,840) / 5. Option C is the year 2 charge, and option D wrongly applies 20% to the original cost every year."}},{"itemId":"71a606b3-94bd-537e-9717-f9c00d64d02c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4c64341d-0e42-5121-82d4-bc08e226889a","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0080","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"XYZ Nigeria Limited received a grant of N10,000,000 to finance the acquisition of a special model of Micro Magnetic machine used in a specialist hospital. The cost of the machine is N100,000,000. The machine is to be depreciated on a straight-line basis over five years with no residual value. If the grant is deducted from the cost of the asset, what is the annual depreciation charge?","options":["N20,000,000","N18,000,000","N22,000,000","N2,000,000"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 20 Government Grants","explanation":"Deducting the grant gives a net cost of N100,000,000 - N10,000,000 = N90,000,000, and N90,000,000 / 5 = N18,000,000 a year. Option A depreciates the full cost, which is what happens under the deferred income method instead. Option C adds the grant to the cost, and option D is the yearly share of the grant, not the depreciation."}},{"itemId":"ef861f48-1f45-5f26-9845-a7c77a239132","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5266d1f4-a3e1-55cb-a908-403ba197bf2a","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0080-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"XYZ Nigeria Limited received a grant of N10,000,000 to finance the acquisition of a special model of Micro Magnetic machine used in a specialist hospital. The cost of the machine is N100,000,000. The machine is to be depreciated on a straight-line basis over five years with no residual value. If the grant is treated as deferred income, how much of the grant is credited to profit or loss each year?","options":["N10,000,000","N2,000,000","N20,000,000","N18,000,000"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 20 Government Grants","explanation":"Under the deferred income method the machine is depreciated at N100,000,000 / 5 = N20,000,000 a year, and the N10,000,000 grant is released to profit or loss over the same five years at N10,000,000 / 5 = N2,000,000 a year. Option A credits the whole grant in year 1, which ignores matching the grant with the depreciation it helps fund. Option C is the depreciation charge, and option D is the net depreciation under the deduction method."}},{"itemId":"5d83af32-9783-5e21-9786-a887418e1e3e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2568de33-3bc4-5a25-a471-8194bf6b3e77","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:A201-0081","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"XYZ Nigeria Limited received a grant of N30,000,000 to build an aircraft hangar over a period of five years. The construction cost is estimated to be N20,000,000, which would be incurred over the five years as follows: Year 1 N2,000,000; Year 2 N3,000,000; Year 3 N4,000,000; Year 4 N5,000,000; Year 5 N6,000,000. The grant is to be recognised as income in proportion to each year's construction cost. How much grant income should be recognised in Year 4?","options":["N6,000,000","N5,000,000","N7,500,000","N21,000,000"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 20 Government Grants","explanation":"IAS 20 matches grant income with the related costs, so Year 4 income is (N5,000,000 / N20,000,000) x N30,000,000 = N7,500,000. Option A spreads the grant equally over five years (N30,000,000 / 5) and ignores the cost pattern. Option B is the cost incurred in Year 4, not the grant income, and option D is the cumulative grant income for Years 1 to 4 (N3,000,000 + N4,500,000 + N6,000,000 + N7,500,000)."}},{"itemId":"3fc942c0-f15a-5048-ba83-90ec2a818a60","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8d7e053e-96bc-5526-8ab0-610c2b13a197","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:C201N1-001","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Power Holding Nigeria Limited uses FIFO. Its opening inventory cost N4,000 and purchases for the year cost N15,450. Closing inventory has a cost of N3,500 and a net realisable value of N4,500. What is the cost of sales for the year?","options":["N14,950","N19,450","N22,950","N15,950"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 2 Inventories","explanation":"Cost of sales is opening inventory plus purchases less closing inventory: N4,000 + N15,450 - N3,500 = N15,950. Closing inventory stays at its cost of N3,500 because that is lower than its NRV of N4,500. N14,950 wrongly deducts the NRV of N4,500 instead of cost, and N19,450 forgets to deduct closing inventory at all."}},{"itemId":"6667e2ef-4424-5259-987f-2d5613d925bb","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"dc34f8c2-00a1-5223-9aa5-e84322969c57","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:C201N1-002","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A farm in Epe harvests tomatoes from its plants. Which standard, rather than IAS 2, deals with the tomatoes at the point of harvest?","options":["IAS 23","IAS 16","IAS 41","IFRS 9"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 2 Inventories","explanation":"Agricultural produce at the point of harvest, together with biological assets related to agricultural activity, is excluded from IAS 2 and dealt with by IAS 41 Agriculture. IFRS 9 is the other scope exclusion, but it covers financial instruments, not farm produce. IAS 16 deals with property, plant and equipment, and IAS 23 deals with borrowing costs."}},{"itemId":"7856fe86-d5bc-5fc7-acf5-86fe535eaba3","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e390e9b5-b4d9-507f-94f0-e0b8909da6b5","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:C201N1-003","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A manufacturing firm in Ikeja measures its finished goods using the standard cost method. How does IAS 2 treat this?","options":["It is never allowed, as only FIFO is permitted","It is allowed if the results approximate cost","It is allowed only if the results equal selling price","It is allowed only for retailers, not manufacturers"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 2 Inventories","explanation":"IAS 2 permits techniques such as the standard cost method and the retail method for convenience, provided the results approximate cost. The idea that only FIFO is permitted is wrong, because weighted average and specific identification are also allowed cost formulas. Limiting the method to retailers is also wrong; it is the retail method, not standard cost, that suits retailers."}},{"itemId":"b2ee4fb4-f17b-5c91-80db-00a3c9d943f7","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4981029c-039b-5522-ab06-69e33acbf365","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:C201N1-004","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Platinum Nigeria Limited has general borrowings outstanding all year: 10% loan of N10m, 5% debenture of N20m and 20% loan of N50m, giving a capitalisation rate of 15%. On its qualifying asset it spent N10m on 1 April and N40m on 1 June, the start of construction being 1 April. For the year ended 31 December, how much borrowing cost is recognised as an expense?","options":["N4,500,000","N12,000,000","N10,875,000","N7,375,000"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 23 Borrowing Costs","explanation":"Total interest is N1m + N1m + N10m = N12m. The capitalised part is N10m x 15% x 9/12 = N1,125,000 plus N40m x 15% x 7/12 = N3,500,000, which is N4,625,000, so N12,000,000 - N4,625,000 = N7,375,000 is expensed. N4,500,000 wrongly treats both amounts as outstanding for the full 12 months, but interest before construction began on 1 April cannot be capitalised."}},{"itemId":"8b6083ea-0cee-5d37-a46e-626002a91981","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b1d5d05f-1b40-53fa-a2c0-3ee010333cfd","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:C201N1-005","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A company in Lagos borrows in US dollars to build a factory. Under IAS 23, exchange differences arising on this foreign currency borrowing form part of borrowing costs","options":["To the extent they are regarded as an adjustment to interest costs","In full, whether they are gains or losses on the loan","Never, because they are always taken directly to equity","Only when the loan is repaid before the factory is complete"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 23 Borrowing Costs","explanation":"Borrowing costs include exchange differences on foreign currency borrowings only to the extent that they adjust the interest cost of the loan. Including them in full goes too far, because any part of the exchange difference beyond that adjustment is not a borrowing cost. Saying they are never included is also wrong, since IAS 23 lists them as one component of borrowing costs."}},{"itemId":"cdfe6e86-6777-54ab-b5d0-a905f6870e76","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c4c226a1-ea14-5b9c-887c-4cd7cba39f9d","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:C201N1-006","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An investment firm in Victoria Island uses borrowed money to buy shares quoted on the stock exchange. Why are these shares not a qualifying asset under IAS 23?","options":["Financial assets are not qualifying assets","They were bought with general, not specific, borrowings","Only assets built for sale can be qualifying assets","The interest rate on the borrowing is too low"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 23 Borrowing Costs","explanation":"IAS 23 states that financial assets, such as quoted shares, are not qualifying assets, so interest on money borrowed to buy them is expensed. Whether the funds came from general or specific borrowings affects how the capitalised amount is measured, not whether the asset qualifies. Limiting qualifying assets to items built for sale is wrong, because plants, power stations and investment properties built for use can also qualify."}},{"itemId":"49943914-33bf-542e-97f8-3f783233965e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"486c92b5-5cc2-5e81-bbeb-3b4ec6c751db","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:C201N1-007","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A shipbuilder in Apapa builds an ocean-going vessel for sale to a customer, a job that takes about 18 months. The vessel is held as inventory during construction. Can it be a qualifying asset under IAS 23?","options":["No, because inventories can never be qualifying assets","Yes, but only if the funds came from a specific loan","Yes, inventories that take a substantial period to produce can qualify","No, because only assets for the entity's own use qualify"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 23 Borrowing Costs","explanation":"A qualifying asset is one that necessarily takes a substantial period of time to get ready for its intended use or sale, and inventories that take a long time to produce meet this test. Only inventories produced over a short period are excluded. The idea that inventories can never qualify confuses this long-production case with routine, quickly produced stock, and the type of borrowing does not decide whether an asset qualifies."}},{"itemId":"9eb641f3-a1d4-5593-8737-ea6c5d958261","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a1d2bfba-dc09-5c33-a89d-e90caa9695f1","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-001","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Generally Accepted Accounting Principles (GAAP) vary from country to country mainly because","options":["the legal and regulatory systems of countries are not the same","the IASB issues a different set of standards for each country","each country uses a different reporting currency","companies in different countries differ in size"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"GAAP differ across countries because each country has its own legal and regulatory system, and those rules shape local accounting practice. Option C is wrong because currency does not change the principles applied, and option B is wrong because the IASB issues one uniform set of IFRS for worldwide use."}},{"itemId":"ebbe28f5-4e40-5fb1-a32f-380a5edcc3c6","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3ea2d659-ee6f-563c-848b-d4456c06d5f2","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-002","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Common sets of principles and procedures that define the basis of financial accounting policies and practices are known as","options":["accounting standards","special-purpose financial statements","elements of financial statements","exposure drafts"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"Accounting standards are the common principles and procedures on which an entity's accounting policies and practices are based, covering assets, liabilities, revenue, expenses and equity. Option D is only a proposed standard issued for comment, and option C refers to the broad classes such as assets and income, not to rules of practice."}},{"itemId":"d8a4f9b6-3a5b-522b-b035-f48465ee020b","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"fa9052cd-0662-574c-9bdc-d8f37b31fc97","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-003","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following statements about the legal status of accounting standards is correct?","options":["They are binding only on the members of the IASB","They apply only to companies listed on a stock exchange in the United Kingdom","They automatically become law in every country once issued by the IASB","They do not have the force of law but become effective when a country adopts them"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"Accounting standards are not law by themselves; they become effective in a country only when that country adopts them, as Nigeria did for listed companies. Option C is wrong because IASB pronouncements have no automatic legal force, and option A is wrong because the standards are meant for entities, not for the Board members who write them."}},{"itemId":"5eabe12e-3c31-59aa-b68b-737cd9dd226d","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3b5f475f-f19b-5479-a1de-0869c328c869","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-004","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Accounting standards apply to","options":["only the revenue and expenses of an entity","only the statement of financial position of listed companies","the whole spectrum of an entity's transactions and events, including assets, liabilities, revenue, expenses and equity","only transactions involving cash"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"Accounting standards cover all of an entity's financial transactions and other events, so assets, liabilities, revenue, expenses and equity all fall within them. Options A and D are too narrow, and option B wrongly limits them to one statement and one type of company."}},{"itemId":"e6b9045f-d7e0-59ee-8262-57e56294cd98","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"cbae55ee-48c4-5cfa-a3ae-c50fd528ad2e","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-005","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The body responsible for issuing new International Financial Reporting Standards (IFRSs) is the","options":["Nigerian Exchange Group","Federal Inland Revenue Service","International Accounting Standards Board (IASB)","Financial Reporting Council of Nigeria (FRCN)"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"The IASB, often called the Board, issues new IFRSs and has full responsibility for all technical matters. Option D, the FRCN, is the Nigerian body through which compliance with IFRS is ensured locally, but it does not issue IFRSs, and option A is a stock exchange, not a standard setter."}},{"itemId":"d8bd0049-6d3d-593f-aec6-1ceca1718a96","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"daec07e1-d438-597d-a519-eda26d88a5cc","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-006","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT one of the documents issued by the IASB?","options":["Final interpretations of the IFRS Interpretations Committee","Revised International Accounting Standards and IFRSs","Exposure drafts of standards","Audited financial statements of listed companies"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"Audited financial statements are prepared by companies and audited by their auditors; the IASB does not issue them. Options C, B and A are all genuine IASB outputs: exposure drafts, revised IASs and IFRSs, and final IFRS Interpretations Committee interpretations."}},{"itemId":"ed9b8208-c06c-5de6-a970-a39e7654325b","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"87652537-9036-557d-b8bb-34c40773455c","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-007","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is a responsibility of the IASB?","options":["Preparing the annual financial statements of listed companies","Issuing the Conceptual Framework that sets out the concepts underlying financial statements","Deciding how much tax companies should pay","Auditing the financial statements of multinational companies"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"The IASB issues the Conceptual Framework, which sets out the concepts behind the preparation and presentation of financial statements, as well as the IFRSs themselves. Option A is the job of each company's management, option D is done by external auditors, and option C is a government function."}},{"itemId":"de20d04d-3003-5fbe-9caf-ef340770d698","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"969ccbcc-06f9-5436-b02c-dce4e6078e00","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-008","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A coherent system of interrelated objectives and fundamental concepts that prescribes the nature, function and limits of financial accounting and reporting is called","options":["an IFRS Accounting Standard","an IFRS Interpretation","Generally Accepted Accounting Principles","the Conceptual Framework"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"This is the definition of the Conceptual Framework: a coherent system of objectives and concepts that guides consistent standard-setting. Option A is an individual standard dealing with specific transactions, and option C is the country-specific body of accounting rules and practices, not a system of fundamental concepts."}},{"itemId":"eb9143f2-5974-5952-8d5c-4a5ebcfa71b0","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4855622d-d43c-5c87-9e23-3e2db77dc8e3","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-009","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a stated purpose of the Conceptual Framework?","options":["To assist entities in developing consistent accounting policies when no Standard applies","To assess how efficiently management has used the entity's resources","To assist all stakeholders to understand and interpret IFRS Accounting Standards","To assist the IASB in developing and revising IFRS Accounting Standards"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"Assessing how efficiently management has used resources is stewardship, something users do with financial reports, not a purpose of the Conceptual Framework. Options D, A and C are the three stated purposes: helping the IASB set standards, helping entities develop policies, and helping stakeholders interpret the standards."}},{"itemId":"4c4520a6-8cf7-5280-bbd9-00f4b5608f99","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d6c4a224-e7ea-5163-84d6-c02b6afd8122","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-010","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Ikeja Agro Ltd enters into a new type of transaction for which no IFRS Accounting Standard applies. To develop a consistent accounting policy for it, the company should look to","options":["whatever treatment gives the highest profit","the listing rules of the Nigerian Exchange Group","the Conceptual Framework","its previous year's tax computation"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"One purpose of the Conceptual Framework is to help entities develop consistent accounting policies when no IFRS Accounting Standard applies to a transaction. Option D is a tax document, not a basis for accounting policy, and option A would break the neutrality that faithful representation requires."}},{"itemId":"23cda5d0-147d-5ee8-8494-222949302717","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"fca96269-8fc8-599f-8c8f-7f59a3a41f41","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-011","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Apart from cases where no Standard applies, the Conceptual Framework also assists entities in developing accounting policies when","options":["a Standard allows a choice of accounting policy","the auditor disagrees with management","the entity is not a going concern","the entity wants to reduce its tax liability"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"The Conceptual Framework helps entities choose consistent policies both when no Standard applies and when a Standard allows a choice of accounting policy. Options D and B are not situations the Framework is designed for, and option C has nothing to do with this purpose."}},{"itemId":"576b37e8-474e-53ff-9c0f-14d7dcee22fe","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c9b64bce-7f81-57ab-91f1-e4c75c74d265","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-012","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT set out in the 2018 revised Conceptual Framework?","options":["Concepts relating to capital and capital maintenance","Rates for computing company income tax","The qualitative characteristics of useful financial information","The objective of general purpose financial reporting"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"Tax rates are set by tax law, not by the Conceptual Framework. Options D, C and A are all part of its scope, along with the reporting entity, the elements, recognition and derecognition, measurement bases, and presentation and disclosure."}},{"itemId":"9c5082c0-9d00-5140-bccc-6a0b49d8fb23","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ad362eac-2faf-5f5f-b377-9cbc625680c8","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-013","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is included in the scope of the 2018 revised Conceptual Framework?","options":["Listing requirements for stock exchanges","Measurement bases and guidance on when to use them","Rules for calculating directors' remuneration","Detailed audit procedures for testing inventory"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"The 2018 Conceptual Framework includes measurement bases and guidance on when to use them. Options D, C and A are matters for auditing standards, company law and stock exchange rules, not the Conceptual Framework."}},{"itemId":"aa1be0e4-778b-55c9-bf86-67310eb500f8","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"797aa95f-031a-57ec-98ac-fb3360bc36b0","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-014","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In the Conceptual Framework, the criteria for including assets and liabilities in financial statements and the guidance on when to remove them are referred to respectively as","options":["recognition and derecognition","derecognition and recognition","measurement and disclosure","presentation and derecognition"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"Recognition is about including assets and liabilities in the financial statements, while derecognition is about removing them. Option B reverses the two terms, and option C deals with how amounts are valued and explained, not whether items are included."}},{"itemId":"0f676c36-9432-58b8-8af0-dc039f083b98","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6498d457-a60d-51bf-b39b-d07e740ed7bb","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-015","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Within the scope of the Conceptual Framework, guidance on the 'reporting entity' deals with","options":["measurement bases and when to use them","a description of the reporting entity and its boundary","concepts relating to capital maintenance","the definitions of income and expenses"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"The reporting entity part of the Conceptual Framework describes the reporting entity and its boundary. Options D, C and A are separate items in the scope, dealing with the elements, capital maintenance and measurement."}},{"itemId":"623cd3ed-c6cc-5126-8e30-0216249d5bd0","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b52bcbcf-4dce-5533-80f7-9d7acaa421e1","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-016","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The benefit of the Conceptual Framework that comes from providing a common set of principles, making it easier to compare financial statements across companies and periods, is","options":["enhanced understanding","assistance to preparers","guidance for standard setters","consistency and comparability"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"Consistency and comparability arises because a common set of principles leads to similar accounting practices, so statements can be compared across companies and over time. Option C is about helping the IASB develop standards, and option B is about helping companies develop accounting policies."}},{"itemId":"2400ae22-ab2d-50ea-8dcd-7bb300fa4099","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a1f02018-ba4b-5b95-9eb5-35f263f1d842","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-017","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The benefit of the Conceptual Framework that provides guidance to companies in developing accounting policies, especially when specific standards are not available, is","options":["enhanced understanding","guidance for standard setters","consistency and comparability","assistance to preparers"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"Preparers are the companies that produce financial statements, so guidance for them in developing accounting policies is the assistance to preparers benefit. Option B refers to the IASB and other standard setters, and option A refers to investors, lenders and analysts understanding the statements."}},{"itemId":"56243726-be0a-548e-bb2e-e49415b28ffb","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"917ed59d-6cd4-5585-a397-26faf7db4b46","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-018","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The benefit of the Conceptual Framework that helps investors, lenders and analysts to better understand and interpret financial statements is","options":["consistency and comparability","assistance to preparers","enhanced understanding","guidance for standard setters"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"Enhanced understanding is the benefit to all stakeholders, such as investors, lenders and analysts, in interpreting financial statements. Option B is aimed at the companies preparing the statements, and option D is aimed at the IASB and other standard setters."}},{"itemId":"884a9cc7-968f-5e93-8857-22520ce92234","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"fa75698a-7972-5995-9c3a-c1aed26e14fd","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-019","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Financial statements intended to meet the information needs common to users who are unable to command the preparation of reports tailored to their own needs are called","options":["tax returns","general purpose financial reports","management accounts","special-purpose financial statements"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"General purpose financial reports serve the common needs of users who cannot demand reports made specially for them. Option D is the opposite, being prepared for a particular user or group, and option C is internal reporting for managers."}},{"itemId":"827bc4f2-8967-5a27-a72f-e51b67951f4e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c02a13eb-6c56-5e87-8f43-266d4760c1a1","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-020","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"First Lagoon Bank asks Kano Textiles Ltd to prepare a report in a format designed only for the bank's loan assessment. Such a report is best described as","options":["a conceptual framework","a special-purpose financial statement","a general purpose financial report","an exposure draft"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"A special-purpose financial statement is prepared to meet the demands of a particular user or group of users, as here for one bank. Option C is wrong because general purpose reports serve the common needs of many users who cannot ask for tailored reports, and option D is a draft standard issued by the IASB."}},{"itemId":"4d8a8c53-15b3-5c3b-985b-e54a9339cc58","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4c4fcc2b-9a92-5b7b-a851-b24649c97efa","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-021","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following statements about general purpose financial reports (GPFR) is correct?","options":["GPFR cannot provide all the information users need, so users also consider information from other sources","GPFR need not comply with accounting standards","GPFR provide all the information users need to make economic decisions","GPFR are prepared mainly to meet the needs of a particular user"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"General purpose financial reports cannot provide all the information users need, so users must also consider pertinent information from other sources. Option D describes special-purpose statements, and option B is wrong because GPFR comply with accounting standards and GAAP."}},{"itemId":"0a265ba6-05e7-55bb-8e79-6d111a09aa86","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7b4d5292-cf1d-5e06-b19f-d9b9875af6b4","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-022","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The primary users of general purpose financial reports are","options":["managers and employees of the entity","prudential and market regulators","present and potential investors, lenders and other creditors","the government and tax authorities"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"The primary users are present and potential investors, lenders and other creditors, because they cannot demand tailored information yet must make decisions about the entity. Options B, A and D may find GPFR useful but are not regarded as primary users."}},{"itemId":"a8cfda9e-9e16-5980-aa4d-e94b6705be71","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"54cbb12a-7f1a-5ddf-be26-9cc147fd26fa","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-023","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a primary user of general purpose financial reports?","options":["A potential investor considering buying shares","A bank that has lent money to the entity","A present shareholder of the entity","The government deciding how much tax to collect"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"The government is listed among the other users who may find GPFR useful, but it is not a primary user. Options C and A are present and potential investors, and option B is a lender, all of whom are primary users."}},{"itemId":"ae3df924-1b6e-5479-a347-14d6d5cc42b0","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"edae7fe7-9818-532b-9c40-aa96b0474902","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-024","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Prudential and market regulators such as the Central Bank of Nigeria are best described, in relation to general purpose financial reports, as","options":["preparers of GPFR","primary users to whom GPFR are mainly directed","the only users entitled to receive GPFR","other users who may find GPFR useful but are not primary users"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"Regulators are classed as other users: they may find GPFR useful, but the reports are not primarily directed to them. Option B is wrong because the primary users are investors, lenders and other creditors, and option A is wrong because GPFR are prepared by the entity itself."}},{"itemId":"32a2db37-7d8a-554c-8742-eed2e3a43ca8","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f99e8611-8f17-5fe9-80d4-4d30a28abcd0","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-025","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When primary users assess how effectively and efficiently management has discharged its responsibilities to use the entity's resources, they are assessing","options":["stewardship","verifiability","materiality","going concern"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"Stewardship is how well management has looked after and used the entity's resources, and primary users need information to assess it. Option C is an aspect of relevance, option B is an enhancing qualitative characteristic, and option D is about whether the business will continue operating."}},{"itemId":"2c8250e5-b6e5-5cfa-ad63-2dfb720b5751","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4883a3b3-aab3-505b-9ba6-5e01929dd394","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-026","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Primary users need information about the resources of an entity to assess","options":["the amount of tax payable by the entity","the quality of the entity's products","the entity's prospects for future net cash inflows","the personal wealth of the directors"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"Primary users need information to assess the entity's prospects for future net cash inflows and management's stewardship of resources. Option B is the interest of customers, and option A is the interest of the government."}},{"itemId":"1fada104-7775-57ce-be44-71a76b508958","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4e5a4ca6-2adc-5b76-882c-d70575e07467","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-027","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Primary users use financial information to make decisions about all of the following EXCEPT","options":["buying, selling or holding equity or debt instruments","judging the quality of the entity's products before buying them","providing or settling loans and other forms of credit","exercising rights to vote on management's actions"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"Judging product quality is the concern of customers, who are not primary users. Options A, C and D are exactly the decisions investors, lenders and other creditors make with GPFR."}},{"itemId":"7362656c-29d5-596b-a6c2-1eab83ca97c8","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"43bf2f60-f542-5707-bdcd-12afe2f54d51","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-028","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Trade creditors (payables) of a business are mainly interested in its","options":["overall growth and survival","profitability for tax purposes","quality of products","liquidity"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"Trade creditors want to know whether the business can pay what it owes them as debts fall due, which is liquidity. Option C is the customers' concern, option B is the government's, and option A is management's."}},{"itemId":"4cdde617-0914-5e67-b4bb-267bd385a574","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"21cf4b5d-7824-5fe0-a406-8faef95f6ce7","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-029","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which user group is interested in the profitability of a business for tax purposes and policy formulation?","options":["Customers","Employees","Government","Trade creditors"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"The government looks at profitability to decide tax and to formulate policy. Option A cares about product quality, option D about liquidity, and option B about liquidity, profitability and survival for job security."}},{"itemId":"2fe67528-5a02-581d-a9fc-5be96f310386","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4f0b1b2b-29d9-5579-aad2-e4dfcae9a8bd","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-030","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Customers of a business are mainly interested in","options":["the quality of its products or services","appraisal of the efficiency of management performance","its profitability for tax purposes","its liquidity"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"Customers are interested in the quality of the products or services they buy. Option D is the interest of trade creditors, option B is the interest of owners and investors, and option C is the interest of government."}},{"itemId":"c2a8ef26-a6fb-5735-a584-8698bbcfbc60","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9f07628a-f9f0-5f97-abd3-ba4419c793d8","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-031","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which user group is interested in information that permits appraisal of the efficiency of management performance?","options":["Trade creditors","Customers","Owners of the business/investors","Government"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"Owners and investors entrust their money to management, so they want information to appraise how efficiently management is performing. Option B cares about product quality, option A about liquidity, and option D about profitability for tax and policy."}},{"itemId":"36dea40c-f4ba-5d75-976e-5fd657c0b814","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8e42ea47-9904-589e-a053-b3d62915589d","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-032","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The workers' union of a Lagos manufacturing company studies its financial statements to judge whether jobs and wages are secure. The information this group is interested in is the business's","options":["profitability for tax purposes only","quality of products and services","appraisal of entire business activities","liquidity, profitability and survival"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"Employees are interested in liquidity, profitability and survival, because these determine whether wages will be paid and jobs will last. Option B is the customers' interest, option A is the government's, and option C is the interest of financial analysts."}},{"itemId":"3c38c959-9f05-50fa-ae16-a17673f470de","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"15612a8e-25eb-54b9-b8f0-ac2621da8934","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-033","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which user group is interested in the overall performance, growth and survival of the business?","options":["Government","Trade creditors","Customers","Management"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"Management, who guide the company, are interested in its overall performance, growth and survival. Option C focuses on product quality, option B on liquidity, and option A on profitability for tax and policy."}},{"itemId":"73a78786-509c-57e0-b3bb-3aae2ce70a81","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0b0bcd65-bdb2-5988-9bcf-6fad41370967","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-034","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Financial analysts are interested in information that permits","options":["appraisal of the entire business activities","assessment of product quality","computation of tax payable","confirmation of employees' job security only"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"Financial analysts want information that permits an appraisal of the entire business activities, so they can advise others. Option B is the customers' focus, option C the government's, and option D is narrower than what analysts look at."}},{"itemId":"1f27c602-ffd3-5818-8940-fee055767103","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a1a5860d-18e7-58b9-9929-ed3a1a0db904","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-035","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Lenders to a business are interested in its","options":["growth in market share only","profitability for policy formulation","liquidity, profitability and the reliability of its assets","quality of products and services"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"Lenders want to know that the business can pay interest and repay the loan, so they look at liquidity, profitability and the reliability of the business assets that may secure the loan. Option D is the customers' concern, and option B is the government's."}},{"itemId":"9fc54233-f325-5eae-ac0d-a9490eed99b8","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8b2223c2-f591-5271-a107-2d162c5fea1a","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-037","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT an enhancing qualitative characteristic?","options":["Timeliness","Comparability","Relevance","Verifiability"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"Relevance is a fundamental qualitative characteristic, not an enhancing one. Comparability, verifiability, timeliness and understandability are the enhancing characteristics, so options B, D and A are all genuine enhancing characteristics."}},{"itemId":"3b1cf7f7-862c-5ce2-8280-49429a15afc7","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"944f3b9a-f68b-524e-aa5e-fd466668fe19","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-038","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is an enhancing qualitative characteristic?","options":["Completeness","Neutrality","Understandability","Materiality"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"Understandability is one of the four enhancing characteristics, together with comparability, verifiability and timeliness. Option D is an aspect of relevance, while options B and A are underlying features of faithful representation."}},{"itemId":"4581ecbc-e7a3-5de2-8d53-e6273f76e43e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9a0c18b6-c3f9-5c65-990d-450d01995490","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-039","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Accounting information that has the ability to influence users' decisions and can be used for prediction and confirmatory purposes is said to have","options":["understandability","faithful representation","verifiability","relevance"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"Relevance is the ability of information to make a difference to decisions, through predictive value and confirmatory value. Option B is about depicting the substance of what happened completely, neutrally and without error, and option C is about independent observers reaching consensus."}},{"itemId":"cc167180-40c6-5053-a187-7799b387b387","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d37f9d64-4403-58d0-9d74-2350548b82a5","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-040","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Materiality is an entity-specific aspect of","options":["comparability","relevance","faithful representation","timeliness"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"Materiality is an entity-specific aspect of relevance: it is judged by the nature or size of an item in the context of the individual entity's report. Option C is the other fundamental characteristic, while options A and D are enhancing characteristics."}},{"itemId":"a5a3b4e1-6af7-514c-8127-a6e3ed8a0ecf","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b65b7b92-1337-52ae-ad60-7d9c5488ac9a","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-041","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Materiality is judged on the basis of","options":["the legal form of the transaction only","a fixed percentage of profit set by the IASB for all entities","the nature or magnitude (or both) of the item in the context of the individual entity","whether the item has been audited"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"Materiality depends on the nature or size (or both) of an item in the context of the particular entity's report. Option B is wrong because materiality is entity-specific, not a uniform threshold, and option A confuses it with the idea of legal form."}},{"itemId":"fdb1140b-0e39-56d3-8d06-9b3258f1dbee","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2465e718-097c-5bd3-91e5-5eaa263fe20e","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-042","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An omission of N2 million would seriously mislead the users of a small Yaba printing business, but would be trivial in the accounts of a large commercial bank. This shows that materiality is","options":["an enhancing qualitative characteristic","a uniform amount fixed for all entities","an aspect of verifiability","entity-specific"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"The same amount can matter greatly to one entity and hardly at all to another, because materiality is an entity-specific aspect of relevance. Option B contradicts the scenario, and option A is wrong because materiality belongs under relevance, a fundamental characteristic."}},{"itemId":"a09a2c01-7428-54d5-9a1b-16adf497c3f6","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a86f7dbc-e69c-551c-a88e-de9a13e2655a","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-043","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Faithful representation means representing","options":["information early enough to influence decisions","the legal form of a transaction regardless of its substance","the substance of an economic phenomenon instead of its legal form only","only those transactions that are supported by a written contract"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"Faithful representation requires reporting the economic substance of a transaction, not just its legal form. Option B is the reverse of this, and option A describes timeliness, an enhancing characteristic."}},{"itemId":"88a9e5ba-f43f-5776-ba4d-b5511f93f4e7","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"63bb67c7-24d4-5eb1-9172-a7ea9d4ba756","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-045","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT an underlying characteristic of faithful representation?","options":["Neutrality","Freedom from error","Completeness","Timeliness"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"Timeliness is an enhancing qualitative characteristic about information arriving early enough to be used. Options C, A and B are the three underlying characteristics that faithful representation seeks to maximise."}},{"itemId":"10fb0322-61ab-5377-9ac6-9f147faa5fe7","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a7e17fde-e478-5b51-8267-d5715bde9da1","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-046","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The managing director of Oshodi Foods Ltd chooses estimates that are deliberately slanted to make the company's results look more favourable to investors. The financial statements lack","options":["completeness","comparability","neutrality","timeliness"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"Neutrality means information is not biased or slanted to influence users in a particular direction, so deliberately favourable estimates violate it. Option A is about including all necessary information, option D is about when information is available, and option B is about comparing across entities and periods."}},{"itemId":"874d538b-2237-5aa6-a506-86e8438d2c32","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1ccdb339-054a-5ef8-9e92-b027e4e08a0a","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-047","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Aba Plastics Ltd leaves out of its financial statements all information about a large bank loan it took during the year. Which underlying characteristic of faithful representation is most clearly missing?","options":["Completeness","Understandability","Verifiability","Neutrality"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"A complete depiction includes all the information users need, so omitting a large loan makes the statements incomplete. Option D is about freedom from bias rather than missing items, and options C and B are enhancing characteristics, not underlying features of faithful representation."}},{"itemId":"9f6ee736-51ab-53f9-bebf-b478b6556a8a","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3b2189f0-1470-524d-ab36-67f820fc0d4e","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-049","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"If a business is NOT a going concern, the more relevant valuation for its machinery would be","options":["depreciated cost","original historical cost","net realisable value","zero"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"When a business is not a going concern, its assets are expected to be sold, so net realisable value becomes more relevant to users. Option A is the relevant value only when the business will continue, and option B ignores depreciation and the expected sale."}},{"itemId":"8e072967-0c4f-50bb-a5e9-b5f56b1049bb","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8a428770-6d94-5ddb-9c92-95a02e0447da","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-050","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A business that is expected to continue operating into the future, with no intention or need to liquidate or curtail its operations, is described as","options":["a constructive obligation","a reporting entity","a going concern","a special-purpose entity"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"A going concern is a business that will continue into the foreseeable future, which is why its assets can be valued on the assumption that they will be used rather than sold. Option B is any entity that prepares financial statements, whether or not it will continue, and option A is a type of liability."}},{"itemId":"5262c18a-4055-5be6-8e42-7061d69af2c8","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0b5bd1ff-3278-52b8-b085-34f0d3797840","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-051","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An investor examines the trend in sales revenue of Ogun Cement Plc over the past five years and compares its performance with two similar cement companies. The investor is relying mainly on","options":["materiality","comparability","timeliness","verifiability"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"Comparability lets users identify changes in a business over time, such as a five-year sales trend, and evaluate it against similar businesses. Option C is about information arriving in time, option D is about independent observers reaching consensus, and option A is an aspect of relevance."}},{"itemId":"40dba833-675e-52b2-bec6-09c7b1e186df","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"acbb8f19-10e8-5a2e-a1f9-a2d6b414ee04","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-052","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Understandability assumes that users of financial reports","options":["have a reasonable knowledge of business and economic activities and review the information diligently","are professional accountants only","can demand tailored reports from the entity","have no knowledge of business and need everything simplified"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"Understandability assumes users have a reasonable knowledge of business and economic activities and will review and analyse the information diligently. Option D wrongly assumes no knowledge, and option C describes users of special-purpose reports."}},{"itemId":"174f2a1b-8255-5963-9662-9742aea066fd","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2dfe9b13-55bc-5d13-9de2-4d9faf51f8ee","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-053","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Information that is made available early enough for its use and is still capable of influencing users' decisions has the quality of","options":["comparability","timeliness","neutrality","verifiability"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"Timeliness means having information available to decision-makers in time to influence their decisions. Option A is about comparing over time and across businesses, option D is about independent observers reaching consensus, and option C is about freedom from bias."}},{"itemId":"abb99cde-ba1f-5829-9088-3756a66f545c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0561d8ef-e74b-5920-8e76-21232a4f60c4","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-054","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When different knowledgeable and independent observers could reach consensus that a particular depiction is a faithful representation, the information is said to be","options":["comparable","verifiable","material","timely"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"Verifiability means independent, knowledgeable observers could agree that the depiction is a faithful representation. Option D is about availability in time, option A is about comparing across periods and businesses, and option C is about whether an item could influence decisions."}},{"itemId":"eb1a5825-b1f7-5c70-9527-9f23c5209413","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2e47a297-7f65-507a-9199-bc6df3f3b1c9","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-055","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"For information to be verifiable, knowledgeable and independent observers must be able to reach","options":["complete agreement on every figure","consensus, although not necessarily complete agreement","agreement with the views of management","the same figure as the tax authorities"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"Verifiability requires only consensus among knowledgeable, independent observers, not complete agreement. Option A sets a stricter standard than the definition, and option C is wrong because the observers must be independent, not simply follow management."}},{"itemId":"f5bc934b-02e9-589e-a82a-f9047e0ac8fb","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3a8fa354-80ea-5ac5-8f78-c40808e2b3a5","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-056","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following statements about a reporting entity is correct?","options":["It must always be a single legal entity","It cannot be a portion of an entity","It is not necessarily a legal entity","It must be listed on a stock exchange"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"A reporting entity is any entity that is required, or chooses, to prepare financial statements, and it need not be a legal entity. Option A is wrong because it can be a portion of an entity or more than one entity, and option B directly contradicts the definition."}},{"itemId":"aa0aed87-264f-58ae-8552-ce79bd9d9b63","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"150f7054-8b47-54fc-9495-2f5798a38349","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-057","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An entity that is required, or chooses, to prepare financial statements is known as","options":["a going concern","a primary user","a reporting entity","a standard setter"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"A reporting entity is one that is required, or chooses, to prepare financial statements. Option A describes a business expected to continue operating, option B is an investor, lender or other creditor who uses the statements, and option D is a body such as the IASB."}},{"itemId":"20360e6b-aaac-5385-a6e9-cde6cf790295","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"23e0c1c3-cfc4-588a-975f-2d6207df4cfb","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-058","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Delta Holdings Plc and its three subsidiaries prepare one set of financial statements covering all four companies together. Which statement about this reporting entity is correct?","options":["A reporting entity can comprise more than one entity","This is not allowed because a reporting entity must be a single legal entity","Each subsidiary must stop preparing records","Only Delta Holdings Plc can be a reporting entity"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"A reporting entity can be a single entity, a portion of an entity, or more than one entity, so a group reporting together is acceptable. Options B and D wrongly assume a reporting entity must be one legal entity, and option C has no basis in the definition."}},{"itemId":"397e66a0-519e-523a-a578-afdd4275dba9","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"35610a84-eb33-51ac-af9b-806d82458b1d","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-059","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The broad classes into which the financial effects of transactions and other events are grouped according to their economic characteristics are called","options":["the elements of financial statements","the measurement bases","the qualitative characteristics","the accounting standards"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"Financial statements group transactions into broad classes by economic characteristics, and these classes are the elements: assets, liabilities, equity, income and expenses. Option C describes what makes information useful, and option B is about how amounts are measured."}},{"itemId":"8a5906b6-62f6-5c46-86b8-38028083b039","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"73976178-fd9d-513a-88ef-1b7ac49a5ef8","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-060","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT an element directly related to the statement of financial position?","options":["Liabilities","Assets","Equity","Income"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"Income is an element related to performance, reported in the statement of profit or loss. Options B, A and C are the three elements directly related to the statement of financial position."}},{"itemId":"f0c3fa6d-b85b-5505-ab52-8b22fb44d1e4","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"daa5c895-3ebe-58fd-beed-6dd5cb337eaa","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-061","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The elements of financial statements directly related to the measurement of performance are","options":["liabilities and equity","income and expenses","assets and liabilities","assets and equity"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"Performance is measured by income and expenses, which are reported in the statement of profit or loss. Options C, D and A are combinations of the financial position elements."}},{"itemId":"e44e3950-a3cd-58e0-9f07-ba381cfe89f7","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1096e295-db06-5ee0-8563-3a527e659592","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-062","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The statement of cash flows reflects","options":["none of the elements of financial statements","only assets and liabilities","only income and expenses","both income statement elements and some changes in statement of financial position elements"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"The statement of cash flows draws on both performance elements and some changes in financial position elements. Options C and B each leave out one side, and option A is wrong because the statement is linked to the elements."}},{"itemId":"7ed19d4b-f9b3-5e16-bdab-9912ac64ba59","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"794728e4-da02-594d-bdc4-15bf6e4efd51","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-063","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A resource controlled by the entity as a result of past events and from which future economic benefits are expected to flow to the entity is","options":["income","a liability","equity","an asset"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"This is the definition of an asset: control, a past event and expected future economic benefits. Option B is a present obligation leading to an outflow, option C is the residual interest after deducting liabilities, and option A is an increase in economic benefits during the period."}},{"itemId":"e5dbfb0d-5e7f-5528-98b0-ce0310001479","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7a9226e8-8b85-5086-8d64-2312d061438e","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-064","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In the definition of an asset, 'control' means","options":["physical possession of the item","the ability to obtain the economic benefits and to restrict the access of others","the right to sell the item at any time without restriction","legal ownership evidenced by a title document"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"Control is the ability to obtain the economic benefits from a resource and to restrict others from getting them, for example by being the sole user of plant and machinery. Option D confuses control with legal form, and option A is wrong because physical possession alone does not give control of the benefits."}},{"itemId":"d6501fd9-ac46-5f3d-8f88-9dbb6f403d51","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3cbfcae8-5785-5e8f-a0f6-f902dbf48da5","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-065","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A present obligation of the entity arising from past events, the settlement of which is expected to result in an outflow of resources embodying economic benefits, is","options":["an asset","an expense","a liability","equity"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"This is the definition of a liability: a present obligation from a past event that is expected to lead to an outflow of resources. Option A brings in benefits rather than taking them out, and option B is a decrease in equity during the period rather than an obligation."}},{"itemId":"1d1fefb9-892d-5983-913a-80d768a0badf","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8d552d4d-d006-58b8-b0c5-4b9c18cf3f4f","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-066","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An obligation that results from an entity's expected practice rather than being required by law or a legal contract is called","options":["a contingent asset","a constructive obligation","a contribution from equity participants","a legal obligation"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"A constructive obligation arises from an entity's established practice or published policy, not from law or contract. Option D is the opposite, being required by law or contract, and option C relates to owners' capital, not to obligations."}},{"itemId":"a26f1927-dfe5-521f-9a03-34917a218f28","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8cd951cc-543b-50d6-9ed4-867e296ebaf3","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-067","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Niger Delta Oil Ltd has a published policy of cleaning up any environmental damage it causes, even though no law requires it. The obligation to clean up damage already caused is","options":["a constructive obligation","not an obligation at all","a legal obligation","an item of equity"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"Because the obligation comes from the company's published environmental policy rather than legislation, it is a constructive obligation. Option C is wrong because no law requires the clean-up, and option B is wrong because an obligation can be constructive as well as legal."}},{"itemId":"c9baaef2-e73d-56ff-91c0-119f89503f26","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a70c5cfe-1f12-5ce1-aa47-890a5225ac0b","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-068","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"At its reporting date of 31 December, Lekki Logistics Ltd has only discussed a five-year lease that it plans to sign in March of the following year. Why is no liability recognised for this lease at 31 December?","options":["The amount is not material","The lessor is not a primary user","Lease payments are never liabilities","The past event giving rise to the obligation has not yet happened"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"A liability must arise from a past event, so a lease contract must have been signed before the reporting date to create an obligation. Option C is wrong because signed leases can create liabilities, and option A is not the issue in the scenario."}},{"itemId":"53b33976-0870-53a0-a7c3-94f04f9722f6","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"547fd7a3-80a4-5314-8ef7-f67ab6dcd30f","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-069","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The residual interest in the assets of an entity after deducting all its liabilities is","options":["income","equity","revenue","net realisable value"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"Equity is what is left for the owners once all liabilities are deducted from assets, so Equity = Assets - Liabilities. Option A is an increase in economic benefits during a period, and option D is a valuation basis for assets."}},{"itemId":"e01efb33-31f2-5188-b17d-d3c81eb53387","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9a7358f2-8640-5e2d-aaf5-a84dd77c1cb9","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-070","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An increase in economic benefits during the accounting period, in the form of inflows or enhancements of assets or decreases of liabilities that increase equity, other than contributions from equity participants, is","options":["income","a liability","equity","an asset"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"This is the definition of income, which covers both revenue and gains but excludes contributions from owners. Option D is a resource controlled by the entity, not an increase during a period, and option C is the residual interest itself rather than an increase in it."}},{"itemId":"59c4386f-8cf9-5a7c-9de1-bfe4fe289f63","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3b0ddfa4-6035-5c69-aee4-6e62bc71864f","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-071","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The shareholders of Ibadan Bakeries Ltd pay N5 million in cash for new shares. How is this treated under the definitions of the elements?","options":["It is revenue because it arises from ordinary activities","It is a liability because the shareholders own the company","It is income because cash has increased","It increases equity but is not income, because it is a contribution from equity participants"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"Income excludes increases in equity that come from contributions by equity participants, so a share issue increases equity without being income. Option C ignores this exclusion, and option A is wrong because issuing shares is not part of selling bread."}},{"itemId":"48d11642-cbb9-5645-9581-7747a36cec9d","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"79b05a8b-041f-53be-b6fa-58f350c6904b","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-072","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Ibadan Bakeries Ltd pays a cash dividend of N1.5 million to its shareholders. Under the definitions of the elements, the dividend is","options":["not an expense, because it is a distribution to equity participants","a loss, because no goods were received","income for the company","an expense, because cash has decreased"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"Expenses exclude decreases in equity that relate to distributions to equity participants, so a dividend reduces equity but is not an expense. Option D ignores this exclusion, and option B is wrong because losses are a type of expense and the same exclusion applies."}},{"itemId":"e8ef39de-0b45-531d-be16-ae33ad6be2f2","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"89ff63c6-8929-5e16-a823-ab2821065cd9","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-073","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The definition of income in the Framework encompasses","options":["both revenue and gains","revenue only","gains only","revenue and contributions from owners"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"Income covers both revenue from ordinary activities and gains, which may or may not arise from ordinary activities. Options B and C each leave out one part, and option D is wrong because contributions from owners are excluded from income."}},{"itemId":"75facdf9-955e-5a05-b93c-49cc7c3458a3","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8edd2629-df88-5bda-82ec-b2412bb52bb7","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-074","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Gains are not regarded as a separate element in the Framework because","options":["they always arise outside the ordinary activities of the entity","they represent increases in economic benefits and are no different in nature from revenue","they are contributions from equity participants","they are reported only in the statement of financial position"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"Gains are increases in economic benefits, just like revenue, so they are part of the income element rather than a separate element. Option A is wrong because gains may or may not arise from ordinary activities, and option C is wrong because owner contributions are excluded from income altogether."}},{"itemId":"59d1ed60-4a62-5952-834c-7ab5c14d71e2","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"42fd395a-c87f-546e-898f-ac74f298dbb6","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-075","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Revenue arises in the course of an entity's ordinary activities and is referred to by different names. Which of the following is NOT one of them?","options":["Fees","Interest","Royalties","Capital contributed"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"Revenue goes by names such as sales, fees, interest, dividends, royalties and rent. Capital contributed by owners is a contribution from equity participants and is excluded from income, so it is not revenue, while options C, B and A are all names for revenue."}},{"itemId":"6b5f0208-b75d-58ed-b6a8-19bcdbbbcb6b","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e76eb594-eafb-5ad5-8648-c571d6498ce2","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-076","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is an example of an expense arising in the course of the ordinary activities of an entity?","options":["Dividends paid to shareholders","Repayment of a bank loan's principal","Purchase of a delivery van","Wages"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"Wages, like cost of sales and depreciation, are ordinary expenses that reduce equity. Option A is a distribution to equity participants, option B reduces a liability and an asset equally without reducing equity, and option C swaps one asset for another."}},{"itemId":"357f6c98-5153-53bd-88cc-3688d5b0bdb8","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a0165e98-f33f-5cd4-8f46-544e04ae6fdb","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-077","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Losses are not treated as a separate element of financial statements because","options":["they always arise from the ordinary activities of the entity","they are decreases in economic benefits and are no different in nature from other expenses","they do not affect equity","they are distributions to equity participants"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"Losses are decreases in economic benefits, just like other expenses, so they fall under the expense element. Option A is wrong because losses may or may not arise from ordinary activities, and option C is wrong because losses do reduce equity."}},{"itemId":"ea50c5c9-e703-50d9-8e23-6630366cecce","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f6c71e62-70b0-520c-86c1-33a1415d4a82","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-078","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Yaba Tech Solutions Ltd has total assets of N85m and total liabilities of N32m. What is its equity?","options":["N117m","N53m","N32m","N85m"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"Equity is the residual interest in assets after deducting liabilities, so N85m - N32m = N53m. Option A wrongly adds liabilities to assets, while options D and C simply repeat the asset or liability total."}},{"itemId":"f6a1c845-ed6a-5603-b715-1f0abf9e0aac","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d96eeba0-5812-5c2e-b75b-5314ac00edf5","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-079","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Surulere Stores has equity of N40m and liabilities of N25m. Using the accounting equation, its total assets are","options":["N15m","N25m","N65m","N40m"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"Assets = Equity + Liabilities, so N40m + N25m = N65m. Option A wrongly subtracts liabilities from equity, and options D and B each leave out one side of the equation."}},{"itemId":"60fca404-e15a-54d2-bf4d-518cb7bf3a7f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"aefd7a4c-2db7-556b-8802-0ba1bc4ca067","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-080","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Kaduna Farms Ltd reports total assets of N120m and equity of N70m. What are its total liabilities?","options":["N50m","N190m","N120m","N70m"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"Since Assets = Equity + Liabilities, Liabilities = Assets - Equity = N120m - N70m = N50m. Option B adds the two figures, and options D and C just repeat one of the given amounts."}},{"itemId":"33b1d643-5f38-53a4-8028-eead4bdb0e96","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c0b1fe6d-f544-5dbc-b3c6-8d75c18004e2","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-081","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Ota Furniture Ltd has assets of N50m and liabilities of N20m. It then buys equipment for N5m on credit. What is its equity after the purchase?","options":["N30m","N35m","N55m","N25m"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"Buying on credit increases assets and liabilities by the same N5m, so equity stays at N50m - N20m = N30m. Option D wrongly treats the purchase as an expense, option B wrongly treats it as income, and option C is the new asset total, not equity."}},{"itemId":"f10783b9-744c-5a8d-927a-bce2da7d4452","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"dc2b04c0-54aa-5a32-9c9e-6f7c3cc700cf","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-082","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Abeokuta Motors Ltd has assets of N60m and liabilities of N25m. It repays N4m of a bank loan in cash. What are its total liabilities after the repayment?","options":["N21m","N25m","N29m","N35m"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"Repaying a loan reduces cash (an asset) and the loan (a liability) by N4m, so liabilities fall to N25m - N4m = N21m. Option B ignores the repayment, option C adds it instead of subtracting, and option D is the equity figure, which is unchanged at N35m."}},{"itemId":"3b935ae9-7000-530f-b4c3-e49185b2b123","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d0c46474-bba1-559f-865e-41534b7daa71","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-083","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Ilorin Traders Ltd had equity of N30m at the start of the year and N48m at the end. During the year shareholders contributed N10m of new capital and N4m of dividends were paid. What was the profit (income less expenses) for the year?","options":["N12m","N8m","N24m","N18m"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"Equity changes through profit, owner contributions and distributions, so profit = N48m - N30m - N10m + N4m = N12m. Option D ignores both owner transactions, option B removes the contribution but forgets to add back the dividends, and option C reverses both adjustments."}},{"itemId":"58a8a804-ffd4-536b-8184-fe6ba18ae621","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"fc9ad5ec-aea4-563b-9d3f-c9005872b858","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-084","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In its first month, Enugu Prints Ltd received N8m capital from its owners, earned N3m revenue in cash, paid wages of N1.2m and paid a dividend of N0.5m. What is its total income for the month?","options":["N11m","N3m","N1.8m","N1.3m"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"Income excludes contributions from equity participants, so only the N3m revenue counts as income. Option A wrongly includes the owners' capital, while options C and D deduct wages and dividends, which gives a net figure rather than income."}},{"itemId":"2f3e9933-448a-556c-9a4b-981524445366","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c2c1a844-b2ed-526d-ab68-82ecb82a0c7b","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-085","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Using the same data (owners' capital N8m, cash revenue N3m, wages N1.2m, dividend N0.5m), what are Enugu Prints Ltd's total expenses for the month?","options":["N1.8m","N1.2m","N1.7m","N0.5m"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"Expenses exclude distributions to equity participants, so only the wages of N1.2m are an expense. Option C wrongly adds the dividend, option D counts only the dividend, and option A is profit, not expenses."}},{"itemId":"4c0a3619-de61-5192-a5d1-b0ed522e2b33","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9205a3b2-8a48-57cd-b902-ef3f92f583ba","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:FW-086","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Using the same data (owners' capital N8m, cash revenue N3m, wages N1.2m, dividend N0.5m) and starting from zero, what is Enugu Prints Ltd's equity at the end of the month?","options":["N9.3m","N1.8m","N9.8m","N11m"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IASB Conceptual Framework","explanation":"Equity rises by the owners' capital and the profit and falls by the dividend: N8m + (N3m - N1.2m) - N0.5m = N9.3m. Option B is profit alone, option C forgets the dividend, and option D ignores both wages and dividend."}},{"itemId":"4f1a2c1f-c445-5ba8-bd46-eabd45d55d9a","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6c5d8aeb-6ff1-58e7-b907-3ef2b6990b1b","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-001","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"IAS 1 prescribes the basis for presentation of general purpose financial statements mainly to ensure","options":["uniform tax computation between entities in the same industry","comparability with the entity's own previous periods and with other entities' financial statements","that all assets are measured at fair value at the end of each reporting period","that the statement of cash flows is prepared using the indirect method"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"The objective of IAS 1 is comparability: users should be able to compare an entity's statements with its own earlier periods and with those of other entities. Option C is wrong because IAS 1 does not set measurement rules such as fair value for all assets. Option D relates to IAS 7, and option A is a tax matter outside IAS 1."}},{"itemId":"118cb561-64b5-5f49-8c8a-982bd9bc8724","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"fb2cde62-cc4e-5d72-b9af-0d88f218e47b","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-002","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"IAS 1 sets out the line items to be presented in each of the following financial statements EXCEPT the","options":["statement of profit or loss and other comprehensive income","statement of financial position","statement of cash flows","statement of changes in equity"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"IAS 1 sets out line items for each financial statement except the statement of cash flows, whose presentation and disclosure are governed by IAS 7. Options B, A and D are all statements whose minimum line items or contents are laid down in IAS 1 itself."}},{"itemId":"4fe839dc-35dc-553f-ad70-fb3f79956c9b","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d695b9d6-344d-5e93-9919-08d709d590ba","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-003","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"IAS 1 does NOT apply to the structure and content of","options":["annual consolidated financial statements of a group","annual separate financial statements of a parent","annual general purpose financial statements of a listed bank","condensed interim financial statements prepared under IAS 34"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"Condensed interim financial statements take their structure and content from IAS 34 Interim Financial Reporting, so they fall outside IAS 1 on that point. IAS 1 applies equally to consolidated statements (option A), separate statements (option B) and the annual general purpose statements of any entity, including a bank (option C)."}},{"itemId":"1626b288-b1c9-50cc-9cbc-bc96c90629c1","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"68940f04-bf81-5fa6-9dbd-b60743a9b710","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-004","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Financial statements intended to meet the needs of users who are not in a position to require an entity to prepare reports tailored to their particular information needs are called","options":["special purpose management reports","general purpose financial statements","condensed interim financial statements","consolidated financial statements"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"General purpose financial statements serve users, such as investors and lenders, who cannot demand custom reports and so must rely on the published statements. Option C describes shortened mid-year statements under IAS 34, option A describes reports tailored to a particular user, and option D describes the statements of a group, which is a different idea."}},{"itemId":"2b2c8ac6-bcfc-5885-868b-c538ed04469f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"25b5a242-d216-5bde-bbe6-817857325b03","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-005","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under IAS 1, financial statements are best described as","options":["a narrative review by management of the principal uncertainties the entity faces","a summary of the entity's budget and forecasts for the next financial year","a report on environmental matters and value added by the entity","a structured representation of the financial position and financial performance of an entity"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"Financial statements are a structured representation of an entity's financial position and financial performance, giving information useful to a wide range of users in making economic decisions. Option A is a management financial review and option C describes environmental reports and value added statements, both presented outside the financial statements. Option B is wrong because IAS 1 does not require budgets or forecasts."}},{"itemId":"13c08270-481c-50d3-9b41-7f06663c4e42","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"20a98b45-1b6a-5a44-83e4-2b3ab52ab193","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-006","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Apart from providing information useful for economic decisions, financial statements also show","options":["the tax liability of each individual shareholder","the market value of the entity's shares at the reporting date","the personal financial position of the entity's directors","the results of management's stewardship of the resources entrusted to it"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"Financial statements also show how well management has looked after (stewarded) the resources that owners entrusted to it. Options B, C and A are not things IAS 1 requires financial statements to show; the share price, directors' personal finances and shareholders' own tax are outside the entity's financial statements."}},{"itemId":"6b52679f-8e05-526f-bed7-e4eaa5386f3e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"cf132de8-d8f4-5896-bace-5a6fc9a56a85","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-007","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT part of a complete set of financial statements under IAS 1?","options":["A statement of changes in equity for the period","Notes comprising significant accounting policies and other explanatory information","A statement of cash flows for the period","A financial review by management presented outside the financial statements"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"A management financial review is commonly published alongside the financial statements, but it sits outside them and outside the scope of IFRSs. Options A, C and B are all components of the complete set, together with the statement of financial position and the statement of profit or loss and other comprehensive income."}},{"itemId":"e0eb61d5-72a7-5690-8d56-87bb4039eb1e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"49df11db-a1c7-5e71-a6e4-ea4f8d4095c3","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-008","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Ikeja Paints Plc changed an accounting policy and applied it retrospectively, and the change has a material effect on its statement of financial position at the beginning of the preceding period. How many statements of financial position must it present?","options":["Two: end of current period and end of preceding period only","Four: end of current period and the three preceding period ends","One: end of current period, with the change explained in the notes","Three: end of current period, end of preceding period and beginning of preceding period"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"When a retrospective change in policy, retrospective restatement or reclassification has a material effect on the opening position of the preceding period, a third statement of financial position is required, giving three in total. Option A is the normal minimum without such a change, option C ignores comparatives altogether, and option B goes further than IAS 1 requires."}},{"itemId":"9baf87ac-a5bc-5a9f-98e8-0a8e2c0c8bb3","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5058716c-f5cd-5168-b279-35ff14e52c50","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-009","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An entity that chooses to present its profit or loss section in a separate statement of profit or loss must","options":["present other comprehensive income first, followed by the profit or loss section","place it immediately after the statement of changes in equity","place it immediately before the statement presenting comprehensive income, which begins with profit or loss","present it only in the notes to the financial statements"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"If profit or loss is shown in a separate statement, that statement must come immediately before the statement presenting comprehensive income, and that second statement starts with profit or loss. Option B breaks the required order, option D wrongly moves a primary statement into the notes to the financial statements, and option A reverses the order IAS 1 requires."}},{"itemId":"5f825802-de3f-5fb4-b3ab-1e8998d0f19e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8ef72302-8680-5543-8b59-dfbe98c8864b","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-010","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Lekki Foods Ltd wants to title its performance statement 'statement of comprehensive income' instead of 'statement of profit or loss and other comprehensive income'. Under IAS 1 this is","options":["permitted, because an entity may use titles other than those used in IAS 1","permitted only for entities that are not-for-profit organisations","prohibited, because only the titles used in IAS 1 may be used","permitted only if the entity also presents a separate statement of profit or loss"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"IAS 1 allows other titles for the statements, and it gives 'statement of comprehensive income' as an example. Option C is wrong because the titles are not compulsory, and options D and B add conditions that IAS 1 does not impose."}},{"itemId":"de1d2e43-a44f-5a5f-aff7-11d0ce0c922b","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3a6633a3-a5bd-52c1-9a5d-ba3c002c2e5a","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-011","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Fair presentation under IAS 1 requires","options":["ignoring IFRS requirements whenever management believes profit would be higher","faithful representation of the effects of transactions in line with the Conceptual Framework's definitions and recognition criteria","presenting all assets and liabilities at their market values at the reporting date","presenting the financial statements in the format preferred by the tax authority"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"Fair presentation means faithfully representing the effects of transactions, other events and conditions using the Conceptual Framework's definitions and recognition criteria for assets, liabilities, income and expenses. Applying IFRSs, with extra disclosure when needed, is presumed to achieve it. Options C and D are not requirements of IAS 1, and option A is the opposite of fair presentation."}},{"itemId":"5ceefaab-1408-5bd4-9613-af40ca80a918","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"74321fff-d51b-5fa0-b9cf-5bb7d1a470b0","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-012","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An entity whose financial statements comply with IFRSs shall","options":["obtain approval from the IASB before claiming compliance","state compliance only if it is listed on a stock exchange","describe its statements as complying with IFRSs even if a few requirements are not met","make an explicit and unreserved statement of such compliance in the notes"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"IAS 1 requires an explicit and unreserved statement of compliance with IFRSs in the notes to the financial statements, and statements may be described as compliant only if they meet all IFRS requirements. Option C is wrong because partial compliance cannot be called compliance, and options B and A add conditions that do not exist."}},{"itemId":"99a04be4-8c5d-5d9b-b969-94fcc08a5337","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b999d100-908c-5d6b-bb26-0b2847f8598f","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-013","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An entity shall prepare its financial statements on a going concern basis unless management","options":["plans to raise new share capital in the next financial year","has changed the entity's reporting date during the year","intends to liquidate the entity or cease trading, or has no realistic alternative but to do so","expects the entity to make a loss in the next financial year"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"The going concern basis is used unless management intends to liquidate or stop trading, or has no realistic alternative but to do so. A loss in the next year (option D) does not by itself end going concern, and raising capital (option A) or changing the reporting date (option B) has nothing to do with this test."}},{"itemId":"aa5373a2-400f-50f5-b3df-90b44ba261fb","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a762e50b-ec63-5415-adc7-707021ad305a","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-014","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In assessing whether the going concern assumption is appropriate, management takes into account all available information about the future covering","options":["exactly six months from the date the financial statements are authorised","at least, but not limited to, twelve months from the end of the reporting period","only the period remaining until the next annual general meeting","no more than twelve months from the start of the reporting period"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"Management looks at all available information about the future for at least twelve months from the end of the reporting period, and it may look further. Option A is too short and measured from the wrong date, option D wrongly caps the period and starts it at the wrong point, and option C is not a period IAS 1 uses."}},{"itemId":"1e393fe9-9327-5bed-8c40-0d6ea07d1833","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3e3cf435-02d6-566a-b933-2110fd660a09","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-015","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The shareholders of Agege Bakeries Ltd have resolved to wind up the company soon after the year end, so the statements are not prepared on a going concern basis. IAS 1 requires the company to disclose","options":["only a forecast of the cash expected from selling its assets","nothing, since the company will soon cease to exist","that fact, the basis on which it prepared the statements and the reason it is not regarded as a going concern","only the names of the liquidators appointed"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"When statements are not prepared on a going concern basis, the entity must disclose that fact, the basis used instead and why it is not a going concern. Option B ignores this requirement, and options D and A are not the disclosures IAS 1 requires; IAS 1 does not require forecasts."}},{"itemId":"6f56293f-aecc-5c5e-9b9b-69798ecced7f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ffe98dae-0f16-5840-89bc-ca5034d60982","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-016","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Surulere Prints Ltd delivered goods worth N4 million to a customer in December 2025 (year end 31 December 2025), but the customer paid in February 2026. Recognising the sale in 2025 is an application of which IAS 1 general feature?","options":["Accrual basis of accounting","Consistency of presentation","Frequency of reporting","Offsetting"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"Under the accrual basis, items are recognised when they meet the definitions and recognition criteria, not when cash moves, so the sale belongs to 2025. Option D concerns netting assets with liabilities or income with expenses, option C concerns how often statements are presented, and option B concerns keeping the same presentation from year to year."}},{"itemId":"2109308e-fc02-5483-b7c1-206b116cf5ce","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0e228531-7e3f-5d67-a789-60a76abab221","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-017","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Information whose omission, misstatement or obscuring could reasonably be expected to influence the decisions primary users make on the basis of general purpose financial statements is described as","options":["material","aggregated","comparable","impracticable"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"This is the IAS 1 definition of material information. Option D means a requirement cannot be applied after every reasonable effort, option C is a quality of information that allows comparison, and option B describes combining items into classes; none of these is the test of influence on decisions."}},{"itemId":"3dd2ce2a-2b63-5d4c-84e2-f9de3ad5a264","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"707e8d94-2bc0-55b4-91d5-fd456c279309","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-018","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under IAS 1, materiality depends on","options":["the magnitude of the item only, measured against revenue","the preferences of the entity's directors only","the nature or magnitude of information, or both","whether the item has been audited"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"Materiality depends on the nature of the information, its size, or both, judged in the context of the financial statements as a whole. Option A is too narrow because a small item can be material by nature, while options D and B are not bases IAS 1 uses."}},{"itemId":"ffafd217-8f78-5187-89ca-c5901a2cedae","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1a45e915-6789-5aa6-9485-51d3c3b046ec","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-019","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An item that is not individually material enough to warrant separate presentation in the financial statements","options":["must always be omitted from both the statements and the notes","is aggregated with other items, and may still warrant separate presentation in the notes","must be offset against a related liability","must be presented as an extraordinary item"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"A line item that is not individually material is aggregated with other items, and it may still deserve separate presentation in the notes to the financial statements. Option A is wrong because aggregation does not mean leaving it out, option C confuses materiality with offsetting, and option D is wrong because extraordinary items are prohibited."}},{"itemId":"cccf7fb3-4f42-5b83-82e8-ac751369168f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c731afa4-5174-5903-9cd3-272360a0f491","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-020","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"IAS 1 states that an entity shall not offset assets and liabilities, or income and expenses,","options":["unless required or permitted by an IFRS","unless management considers it convenient","unless the amounts involved are material","under any circumstances whatsoever"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"Offsetting is not allowed unless an IFRS requires or permits it, because netting can hide the transactions and make future cash flows harder to assess. Option C gets materiality the wrong way round, option B is not a valid ground, and option D is too absolute, since some IFRSs do require or permit offsetting."}},{"itemId":"92a6d456-e445-5d98-82d2-b68903f685f4","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8615cbc9-7695-5527-a064-4b52141ead08","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-021","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Kano Textiles Ltd has many small foreign exchange gains and losses arising from a group of similar transactions. Under IAS 1 these are normally presented","options":["on a net basis, but separately if they are material","gross, with every gain and every loss on a separate line","in other comprehensive income only","directly in retained earnings, bypassing profit or loss"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"Gains and losses from a group of similar transactions, such as foreign exchange gains and losses, are presented net, but shown separately if they are material. Option B is not required for a group of similar items, and options C and D take ordinary foreign exchange differences out of profit or loss without any basis."}},{"itemId":"423a0d0d-fc1b-51f3-bd64-6d00d91f9d39","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b455ffa6-3bbb-5cef-997a-2cd2843beb6e","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-022","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An entity changes its reporting date and presents financial statements for an 18-month period. In addition to the period covered, IAS 1 requires disclosure of","options":["the reason for the longer period and the fact that the amounts are not entirely comparable","a restated 12-month version of every statement","the approval of the tax authority for the change","nothing further, provided the statements are audited"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"A complete set must be presented at least annually, and when the period is longer or shorter than a year the entity discloses why and that the amounts are not entirely comparable. Option D ignores the requirement, while options B and C are not what IAS 1 asks for."}},{"itemId":"98e39726-7fae-5708-a5b8-b7666b3c5e0f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4109927f-fa9a-579b-8c16-4291e2724540","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-023","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"As a minimum, IAS 1 requires an entity to present","options":["three of each statement for every reporting period","one of each statement, with comparatives shown only in the notes","two statements of financial position but only one of each other statement","two statements of financial position, two statements of profit or loss and OCI, two statements of cash flows and two statements of changes in equity, with related notes"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"Comparative information for the preceding period is required for all amounts, so the minimum is two of each statement (current and prior period) plus related notes. Option B leaves out the comparative statements, option A goes beyond the minimum, and option C wrongly limits comparatives to the statement of financial position."}},{"itemId":"c3cb564c-7191-517c-a7f0-629f883858c7","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d09fd0e5-ad32-50a8-8046-f73ad088e468","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-024","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Ibadan Ceramics Ltd moved 'delivery costs' from administrative expenses to distribution costs this year. Under IAS 1 it should","options":["present the change as an extraordinary item","reclassify the comparative amounts, unless reclassification is impracticable, and disclose the nature, amount and reason","present the change as a prior period error in the statement of changes in equity","leave the comparatives unchanged and say nothing"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"When presentation or classification changes, comparative amounts are reclassified unless that is impracticable, with disclosure of the nature, the amounts and the reason. Option D damages comparability, option C wrongly treats a reclassification as an error, and option A is not allowed because extraordinary items are prohibited."}},{"itemId":"f6afa798-3c7e-506b-bc08-ee8e5f09a46e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6bff2f3b-407f-5c84-96dd-2d87571422ca","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-025","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the consistency of presentation requirement, an entity may change the presentation or classification of items from one period to the next when","options":["a significant change in the nature of its operations makes another presentation more appropriate, or an IFRS requires a change","a new finance director prefers a different format","the new presentation shows a higher profit","the entity wants to make comparison with the prior year harder"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"Presentation and classification are kept from period to period unless a significant change in operations (or a review) shows that another presentation is more appropriate, or an IFRS requires a change. Options B, C and D are personal or self-serving reasons that would damage comparability."}},{"itemId":"cda82f8f-f2fc-5d2e-aa1e-8d1dfc7a5fc5","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ace248f7-eb90-54d9-b709-bd82fd0c8500","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-026","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a line item IAS 1 requires to be presented in the statement of financial position?","options":["Non-controlling interests, presented within equity","Biological assets","Cost of sales","Investment property"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"Cost of sales is an expense line found in the statement of profit or loss under the function of expense method, not in the statement of financial position. Options D, B and A are all listed among the minimum line items of the statement of financial position."}},{"itemId":"03e1a6cb-8b62-5420-9aa2-d26fc95b6c1d","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"858d6dac-2fe8-58b6-8209-92a4e369659c","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-027","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When an entity uses the current/non-current classification in its statement of financial position, deferred tax assets and liabilities shall be classified as","options":["current or non-current depending on when they reverse","current","part of equity","non-current"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"IAS 1 does not allow deferred tax assets or liabilities to be classified as current when the current/non-current split is used, so they appear as non-current. Option B is prohibited, option A is a timing test IAS 1 does not apply to deferred tax, and option C is wrong because deferred tax is not equity."}},{"itemId":"6b32b27c-c005-595f-89ae-1512125693a2","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d7b40919-7ea7-5aef-9073-2dec3527e809","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-028","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"IAS 1 permits an entity to present all assets and liabilities in order of liquidity, instead of a current/non-current split, when","options":["the entity wishes to hide its long-term debt","the entity has more current assets than non-current assets","a liquidity presentation provides information that is reliable and more relevant, as for many financial institutions","the entity has made a loss for the year"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"The current/non-current split is the normal approach, but a liquidity-based presentation is used when it gives reliable and more relevant information, for example for financial institutions without a clearly identifiable operating cycle. Options B, A and D are not grounds IAS 1 recognises."}},{"itemId":"31b778ed-9d90-56f7-88ef-6b4d04dd28c5","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e38d809b-01de-53df-a27e-e0149612599f","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-029","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An asset is classified as current under IAS 1 when any of the following applies, EXCEPT when","options":["the entity intends to use it to generate income over several years","it is held primarily for the purpose of trading","the entity expects to realise it, or intends to sell or consume it, in its normal operating cycle","the entity expects to realise it within twelve months after the reporting period"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"An asset used to earn income over several years, such as a factory machine, fails all four current-asset tests and is non-current. Options C, B and D are three of the criteria for a current asset; the fourth is cash or cash equivalents that are not restricted."}},{"itemId":"fa049c85-537c-5a95-a359-dc8402c41d1e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7d81b40e-714d-5160-bd51-c4388b5f77ca","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-030","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Onitsha Spirits Ltd matures whisky for three years before sale, as part of its normal operating cycle. At the reporting date, how should this maturing inventory be classified?","options":["As a non-current asset until the final year of maturing","As investment property, because it is held for capital appreciation","As a non-current asset, because it will not be sold within twelve months","As a current asset, because it will be realised in the entity's normal operating cycle"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"Assets sold, consumed or realised as part of the normal operating cycle are current even when they will not be realised within twelve months after the reporting period. Options C and A wrongly apply only the twelve-month test, and option B is wrong because inventory is not investment property."}},{"itemId":"7ac26ba7-3936-59a0-86c2-d6524a10ede2","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"dec48e67-337b-5e5a-9311-f02f5d288a34","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-031","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When an entity's normal operating cycle is not clearly identifiable, IAS 1 assumes it to be","options":["the period until the next annual general meeting","twelve months","the life of the entity's longest-lived asset","six months"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"The operating cycle is the time from acquiring assets for processing until they are realised in cash, and when it cannot be clearly identified it is assumed to be twelve months. Options D, C and A are not periods IAS 1 uses."}},{"itemId":"256c80af-f243-5d57-ae36-95b3cca98456","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"250b7a92-5a5c-5adf-962e-657dbedeb8e7","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-032","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A liability is classified as current under IAS 1 when any of the following applies, EXCEPT when","options":["management merely intends to repay it early, although the entity has the right to defer settlement for at least twelve months","the entity expects to settle it in its normal operating cycle","it is held primarily for the purpose of trading","it is due to be settled within twelve months after the reporting period"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"Classification is unaffected by management's intention or expectation to settle early; if the entity has the right at the reporting date to defer settlement for at least twelve months, the liability stays non-current. Options B, C and D are three of the four criteria for a current liability."}},{"itemId":"b6923ec1-ad12-5319-a19c-8ec013efd11a","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"eb1ff271-a20e-5845-8604-92653b88e7bc","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-033","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Port Harcourt Oil Services Ltd has trade payables that are part of its normal operating cycle but are due 14 months after the reporting date. They should be classified as","options":["current liabilities, because they are part of the working capital used in the normal operating cycle","non-current liabilities, because they are due after more than twelve months","contingent liabilities disclosed only in the notes","equity, because they are not yet due"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"Operating items such as trade payables are current liabilities even if they are due more than twelve months after the reporting period, because they form part of working capital in the normal operating cycle. Option B applies only the twelve-month test, and options D and C are wrong because trade payables are present obligations recognised as liabilities."}},{"itemId":"b1ff4615-70f1-51d8-ad37-7c1c473501db","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"31116ad4-2f74-5c01-8da5-c24c083b3bad","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-034","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Abuja Cement Plc has a bank loan due for repayment four months after its reporting date of 31 December 2025. On 20 February 2026, before the statements are authorised for issue, the bank agrees to refinance it on a long-term basis. At 31 December 2025 the loan is","options":["split equally between current and non-current liabilities","a current liability, with the refinancing disclosed as a non-adjusting event","removed from the statement of financial position","a non-current liability, because it has been refinanced long-term"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"A financial liability due within twelve months is current even if a long-term refinancing is agreed after the reporting period but before authorisation; at the reporting date the entity had no right to defer settlement. Option D wrongly uses an event after the reporting period to change the classification, option A has no basis, and option C is wrong because the debt still exists."}},{"itemId":"23605221-15cc-5768-a696-2ac70b30101a","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0f69acc6-70b5-5c64-8f70-28d973e512c1","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-035","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Benin Rubber Ltd breached a covenant on its long-term loan before its year end, making the loan payable on demand. After the year end, but before the statements were authorised, the lender agreed not to demand payment. At the reporting date the loan is","options":["a current liability","a non-current liability, because the lender later waived the breach","a contingent liability disclosed only in the notes","part of equity until the breach is rectified"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"Because the breach made the loan payable on demand and, at the reporting date, the entity had no right to defer settlement for twelve months, the loan is current, even though the lender agreed after the year end not to demand payment. Option B wrongly relies on the later waiver, and options C and D misdescribe a recognised loan."}},{"itemId":"6c647306-4f12-5f47-afa6-8b0d1e4faf49","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b97f45cb-9275-56d7-a655-0d1a7f880638","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-036","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Calabar Fisheries Ltd breached a loan covenant, but by its year end the lender had agreed a grace period ending 15 months after the reporting date, during which the lender cannot demand repayment. The loan should be classified as","options":["current, until the breach is actually rectified","current, because a breach occurred during the year","non-current","equity"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"If the lender agrees, by the end of the reporting period, to a grace period ending at least twelve months after the reporting date, during which it cannot demand repayment, the liability is non-current. Options B and A would apply only if no such grace period existed at the reporting date, and option D is wrong because a loan is a liability."}},{"itemId":"1583c6bb-af76-57c1-ab23-29b40f452de0","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f5821c9e-72bb-5f4f-aa7d-89cd5a87a888","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-037","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In addition to the profit or loss and other comprehensive income sections, the statement of profit or loss and other comprehensive income shall present","options":["profit or loss, total other comprehensive income and comprehensive income for the period","opening and closing retained earnings","cash generated from operations","only revenue and profit before tax"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"The statement must show profit or loss, total other comprehensive income, and comprehensive income for the period (the total of the two). Option D is incomplete, option B belongs in the statement of changes in equity, and option C belongs in the statement of cash flows."}},{"itemId":"16013a18-c73b-57a1-9ad0-a7f6548085c6","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a8e5e001-975d-55a7-aab7-22f9ccff1b8d","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-038","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a line item required in the profit or loss section of the statement of profit or loss and other comprehensive income?","options":["Finance costs","Changes in revaluation surplus","A single amount for the total of discontinued operations","Tax expense"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"Changes in revaluation surplus are a component of other comprehensive income, not of profit or loss. Options A, D and C are all minimum line items of the profit or loss section."}},{"itemId":"db559e72-071e-51ca-9eaa-05eac430dd3d","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"065c6a3d-d219-54db-ad90-211c25da5692","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-039","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Ilorin Hotels Ltd revalued its hotel building upwards by N30 million under the IAS 16 revaluation model. The revaluation gain is recognised in","options":["revenue","profit or loss, as other income","other comprehensive income","the statement of cash flows as an investing inflow"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"Changes in revaluation surplus under IAS 16 are a component of other comprehensive income. Options B and A wrongly put an unrealised revaluation gain in profit or loss, and option D is wrong because no cash has been received."}},{"itemId":"2edf0241-e162-5f3c-9676-6947cadcbffa","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"49ae6943-7720-54f7-8111-4cd956935e6b","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-040","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following items is recognised in profit or loss rather than in other comprehensive income?","options":["An impairment loss on trade receivables under IFRS 9","A remeasurement of a defined benefit pension plan","A gain on translating the financial statements of a foreign operation","A gain on an equity investment designated at fair value through OCI"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"Impairment losses (and their reversals) determined under IFRS 9 are a required line item in the profit or loss section. Options B, C and D are all listed components of other comprehensive income."}},{"itemId":"6f86f544-a060-5afd-83c5-452cbee3adcf","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7242261f-dd0a-53d0-9a4f-60b594d806fd","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-041","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is a component of other comprehensive income?","options":["A gain on the disposal of a delivery van","Interest revenue calculated using the effective interest method","The share of the profit of an associate accounted for using the equity method","The effective portion of gains and losses on hedging instruments in a cash flow hedge"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"The effective portion of gains and losses on hedging instruments in a cash flow hedge is listed as a component of OCI. Option A is a disposal gain reported in profit or loss, while options B and C are required line items of the profit or loss section."}},{"itemId":"b7e5fc95-8301-51cb-ba11-fee09d558a2d","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4dc123a0-1cdf-5625-a13e-854db76f6fcb","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-042","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Nigerian company Zaria Holdings Plc translates the financial statements of its Ghanaian subsidiary into naira and makes a translation loss. This loss is reported in other comprehensive income as an item that","options":["is reported as an extraordinary item","will be reclassified subsequently to profit or loss when specific conditions are met, such as disposal of the foreign operation","is transferred directly to retained earnings each year","will never be reclassified subsequently to profit or loss"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"Gains and losses from translating a foreign operation are OCI items that are reclassified to profit or loss when specific conditions are met, for example when the foreign operation is disposed of. Option D describes items such as revaluation surplus and defined benefit remeasurements, option C has no basis, and option A is prohibited."}},{"itemId":"033b9c87-67b6-5920-aefe-77b1a1167ce0","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7268aca1-d388-5e88-bd95-bf5fcb31cbc7","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-043","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which pair of OCI items will NOT be reclassified subsequently to profit or loss?","options":["Foreign operation translation differences and cash flow hedge gains","Remeasurements of defined benefit plans and cash flow hedge gains","Revaluation surplus changes and foreign operation translation differences","Changes in revaluation surplus and remeasurements of defined benefit plans"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"Reclassification adjustments do not arise on changes in revaluation surplus or on remeasurements of defined benefit plans; these stay out of profit or loss (revaluation surplus may be moved to retained earnings instead). Options A, C and B each include at least one item, foreign translation differences or cash flow hedge gains, that can be reclassified to profit or loss."}},{"itemId":"730b70f0-2306-5602-b2b3-00cef903f838","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4021da5e-cd4e-57ce-9df7-f375b3892686","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-044","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Amounts reclassified to profit or loss in the current period that were recognised in other comprehensive income in the current or previous periods are called","options":["transactions with owners","reclassification adjustments","retrospective restatements","prior period errors"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"Reclassification adjustments move amounts previously recognised in OCI into profit or loss, so that they are not counted twice in total comprehensive income. Options C and D relate to IAS 8 corrections of earlier periods, and option A covers items such as dividends and share issues."}},{"itemId":"f942336e-5536-5cee-94fb-6a0749b41773","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"246c4d9a-e1bb-5738-a521-b61761f0d997","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-045","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"IAS 1 states that items of income or expense may be presented as extraordinary items","options":["only in the other comprehensive income section","only in the notes to the financial statements","only when they are both unusual and material","in neither the statements presenting profit or loss and OCI nor the notes"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"IAS 1 bans the presentation of any item of income or expense as extraordinary, whether in the statements or in the notes to the financial statements. Options B, C and A each suggest a place or condition where extraordinary items would be allowed, and there is none."}},{"itemId":"4631b1a3-b71d-50d2-a2ff-fc3d1676ceaa","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d1962e43-9c3b-5bd5-bc5d-f914f3aef193","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-046","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Total comprehensive income is the change in equity during a period resulting from transactions and other events, other than","options":["changes resulting from foreign currency translation","changes resulting from transactions with owners in their capacity as owners","changes resulting from the sale of inventory","changes resulting from revaluation of property"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"Total comprehensive income covers all income and expense, both profit or loss and OCI, and excludes only owner changes such as share issues and dividends. Options D and A are OCI items and option C gives rise to profit or loss, so all three are part of total comprehensive income."}},{"itemId":"29cd05b8-4b73-5b49-a4ca-b657a30614c8","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"621d435b-b6cc-5227-93fa-4ed474b7cbff","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-047","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following line items appears in a statement of profit or loss prepared using the nature of expense method?","options":["Gross profit","Changes in inventories of finished goods and work in progress","Distribution costs","Cost of sales"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"The nature of expense method groups expenses by their nature, such as changes in inventories, raw materials used, employee benefits and depreciation. Cost of sales (option D), distribution costs (option C) and gross profit (option A) belong to the function of expense (cost of sales) method."}},{"itemId":"67629348-311d-5b23-b66d-b6f1b9808028","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"97bac44a-0348-5dc8-b129-326d664e48cb","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-048","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An entity that classifies expenses by function is required to disclose additional information on the nature of expenses, including","options":["the expected cash flows for the next five years","the market value of its shares","depreciation and amortisation expense and employee benefits expense","the names of its main suppliers"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"Because information on the nature of expenses helps users predict future cash flows, entities using the function method must also disclose depreciation and amortisation and employee benefits expense. Options A, B and D are not disclosures required by this rule; IAS 1 does not require forecasts."}},{"itemId":"fafd614e-c1ff-541d-8448-6ef083173653","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"efdac06e-c8c2-5b80-b571-7f625abd7f40","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-049","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which statement correctly compares the two methods of analysing expenses?","options":["The function method is simple because no allocation is needed, while the nature method needs arbitrary allocations","Both methods require expenses to be allocated to functions","IAS 1 requires all entities to use the nature method","The nature method is simple because no allocation to functions is needed, while the function method can be more relevant but may need arbitrary allocations"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"Under the nature method, expenses are not reallocated among functions, which makes it simple to apply; the function method can give more relevant information but allocating costs may involve arbitrary choices and judgement. Option A reverses the two methods, option B is wrong for the nature method, and option C is wrong because management chooses whichever is reliable and more relevant."}},{"itemId":"262d8eb1-86cc-59d1-8b57-b3ffee1a46cd","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f6a2229e-a541-52b0-8407-9c9fd870f36c","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-050","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The statement of changes in equity is described as linking which two statements?","options":["The statement of financial position and the statement of comprehensive income","The statement of comprehensive income and the statement of cash flows","The statement of cash flows and the notes","The statement of financial position and the statement of cash flows"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"The statement of changes in equity carries total comprehensive income for the period into the equity balances shown in the statement of financial position, so it links those two statements. Options C, D and B pair statements that the statement of changes in equity does not connect in this way."}},{"itemId":"c2bfe3f0-8fab-5ea5-857f-7411e806c8c0","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"979af167-ab93-5b34-93bb-09c3e57c77e0","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-051","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"For each component of equity, the statement of changes in equity reconciles opening and closing carrying amounts, separately disclosing changes resulting from","options":["profit or loss, other comprehensive income and transactions with owners in their capacity as owners","current assets, non-current assets and liabilities","operating, investing and financing activities","revenue, cost of sales and administrative expenses"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"The reconciliation for each component of equity shows, as a minimum, changes from profit or loss, from other comprehensive income and from transactions with owners as owners. Option C is the classification used in the statement of cash flows, option B relates to the statement of financial position, and option D lists profit or loss line items."}},{"itemId":"fc52862f-4a71-5cfe-971a-36b99dc9c0e2","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d398b25b-cac2-5ac1-a94c-c1b01d615086","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-052","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In the statement of changes in equity, which of the following is a transaction with owners in their capacity as owners?","options":["A revaluation gain on land","Profit for the year","Payment of a dividend to ordinary shareholders","A remeasurement loss on a defined benefit plan"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"Dividends paid are distributions to owners in their capacity as owners, as are share issues. Option A and option D are OCI items and option B is profit or loss, so all three form part of total comprehensive income rather than owner transactions."}},{"itemId":"fc44bcdb-dcd2-5a8e-ab6f-ff9cc61a0c53","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"32520300-9d1c-56b1-b252-458d443e7bb1","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-053","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The amount of dividends recognised as distributions to owners during the period, and the related dividends per share, shall be presented","options":["in the other comprehensive income section","in the profit or loss section as an expense","either in the statement of changes in equity or in the notes","only in the directors' report outside the financial statements"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"Dividends recognised as distributions, and dividends per share, go in the statement of changes in equity or the notes to the financial statements, because they are owner transactions and not expenses. Options B and A wrongly treat dividends as part of comprehensive income, and option D puts required information outside the financial statements."}},{"itemId":"0818f5b2-fe50-5f7f-b226-121beeaf3403","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f235f50c-05b1-59ce-ae0d-ad90176eeb4e","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-054","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under IAS 1, the statement of cash flows provides information about","options":["the entity's significant accounting policies","the entity's assets and liabilities at the reporting date","the ability of the entity to generate cash and cash equivalents and its needs to utilise those cash flows","changes in each component of equity during the period"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"Cash flow information shows users the entity's ability to generate cash and cash equivalents and its needs to use them, with detailed rules in IAS 7. Option D describes the statement of changes in equity, option A the notes to the financial statements, and option B the statement of financial position."}},{"itemId":"ce2a9fed-ad7f-5922-85a1-fe532d7c924a","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c221c900-868a-5dd4-9a53-ce7363237ae3","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-055","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is a function of the notes to the financial statements under IAS 1?","options":["Presenting management's forecast profit for the next year","Disclosing information required by IFRSs that is not presented elsewhere in the financial statements","Correcting inappropriate accounting policies through explanation","Replacing the statement of financial position where line items are numerous"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"The notes present the basis of preparation and accounting policies, give information required by IFRSs that is not presented elsewhere, and provide other information relevant to understanding the statements. Option D is wrong because notes support the statements and do not replace them, option C is ruled out because disclosure cannot fix bad policies, and option A is not required."}},{"itemId":"3ec4f1b8-081e-5aff-9cb8-16663c0be345","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"443702fb-7115-58e5-baa4-67f2a2d9f504","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-056","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When the notes follow the order of the line items in the statements, which of the following normally comes FIRST?","options":["Supporting information for items presented in the statements","Other disclosures, including contingent liabilities","Statement of compliance with IFRSs","Summary of significant accounting policies applied"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"The usual order starts with the statement of compliance with IFRSs, then the significant accounting policies, then supporting information for line items, then other disclosures such as contingent liabilities. Options D, A and B come second, third and fourth in that order."}},{"itemId":"b769584d-930b-5b7b-a643-dca94f166113","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9170fb9d-dce9-5453-a6fd-24893cc52ff0","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-057","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following circumstances would give rise to separate disclosure of the nature and amount of an item of income or expense, if material?","options":["Regular cash sales to customers","A write-down of inventories to net realisable value","Routine monthly payment of electricity bills","Payment of ordinary monthly salaries"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"IAS 1 lists write-downs of inventories to net realisable value as a circumstance giving rise to separate disclosure, along with restructurings, disposals of property, plant and equipment, litigation settlements and similar items. Options C, A and D are ordinary recurring transactions that are not on that list."}},{"itemId":"76ae76de-9676-561c-9e1f-fea2e0d63d92","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5292e9b0-4175-51b1-b6c0-f57309a51bf3","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-058","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Okene Foods Plc reports profit for the year of N45 million. During the year it recognised a revaluation gain on land of N8 million and a loss of N3 million on translating a foreign operation. What is total comprehensive income for the year?","options":["N50m","N53m","N45m","N56m"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"Total comprehensive income is profit or loss plus other comprehensive income: N45m + N8m - N3m = N50m. Option B leaves out the translation loss, option D adds the loss as if it were a gain, and option C is profit alone without any OCI."}},{"itemId":"e1d539f8-5f86-56c3-85b3-91f12a68aec7","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e84fca59-60b9-5975-8597-366f5421a723","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-059","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Gombe Agro Ltd reports total comprehensive income of N72 million, which includes other comprehensive income of N12 million. What is its profit for the year?","options":["N72m","N60m","N84m","N12m"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"Profit or loss is total comprehensive income less other comprehensive income: N72m - N12m = N60m. Option C adds OCI instead of deducting it, option A treats total comprehensive income as profit, and option D is OCI alone."}},{"itemId":"5623622e-b48e-569f-a21e-9a73059b0834","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1c54b95d-0b08-5c20-9c53-a6c4d952f852","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-060","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Asaba Hotels Ltd recognises a revaluation gain of N10 million on its building, with related deferred tax at 30%. If it presents OCI items net of related tax effects, what amount appears for this item in OCI?","options":["N7m","N3m","N13m","N10m"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"Presenting net of tax means showing the gain after deducting its tax: N10m x (1 - 0.30) = N7m. Option D is the before-tax amount, option B is only the tax, and option C wrongly adds the tax to the gain."}},{"itemId":"5a23d7db-61cf-5ada-8ca9-2816ffe438fd","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ef0cd10f-1371-5a76-872f-156851619787","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-061","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A Lagos-based group has total comprehensive income of N60 million, of which N12 million is attributable to non-controlling interests. How much total comprehensive income is attributable to owners of the parent?","options":["N12m","N60m","N72m","N48m"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"Total comprehensive income is split between owners of the parent and non-controlling interests, so the parent's share is N60m - N12m = N48m. Option B ignores the NCI share, option C adds it, and option A is the NCI share itself."}},{"itemId":"550e143c-436f-5dd4-8f6a-7b544298728f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1a9983d0-d3c0-540e-8f8c-b1cf138a6cb4","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-062","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Ota Packaging Ltd had opening retained earnings of N120 million. Profit for the year was N35 million, dividends paid were N12 million and N5 million was transferred to general reserve. What is closing retained earnings in the statement of changes in equity?","options":["N143m","N148m","N155m","N138m"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"Retained earnings increase by profit and decrease by dividends and the transfer to general reserve: N120m + N35m - N12m - N5m = N138m. Option A forgets the transfer, option B adds the transfer instead of deducting it, and option C ignores both the dividends and the transfer."}},{"itemId":"0df2429f-2959-5a86-b521-130a6170468e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c0dc75f7-692a-507c-9807-b81d8cd5181c","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-063","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Ijebu Steel Plc had opening share premium of N4 million. During the year it issued 2,000,000 ordinary shares of N1 each at N1.50 per share. What is the closing share premium?","options":["N7m","N6m","N5m","N1m"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"Share premium rises by the excess of the issue price over par value: 2,000,000 x (N1.50 - N1) = N1m, so the closing balance is N4m + N1m = N5m. Option A adds the whole proceeds, option B adds the par value that belongs to share capital, and option D leaves out the opening balance."}},{"itemId":"8a8c9a0f-9f18-54d9-8f67-3be178822972","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"55c48821-3e9a-5159-9063-2a5f19a39f2d","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-064","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Enugu Coal Ltd had total equity of N250 million at the start of the year. Total comprehensive income was N40 million, new shares were issued for N15 million and dividends of N10 million were paid. What is total equity at the end of the year?","options":["N295m","N305m","N280m","N315m"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"Closing equity is opening equity plus total comprehensive income plus owner contributions less owner distributions: N250m + N40m + N15m - N10m = N295m. Option C leaves out the share issue, option B leaves out the dividend, and option D adds the dividend instead of deducting it."}},{"itemId":"418c304b-e42f-5bcc-bada-4ec7b497ef1e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3d190650-1af7-5cfa-9dbf-7ae026a16e81","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-065","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Opening retained earnings of Lokoja Mills Ltd were N80 million before correcting a prior period error that overstated profits by N6 million. Profit for the current year is N20 million and dividends paid are N10 million. What is closing retained earnings?","options":["N84m","N96m","N94m","N90m"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"The error correction adjusts opening retained earnings to N74m (N80m - N6m); adding profit of N20m and deducting dividends of N10m gives N84m. Option D ignores the restatement, option B adds the overstatement instead of removing it, and option C forgets the dividends."}},{"itemId":"080f6a32-7c0a-5a1a-b86c-2305a2a02ab4","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3f05c2ab-011d-5c1d-ba26-e04676f41acf","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-066","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"At 31 December 2025, Kaduna Textiles Ltd has a bank loan of N50 million repayable in five equal annual instalments, the first due on 30 June 2026. The entity has no right to defer any instalment. How much of the loan is a non-current liability?","options":["N50m","N10m","N0m","N40m"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"The instalment due within twelve months (N10m) is current; the remaining N40m is due after more than twelve months and is non-current. Option A ignores the current portion, option B is the current portion, and option C wrongly treats the whole loan as current."}},{"itemId":"5e90c152-08d9-5711-8b1e-a60b5ed926e6","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7c5e6f52-db68-5a31-b1a6-a893963ad7c5","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-067","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Sokoto Grains Ltd has: inventories N18m, trade receivables N12m, unrestricted cash N7m, cash restricted by a court order for three years N5m, plant N60m and investment property N25m. What are its total current assets?","options":["N42m","N30m","N37m","N67m"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"Current assets are inventories, receivables and unrestricted cash: N18m + N12m + N7m = N37m. Cash restricted for at least twelve months is non-current, so option A wrongly includes it; option D also includes investment property, which is long-term, and option B leaves out the unrestricted cash."}},{"itemId":"ce0cc389-bba7-5459-97b3-e98fe6fdc1c7","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"24554f11-83d0-5cb3-944b-a4c020353e87","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-068","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Warri Marine Ltd has: trade payables N22m, bank overdraft N6m, current tax payable N9m, a long-term loan of N40m not due within twelve months, and the current portion of another loan N8m. What are its total current liabilities?","options":["N37m","N85m","N45m","N39m"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"Trade payables, bank overdrafts, current tax and the current portion of loans are all current: N22m + N6m + N9m + N8m = N45m. Option A leaves out the current portion of the loan, option B wrongly adds the long-term loan, and option D leaves out the overdraft."}},{"itemId":"174f50cf-8798-5b1c-89d1-79695a01760e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6d8a99aa-bf83-5113-863f-f8927d01fce3","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-069","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the function of expense method, Akure Furniture Ltd reports revenue of N150 million and cost of sales of N90 million. Distribution costs are N15 million. What is gross profit?","options":["N150m","N45m","N60m","N240m"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"Gross profit is revenue less cost of sales: N150m - N90m = N60m. Option B also deducts distribution costs, which come after gross profit, option A is revenue alone, and option D adds cost of sales instead of deducting it."}},{"itemId":"1569d6a7-604a-5893-899c-7ba6ad43ef07","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b4b29d45-40d6-51f3-a22d-61305552ed08","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-070","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Using the function of expense method, Minna Plastics Ltd has: revenue N200m, cost of sales N120m, other income N5m, distribution costs N18m, administrative expenses N22m and other expenses N4m. What is profit before tax?","options":["N45m","N80m","N31m","N41m"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"Profit before tax = N200m - N120m + N5m - N18m - N22m - N4m = N41m. Option C deducts other income instead of adding it, option B stops at gross profit, and option A leaves out other expenses."}},{"itemId":"a1b881b4-17b6-5075-9208-2386fa13a514","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ce7a9d77-31c3-516a-85c1-7061497ea1d0","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-071","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Using the nature of expense method, Jos Dairies Ltd has: revenue N300m, other income N10m, an increase in inventories of finished goods and work in progress of N12m, raw materials and consumables used N140m, employee benefits expense N60m, depreciation N25m and other expenses N20m. What is profit before tax?","options":["N67m","N77m","N102m","N53m"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"An increase in inventories of finished goods and work in progress reduces total expenses, so total expenses = -N12m + N140m + N60m + N25m + N20m = N233m and profit before tax = N300m + N10m - N233m = N77m. Option D treats the inventory increase as an extra expense, option A leaves out other income, and option C leaves out depreciation."}},{"itemId":"bb9827e2-aad6-5dd9-8d3e-7fcba115a337","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"062d7e1c-b294-586b-a163-fc15eb12ff32","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-072","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Bauchi Haulage Ltd sold a truck for N12 million. The truck's carrying amount was N9 million and selling expenses were N0.5 million. Under IAS 1's netting rule for disposals, what gain is presented?","options":["N12m","N2.5m","N11.5m","N3m"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"Gains on disposal of non-current assets are shown net: proceeds less carrying amount less related selling expenses, N12m - N9m - N0.5m = N2.5m. Option D ignores selling expenses, option A presents the gross proceeds, and option C ignores the carrying amount."}},{"itemId":"2bd654bc-0958-57c9-b06f-6cd61a741aa0","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6eb18cf2-ee94-571b-a92f-8dc7a6f81050","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-073","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Yola Distributors Ltd has gross trade receivables of N30 million and an allowance for doubtful debts of N2 million. What amount is presented for trade receivables in the statement of financial position?","options":["N30m","N2m","N32m","N28m"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"Receivables are measured net of the valuation allowance, N30m - N2m = N28m, and IAS 1 says this is not offsetting. Option A ignores the allowance, option C adds it, and option B is the allowance alone."}},{"itemId":"3e6ba1ff-3234-53fd-b244-4a2f3768c40c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c43d216d-ae9a-5efe-9440-66f35a0cf48a","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-074","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"During the year, Nnewi Motors Plc recognised a translation gain of N1 million on its foreign subsidiary and then sold the subsidiary, reclassifying cumulative translation gains of N4 million to profit or loss. What is the net amount shown for this item in OCI for the year?","options":["N4m","N5m","N1m","(N3m)"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"The N4m reclassification adjustment is deducted from OCI in the year it moves into profit or loss, so that it is not counted twice in total comprehensive income: N1m - N4m = a net loss of N3m. Option B adds the reclassification instead of deducting it, option C ignores it, and option A shows only the reclassified amount as a gain."}},{"itemId":"0033636a-56f7-50d9-affb-2d37aede89e2","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0e15f2d8-9456-5fd7-96ab-28aa01ac9fb6","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-075","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Ogbomoso Farms Ltd has property, plant and equipment of N80m, inventory of N15m, trade and other receivables of N10m and cash and cash equivalents of N5m. Its share capital is N50m. What is the total assets figure in its statement of financial position?","options":["N30m","N110m","N60m","N160m"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"Total assets are non-current assets plus current assets: N80m + (N15m + N10m + N5m) = N110m. Option A is current assets only, while options C and D wrongly deduct or add share capital, which is part of equity and liabilities, not assets."}},{"itemId":"4e00b8cb-5332-55eb-927d-a3a408975dee","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"60958c66-84b8-5918-9a66-997131ef5858","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I1-076","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Kebbi Rice Plc's OCI for the year comprises: revaluation gain on land N6m, remeasurement loss on its defined benefit plan N2m, gain on translating a foreign operation N3m and a loss on the effective portion of a cash flow hedge N1m. What total is presented for items that will NOT be reclassified subsequently to profit or loss?","options":["N2m","N8m","N4m","N6m"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements","explanation":"Revaluation surplus changes and defined benefit remeasurements are never reclassified to profit or loss, so that group totals N6m - N2m = N4m. Option A is the group that may be reclassified (translation and cash flow hedge items), option D is total OCI for the year, and option B adds the remeasurement loss as if it were a gain."}},{"itemId":"e781edf7-be20-501d-b0ba-1087ad642787","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0403703a-0966-5953-a053-7ec87477b2fb","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I16-001","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The objective of IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment is to prescribe the accounting treatment for PPE so that users of financial statements can","options":["determine the market value of the entity's shares at the reporting date","discern information about an entity's investment in its PPE and the changes in that investment","compare the selling prices of the entity's inventories with their cost","assess the entity's ability to settle its short-term liabilities as they fall due"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment","explanation":"IAS 16 exists so that users can see how much an entity has invested in its property, plant and equipment and how that investment changes over time. Option A is about share valuation and option D describes liquidity analysis, neither of which is the aim of IAS 16. Option C relates to inventory measurement, which is dealt with under IAS 2."}},{"itemId":"124c0644-a697-571f-99d5-f42499a1ff4d","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f258852c-96ad-54af-b07b-a3bb07d3da5f","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I16-002","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Tangible items that are held for use in the production or supply of goods or services, for rentals to others, or for administrative purposes, and are expected to be used during more than one period, are called","options":["property, plant and equipment","inventories","intangible assets","current assets"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment","explanation":"This is the IAS 16 definition of property, plant and equipment: tangible, held for use (not for sale), and used for more than one period. Option B is wrong because inventories are held for sale or consumed in production, and option C is wrong because intangible assets have no physical substance. Option D is wrong because PPE is used over more than one period, so it is non-current."}},{"itemId":"65700624-06d4-5988-a6a8-2db4508eeb7a","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a362c1f6-e205-5cd4-b114-4911a9490cd4","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I16-003","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Ikeja Motors Ltd sells new cars. Which of the following items would it classify as property, plant and equipment?","options":["Cash held in the company's bank account","The showroom building from which it sells the cars","The new cars displayed in the showroom for sale to customers","Amounts owed by customers who bought cars on credit"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment","explanation":"The showroom is a tangible item held for use in supplying goods and will be used for more than one period, so it is PPE. Option C is wrong because cars held for sale are the dealer's inventory. Options A and D are financial assets (cash and receivables), not tangible items held for use."}},{"itemId":"9ba7814e-ed52-54ef-881a-e3309a01aaef","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b7f36b5e-5b0c-5ed6-8d7d-b7c229a03b2f","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I16-004","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The amount of cash or cash equivalents paid, or the fair value of the other consideration given, to acquire an asset at the time of its acquisition or construction is the asset's","options":["cost","recoverable amount","residual value","carrying amount"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment","explanation":"Cost is what is given up to acquire or construct the asset, measured at the time of acquisition or construction. Option D is cost less accumulated depreciation and impairment, option C is the estimated disposal amount at the end of useful life, and option B is the higher of fair value less costs of disposal and value in use."}},{"itemId":"fce63d5c-6cb6-5566-be7b-a9953eb4387f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"96238989-43b6-5c7b-9975-71c488c8b4eb","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I16-005","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The cost of an asset, or other amount substituted for cost, less its residual value is known as the","options":["carrying amount","depreciable amount","accumulated depreciation","recoverable amount"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment","explanation":"Depreciable amount is the part of the cost that will be spread over the useful life, so it is cost less residual (scrap) value. Option A deducts accumulated depreciation and impairment, not residual value. Option C is the total depreciation charged to date, and option D is used for impairment testing."}},{"itemId":"06b76171-d306-50d9-96a4-cfe4d22c5e65","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8401f286-fb95-5946-936c-0db4968f1537","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I16-007","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The carrying amount of an item of property, plant and equipment is the amount at which it is recognised after deducting","options":["the costs of disposal from its fair value","import duties and non-refundable purchase taxes","its residual value only","any accumulated depreciation and accumulated impairment losses"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment","explanation":"Carrying amount is cost (or revalued amount) minus accumulated depreciation and accumulated impairment losses. Option C gives the depreciable amount, not the carrying amount. Option A describes fair value less costs of disposal, and option B lists items that are added to cost, not deducted."}},{"itemId":"047e40d8-425f-5886-acc4-48a66afc0dba","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f75d0731-809b-5446-a45c-fde1c97e5173","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I16-008","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The price that would be received to sell an asset or paid to transfer a liability in an orderly transaction between market participants at the measurement date is","options":["residual value","fair value","value in use","cost"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment","explanation":"This is the definition of fair value, an exit price between market participants (see IFRS 13). Option C is the present value of cash flows from using the asset, option D is what was paid to acquire it, and option A is the estimated disposal amount at the end of useful life."}},{"itemId":"e2e248f1-9c2a-545c-b992-1b35d226808e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3b0972e8-dc00-56ca-a7e3-50ed0d903994","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I16-009","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An impairment loss is the amount by which","options":["the recoverable amount of an asset exceeds its carrying amount","the carrying amount of an asset exceeds its recoverable amount","the cost of an asset exceeds its residual value","the fair value of an asset exceeds its cost"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment","explanation":"An impairment loss arises when an asset is carried at more than it can recover, so it is carrying amount minus recoverable amount. Option A is the reverse situation, where there is no impairment. Option C is the depreciable amount, and option D would be a revaluation increase, not a loss."}},{"itemId":"32780df4-94e7-5ff3-9981-c7daf6bc3ee4","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9239c7c1-8e57-5c18-883a-5d113515efb9","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I16-010","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Recoverable amount is the","options":["lower of an asset's fair value less costs of disposal and its value in use","lower of an asset's carrying amount and its residual value","higher of an asset's cost and its fair value","higher of an asset's fair value less costs of disposal and its value in use"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment","explanation":"Recoverable amount is the HIGHER of fair value less costs of disposal and value in use, because a sensible entity would either sell or keep using the asset, whichever gives more. Option A wrongly uses the lower figure. Options C and B mix up cost, carrying amount and residual value, which are not part of this definition."}},{"itemId":"dee4f411-5d4c-5654-bf6e-b2e5a8f9cbaa","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"bb75bd2a-5ea8-5d0b-adf1-3fd1b836cd14","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I16-011","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The estimated amount that an entity would currently obtain from disposal of an asset, after deducting the estimated costs of disposal, if the asset were already of the age and in the condition expected at the end of its useful life, is the","options":["carrying amount","residual value","recoverable amount","fair value"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment","explanation":"This is residual (scrap) value: today's estimate of what the asset would fetch, net of disposal costs, if it were already at the end of its useful life. Option C is the higher of fair value less costs of disposal and value in use, option D is a current exit price for the asset in its present condition, and option A is cost less accumulated depreciation and impairment."}},{"itemId":"3c8ec554-f22e-5d3a-9370-2d60539bb0f4","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6cda15e6-886c-5922-8922-666bce8c299f","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I16-012","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under IAS 16, the useful life of an asset may be expressed as either the period over which it is expected to be available for use, or","options":["the period until its fair value falls to zero","the number of years its manufacturer's warranty lasts","the period remaining until its loan is fully repaid","the number of production or similar units expected to be obtained from it"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment","explanation":"Useful life can be measured in time or in output: the number of production or similar units the entity expects to get from the asset. Options C and B tie useful life to financing or warranty terms, which do not define how long the entity will use the asset. Option A is wrong because useful life is about expected use by the entity, not market value."}},{"itemId":"c8f67de7-8d04-5b9a-8591-39247d42f724","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"28ca487e-6510-59e8-b324-158e8db96655","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I16-013","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under IAS 16, the cost of an item of property, plant and equipment is recognised as an asset if, and only if,","options":["the item has been fully paid for in cash and it will be used for more than one period","it is probable that future economic benefits associated with the item will flow to the entity and its cost can be measured reliably","the item's fair value can be measured reliably and the item is insured against loss","it is probable that the item will be sold within twelve months and its cost exceeds a set minimum amount"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment","explanation":"The two recognition criteria are that future economic benefits are probable and that the cost can be measured reliably. Option A is wrong because an item bought on credit can still be recognised. Option C is wrong because recognition depends on cost, not fair value or insurance, and option D describes something held for sale, which is not PPE."}},{"itemId":"f4d752e4-5d0e-5fae-8393-f0cbe1073e3e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ec4bfdbd-8bb9-5892-8038-1db22937cc89","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I16-014","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"At initial recognition, IAS 16 requires an item of property, plant and equipment to be measured at","options":["cost","fair value","value in use","net realisable value"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment","explanation":"IAS 16 requires PPE to be measured at cost when it is first recognised. Option B can only be used later if the entity chooses the revaluation model. Option D is an inventory measure, and option C is used only in impairment testing."}},{"itemId":"b5a488d5-4dde-5d60-912d-6679fa4d4f61","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"234214a1-8bf3-5662-b4da-3498cb1102e1","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I16-015","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Directly attributable costs that may be included in the cost of PPE are best described as costs that","options":["are incurred to run the asset after it has been brought into use","are shared by all departments of the business","arise from training staff to operate the asset","would have been avoided if the asset had not been purchased or constructed"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment","explanation":"A directly attributable cost exists only because the asset is being bought or built, so it would have been avoided otherwise. Option A describes running costs after the asset is ready for use, which are expensed. Option B describes general overheads, and option C describes training costs, both of which are excluded from cost."}},{"itemId":"9e5abd81-485e-5c6f-9320-d2452822e69e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4d179c40-7d7d-517c-9d3f-d072c67dc97c","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I16-016","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a directly attributable cost of an item of property, plant and equipment?","options":["Initial delivery and handling costs","Repainting the asset after it has been in use","Installation and assembly costs","Costs of site preparation"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment","explanation":"Repainting an asset that is already in use is a maintenance cost and is listed among the expenses excluded from the cost of PPE. Site preparation, initial delivery and handling, and installation and assembly (options D, A and C) are all directly attributable costs that are capitalised."}},{"itemId":"e5c4d081-a86f-5a01-bfd9-5c130c2607f2","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f38384e2-ba8e-57e8-957c-6026142d903e","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I16-017","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"How are the expected future costs of dismantling and removing an item of PPE and restoring its site treated at initial recognition?","options":["They are expensed only when the dismantling actually takes place","The full undiscounted estimate is included in cost","The initial estimate is included in cost, discounted to its present value","They are deducted from the purchase price as a rebate"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment","explanation":"The initial estimate of dismantling, removal and site restoration costs forms part of the cost of the asset, and because the cash is paid in future it is discounted to present value. Option B ignores discounting, option A ignores the requirement to include the estimate in cost at the start, and option D confuses it with trade discounts and rebates."}},{"itemId":"323fbb01-e8d9-582a-b3d4-d21690bd1ee3","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"762feee8-8be7-5d8e-adc4-c96e54d6057c","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I16-018","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Lekki Bottling Ltd finished installing a new bottling line on 1 March and it was capable of operating from that date. Because orders were low, it was not used until 1 June, and it then ran below normal capacity for two months. How should costs incurred after 1 March be treated?","options":["They are capitalised until the line reaches normal capacity","They are capitalised until the end of the financial year","They are expensed, because capitalisation stops when the asset is ready for use","They are capitalised until 1 June, when the line was first used"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment","explanation":"Capitalisation ends when the asset is ready for use, meaning capable of operating as intended by management, even if it is brought into use later. Options D and A are wrong because waiting for first use or for demand to build up does not justify further capitalisation. Option B has no basis in IAS 16."}},{"itemId":"fc4b79c7-189e-5cae-9f24-9a3b6bcf18a4","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1b3afff6-b196-535b-b3d3-ecd9cfdd4b56","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I16-019","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Subsequent expenditure on an item of property, plant and equipment should be capitalised only when it","options":["enhances the benefits of the asset, such as increasing the revenue it can generate","is incurred to restore the asset after normal wear and tear","is larger than the annual depreciation charge on the asset","is paid for in cash rather than on credit"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment","explanation":"Subsequent costs are added to the asset only if they enhance its benefits, for example by extending its life or increasing the revenue it can generate. Option D is irrelevant because the payment method does not decide capitalisation. Option B describes repairs, which are expensed, and option C is not a test used by IAS 16."}},{"itemId":"39693161-28dc-52c0-a880-924f57430ded","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b85d5bbf-9764-56d0-879c-7f124195f1fb","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I16-020","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In simple terms, the main purpose of charging depreciation is to","options":["build up a cash fund to replace the asset at the end of its life","match the cost of using a non-current asset with the revenue it generates over its useful life","show the asset at its current market value each year","reduce the amount of tax the business pays"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment","explanation":"Depreciation spreads the cost of an asset over the periods in which it is used, matching that cost against the revenue the asset helps to earn. Option A is wrong because depreciation is not a cash fund. Option C is wrong because depreciation is an allocation of cost, not a valuation, and option D is not the accounting purpose of depreciation."}},{"itemId":"ce8fff60-aefa-5e59-a2cd-c3bf614abb0f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d263dc6d-8232-5dfe-b1d4-df2f66d3a9e2","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I16-021","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a cause of depreciation?","options":["Passage of time","Obsolescence","Physical wear and tear","An increase in the market price of similar assets"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment","explanation":"A rise in market prices does not reduce an asset's usefulness, so it does not cause depreciation. Physical wear and tear, obsolescence and passage of time (options C, B and A) are all causes of depreciation, along with use, change in technology or fashion, and depletion."}},{"itemId":"23712cb3-3428-5c79-977e-80f138528dcc","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"198d86e7-1e9e-5084-aee5-9479a61484a3","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I16-022","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An oil company's well in the Niger Delta loses value as crude oil is extracted from it each year. This cause of depreciation is best described as","options":["change in fashion","depletion","obsolescence","passage of time"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment","explanation":"Depletion is the cause of depreciation for mineral resources: as the oil is extracted, less remains. Option A applies to items that go out of style, option D applies to assets with a fixed legal or time-based life, and option C applies when an asset is overtaken by newer ones."}},{"itemId":"011d00c6-84e7-5c2d-91c3-8e92d23e0bdf","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8323cfa1-9066-58e0-954b-07f40c794b81","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I16-023","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a time-based method of depreciation?","options":["Units of production method","Sum of the digit method","Annuity method","Sinking fund method"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment","explanation":"The units of production method charges depreciation based on output, so it is a service-based method. The sum of the digit, sinking fund and annuity methods (options B, D and C) are time-based methods, together with straight line and reducing balance."}},{"itemId":"2e7d717f-51d6-5c5c-82a4-9b80200f2820","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4d5be72c-29ff-5d52-93e8-e69e9cc879f5","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I16-024","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under IAS 16, the depreciation method applied to an asset should reflect","options":["the pattern in which the asset's future economic benefits are expected to be consumed","the method that gives the lowest charge in the first year","the method used by the entity's main competitors","the pattern in which the asset's purchase price was paid"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment","explanation":"IAS 16 requires the depreciation method to follow the pattern in which the entity expects to consume the asset's future economic benefits. Options B and C pick a method for convenience or comparison rather than consumption pattern. Option D confuses depreciation with the payment schedule."}},{"itemId":"3328193d-bce3-511b-9a51-6b27fd2eaa01","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"cdc1d1ec-ab38-5315-a174-9d51b474e7cf","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I16-025","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The depreciation method that charges the same amount every year and is also known as the fixed instalment method is the","options":["straight line method","units of production method","reducing balance method","sum of the digit method"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment","explanation":"The straight line method divides the depreciable amount equally over the useful life, so each year bears the same charge. Option C charges more in early years, option B varies with output, and option D also gives a falling charge over time."}},{"itemId":"d937c751-652e-528a-a7f7-92c0a7caa954","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2bf1db9d-247f-5ea2-a7fe-2293bf1ebc3a","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I16-026","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which statement correctly describes the reducing balance method of depreciation?","options":["A fixed rate is applied to the original cost each year, giving the same charge every year","The depreciable amount is divided equally by the number of years of useful life","A fixed rate is applied to the carrying amount at the beginning of each year, giving higher charges in early years","The charge depends on the number of units produced by the asset in each year"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment","explanation":"Under reducing balance, a fixed rate is applied to the opening carrying amount, so the charge is highest at the start and falls each year. Options A and B describe the straight line method, which gives an equal charge. Option D describes the units of production method."}},{"itemId":"6217811c-8503-51a5-8fa5-d53676228b53","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a6a94249-0bf6-5adb-9292-1f5ab5842fc1","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I16-027","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"IAS 16 requires the residual value and useful life of an asset to be reviewed at least at each financial year-end. If expectations differ from previous estimates, the change is accounted for as","options":["a change in accounting policy, applied retrospectively","a change in accounting estimate, applied prospectively","a prior period error, corrected by restating comparatives","an impairment loss recognised in other comprehensive income"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment","explanation":"A revised useful life or residual value is a change in accounting estimate, so it affects the current and future periods only. Options A and C would require restating earlier years, which is wrong because earlier estimates were reasonable when made. Option D confuses a revised estimate with impairment."}},{"itemId":"2b179c56-4f89-51c7-aecf-73550c74298b","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a2a891fa-edc2-5cda-8574-15bfca8749d4","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I16-028","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the cost model in IAS 16, after recognition an item of PPE is carried at","options":["the lower of cost and net realisable value","fair value at each reporting date with no depreciation","fair value at the date of revaluation less subsequent depreciation and impairment","cost less accumulated depreciation and accumulated impairment losses"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment","explanation":"The cost model keeps the asset at its original cost and deducts accumulated depreciation and accumulated impairment losses. Option C describes the revaluation model. Option B is not permitted for PPE, which must still be depreciated, and option A is the inventory rule."}},{"itemId":"374f21af-8eff-5162-ac0d-4e95d6162fcd","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"801b8f59-1a90-54bb-ba17-9ee0409bf159","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I16-029","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the revaluation model in IAS 16, an item of PPE whose fair value can be measured reliably is carried at","options":["its residual value until it is disposed of","its original cost less accumulated depreciation and impairment losses","its fair value at the date of revaluation less subsequent accumulated depreciation and impairment losses","its recoverable amount at each reporting date"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment","explanation":"The revaluation model carries the asset at a revalued amount, which is fair value at the revaluation date less any later accumulated depreciation and impairment losses. Option B is the cost model. Option D is used only to measure impairment, and option A confuses carrying amount with residual value."}},{"itemId":"44a90ca0-63b2-596f-b628-9cb651b2d943","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"949bfaab-8f3e-56c7-acff-3b95c70425dd","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I16-030","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When the carrying amount of an item of PPE is increased as a result of a revaluation (with no previous revaluation decrease), the increase is","options":["recognised as a liability until the asset is sold","recognised as revenue in profit or loss","deducted from accumulated depreciation of other assets","recognised in other comprehensive income and accumulated in equity as revaluation surplus"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment","explanation":"A revaluation increase goes to other comprehensive income and builds up in equity under the heading revaluation surplus. Option B is wrong because an unrealised gain from revaluation is not revenue. Options C and A have no basis in IAS 16."}},{"itemId":"24f42313-5230-5f24-8414-4aa63cfb3b30","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"95478148-ece9-510e-9c11-0f8c8421cad2","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I16-031","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Benin Foods Ltd decides to revalue one of its factory buildings under the revaluation model. IAS 16 requires that","options":["all assets of the company, including inventories, are revalued","only that one building is revalued and the others stay at cost","the entire class of property, plant and equipment to which that building belongs is revalued","the building is revalued only if its fair value is below cost"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment","explanation":"If an item of PPE is revalued, the whole class to which it belongs must be revalued, which stops entities from picking only favourable items. Option B allows selective revaluation, which is not allowed. Option A goes too far because inventories are not PPE, and option D is wrong because revaluation applies whether fair value rises or falls."}},{"itemId":"78c16cd9-a7a9-51f7-8af5-bf71e3e61796","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b96a3f40-9658-54b8-8be2-637c60ad9f6c","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I16-032","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An item of property, plant and equipment should be derecognised","options":["at the end of each financial year and then recognised again","when its fair value falls below its carrying amount","on disposal, or when no future economic benefits are expected from its use or disposal","when it has been fully depreciated, even if it is still in use"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment","explanation":"PPE is removed from the statement of financial position when it is disposed of, or when no future economic benefits are expected from using or disposing of it. Option D is wrong because a fully depreciated asset still in use is kept in the records. Option B may indicate impairment but is not derecognition, and option A has no basis."}},{"itemId":"e3c4fb45-de90-5b3c-afcd-920752a8e68e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5e323f69-e78b-5702-b3aa-6c89163110b7","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I16-033","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The gain or loss on derecognition of an item of PPE is calculated as the difference between the net disposal proceeds and the carrying amount, and it is","options":["recognised as revenue from contracts with customers","added to or deducted from share capital","recognised in profit or loss, with any gain not classified as revenue","recognised in other comprehensive income"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment","explanation":"The gain or loss on disposal goes to profit or loss when the item is derecognised, and IAS 16 says gains are not classified as revenue. Option D is wrong because disposal gains and losses do not go to OCI. Option B has no basis, and option A contradicts the rule that gains are not revenue."}},{"itemId":"cd8bcdc3-e402-59fb-a855-480fd7c70dc9","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e8db8a8b-d400-5327-96e0-4295cae51c79","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I16-034","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Apapa Plastics Ltd bought a moulding machine. Details: list price N6,000,000; import duty N450,000; recoverable VAT N450,000; trade discount N300,000; delivery N120,000; installation N200,000; testing N80,000; staff training N150,000; allocated administration overheads N100,000. What cost should be capitalised?","options":["N6,800,000","N6,850,000","N6,550,000","N7,000,000"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment","explanation":"Cost = price N6,000,000 + import duty N450,000 - trade discount N300,000 + delivery, installation and testing N400,000 = N6,550,000. Option D wrongly adds the VAT, which is recoverable so it is not part of cost. Option A includes training and administration overheads, which are excluded, and option B forgets to deduct the trade discount."}},{"itemId":"07696e28-b750-5e34-a5e0-194297c0ec07","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"741d6985-b2bc-5ed5-99c9-67b563bcfb7e","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I16-035","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Yaba Logistics Ltd bought a delivery van. Costs: van N3,800,000; registration number N120,000; road licence fee N30,000; delivery charge for the van N45,000; cost of testing the van N60,000; driver's wages for the first year of operation N900,000. What amount should be capitalised as motor vehicle?","options":["N3,845,000","N4,955,000","N4,025,000","N4,055,000"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment","explanation":"The van, registration, delivery and testing are needed to get the van ready for use: N3,800,000 + N120,000 + N45,000 + N60,000 = N4,025,000. Option D adds the road licence fee, which is a running cost of operating the vehicle. Option B also adds the driver's wages, an operating expense, and option A leaves out registration and testing, which are capitalised."}},{"itemId":"71516e03-f490-54d0-b173-8a5f0ebdfb30","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2dd1c50d-e9b7-523a-9eda-d051dfddc6dd","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I16-036","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Bonny Offshore Ltd built a drilling platform at a cost of N50,000,000. It must dismantle the platform and restore the site in 10 years, at an estimated cost of N20,000,000. The present value of that estimate is N7,700,000. What is the initial cost of the platform?","options":["N42,300,000","N70,000,000","N50,000,000","N57,700,000"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment","explanation":"The initial estimate of dismantling and restoration costs is included in cost at its discounted (present) value: N50,000,000 + N7,700,000 = N57,700,000. Option B adds the undiscounted amount, option C ignores the dismantling cost entirely, and option A wrongly deducts it."}},{"itemId":"0b867984-f035-550c-9fcf-173a947ceb81","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8870f209-5017-550a-9bf3-176cba05c51a","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I16-037","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Agege Foods Ltd built its own storage facility. Costs: building supplies used N4,000,000; employee benefits of staff working directly on the construction N1,500,000; building supplies wasted through an abnormal accident N300,000; site preparation N500,000; general overheads of the company N250,000. What cost should be capitalised?","options":["N6,000,000","N6,250,000","N6,300,000","N6,550,000"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment","explanation":"Building supplies used, directly related employee benefits and site preparation are capitalised: N4,000,000 + N1,500,000 + N500,000 = N6,000,000. Option C includes the wasted supplies, which are abnormal costs. Option B includes general overheads, which are excluded, and option D includes both."}},{"itemId":"00276b5d-5791-5b85-9a37-f3e8e8713e32","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2cc1880d-5c40-5545-9ffc-3e4643015941","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I16-038","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A generator was bought for N3,600,000 with an expected residual value of N400,000 and a useful life of 8 years. What is the annual depreciation under the straight line method?","options":["N500,000","N450,000","N400,000","N457,143"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment","explanation":"Straight line depreciation = (cost - residual value) / useful life = (N3,600,000 - N400,000) / 8 = N400,000. Option B ignores the residual value, option A adds the residual value instead of deducting it, and option D divides by the wrong number of years."}},{"itemId":"49e8e047-fbab-5616-b6c5-282d52c74522","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"fb72a884-73dc-5ac7-b141-14c8ae1abd3d","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I16-039","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A generator cost N3,600,000, has a residual value of N400,000 and a useful life of 8 years. It is depreciated on the straight line basis. What is its carrying amount at the end of year 3?","options":["N2,400,000","N2,250,000","N2,000,000","N1,200,000"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment","explanation":"Annual depreciation is (N3,600,000 - N400,000) / 8 = N400,000, so accumulated depreciation after 3 years is N1,200,000. Carrying amount = N3,600,000 - N1,200,000 = N2,400,000. Option D is the accumulated depreciation, option B ignores the residual value when working out depreciation, and option C wrongly deducts the residual value again."}},{"itemId":"18bcb48c-402e-5500-bd23-f1f7cb786fd2","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0e4fb588-acb5-57d6-94bf-d9441994231b","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I16-040","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Equipment costing N4,800,000 has an estimated residual value of N300,000 and a useful life of 6 years. Using the straight line method, what is the accumulated depreciation at the end of year 4?","options":["N2,250,000","N3,200,000","N1,800,000","N3,000,000"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment","explanation":"Annual depreciation = (N4,800,000 - N300,000) / 6 = N750,000, so accumulated depreciation after 4 years = N750,000 x 4 = N3,000,000. Option B ignores the residual value, option C is the carrying amount rather than accumulated depreciation, and option A counts only three years."}},{"itemId":"e5921612-f03d-5159-bb74-37f775ed9d9c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a7d6fe3b-129b-53ba-9604-a2f05751810a","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I16-041","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A bus costs N9,000,000 and is expected to have a residual value of N1,500,000 at the end of its useful life of 5 years. What is its depreciable amount?","options":["N7,500,000","N10,500,000","N9,000,000","N1,500,000"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment","explanation":"Depreciable amount = cost - residual value = N9,000,000 - N1,500,000 = N7,500,000. Option C ignores the residual value, option B adds it, and option D is the annual straight line charge, not the depreciable amount."}},{"itemId":"f7aa0bde-08ce-53fa-91ec-d9ff3a167c18","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"62d770a6-391a-51da-9c37-6642ca678385","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I16-042","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A machine costing N2,000,000 is depreciated using the reducing balance method at 25% per annum. What is the depreciation charge for year 1?","options":["N500,000","N375,000","N1,500,000","N125,000"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment","explanation":"In year 1, depreciation = rate x cost = 25% x N2,000,000 = N500,000. Option C is the carrying amount at the end of year 1, option B is the year 2 charge, and option D wrongly spreads the rate as if over four years."}},{"itemId":"2d8e4fb5-0058-5956-80b5-4f3967105bab","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"45656c56-9f75-511d-9489-5bb7aa1425a0","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I16-043","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A machine costing N2,000,000 is depreciated using the reducing balance method at 25% per annum. What is the depreciation charge for year 2?","options":["N1,125,000","N1,000,000","N375,000","N500,000"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment","explanation":"Year 1 depreciation is N500,000, leaving a carrying amount of N1,500,000. Year 2 depreciation = 25% x N1,500,000 = N375,000. Option D applies the rate to cost again (straight line thinking), option B is accumulated depreciation under that same mistake, and option A is the carrying amount at the end of year 2."}},{"itemId":"d7e32b95-26df-529f-8e52-73df9f9b1074","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"afe5db8e-7195-5c17-bd9b-ba8dfd0031b4","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I16-044","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A machine cost N2,000,000 and is depreciated using the reducing balance method at 25% per annum. What is its carrying amount at the end of year 3?","options":["N1,156,250","N843,750","N1,125,000","N500,000"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment","explanation":"Using carrying amount = P(1 - r)^n: N2,000,000 x 0.75^3 = N843,750. Option D deducts 25% of cost each year, which is straight line thinking. Option C is the carrying amount after only two years, and option A is the accumulated depreciation."}},{"itemId":"17cd5508-f880-50d2-ade5-63ee66bc80f9","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"98a89f97-168f-5142-aaeb-2d9b89b3f2b6","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I16-045","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Ojota Printers Ltd bought a press for N800,000. Its useful life is 4 years and its expected residual value is N192,080. Using R = (1 - nth root of (S/C)) x 100, what is the reducing balance rate?","options":["70%","30%","19%","25%"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment","explanation":"S/C = N192,080 / N800,000 = 0.2401, and the 4th root of 0.2401 is 0.7. R = (1 - 0.7) x 100 = 30%. Option C is the straight line charge expressed as a percentage of cost, option D simply divides 100% by four years, and option A forgets to subtract the root from 1."}},{"itemId":"6c76045e-6475-5442-b79f-ff68e393d79f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a51ec5b0-4e93-5cd6-a46c-7e3ec42d8dac","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I16-046","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A vehicle that cost N1,250,000 is depreciated at 20% per annum on the reducing balance basis. Using A = P(1 - r)^n, what is its carrying amount at the end of year 3?","options":["N500,000","N800,000","N610,000","N640,000"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment","explanation":"A = N1,250,000 x (1 - 0.2)^3 = N1,250,000 x 0.512 = N640,000. Option A deducts 20% of cost each year, which is the straight line approach. Option B is the carrying amount after two years, and option C is the accumulated depreciation after three years."}},{"itemId":"062c8583-0e8e-59b7-b121-78f902c1de96","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"925cd078-c254-5c09-b6ed-45368883dfd1","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I16-047","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A bottling machine costs N12,000,000, has a residual value of N2,000,000 and is expected to produce 200,000 crates over its useful life. In year 1 it produced 30,000 crates. Using the units of production method, what is the year 1 depreciation?","options":["N1,800,000","N1,500,000","N2,100,000","N2,000,000"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment","explanation":"Depreciation per crate = (N12,000,000 - N2,000,000) / 200,000 = N50; year 1 charge = N50 x 30,000 = N1,500,000. Option A ignores the residual value, option D uses an assumed five-year straight line life instead of output, and option C adds the residual value."}},{"itemId":"e6c32fd1-2002-5a7f-9cba-d8c7e62e09ea","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d34ead12-66d2-53cc-9ef2-d7ca876d72c7","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I16-048","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Machinery that cost N5,000,000 with accumulated depreciation of N3,200,000 was sold for N2,100,000. What is the gain or loss on disposal?","options":["Gain of N300,000","Loss of N300,000","Gain of N2,100,000","Loss of N2,900,000"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment","explanation":"Carrying amount = N5,000,000 - N3,200,000 = N1,800,000. Proceeds of N2,100,000 exceed that by N300,000, so there is a gain. Option B has the right figure but the wrong direction, option C treats all the proceeds as gain, and option D compares proceeds with cost instead of carrying amount."}},{"itemId":"d746fa6a-0fed-54ef-855c-d442e623863e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c011a600-dd6c-5cdb-a741-dbf9c6875add","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I16-049","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A truck was bought for N6,000,000 with a residual value of N600,000 and a useful life of 5 years, depreciated on the straight line basis. It was sold at the end of year 3 for N2,500,000. What is the gain or loss on disposal?","options":["Gain of N100,000","Loss of N3,500,000","Loss of N260,000","Gain of N260,000"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment","explanation":"Annual depreciation = (N6,000,000 - N600,000) / 5 = N1,080,000; after 3 years accumulated depreciation is N3,240,000 and carrying amount is N2,760,000. Proceeds of N2,500,000 are below that, giving a loss of N260,000. Option D reverses the direction, option A ignores the residual value when calculating depreciation, and option B compares proceeds with cost."}},{"itemId":"2bcd1bd2-8a6f-5846-b169-0d8ae9e6b178","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1f8b8fc2-051b-5af1-badc-1779282d7b2c","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I16-050","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Office equipment with a cost of N4,000,000 and accumulated depreciation of N2,500,000 was sold for N1,800,000. Selling costs of N100,000 were paid. What is the gain on disposal?","options":["N300,000","N1,700,000","N100,000","N200,000"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment","explanation":"Net disposal proceeds = N1,800,000 - N100,000 = N1,700,000; carrying amount = N4,000,000 - N2,500,000 = N1,500,000. Gain = N1,700,000 - N1,500,000 = N200,000. Option A ignores the selling costs, option B treats the net proceeds as the gain, and option C deducts the selling costs twice."}},{"itemId":"b0702410-6e7e-5aeb-93aa-227960526c22","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a60d3a7b-531a-547a-9481-7083f0c1c310","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I16-051","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Epe Farms Ltd uses the revaluation model for land. Land that cost N20,000,000 was revalued to N26,000,000. There was no previous revaluation. What revaluation surplus is recognised in other comprehensive income?","options":["N20,000,000","N26,000,000","N6,000,000","Nil, because land is not depreciated"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment","explanation":"Revaluation surplus = fair value - carrying amount = N26,000,000 - N20,000,000 = N6,000,000, credited to other comprehensive income. Option B is the new carrying amount, option A is the original cost, and option D is wrong because a surplus arises whenever the revalued amount exceeds carrying amount, depreciated or not."}},{"itemId":"5e19fa86-48b9-513b-9122-3ec5ac0e9019","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5f4e2de8-283e-5063-a2c9-1777a646fbd3","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I16-052","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A building with a cost of N10,000,000 and accumulated depreciation of N2,000,000 was revalued to a fair value of N11,000,000. There was no previous revaluation. What is the revaluation surplus?","options":["N11,000,000","N1,000,000","N3,000,000","N8,000,000"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment","explanation":"Carrying amount before revaluation = N10,000,000 - N2,000,000 = N8,000,000. Surplus = N11,000,000 - N8,000,000 = N3,000,000. Option B compares fair value with cost and ignores depreciation, option A is the new carrying amount, and option D is the old carrying amount."}},{"itemId":"e77cbde4-d7b9-582f-9be7-2353d224cb5e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"baff862e-584e-5451-ac7c-e2f9ac22618b","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I16-053","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A building carried at N12,000,000 is revalued to N10,500,000. A revaluation surplus of N1,000,000 relating to this building is already held in equity. How much of the decrease is recognised in profit or loss?","options":["N500,000","Nil","N1,500,000","N1,000,000"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment","explanation":"The total decrease is N1,500,000. The first N1,000,000 is debited to other comprehensive income against the existing surplus, and only the remaining N500,000 goes to profit or loss. Option C charges the whole decrease to profit or loss, option D is the part charged to OCI, and option B wrongly assumes the surplus covers the whole decrease."}},{"itemId":"19d7da32-08d8-5f0c-ba21-9ff4b61be6d1","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ab8595f4-75fe-5ecb-8601-84e461fc173e","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I16-054","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A machine cost N5,000,000 with nil residual value and a useful life of 10 years, depreciated on the straight line basis. At the start of year 5, its remaining useful life is revised to 3 years. What is the depreciation charge for year 5?","options":["N500,000","N1,666,667","N714,286","N1,000,000"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment","explanation":"After 4 years the carrying amount is N5,000,000 - (N500,000 x 4) = N3,000,000. As a change in estimate, it is spread over the remaining 3 years: N3,000,000 / 3 = N1,000,000. Option A keeps the old charge, option C spreads the original cost over a revised total life (a retrospective approach), and option B spreads the full cost over the remaining life."}},{"itemId":"6aa10667-c2d8-5552-a98a-d520b99b1caa","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0e48ce3a-fb35-51ce-b862-c783a96dd4cd","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I16-055","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A bus has a carrying amount of N2,000,000. During the year the company spent N800,000 on a new engine that extends the bus's useful life and N50,000 on repainting it. Ignoring depreciation for the year, what is the carrying amount of the bus after these costs?","options":["N2,800,000","N2,050,000","N2,850,000","N2,000,000"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment","explanation":"The engine enhances the bus's benefits by extending its life, so the N800,000 is capitalised, giving N2,000,000 + N800,000 = N2,800,000. The repainting does not increase the bus's revenue-earning capacity, so it is expensed. Option C capitalises the repainting too, option D expenses the engine, and option B capitalises the wrong item."}},{"itemId":"13480091-e9e2-55a0-b760-a79809904f69","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"568279b7-46c0-5a4c-b97e-5ad917e6fa2a","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I2-001","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Assets held for sale in the ordinary course of business, in the process of production for such sale, or in the form of materials or supplies to be consumed in the production process or in the rendering of services are called","options":["Investment property","Property, plant and equipment","Biological assets","Inventories"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 2 Inventories","explanation":"This is the IAS 2 definition of inventories: goods held for sale, work in progress, and materials or supplies used in production or in rendering services. Option B describes assets held for use in production or supply over more than one period, not for sale. Option A is property held to earn rentals or for capital appreciation, and option C refers to living animals or plants covered by IAS 41."}},{"itemId":"8bd44e38-c064-5fd1-b8bc-8340facc45fb","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5502adf7-f46a-53b5-9b7e-aaa53664b032","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I2-002","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT one of the three categories in the IAS 2 definition of inventories?","options":["Assets held for use in the supply of goods over more than one period","Assets held for sale in the ordinary course of business","Materials or supplies to be consumed in the production process","Assets in the process of production for sale"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 2 Inventories","explanation":"Holding an asset for use over more than one period describes property, plant and equipment under IAS 16, not inventories. Options B, D and C are exactly the three limbs of the inventories definition: finished goods for sale, work in progress, and materials or supplies."}},{"itemId":"53db2968-6f23-5ff7-a70e-0813e7e80d1e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"98d4c29d-2220-5aed-a40b-51a8e7585d6e","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I2-003","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Ikeja Motors Ltd, a car dealer, holds 40 new vehicles in its showroom for sale to customers and 3 vehicles used by its sales staff for daily operations. How should the 40 showroom vehicles be classified?","options":["As inventories under IAS 2","As property, plant and equipment under IAS 16","As intangible assets under IAS 38","As investment property under IAS 40"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 2 Inventories","explanation":"The 40 showroom vehicles are held for sale in the ordinary course of the dealer's business, so they are inventories. Option B would fit only the 3 vehicles the staff use in operations, not the ones held for sale. Options D and C do not apply because the cars are neither held for rental or capital appreciation nor without physical substance."}},{"itemId":"2a74066f-d45c-5048-8a9b-0cf03e50c106","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"94368e6f-ea2e-55ed-9871-d96e87ad5a57","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I2-004","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is excluded from the scope of IAS 2?","options":["Work in progress of a furniture maker","Biological assets related to agricultural activity","Raw materials held by a manufacturer","Finished goods held by a retailer"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 2 Inventories","explanation":"Biological assets related to agricultural activity, and agricultural produce at the point of harvest, are dealt with by IAS 41 Agriculture, so they fall outside IAS 2. Options C, D and A are ordinary raw materials, finished goods and work in progress, which are the typical inventories IAS 2 covers."}},{"itemId":"517fdee4-d301-5f9c-8ceb-f1443271878e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"13f4d987-0c7e-546b-8303-43af3c59c5c2","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I2-005","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following items is excluded from the scope of IAS 2 Inventories?","options":["Goods purchased and held for resale","Partly finished goods in a factory","Financial instruments","Spare parts and consumable supplies held for production"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 2 Inventories","explanation":"Financial instruments are excluded from IAS 2 because they are covered by the standards on financial instruments. Option D is materials or supplies to be consumed in production, option A is goods held for sale, and option B is work in progress, so all three are inventories within IAS 2."}},{"itemId":"e827bd44-9f79-5e8b-812f-1b036bb29129","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"873435bb-937a-5351-b32a-9815224358f1","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I2-006","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under IAS 2, inventories shall be measured at","options":["Historical cost less accumulated depreciation","Fair value less costs to sell","The higher of cost and net realisable value","The lower of cost and net realisable value"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 2 Inventories","explanation":"IAS 2 requires inventories to be measured at the lower of cost and net realisable value, so they are never carried above the amount expected to be recovered from sale. Option C would overstate assets, option B is the basis used for certain items such as biological assets rather than general inventories, and option A applies to depreciable assets like property, plant and equipment."}},{"itemId":"124869a3-250e-5b19-bd92-0ea434f28583","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"856fa3d4-0462-5e80-9093-7bf3dbd58202","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I2-007","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The estimated selling price in the ordinary course of business less the estimated costs of completion and the estimated costs necessary to make the sale is called","options":["Cost of conversion","Fair value","Net realisable value","Replacement cost"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 2 Inventories","explanation":"This is the definition of net realisable value, which is entity-specific and looks at what the entity itself expects to recover from selling the item. Option B is a market-based exit price between participants, not reduced by completion costs. Option D is the current cost of buying the item again, and option A is the cost of turning materials into finished goods."}},{"itemId":"84f6fa3b-c3b3-5ad1-be7e-38a326f86c11","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0ad416c8-3271-50d1-8a67-5efdba38fc1b","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I2-008","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is deducted from the estimated selling price in arriving at net realisable value?","options":["Administrative overheads and finance costs","Estimated costs of completion and estimated costs necessary to make the sale","Accumulated depreciation and impairment losses","Original purchase price and import duties"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 2 Inventories","explanation":"Net realisable value equals estimated selling price less the estimated costs to complete the item and the estimated costs needed to sell it. Option D lists elements of cost, which is compared with NRV rather than deducted from selling price. Options A and C are not part of the NRV formula at all."}},{"itemId":"149f1dbf-8081-5609-83af-7435822b85c0","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"044a79c5-4831-5ccf-962a-51b70653e5cd","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I2-009","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Oshodi Textiles Ltd holds a batch of fabric that cost N500,000. It is expected to sell for N480,000 after further finishing costing N30,000, and selling costs of N20,000 will be incurred. What write-down is required under IAS 2?","options":["N50,000","Nil","N20,000","N70,000"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 2 Inventories","explanation":"Net realisable value is N480,000 - N30,000 - N20,000 = N430,000, which is below cost of N500,000, so the batch is written down by N70,000. Option C compares cost with the selling price only and ignores the deductions. Option A deducts only one of the two costs, and option B wrongly assumes no write-down is needed."}},{"itemId":"1a8742c8-42e1-5fea-9ca7-390d2ff8ef9f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"957cae4e-edf8-52c8-9706-31d366e509de","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I2-010","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Yaba Electronics Ltd has a stock of 200 phone chargers costing N3,000 each. Each can be sold for N3,200, but packaging and delivery costs of N400 per unit will be incurred to sell them. At what amount should the chargers be reported in the statement of financial position?","options":["N560,000","N480,000","N600,000","N640,000"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 2 Inventories","explanation":"NRV per unit is N3,200 - N400 = N2,800, which is below cost of N3,000, so the chargers are carried at 200 x N2,800 = N560,000. Option C is cost, which is higher than NRV here. Option D uses the gross selling price without deducting selling costs, and option B deducts the selling costs twice."}},{"itemId":"a392adcd-5a27-5a79-be90-92ffcddb1402","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3a28aeba-11d2-5150-8cc3-c70283a00406","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I2-011","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Surulere Stores Ltd holds three unrelated product lines at year end. Line A: cost N200,000, NRV N260,000. Line B: cost N150,000, NRV N120,000. Line C: cost N90,000, NRV N100,000. Applying IAS 2 item by item, what is the total carrying amount of inventories?","options":["N480,000","N380,000","N440,000","N410,000"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 2 Inventories","explanation":"Taking the lower figure for each line gives N200,000 + N120,000 + N90,000 = N410,000. Option C is the total cost, which comes from comparing totals and lets the gain on A and C hide the loss on B. Option A is the total NRV, and option B wrongly takes the lowest possible combination by deducting B's shortfall twice."}},{"itemId":"b5d5a74b-1793-5540-a579-c0f8bc7aafff","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"560903c4-2637-5f5f-9fb5-3ed6506b4a08","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I2-012","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"How does IAS 2 require write-downs of inventories to net realisable value normally to be made?","options":["Only on items that have been physically damaged","Item by item, or by grouping similar or related items where appropriate","On the total of all inventories held by the entity","On the basis of a whole classification such as all finished goods"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 2 Inventories","explanation":"Write-downs are usually made item by item, although similar or related items may sometimes be grouped. Option C is wrong because comparing totals allows profits on some items to cover losses on others. Option D is specifically considered inappropriate, and option A is too narrow since NRV can also fall because of lower selling prices or higher completion costs."}},{"itemId":"ce2d63cd-72ff-5e1d-8358-6b327077d011","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5d78ac2f-0e6c-57d1-8a33-08ec24d4c07d","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I2-013","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Agege Bakery Ltd holds flour whose market price has fallen below its cost. The bread made from this flour is expected to sell at a price above its total cost. What should happen to the flour?","options":["It is written down to its current market price","It is not written down below cost","It is written off completely","It is written down to replacement cost"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 2 Inventories","explanation":"Materials held for use in production are not written down below cost if the finished products they go into are expected to sell at or above cost. Options A and D would only be relevant if the bread itself were expected to sell below cost, in which case replacement cost may be the best measure of the flour's NRV. Option C has no basis since the flour is still usable."}},{"itemId":"d79d42a0-5495-59a5-a1b6-9d7d73636b11","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"538eb60f-96bc-5e5b-ad51-62d6e3f69b51","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I2-014","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under IAS 2, the cost of inventories comprises","options":["Costs of conversion and all distribution costs","Costs of purchase, administrative overheads and storage costs","Costs of purchase and selling costs only","Costs of purchase, costs of conversion and other costs incurred in bringing the inventories to their present location and condition"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 2 Inventories","explanation":"Cost of inventories is made up of costs of purchase, costs of conversion and other costs needed to bring the items to their present location and condition. Options C and A include selling and distribution costs, which are specifically excluded. Option B includes administrative overheads and general storage costs, which are also excluded unless they meet narrow conditions."}},{"itemId":"cae773a4-a23f-5c07-b47a-a11d66d6c265","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5b21a148-b6ad-563d-8a93-44ea735b1a5c","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I2-015","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT included in the costs of purchase of inventories?","options":["Import duties that cannot be recovered","Handling costs directly attributable to acquiring the goods","Transport costs to bring the goods to the warehouse","Value added tax that the entity can subsequently recover from the tax authorities"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 2 Inventories","explanation":"Taxes that the entity can later recover are not a real cost to it, so they are excluded from cost of purchase. Options A, C and B are import duties and other non-recoverable taxes, transport and handling, which IAS 2 includes in costs of purchase."}},{"itemId":"166807dc-f16b-5118-8f38-e239d2c2e3a4","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b2b9aee8-fadb-5ab6-9967-03c332a3294a","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I2-016","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following costs may be included in the cost of inventories under IAS 2?","options":["Administrative overheads that do not help bring inventories to their present location and condition","Fixed production overheads allocated on the basis of normal capacity","Abnormal amounts of wasted materials","Selling costs"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 2 Inventories","explanation":"Fixed production overheads are part of costs of conversion and are allocated to inventory based on the normal capacity of the production facilities. Options C, D and A are among the costs IAS 2 specifically excludes from cost and recognises as expenses when incurred."}},{"itemId":"61856635-85e9-5d60-b9eb-fa84192decd9","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b3c6e28b-68a3-5020-a572-c8030621e620","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I2-017","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT one of the costs that IAS 2 excludes from the cost of inventories?","options":["Selling costs","Abnormal amounts of wasted labour","Direct labour used in making the goods","Storage costs not necessary in the production process"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 2 Inventories","explanation":"Direct labour is a cost of conversion, so it is included in the cost of inventories. Options B, D and A are listed exclusions: abnormal waste, storage costs not needed before a further production stage, and selling costs are expensed when incurred."}},{"itemId":"7705159f-4b90-50a1-a87a-6ff824fced83","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e2817007-17e4-58df-8685-145e22ea8147","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I2-018","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In which situation may storage costs be included in the cost of inventories?","options":["When storage is necessary in the production process before a further production stage","When the storage costs relate to the head office records room","When the finished goods are stored awaiting sale to customers","When the entity chooses to capitalise them to increase profit"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 2 Inventories","explanation":"Storage costs are excluded from cost unless they are necessary in the production process before a further stage, such as maturing a product before final processing. Option C is storage of finished goods awaiting sale, which is expensed. Option B is an administrative cost, and option D is not a valid reason under any standard."}},{"itemId":"538cb0e1-bc5b-5e60-be76-cbdb0f6f5df2","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4664450a-0f5b-502a-b1a8-7a13c92cf676","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I2-019","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Apapa Imports Ltd bought goods with a list price of N1,000,000 and received a trade discount of N50,000. It also paid non-recoverable import duties of N80,000, recoverable VAT of N75,000, transport inward of N40,000, and N30,000 to advertise the goods. What is the cost of the inventories under IAS 2?","options":["N1,145,000","N1,120,000","N1,070,000","N1,100,000"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 2 Inventories","explanation":"Cost is N1,000,000 - N50,000 + N80,000 + N40,000 = N1,070,000, since trade discounts are deducted and only non-recoverable taxes and transport are added. Option A wrongly includes the recoverable VAT, and option D adds advertising, which is a selling cost. Option B ignores the trade discount."}},{"itemId":"6f51f8da-6672-5ac2-b9a9-0aa948f722aa","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"50719f03-d617-57fd-a5e6-228d66676788","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I2-020","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Ikorodu Furniture Ltd incurred the following in producing a batch of chairs: materials N800,000 (including N50,000 of abnormal waste), direct labour N300,000, production overheads N200,000 and general administration N100,000. What is the cost of the batch under IAS 2?","options":["N1,300,000","N1,350,000","N1,250,000","N1,400,000"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 2 Inventories","explanation":"Cost is materials excluding abnormal waste (N750,000) plus labour N300,000 plus production overheads N200,000, giving N1,250,000. Option A keeps the abnormal waste, option B adds the administration cost, and option D includes both items that IAS 2 excludes."}},{"itemId":"1a170888-f143-530f-9d68-4b9c07a92fc2","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c42ea900-4c45-5604-9322-bef044c800d0","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I2-021","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Kano Oil Mills Ltd has fixed production overheads of N2,000,000 for the year. Normal capacity is 100,000 units, but only 80,000 units were produced because of low demand. Under IAS 2, how much of the fixed overhead is recognised as an expense in the period rather than included in inventory?","options":["Nil","N1,600,000","N400,000","N2,000,000"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 2 Inventories","explanation":"Fixed overhead is absorbed at N2,000,000 / 100,000 = N20 per unit, so 80,000 units absorb N1,600,000 and the unallocated N400,000 is expensed. Option A spreads all the overhead over actual output, which IAS 2 does not allow when production is low. Option D expenses all of the fixed overhead, even though fixed production overheads are part of costs of conversion, and option B is the amount included in inventory, not the amount expensed."}},{"itemId":"182c4e7b-f24d-5d22-b22f-59138bb959f9","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"50d7475d-3827-5b31-a7ad-f63b76b08b95","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I2-022","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Lekki Jewellers Ltd makes unique custom pieces, each made for a named customer. Which cost formula must it use for these items?","options":["Specific identification of individual costs","Weighted average cost","Last-in, first-out (LIFO)","First-in, first-out (FIFO)"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 2 Inventories","explanation":"Items that are not ordinarily interchangeable, or goods produced and kept separate for specific projects, are costed by specific identification. Options D and B are used for large numbers of interchangeable items, and option C is not permitted under IAS 2 at all."}},{"itemId":"6d33077e-92ac-5dfe-9b52-56596edbfa55","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"50c805bf-0ebf-5c79-b318-99c559261229","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I2-024","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An entity holds several groups of inventories. Which statement about its choice of cost formula is correct under IAS 2?","options":["The same cost formula must be used for all inventories of a similar nature and use to the entity","Inventories in different branches must always use different formulas","A different cost formula may be chosen each year for the same inventories","FIFO must be used for all inventories without exception"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 2 Inventories","explanation":"An entity must use the same cost formula for all inventories that have a similar nature and use, although different formulas may be justified for inventories of a different nature or use. Option C would destroy comparability, option D ignores weighted average and specific identification, and option B confuses location with nature and use."}},{"itemId":"0e916291-75ef-5b70-991f-59caeaa2c510","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d36a391e-82f2-5b0e-b448-18209ca55461","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I2-025","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Ibadan Cement Distributors had opening inventory of 100 bags at N200 each. During the period it bought 300 bags at N220 each and then 200 bags at N250 each. It sold 450 bags. Using FIFO, what is the cost of closing inventory?","options":["N33,000","N37,500","N30,000","N34,000"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 2 Inventories","explanation":"Closing inventory is 600 - 450 = 150 bags, and under FIFO the bags left are the most recent purchases, so 150 x N250 = N37,500. Option D is the weighted average result, option C wrongly values the remaining bags at the opening price, and option A uses the middle purchase price."}},{"itemId":"eccdfb8d-6f70-5de9-aa26-4065619475cb","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2ce096d5-ac89-50a6-b967-9917677cdd56","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I2-026","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Ibadan Cement Distributors had opening inventory of 100 bags at N200 each. During the period it bought 300 bags at N220 each and then 200 bags at N250 each. It sold 450 bags. Using the weighted average cost formula (calculated on a periodic basis), what is the cost of closing inventory?","options":["N33,500","N102,000","N34,000","N37,500"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 2 Inventories","explanation":"Total cost is N20,000 + N66,000 + N50,000 = N136,000 for 600 bags, an average of about N226.67 per bag, so 150 bags cost N34,000. Option D is the FIFO result, option A uses a simple average of the three prices without weighting by quantity, and option B is the cost of sales, not closing inventory."}},{"itemId":"e924ffde-e427-583d-b777-4edb39f905dc","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"09cd0616-16be-51bd-9b85-524c2fb458a1","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I2-027","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Ibadan Cement Distributors had opening inventory of 100 bags at N200 each. During the period it bought 300 bags at N220 each and then 200 bags at N250 each. It sold 450 bags. Using FIFO, what is the cost of sales?","options":["N102,000","N37,500","N98,500","N105,000"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 2 Inventories","explanation":"Under FIFO the 450 bags sold are the 100 opening bags (N20,000), all 300 at N220 (N66,000) and 50 at N250 (N12,500), totalling N98,500. Option A is the weighted average cost of sales, and option B is the FIFO closing inventory. Option D wrongly charges the most recent purchases to cost of sales first."}},{"itemId":"c40d15a2-4d6b-5d43-8f92-494843e37b40","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"91b9d730-9a42-56f3-979b-47c1a5392a5f","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I2-028","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A large supermarket with many fast-moving items with similar margins wants to estimate the cost of its inventories. Which statement is correct under IAS 2?","options":["It must use specific identification for every item","It must measure inventory at selling price","It may use LIFO because it is a retailer","It may use the retail method if the results approximate cost"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 2 Inventories","explanation":"Techniques such as the standard cost method or the retail method may be used for convenience if the results approximate cost; the retail method is often used in retail for large numbers of fast-changing items with similar margins. Option A is impractical and only required for non-interchangeable items. Option B would overstate inventory, and option C is not permitted for anyone."}},{"itemId":"ff923416-a818-5ed3-b0b3-55594c6716f1","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2942b30b-01f2-5db5-bc00-292875de42e7","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I2-029","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Inventory written down to net realisable value last year is still held, and its net realisable value has now risen because selling prices have recovered. What does IAS 2 require?","options":["Recognise the increase in other comprehensive income","Reverse the write-down so the new carrying amount is the lower of cost and the revised NRV","Increase the carrying amount to the new NRV even if it is above original cost","Leave the write-down in place because reversals are not permitted"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 2 Inventories","explanation":"When the circumstances that caused the write-down no longer exist, the write-down is reversed, but only up to the original cost, so the item is carried at the lower of cost and the revised NRV. Option D is wrong because IAS 2 does require reversal. Option C would carry inventory above cost, and option A is wrong because the reversal reduces the inventory expense in profit or loss."}},{"itemId":"71d747c4-c213-5c40-8be5-46771bd6e27d","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"eb0a00f0-e8df-5d55-976b-2ab12fff05df","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I2-030","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Onitsha Spare Parts Ltd had an item costing N350,000 that was written down to its NRV of N300,000 last year. The item is still unsold, and its NRV is now N370,000. What amount of reversal is recognised this year?","options":["N20,000","N70,000","Nil","N50,000"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 2 Inventories","explanation":"The write-down is reversed only up to original cost, so the carrying amount goes from N300,000 back to N350,000, a reversal of N50,000. Option B raises the item to N370,000, which is above cost. Option A is the excess of NRV over cost, and option C wrongly assumes reversals are not allowed."}},{"itemId":"a80e6342-0afa-5d4d-ac1b-97d034a6f15b","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"677f24a4-2773-513c-a397-d0381fd7d98a","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I2-031","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When inventories are sold, when is their carrying amount recognised as an expense under IAS 2?","options":["In the period in which the related revenue is recognised","In the period in which the goods were purchased","At the end of the year in which the goods were produced","When cash is received from the customer"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 2 Inventories","explanation":"The carrying amount of inventories sold is recognised as an expense in the same period as the related revenue, which matches cost of sales with sales. Option B would expense goods before they are sold, option D ties the expense to cash flow rather than the sale, and option C ignores whether the goods have actually been sold."}},{"itemId":"bc8c8545-4220-59aa-867d-9af0f881e384","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"50352d31-7e28-544e-98bd-84cef78fca86","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I2-032","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"How should a write-down of inventories to net realisable value, and a loss of inventories through theft, be treated under IAS 2?","options":["Both are recognised as an expense in the period the write-down or loss occurs","Both are deferred and spread over future periods","Both are recognised in other comprehensive income","Both are adjusted against the opening retained earnings"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 2 Inventories","explanation":"IAS 2 requires the amount of any write-down to NRV and all losses of inventories to be recognised as an expense in the period the write-down or loss occurs. Option B delays a loss that has already happened. Option C and option D bypass profit or loss, which IAS 2 does not allow for these items."}},{"itemId":"6a0ba0e6-a6eb-5ebd-8f08-9f86188b6383","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"eab8073c-1828-5289-82ca-fc4b70d5620c","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I20-001","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under IAS 20, the government of nations, government agencies and similar bodies, whether local, national or international, are collectively referred to as","options":["Government assistance","Government","Public sector entities","Regulatory authorities"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 20 Government Grants","explanation":"IAS 20 uses the single word government to cover nations' governments, government agencies and similar bodies at local, national or international level. Option A is an action that gives economic benefits, not the body that gives it. Options D and C are not the defined term used in the standard."}},{"itemId":"1cf80836-0789-50d0-97c2-8e855edb9cce","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"81a92a8b-3044-5b33-953d-0f26f6f5844b","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I20-002","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"For the purpose of IAS 20, which of the following would NOT fall within the meaning of government?","options":["A federal government agency","An international authority","A local government council","A privately owned commercial bank lending to the entity"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 20 Government Grants","explanation":"Government covers the government of nations, government agencies and similar bodies, whether local, national or international. A privately owned commercial bank is not a government body, so its lending is outside IAS 20. Options C, A and B are all expressly within the meaning of government."}},{"itemId":"f0389e24-f7b6-57ff-887a-a3b4cec87bd8","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"74828c9b-c834-54ac-b7e4-9add6895fc07","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I20-003","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Government assistance, quantified in monetary terms, in the form of transfers of resources to an entity in return for past or future compliance with certain conditions relating to its operating activities, is called","options":["A government grant","A tax holiday","Government assistance without monetary value","Deferred income"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 20 Government Grants","explanation":"A government grant is assistance that has a monetary value and involves a transfer of resources in return for compliance with conditions. Option C and option B describe help with no monetary value, such as a tax holiday, which is assistance but not a grant. Option D is only the account used to hold a grant under one method, not the definition of a grant."}},{"itemId":"5cfe8e71-d8f3-5b43-937c-294eade35d29","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0bb654db-3db1-518c-8514-1c637a329f9d","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I20-004","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A government grant is given to an entity in return for","options":["Taxes the entity has paid in prior years","Shares issued by the entity to the government","Past or future compliance with certain conditions relating to the entity's operating activities","The entity agreeing to become a government agency"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 20 Government Grants","explanation":"A grant is a transfer of resources in return for past or future compliance with conditions relating to the entity's operating activities. Option B would make the government an investor, not a grantor. Options A and D are not conditions linked to the entity's operating activities."}},{"itemId":"1e927690-1a9a-585b-9232-ee9b1bc2ba0d","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"66d6f1f2-34c7-57bd-aa96-2207f71b1726","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I20-005","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Any action of the government aimed at giving economic benefits to an entity or range of entities qualifying under certain criteria is known as","options":["A grant related to assets","A grant related to income","Government assistance","Deferred income"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 20 Government Grants","explanation":"Government assistance is the broad term for any government action that gives economic benefits to qualifying entities, and it includes grants. Options A and B are the two narrower types of grant, which must involve a transfer of resources with a monetary value. Option D is an accounting balance, not a government action."}},{"itemId":"27a02669-f9a6-5a3b-9e59-8d152e0732f7","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f7370812-f34f-5baa-a050-9e22af8db006","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I20-006","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following statements about government grants and government assistance is correct?","options":["Government assistance must always have a monetary value placed on it","All government grants are assistance, but not all assistance is a grant","All government assistance is a grant, but not all grants are assistance","Government grants and government assistance mean exactly the same thing"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 20 Government Grants","explanation":"Grants are the part of government assistance that has a monetary value, so every grant is assistance but some assistance, like a tax holiday, is not a grant. Option C reverses the relationship. Options D and A are wrong because assistance can exist without any monetary value."}},{"itemId":"a66012b2-7c70-5043-ba26-17c0b2dbd8ec","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"338bd739-8179-5fe4-9e51-d96a50ff2f3c","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I20-007","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The Federal Government grants Ikeja Foods Ltd a five-year tax holiday under a pioneer status scheme, with no cash transferred. Under IAS 20, this is best described as","options":["Government assistance without monetary value, not a government grant","A government grant related to assets","A government grant related to income","A government grant to be credited to deferred income"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 20 Government Grants","explanation":"A tax holiday is a classic example of government assistance that has no monetary value placed on it, so it is not a grant. Options B, C and D all treat it as a grant, but a grant requires a transfer of resources quantified in monetary terms."}},{"itemId":"fe6f2691-5f9f-5153-8c54-f3b92f7cb4af","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"fdfd30ee-796d-5cfd-bb3a-660f8937dcd9","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I20-008","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"What distinguishes a government grant from other forms of government assistance?","options":["It involves a transfer of resources with a monetary value placed on it","It is given only to government-owned companies","It is always recognised directly in equity","It never carries any conditions"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 20 Government Grants","explanation":"Grants have monetary values placed on them and involve a transfer of money or resources, which is why they must be accounted for. Option D is wrong because grants are given in return for compliance with conditions. Options B and C are not features of a grant at all."}},{"itemId":"eb259739-196a-5d35-9483-9b86d2815f9d","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0ef3263b-931b-573c-90d1-8c9df81b2d70","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I20-009","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Grants given to businesses with the general condition that they purchase or construct non-current assets are called","options":["Forgivable loans","Government assistance without monetary value","Grants related to income","Grants related to assets"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 20 Government Grants","explanation":"Grants related to assets carry the condition that the entity buys or builds non-current assets. Option C covers grants for any purpose other than acquiring non-current assets. Option B is help without a monetary value, such as a tax holiday, and option A is not one of the two types of grant."}},{"itemId":"32adaf29-d813-5ec8-8dad-b5020976b354","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"02daf01c-6971-527d-bbb5-35b3d9c2285c","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I20-010","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Grants related to income are best described as grants given to businesses","options":["For any purpose other than to support the acquisition of non-current assets","Only after the related asset has been fully depreciated","Only to purchase or construct non-current assets","Only in the form of tax holidays"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 20 Government Grants","explanation":"Grants related to income are all grants other than those given to support the acquisition of non-current assets. Option C describes grants related to assets. Option D is wrong because a tax holiday is not a grant at all, and option B is not part of the definition."}},{"itemId":"26d6a258-14fc-5858-b66a-ac7c007edc15","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"dc85fd51-1bcc-5f6b-aac5-8e595a822527","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I20-011","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The Lagos State Government pays N40 million to Ota Clinics Ltd on condition that it buys a new dialysis machine. This grant is","options":["A grant related to assets","Government assistance without monetary value","Not a grant because it is from a state, not federal, government","A grant related to income"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 20 Government Grants","explanation":"The grant defrays the cost of acquiring a non-current asset, which makes it a grant related to assets. Option D applies to grants for purposes other than acquiring non-current assets. Option C is wrong because government includes local and state-level authorities, and option B fails because cash with a monetary value was transferred."}},{"itemId":"2a55d295-9612-5d24-bc70-1d9e15403f24","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8b96c8db-3899-54e1-ba0c-1ecd3c379bfc","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I20-012","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A government agency gives Kano Textiles Ltd N15 million to cover part of its staff wage costs for the next two years. No asset purchase is required. This is","options":["Government assistance without monetary value","A grant related to assets","A capital contribution by the government","A grant related to income"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 20 Government Grants","explanation":"Since the money is not given to acquire non-current assets, it is a grant related to income and will be matched against the wage costs it compensates. Option B requires a condition to buy or build non-current assets. Option A fails because cash was transferred, and option C would make the government an owner, which is not the case."}},{"itemId":"7c4d0f19-e400-5338-8ba3-a43398db774c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"96009206-cd5f-5cc9-a5c6-936c635dae98","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I20-014","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Enugu Agro Ltd has been told it will receive a N20 million grant from a state agency, and it is reasonably assured the cash will be paid. However, it is not yet reasonably assured that it can meet the job-creation condition attached. How should the grant be treated now?","options":["It should be recognised as deferred income immediately","It should be recognised in full as income immediately","It should not be recognised until both conditions are met","It should be deducted from the cost of its assets immediately"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 20 Government Grants","explanation":"A grant is recognised only when there is reasonable assurance both that the conditions will be met and that the grant will be received. Here only one condition is satisfied, so nothing is recognised yet. Options B, A and D all recognise the grant in some form too early."}},{"itemId":"3af22498-1892-579c-be04-c7af6b827598","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e98bb61c-51b7-5dc2-832b-06453fe3f168","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I20-018","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When a grant related to an asset is written off against the cost of the non-current asset, depreciation is based on","options":["The amount of the grant only","The cost of the asset reduced by the grant","The full cost of the asset before the grant","The fair value of the asset at year end"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 20 Government Grants","explanation":"Deducting the grant from cost reduces the asset's value, and depreciation is then charged on that reduced amount. Option C applies to the deferred income method, where depreciation is on the full cost. Options A and D are not bases used for depreciation here."}},{"itemId":"1c2064f4-e958-5234-a29d-88415564a765","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"17e3964f-0ece-5e18-a5ec-e841cd5c74d4","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I20-019","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the deferred income method for a grant related to an asset, which of the following is correct?","options":["The grant is credited to the asset account and the asset is depreciated on the net amount","Depreciation is based on the full cost, and a portion of the grant is transferred to income each year","Depreciation is based on cost less grant, and nothing is transferred to income","The whole grant is taken to income in the year of receipt"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 20 Government Grants","explanation":"With the deferred income method, the asset stays at full cost and is depreciated on that amount, while part of the deferred grant is released to the income statement each year to offset the higher depreciation. Options C and A describe deducting the grant from the asset's cost. Option D ignores the systematic matching of grant income with depreciation."}},{"itemId":"14c1a3ca-2283-5abe-b55f-26ef7ecc8fbe","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"03fd010d-4672-5337-8d82-307e159721cb","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I20-020","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which method of accounting for a grant related to an asset shows the asset in the statement of financial position at its full cost and shows the unreleased grant as a separate balance?","options":["The method of deducting the grant from the cost of the asset","The deferred income method","Treating the grant as government assistance without monetary value","Recognising the grant as income on receipt"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 20 Government Grants","explanation":"The deferred income method leaves the asset at its full cost and carries the unreleased grant in a deferred income account. Option A reduces the asset's carrying amount by the grant, so no separate grant balance remains. Options D and C are not acceptable treatments for a grant related to an asset."}},{"itemId":"66f9db41-0e79-56d4-a806-2f04131b05b6","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4ef81daf-490e-55bc-8f4a-ea7c7ad1e7d2","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I20-021","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When a grant related to an asset is deducted from the cost of the asset, the grant received is recorded as","options":["A debit to the asset (machine) account","A credit to the asset (machine) account","A credit to a deferred income account","A credit to share capital"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 20 Government Grants","explanation":"Deducting the grant from cost means the grant cash is credited to the machine account, which reduces the asset's balance. Option A would increase the asset instead. Option C is the deferred income method, and option D treats the government as a shareholder."}},{"itemId":"4fe4c455-7a0b-565b-8a02-64cfc0af0e8a","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c3a93a41-6e75-5b47-8684-037626cfdf68","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I20-022","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the deferred income method, the yearly portion of a grant related to an asset is transferred by debiting deferred income and crediting","options":["Share capital","Bank","The machine account","The income statement"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 20 Government Grants","explanation":"Each year part of the deferred grant is released to the income statement, where it offsets the depreciation charge. Option C is the treatment when the grant is deducted from cost. Options A and B are not where the yearly release goes."}},{"itemId":"5724c760-a8f6-569c-af40-877574be74ba","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"18f59469-59d7-565b-96fe-65599efb0094","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I20-023","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"How are grants related to income treated when the cash is received?","options":["They are recognised as income in full immediately upon receipt","They are credited directly to retained earnings","They are not recognised as income immediately; they are matched against the related expense","They are deducted from the cost of non-current assets"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 20 Government Grants","explanation":"Grants related to income are recognised in the income statement, matched against the expenses they are meant to cover, so they are not income just because the money has arrived. Option A ignores this matching. Option D is for grants related to assets, and option B bypasses profit or loss altogether."}},{"itemId":"651977a7-29f8-5028-9ebd-6886291ea49b","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"70e56e99-d3ac-5254-ba81-f0d063b112f4","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I20-024","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"IAS 20 permits grants related to income to be presented either","options":["In other comprehensive income or directly in equity","As deferred tax or as a provision","As a reduction of the cost of non-current assets or as share capital","Separately as other income or as a reduction of the related expense"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 20 Government Grants","explanation":"Income-related grants are shown either separately as other income or deducted from the related expense. Option C mixes up the asset-grant treatment with share capital. Options B and A are not presentation choices for income-related grants."}},{"itemId":"800097c2-d57b-5505-bee1-35f1e472c7f1","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"897e8b4e-5807-5646-8794-f56080272b2f","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I20-025","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT an acceptable way to present a grant related to income?","options":["Crediting it directly to equity when received","Showing it separately as other income","Deducting it from the related expense","Recognising it in the income statement as the related costs are incurred"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 20 Government Grants","explanation":"Grants related to income go through the income statement, matched against the expenses they compensate, so crediting them straight to equity is not acceptable. Options B and C are the two permitted presentation choices, and option D describes the required matching."}},{"itemId":"79853a5d-0c03-518a-8bcf-932dff871559","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5c5aa014-d7cd-5f1d-8c15-69a3dc067ec4","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I20-027","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In allocating a grant related to income over years of construction, the step of dividing each year's cost by the total cost and multiplying by the grant value is called","options":["Ascertaining the total value of the grant","Ascertaining the total cost","Prorating the grant using each year's estimated cost","Writing off the grant against the cost of the asset"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 20 Government Grants","explanation":"Prorating means sharing the grant in proportion to each year's cost, that is (year's cost / total cost) x grant. Options B and A are the earlier steps that supply the figures for this calculation. Option D is a method for grants related to assets, not this allocation."}},{"itemId":"2b959b66-a796-5181-85c7-02d8fcf07694","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"da5fe096-324c-56c2-9edf-914ef0db7062","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I20-028","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Sokoto Mills Ltd buys a machine for N60,000,000 and receives a government grant of N12,000,000 towards it. The machine is depreciated straight-line over 6 years. If the grant is deducted from the cost of the machine, the annual depreciation charge is","options":["N12,000,000","N8,000,000","N10,000,000","N2,000,000"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 20 Government Grants","explanation":"Deducting the grant gives a net cost of N60,000,000 - N12,000,000 = N48,000,000, and N48,000,000 / 6 = N8,000,000 a year. Option C depreciates the full cost, which is done only under the deferred income method. Option D spreads only the grant, and option A wrongly adds the grant to cost."}},{"itemId":"e5d7ba95-61a1-5fce-a59d-93615d22e34f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"abf8fb29-d826-5cbd-8ddd-93c3610b402b","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I20-029","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Abeokuta Diagnostics Ltd buys a scanner for N80,000,000 and receives a grant of N20,000,000 towards it. The scanner is depreciated straight-line over 5 years. Using the method that deducts the grant from the cost of the asset, what is the carrying amount of the scanner at the end of year 2?","options":["N48,000,000","N36,000,000","N56,000,000","N60,000,000"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 20 Government Grants","explanation":"Net cost is N80,000,000 - N20,000,000 = N60,000,000, depreciated at N12,000,000 a year, so after 2 years the carrying amount is N60,000,000 - N24,000,000 = N36,000,000. Option A ignores the grant and depreciates the full cost. Option D is the net cost before any depreciation, and option C subtracts two years of depreciation from the full cost but forgets to deduct the grant itself."}},{"itemId":"0ee95f70-d124-5599-9ceb-3cb9613c2da5","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a6b6f1f5-d78b-58e0-a37d-be9d33487b60","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I20-030","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Ilorin Dairy Ltd receives a government grant of N18,000,000 towards a cold room costing N75,000,000, depreciated straight-line over 5 years. Under the deferred income method, how much of the grant is transferred to the income statement each year?","options":["N15,000,000","N11,400,000","N3,600,000","N18,000,000"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 20 Government Grants","explanation":"The grant is released in line with the asset's 5-year life: N18,000,000 / 5 = N3,600,000 a year. Option A is the yearly depreciation on full cost, not the grant release. Option B is depreciation on cost less grant, used when the grant is deducted from cost, and option D recognises the whole grant at once."}},{"itemId":"3812e9b3-c7a0-5b31-a416-a78a90fb69f3","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d79f40b0-3796-5ed3-bc35-868f4ad263d6","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I20-031","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Owerri Bottling Ltd received a grant of N24,000,000 for a bottling line depreciated straight-line over 6 years, and uses the deferred income method. What is the balance on the deferred income account at the end of year 3?","options":["N12,000,000","N4,000,000","N24,000,000","N16,000,000"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 20 Government Grants","explanation":"The annual release is N24,000,000 / 6 = N4,000,000, so after 3 years N12,000,000 has been released and N12,000,000 remains as deferred income. Option D deducts only 2 years of release. Option B is just one year's release, not the balance remaining, and option C ignores the releases entirely."}},{"itemId":"c2e237fc-de8f-5b61-bdf9-edd5f175c1ce","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2e106c66-5900-5116-8f17-65239744a289","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I20-032","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Port Harcourt Marine Ltd buys a vessel engine for N60,000,000 with the help of a N10,000,000 grant. The engine is depreciated straight-line over 5 years and the deferred income method is used. What is the carrying amount of the engine at the end of year 2?","options":["N48,000,000","N30,000,000","N50,000,000","N36,000,000"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 20 Government Grants","explanation":"Under the deferred income method the engine stays at full cost, depreciated at N60,000,000 / 5 = N12,000,000 a year, so after 2 years it is carried at N36,000,000. Option B is the answer if the grant had been deducted from cost, which is the other method. Option C is cost less grant with no depreciation, and option A charges only one year."}},{"itemId":"0546ec84-c852-562d-833f-b6fc64c7eaeb","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6f745aaa-b5d6-5400-8d92-e07e4f6b3bca","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I20-033","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Jos Agro Ltd buys a tractor for N45,000,000 with a N9,000,000 government grant. The tractor is depreciated straight-line over 3 years and the deferred income method is used. What is the net charge to profit or loss each year (depreciation less grant income)?","options":["N12,000,000","N15,000,000","N18,000,000","N3,000,000"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 20 Government Grants","explanation":"Depreciation on full cost is N45,000,000 / 3 = N15,000,000 and the grant released is N9,000,000 / 3 = N3,000,000, so the net charge is N12,000,000. Option B ignores the grant income. Option D is only the grant release, and option C wrongly adds the grant income to depreciation."}},{"itemId":"4d3f66b0-d76e-5b16-8202-04229cc1e563","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6152f4cf-252c-5697-936e-295602d0a933","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I20-034","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Kaduna Steel Ltd buys a furnace for N72,000,000 and receives a grant of N18,000,000 towards it. Using the deferred income method, with straight-line depreciation over 4 years, what is the annual depreciation charge?","options":["N13,500,000","N18,000,000","N4,500,000","N22,500,000"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 20 Government Grants","explanation":"Under the deferred income method, depreciation is based on the full cost: N72,000,000 / 4 = N18,000,000. Option A depreciates cost less grant, which is the method that deducts the grant from cost. Option C is the yearly grant release, not depreciation, and option D wrongly adds the grant to cost."}},{"itemId":"9c4afe27-50ad-5d87-a778-081f482b1e4b","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"cf4436fa-f248-5455-9286-d8b9b94eb556","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I20-035","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Akure Printing Ltd buys a press for N35,000,000 and receives a N7,000,000 grant towards it. If the grant is deducted from the cost of the asset, what balance is carried down on the press account immediately after recording the grant (before any depreciation)?","options":["N7,000,000","N35,000,000","N28,000,000","N42,000,000"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 20 Government Grants","explanation":"The grant is credited to the press account, so the balance carried down is N35,000,000 - N7,000,000 = N28,000,000. Option B is the balance under the deferred income method, where the asset stays at full cost. Option D adds the grant instead of deducting it, and option A is the grant alone."}},{"itemId":"9ce8526d-1daf-5326-bc3e-f9066f5140d4","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d64d7332-27d1-59a6-92e6-71d0d88f94ed","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I20-036","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Calabar Foods Ltd receives a grant of N8,000,000 for equipment depreciated straight-line over 4 years and uses the deferred income method. What deferred income balance appears in the statement of financial position at the end of year 1?","options":["N2,000,000","N8,000,000","N6,000,000","N4,000,000"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 20 Government Grants","explanation":"N8,000,000 / 4 = N2,000,000 is released in year 1, leaving N6,000,000 as deferred income. Option B ignores the year 1 release. Option A is the amount released, not the balance remaining, and option D deducts two years of release."}},{"itemId":"23d7025b-371c-5479-ae90-5c26597e5003","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f4ea784f-370f-5bcb-bb75-30599d80ddc9","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I20-037","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Benin Plastics Ltd buys a moulding machine for N90,000,000 with a N30,000,000 grant, depreciated straight-line over 5 years. If the grant is deducted from cost, what is the TOTAL depreciation charged over the machine's whole life?","options":["N90,000,000","N30,000,000","N60,000,000","N120,000,000"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 20 Government Grants","explanation":"When the grant is deducted from cost, only the net cost of N90,000,000 - N30,000,000 = N60,000,000 is depreciated over the 5 years. Option A is the total depreciation under the deferred income method. Option B is the grant itself, and option D wrongly adds the grant to the cost."}},{"itemId":"14e0cbdb-eb02-5368-ba86-73b2a4bbdde6","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5f44f020-f2f9-506a-9462-44e83b9af901","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I20-038","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Warri Fabrication Ltd received a N12,500,000 grant for a crane depreciated straight-line over 5 years and uses the deferred income method. What is the deferred income balance at the end of year 4?","options":["N10,000,000","N0","N5,000,000","N2,500,000"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 20 Government Grants","explanation":"The annual release is N12,500,000 / 5 = N2,500,000, so after 4 years N10,000,000 has been released and N2,500,000 remains for year 5. Option C deducts only 3 years. Option A is the total released so far, not the balance, and option B would apply only at the end of year 5."}},{"itemId":"a2d68192-a49d-5ff4-808a-18125db15e77","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e58d7b92-47c3-58c1-834f-7c3e633acc3d","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I20-039","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Asaba Pharma Ltd buys a production line for N120,000,000 with a N24,000,000 grant, depreciated straight-line over 8 years. What is the net annual effect on profit (expense) under EACH method?","options":["N12,000,000 if deducted from cost; N18,000,000 under deferred income","N12,000,000 if deducted from cost; N15,000,000 under deferred income","N12,000,000 under both methods","N15,000,000 under both methods"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 20 Government Grants","explanation":"Deducting the grant gives depreciation of N96,000,000 / 8 = N12,000,000, while the deferred income method charges N15,000,000 depreciation less N3,000,000 grant income, also N12,000,000. Option B forgets to offset the grant income under the deferred income method. Option D ignores the grant completely, and option A adds the grant income to depreciation instead of offsetting it."}},{"itemId":"2d4e9a96-5b3f-539c-b53a-fd285fab38fa","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8f84ac87-e6b3-5a4a-a21d-6f430b2c77fa","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I20-040","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Ibadan Builders Ltd receives a N18,000,000 government grant related to a four-year construction project. Estimated costs are: Year 1 N2,000,000; Year 2 N3,000,000; Year 3 N4,000,000; Year 4 N3,000,000. Prorating the grant on each year's cost, how much grant income is recognised in Year 3?","options":["N6,000,000","N4,500,000","N4,000,000","N2,666,667"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 20 Government Grants","explanation":"Total cost is N12,000,000, so Year 3 income is (N4,000,000 / N12,000,000) x N18,000,000 = N6,000,000. Option B spreads the grant equally over 4 years instead of in line with the costs. Option C is the Year 3 cost itself, and option D inverts the ratio by dividing the cost by the grant."}},{"itemId":"4f7bde48-508f-5933-8b5a-ac794016c01c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"55bb829b-6d05-527e-b1e5-17199c17cf83","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I20-041","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Ibadan Builders Ltd receives a N18,000,000 government grant related to a four-year construction project, with estimated costs of Year 1 N2,000,000, Year 2 N3,000,000, Year 3 N4,000,000 and Year 4 N3,000,000. Prorating the grant on each year's cost, how much grant income is recognised in Year 1?","options":["N4,500,000","N3,000,000","N16,000,000","N2,000,000"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 20 Government Grants","explanation":"Year 1 income is (N2,000,000 / N12,000,000) x N18,000,000 = N3,000,000. Option D is just the Year 1 cost, not a share of the grant. Option A is an equal split over 4 years, which ignores the cost pattern, and option C wrongly subtracts the cost from the grant."}},{"itemId":"f4313ed7-2784-52a3-8c0e-23001a234808","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e8d40c8a-8010-5fd2-a2f6-2ae69bbfd435","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I20-042","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Yola Hangars Ltd receives a N24,000,000 grant related to a five-year construction programme with estimated costs of: Year 1 N1,000,000; Year 2 N2,500,000; Year 3 N3,500,000; Year 4 N4,000,000; Year 5 N5,000,000. Prorating the grant on each year's cost, how much grant income is recognised in Year 5?","options":["N5,000,000","N8,000,000","N4,800,000","N7,500,000"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 20 Government Grants","explanation":"Total cost is N16,000,000, so Year 5 income is (N5,000,000 / N16,000,000) x N24,000,000 = N7,500,000. Option C is an equal yearly split that ignores the cost pattern. Option A is the Year 5 cost, and option B is simply the grant minus total cost, which has no meaning here."}},{"itemId":"33b4a462-ae4c-51fd-af92-7bb6b3fc1023","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7d6b0233-b3cc-5369-bf99-59d5db746130","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I20-043","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Yola Hangars Ltd receives a N24,000,000 grant related to a five-year construction programme with total estimated costs of N16,000,000. The grant is prorated on each year's cost. What TOTAL grant income is recognised over the five-year period?","options":["N16,000,000","N40,000,000","N8,000,000","N24,000,000"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 20 Government Grants","explanation":"Prorating shares out the whole grant across the years in proportion to cost, so the yearly amounts add up to the full grant of N24,000,000, even though it is more than the total cost. Option A is the total cost, not the grant income. Options C and B combine grant and cost in ways the method never uses."}},{"itemId":"61eeebed-7f82-5a08-b329-12428d80308c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"67616b29-5742-511b-bbe5-bce381814751","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I20-044","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Yola Hangars Ltd receives a N24,000,000 grant related to a five-year construction programme with estimated costs of: Year 1 N1,000,000; Year 2 N2,500,000; Year 3 N3,500,000; Year 4 N4,000,000; Year 5 N5,000,000. Prorating the grant on each year's cost, what is the cumulative grant income recognised by the end of Year 2?","options":["N3,750,000","N9,600,000","N3,500,000","N5,250,000"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 20 Government Grants","explanation":"Year 1 income is N1,500,000 and Year 2 income is N3,750,000 (each year's cost / N16,000,000 x N24,000,000), giving N5,250,000 in total. Option B uses an equal split over 5 years. Option C is the cumulative cost, not grant income, and option A is the Year 2 amount alone."}},{"itemId":"63a36326-b0af-54de-96b3-2ec59c905119","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0e9ae6a5-6f35-5be1-bafc-531893340380","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I23-001","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Interest and other costs that an entity incurs in connection with the borrowing of funds are called","options":["Transaction costs","Borrowing costs","Directly attributable costs","Finance lease payments"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 23 Borrowing Costs","explanation":"Borrowing costs are interest and other costs an entity incurs in connection with borrowing funds. Option A is a broader term for costs of acquiring or issuing financial instruments, option C is a general phrase used for costs of bringing assets into use, and option D is only one possible source of borrowing costs, namely the finance charge element."}},{"itemId":"55a96b6b-f93b-5f7b-8661-1168942c1446","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a746151d-148f-58d3-ac70-0d6b1a11b393","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I23-002","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An asset that necessarily takes a substantial period of time to get ready for its intended use or sale is called","options":["A current asset","A non-current asset held for sale","A qualifying asset","An investment property"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 23 Borrowing Costs","explanation":"A qualifying asset is one that necessarily takes a substantial period of time to get ready for its intended use or sale, and only such assets attract capitalised borrowing costs. Option B is an asset whose carrying amount will be recovered mainly through sale, option D may or may not be a qualifying asset depending on how long it takes to get ready, and option A is a general classification, not the IAS 23 term."}},{"itemId":"8d882eae-a9b6-5ae3-a5bd-a197ecbaa4e7","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"57b78395-0f73-5b49-b425-c97046b26fc7","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I23-004","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT an example of borrowing costs under IAS 23?","options":["Interest in respect of lease liabilities","Interest expense calculated using the effective interest method","Dividends paid to ordinary shareholders","Exchange differences on foreign currency borrowings to the extent regarded as an adjustment to interest costs"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 23 Borrowing Costs","explanation":"Dividends are distributions to owners from equity, so they are not costs of borrowing funds. Options A, B and D are the items that IAS 23 lists as possible components of borrowing costs."}},{"itemId":"b8b87d60-44ee-5172-b8d1-6149ab3bad5d","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e7c3c95b-36a1-5762-89a3-683a6ae365f2","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I23-005","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is most likely to be a qualifying asset under IAS 23?","options":["Machinery that is ready for use when it is purchased","Financial assets","A power generation plant that takes two years to construct","Inventories manufactured in large quantities over a short period"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 23 Borrowing Costs","explanation":"A power plant that takes a long time to build necessarily takes a substantial period to get ready for use, so it is a qualifying asset. Option D describes inventories made over a short period, option B is specifically stated not to be a qualifying asset, and option A is ready for its intended use when acquired, so none of these qualify."}},{"itemId":"763913d4-a3ef-5982-8081-6c673d04c7ab","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1db4a33d-798c-5eb6-b5e3-520e6314acc3","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I23-006","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a qualifying asset under IAS 23?","options":["A toll bridge being built over several years","A delivery van bought ready for use from a dealer","An office complex under construction for two years","A refinery under construction for the entity's own use"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 23 Borrowing Costs","explanation":"Assets that are ready for their intended use or sale when acquired are not qualifying assets, so a van bought ready for use attracts no capitalised borrowing costs. Options C, A and D are major construction projects that necessarily take a substantial period of time to get ready, which makes them qualifying assets."}},{"itemId":"193f0b87-a3ee-5c65-b13d-51822da64153","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"99c12358-0182-5fe7-92f0-2c2bddc13e7a","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I23-007","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Lagos Breweries Ltd borrows to finance its routine production of bottled drinks, which are produced in large quantities on a repetitive basis within a few days. Are these drinks qualifying assets?","options":["Yes, because the entity borrowed money to produce them","No, because inventories produced over a short period of time are not qualifying assets","Yes, because all inventories are qualifying assets","No, because inventories can never be qualifying assets"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 23 Borrowing Costs","explanation":"Inventories manufactured over a short period of time do not necessarily take a substantial period to get ready for sale, so they are not qualifying assets. Option C is wrong because only inventories that take a substantial period, such as maturing products, can qualify. Option A confuses the source of finance with the nature of the asset, and option D is too absolute because some inventories can be qualifying assets."}},{"itemId":"b93e937a-f0a8-5744-b7e4-848922c90de0","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f68d76d1-6b55-5349-9232-056bd9575457","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I23-009","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"How should borrowing costs that are NOT directly attributable to a qualifying asset be treated under IAS 23?","options":["Charged directly to retained earnings","Deferred and amortised over the term of the loan","Capitalised as part of the cost of property, plant and equipment","Recognised as an expense in the period in which they are incurred"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 23 Borrowing Costs","explanation":"Only borrowing costs directly attributable to a qualifying asset are capitalised; all other borrowing costs are expensed in the period they are incurred. Option C would capitalise costs that do not relate to a qualifying asset, option B delays an expense that belongs to the current period, and option A bypasses profit or loss."}},{"itemId":"5063fd1e-e863-54ff-8b37-30dd8f65ca66","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"dc04ba89-3072-5525-97f4-a2c1da2c772b","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I23-010","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Borrowing costs are directly attributable to a qualifying asset when","options":["They are paid in cash during the year","They are higher than the market rate of interest","They would have been avoided if the expenditure on the qualifying asset had not been made","They relate to any loan taken by the entity during the year"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 23 Borrowing Costs","explanation":"Directly attributable borrowing costs are those that would have been avoided if the expenditure on the qualifying asset had not been made. Option A is about timing of payment, which is irrelevant, option D is too wide because loans may have nothing to do with the asset, and option B confuses the level of the rate with whether the cost relates to the asset."}},{"itemId":"e88bc997-b78c-5c0c-bbae-4c94fef6affe","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7bb61d0b-e504-5f59-8c7b-bfa769ccc554","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I23-011","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When an entity borrows funds specifically to obtain a qualifying asset, the amount of borrowing costs eligible for capitalisation is","options":["The actual borrowing costs incurred on that borrowing during the period less any investment income on the temporary investment of those borrowings","The weighted average rate applied to all the entity's borrowings","The interest that would have been paid at the central bank rate","The actual borrowing costs incurred plus any investment income on temporary investment"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 23 Borrowing Costs","explanation":"For specific borrowings, the capitalised amount is the actual borrowing costs incurred during the period less any investment income earned by temporarily investing the unused funds. Option D adds the income instead of deducting it. Option B is the method for general borrowings, and option C is not a basis in IAS 23."}},{"itemId":"16576f7b-ff43-57c4-b480-8703cf738a8c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c58d18b7-0d6e-52db-917d-faed8607abe4","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I23-012","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Abuja Estates Ltd took a N100 million loan at 12% per annum specifically to build an office tower. Construction ran throughout the year. Before the funds were spent, the company temporarily invested part of the loan and earned N2 million. How much borrowing cost should be capitalised for the year?","options":["N2 million","N14 million","N12 million","N10 million"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 23 Borrowing Costs","explanation":"Actual interest is N100 million x 12% = N12 million, and the N2 million investment income is deducted, so N10 million is capitalised. Option C ignores the investment income, option B adds the income instead of deducting it, and option A capitalises only the income."}},{"itemId":"e1b09493-dceb-545e-8224-42ca967eacd4","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5151d9c4-134d-5210-b381-40b4cb857fae","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I23-013","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Port Harcourt Marine Ltd borrowed N80 million at 12% per annum at the start of the year specifically to build a jetty. Expenditure on the jetty and construction activities both began at the start of month 4, and work continued to the end of the year. Ignoring investment income, how much interest is capitalised for the year?","options":["N2.4 million","N9.6 million","N8.0 million","N7.2 million"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 23 Borrowing Costs","explanation":"Capitalisation begins only when all the commencement conditions are met, so 9 months of interest is capitalised: N80 million x 12% x 9/12 = N7.2 million. Option B capitalises the full year including the 3 months before activities started, and option A is the 3 months of interest that must be expensed. Option C wrongly uses 10 months."}},{"itemId":"080cacea-dc0c-5f71-83f7-e637eb8e0a0e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5385328c-6d7c-5cdf-894f-2774e731466f","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I23-014","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When funds are borrowed generally and used to obtain a qualifying asset, how is the amount of borrowing costs eligible for capitalisation determined?","options":["By applying a capitalisation rate to the expenditures on that asset","By capitalising all interest incurred by the entity during the year","By taking the actual interest on the single largest loan","By applying the central bank's lending rate to the asset's cost"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 23 Borrowing Costs","explanation":"For general borrowings, the entity applies a capitalisation rate to the expenditures on the qualifying asset. Option C picks an arbitrary loan, option B ignores the limit that capitalised costs relate only to the asset's expenditure, and option D uses an external rate rather than the entity's own borrowing costs."}},{"itemId":"85b1ae36-1391-5e51-8f29-c5869fc6472b","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"82545f28-c978-518e-9f4e-172a88056adb","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I23-015","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under IAS 23, the capitalisation rate applied to expenditures on a qualifying asset financed from general borrowings is","options":["The weighted average of the borrowing costs applicable to the entity's general borrowings outstanding during the period","The highest interest rate on any of the entity's borrowings","The rate on the borrowing taken specifically for the asset","The simple average of the interest rates on all loans regardless of size"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 23 Borrowing Costs","explanation":"The capitalisation rate is the weighted average of the borrowing costs on the entity's general borrowings outstanding during the period, excluding borrowings made specifically for a qualifying asset. Option B overstates the cost, option D ignores the size of each loan, and option C belongs to the specific borrowings method."}},{"itemId":"bc953081-b889-5ea3-b943-3da420ddffa6","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"82c680c1-bd87-5134-88ca-a202f593b991","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I23-016","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Kaduna Steel Ltd had two general loans outstanding throughout the year: N200 million at 10% and N300 million at 15%. What is the capitalisation rate for general borrowings?","options":["15%","12.5%","12%","13%"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 23 Borrowing Costs","explanation":"Total interest is N20 million + N45 million = N65 million on N500 million of borrowings, giving a weighted average rate of 13%. Option B is a simple average of the two rates that ignores loan sizes. Option A uses only the higher rate, and option C weights the rates the wrong way round."}},{"itemId":"18bfb311-8d30-5e83-b74a-c1591cf4ba54","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e4ddb6c0-85fc-5c6b-b97c-50a0e5623add","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I23-017","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Kaduna Steel Ltd uses a capitalisation rate of 13% for its general borrowings. On a factory under construction all year, it spent N50 million at the start of the year and a further N40 million exactly halfway through the year. What amount of borrowing costs is capitalised for the year?","options":["N6.5 million","N5.2 million","N9.1 million","N11.7 million"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 23 Borrowing Costs","explanation":"Weighted expenditure is N50 million for 12 months plus N40 million for 6 months, so capitalised cost is N6.5 million + N2.6 million = N9.1 million. Option D applies 13% to N90 million for the full year, ignoring when the second payment was made. Option A ignores the second payment, and option B applies the rate only to the second payment for a full year."}},{"itemId":"765e3b4d-f180-5b3a-ada9-6bd2bef5c401","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"93776445-9173-5d62-b35f-740eb36e44c5","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I23-018","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Benin Builders Ltd has general borrowings on which it incurred total interest of N65 million in the year. Applying its capitalisation rate to the weighted expenditure on a qualifying asset gives N78 million. How much may be capitalised?","options":["N65 million","N143 million","N13 million","N78 million"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 23 Borrowing Costs","explanation":"The amount of borrowing costs capitalised in a period cannot exceed the borrowing costs actually incurred in that period, so capitalisation is limited to N65 million. Option D exceeds the interest actually incurred. Option C is the excess, which is not a capitalised amount, and option B adds the two figures together."}},{"itemId":"22f1618b-f298-5cc1-a406-2420769fa088","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"463139e1-cc96-5ac1-bf45-d52c55c80427","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I23-019","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When measuring expenditures on a qualifying asset for capitalisation purposes, which of the following is deducted?","options":["Dividends paid during the year","Interest paid on specific borrowings","Progress payments received and grants received in connection with the asset","Depreciation charged on other assets"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 23 Borrowing Costs","explanation":"Expenditures on a qualifying asset include only payments of cash, transfers of other assets or the assumption of interest-bearing liabilities, and they are reduced by any progress payments and grants received for the asset. Options D and A have nothing to do with the asset's expenditure. Option B is a borrowing cost itself, not a deduction from expenditure."}},{"itemId":"02a3cf51-9121-570a-a9e4-c8d168bc6053","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"29f69883-0dd8-5463-898d-6f6e0901dbe6","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I23-020","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT one of the conditions that must all be met before an entity begins capitalising borrowing costs?","options":["The entity undertakes activities necessary to prepare the asset for its intended use or sale","The entity incurs borrowing costs","The entity incurs expenditures for the asset","The asset has been approved for use by a regulator"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 23 Borrowing Costs","explanation":"Capitalisation begins when the entity first meets all three conditions: it incurs expenditures for the asset, incurs borrowing costs, and undertakes the activities needed to prepare the asset. Regulatory approval is not one of those conditions. Options C, B and A are the three commencement conditions themselves."}},{"itemId":"15b443bd-a5e6-5d4c-81a4-45928baa4b55","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"44a7961f-bf0b-54b7-8744-267ae42b345d","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I23-021","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Owerri Hotels Ltd bought land and took a loan to build a hotel on it, but it is holding the land with no development activity while it waits for a better market. What is the treatment of borrowing costs during this holding period?","options":["They are capitalised, because borrowing costs are being incurred","They are capitalised, because expenditure has been incurred on the land","They are not capitalised, because activities necessary to prepare the asset are not being undertaken","They are capitalised as part of the cost of the land only"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 23 Borrowing Costs","explanation":"Merely holding an asset when no production or development is taking place does not qualify, so borrowing costs incurred while land is simply held are not capitalised. Options B and A each rely on just one of the three conditions, but all three must be met together. Option D is wrong for the same reason."}},{"itemId":"31ee2dbe-a9cd-5590-becc-9b722607c9aa","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1d80e27a-5f35-529f-bcf5-48e4fd229a61","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I23-022","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following counts as an activity necessary to prepare an asset for its intended use, so that capitalisation can begin?","options":["Technical and administrative work before physical construction, such as obtaining permits","Advertising the finished building to future tenants","Simply holding the land while no development takes place","Paying the annual insurance on head office"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 23 Borrowing Costs","explanation":"Activities necessary to prepare an asset include more than physical construction; they also cover technical and administrative work beforehand, such as obtaining permits. Option C is holding the asset with no development, which does not count. Options B and D are marketing and general administrative costs unrelated to getting the asset ready."}},{"itemId":"b0bf2829-645c-5467-857f-c7916e3c1e1d","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9f0728da-9682-55e9-ad5d-d6f27c29b08c","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I23-023","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When should an entity suspend the capitalisation of borrowing costs on a qualifying asset?","options":["During extended periods in which it suspends active development of the asset","While substantial technical and administrative work is being carried out","Whenever interest rates rise during construction","During any temporary delay that is a necessary part of the process"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 23 Borrowing Costs","explanation":"Capitalisation is suspended during extended periods in which active development of the qualifying asset is suspended. Option D is wrong because temporary delays that are a necessary part of the process do not cause suspension. Option B is still active work on the asset, and option C has nothing to do with whether development is continuing."}},{"itemId":"41b6062b-e208-5bb6-92b5-70f7f958d88e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"381e1b44-4997-598f-80bf-fcf40132f5bc","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I23-024","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Construction of a bridge in the Niger Delta is held up for some weeks each year because high water levels are common in the region during that season. How are borrowing costs treated during these weeks?","options":["Capitalisation ceases permanently because the asset is substantially complete","Capitalisation continues because the delay is a normal, necessary part of the process","Capitalisation is suspended because no physical work is taking place","The borrowing costs are written off as an impairment loss"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 23 Borrowing Costs","explanation":"A temporary delay that is a necessary part of the process, such as high water levels that are common in the region, does not cause capitalisation to be suspended. Option C applies only to extended periods of suspended development that are not part of the normal process. Option A is wrong because the bridge is not complete, and option D has no basis in IAS 23."}},{"itemId":"bff93a5a-fe38-5a00-bae8-70cd6c0b20fc","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d0fcd7c1-3dad-53f7-8899-22eb874afe47","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I23-025","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Jos Mining Ltd borrowed N60 million at 10% per annum specifically to build a processing plant. Construction ran for the whole year except for a 3-month period when the company deliberately halted all development because of a dispute with its contractor. This halt was not a necessary part of the process. How much interest is capitalised for the year?","options":["N1.5 million","N3.0 million","N6.0 million","N4.5 million"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 23 Borrowing Costs","explanation":"Capitalisation is suspended during the 3-month extended halt, so only 9 months is capitalised: N60 million x 10% x 9/12 = N4.5 million, and the remaining N1.5 million is expensed. Option C capitalises the full year, ignoring the suspension. Option A is the amount expensed, and option B wrongly suspends for 6 months."}},{"itemId":"0cd6e8d6-631e-5f2a-a30d-f769763ebf10","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"92c40a99-5dda-565a-afef-2d66ef923f4c","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I23-026","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When should an entity cease capitalising borrowing costs on a qualifying asset?","options":["When the asset is first actually used or sold","When substantially all the activities necessary to prepare the asset for its intended use or sale are complete","When all decoration requested by the buyer has been finished","When the loan used to finance it is fully repaid"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 23 Borrowing Costs","explanation":"Capitalisation ceases when substantially all the activities needed to prepare the asset for its intended use or sale are complete. Option A waits for actual use, which may come later than readiness. Option D ties cessation to the loan rather than the asset, and option C is wrong because minor outstanding work such as decoration does not keep capitalisation going."}},{"itemId":"4a045cf3-1d23-578d-bf96-f2b84e610de0","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"16ca2a6a-639f-515f-9350-7eb5e62f38a9","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I23-027","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Construction of an apartment block by Ajah Homes Ltd is physically complete, and the only remaining work is minor decoration to suit each buyer's specification. What is the status of borrowing cost capitalisation?","options":["It should continue until the loan is repaid","It should continue until the decoration is finished","It should be suspended and restarted after decoration","It should cease, because substantially all the activities are complete"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 23 Borrowing Costs","explanation":"An asset is normally ready for its intended use or sale when physical construction is complete, even if minor modifications such as decoration to a buyer's specification are still outstanding. Option B keeps capitalising for minor work, option A wrongly links cessation to the loan, and option C treats completion as a temporary suspension."}},{"itemId":"98cc7391-28d3-500b-9462-b32fdb528688","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9ee06670-5ced-5822-bfc6-6fa041737770","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I23-028","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An entity is building a business park made up of several buildings, each of which can be used on its own while construction continues on the others. When does capitalisation stop for a finished building?","options":["Only when the whole business park is complete","When substantially all the activities needed to prepare that building for use are complete","When the first tenant signs a lease for the park","At the end of the financial year in which construction began"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 23 Borrowing Costs","explanation":"Where an asset is completed in parts and each part can be used while work continues on others, capitalisation ceases for each part when substantially all the activities to prepare that part are complete. Option A applies to assets such as an industrial plant whose parts cannot be used separately. Options C and D are not triggers recognised by IAS 23."}},{"itemId":"be5c5105-bf1a-578f-91b0-30bc0b8e6dbf","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"dda250a7-320b-5656-8165-beb9d6171ac6","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I23-029","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Calabar Ports Ltd borrowed N120 million at 15% per annum specifically for a warehouse. Construction, expenditure and borrowing began at the start of the year, and substantially all activities were complete at the end of month 10. The loan remained outstanding for the whole year. How much interest is capitalised for the year?","options":["N15.0 million","N13.5 million","N3.0 million","N18.0 million"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 23 Borrowing Costs","explanation":"Capitalisation stops when the asset is substantially complete, so 10 months is capitalised: N120 million x 15% x 10/12 = N15 million, and the last 2 months' interest is expensed. Option D capitalises the full year, option C is the 2 months that must be expensed, and option B uses 9 months."}},{"itemId":"1d83462f-6885-5356-a341-46c0e941ef56","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1a9a2a7b-4d2d-50ac-b2f4-676e9578462a","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I23-030","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following must an entity disclose under IAS 23?","options":["The interest rate it expects to pay next year","The amount of borrowing costs capitalised during the period and the capitalisation rate used","The names of the banks that provided the loans","The market value of the qualifying asset at the reporting date"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 23 Borrowing Costs","explanation":"IAS 23 requires disclosure of the amount of borrowing costs capitalised during the period and the capitalisation rate used to determine the amount eligible for capitalisation. Options C, A and D are not disclosures required by IAS 23."}},{"itemId":"ade9d024-e30e-5ddd-942e-fc5b021a7b8c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d4078b77-a4f4-53f8-a356-1172e7afe27f","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I8-001","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The main aim of IAS 8 Accounting Policies, Changes in Accounting Estimates and Errors is to:","options":["Set out when revenue from contracts with customers should be recognised","Prescribe the minimum line items to be shown on the face of the statement of financial position","Enhance the comparability of an entity's financial statements with previous periods and with other entities","Prescribe how events after the reporting period should be adjusted for"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 8 Accounting Policies, Estimates and Errors","explanation":"IAS 8 exists to improve comparability, both over time for the same entity and across different entities, by setting rules for policy changes, estimate changes and errors. Option B describes IAS 1, option A is the job of IFRS 15 and option D belongs to IAS 10."}},{"itemId":"686491ee-5e7e-58bd-b157-a99803e2ebd6","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"29296df0-99a1-56b9-aef6-7f4f47d89c87","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I8-002","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"IAS 8 prescribes the treatment of three areas. Which of the following is NOT one of them?","options":["Events after the reporting period","Changes in accounting estimates","Changes in accounting policies","Errors"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 8 Accounting Policies, Estimates and Errors","explanation":"Events after the reporting period are dealt with by IAS 10, not IAS 8. IAS 8 covers exactly three areas: changes in accounting policies (option C), changes in accounting estimates (option B) and errors (option D)."}},{"itemId":"57a0e75b-8b8d-57ee-b759-a7b8b5d50b1c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0f4c2c90-8215-5ca2-8899-92d259fc2d82","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I8-004","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following best describes how materiality is judged?","options":["By the size or the nature of the item, or a combination of both, in the surrounding circumstances","By the size of the item alone, using a fixed percentage of profit before tax","By whether the item has been reviewed by the external auditor","By the nature of the item alone, regardless of its amount"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 8 Accounting Policies, Estimates and Errors","explanation":"The test looks at the size and nature of an omission or misstatement in its surrounding circumstances, and either one, or both together, can be the deciding factor. Options B and D each look at only one factor, and option C has nothing to do with whether information would influence users."}},{"itemId":"e4df465d-552a-56f1-9074-712a2c859919","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2aee59a3-1607-50fe-9245-0cfc6a092e15","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I8-005","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Where an IFRS specifically applies to an item in the financial statements, the accounting policy for that item must be determined by:","options":["The industry practice followed by the entity's main competitors","Management's own judgement of what gives the most relevant information","The policy that produces the highest reported profit for the year","Applying that Standard or Interpretation and any relevant implementation guidance"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 8 Accounting Policies, Estimates and Errors","explanation":"When a Standard or Interpretation covers an item, the entity must follow it, together with any relevant implementation guidance. Management judgement (option B) is only used when no IFRS specifically applies, and options A and C are not bases for choosing a policy at all."}},{"itemId":"34b462d3-3395-5a32-8323-92aebf5c15c8","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7dc9fcd3-de54-56ef-b09c-7e7ff5578d9c","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I8-006","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Kano Agro Ltd enters into a type of transaction for which no IFRS specifically applies. What must management do?","options":["Apply the treatment required by the Nigerian tax authorities","Use its judgement to develop and apply a policy that gives relevant and reliable information","Choose any policy, as long as it is disclosed in the notes","Leave the transaction out of the financial statements until an IFRS is issued"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 8 Accounting Policies, Estimates and Errors","explanation":"Where no IFRS specifically applies, management must use judgement to develop a policy whose result is information that is relevant to users' decisions and reliable. Leaving the item out (option D) would make the statements incomplete, and options A and C ignore the relevance and reliability test."}},{"itemId":"2785827f-7e20-59a7-9a93-ed27cf595611","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"25106d2a-b09c-5785-82c0-a128cd016cd8","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I8-007","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When management develops a policy for an item not covered by any IFRS, the information must be reliable. Which of the following is NOT one of the features of reliable information listed in IAS 8?","options":["It reflects the legal form of transactions rather than their economic substance","It is neutral, that is, free from bias","It is complete in all material respects","It represents faithfully the results and financial position of the entity"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 8 Accounting Policies, Estimates and Errors","explanation":"Reliable information must reflect the economic substance of transactions and not merely their legal form, so option A reverses the actual requirement. Being neutral (option B), complete in every significant respect (option C) and faithfully representing results and position (option D) are all on the list."}},{"itemId":"02ed7662-5014-5a7f-b2cb-40dd0e14bdd6","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f5d30e78-ae98-5741-83a4-dead97c77319","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I8-008","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In the absence of an IFRS that specifically applies to a transaction, what should management refer to FIRST in making its judgement?","options":["Accepted practices in the entity's industry","Requirements in IFRSs dealing with similar and related issues","The definitions and recognition criteria in the Conceptual Framework","The most recent pronouncements of other standard-setting bodies"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 8 Accounting Policies, Estimates and Errors","explanation":"Management first looks at IFRSs that deal with similar and related issues, because they are the closest authoritative guidance. The Conceptual Framework (option C) is considered next, while pronouncements of other standard-setters and industry practice (options D and A) may be considered only so far as they do not conflict with these sources."}},{"itemId":"5a41289d-15e1-5737-a43a-21fb2586ab9c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8a35fc7e-e00d-50be-b8fc-943f03909f22","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I8-009","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An entity must apply consistent accounting policies for similar transactions in a period unless:","options":["The items are below the entity's capitalisation threshold for the year","Management believes a different policy would give a higher profit for some items","An IFRS specifically requires or permits categorisation of items for which different policies may be appropriate","The transactions occurred in different months of the year"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 8 Accounting Policies, Estimates and Errors","explanation":"Consistency is the rule for similar transactions, events and circumstances, with the only exception being where an IFRS itself requires or permits items to be grouped into categories with different policies. Options B, D and A are management preferences or timing points that do not justify inconsistent treatment."}},{"itemId":"484d955c-f4d5-5661-8171-2e83c1e3ea8c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b8ae1dd3-3441-5825-a264-457c5ffb70b5","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I8-010","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Why are frequent changes in accounting policies considered undesirable?","options":["They require the entity to change its auditors","They make it harder to compare an entity's financial statements with previous periods","They always reduce the entity's reported profit","They are prohibited by IAS 8 in all circumstances"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 8 Accounting Policies, Estimates and Errors","explanation":"Users need to compare an entity's statements over time to spot trends, and frequent policy changes make comparisons with previous periods more difficult. Option D is wrong because changes are allowed when required by IFRS or when they give reliable and more relevant information, and options C and A are not consequences of a policy change."}},{"itemId":"bdfc913c-69db-56ce-b33d-2f9b62ca1f3a","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"96b04398-14fb-5d05-a728-22c5e2cfa86a","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I8-012","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The directors of Ibadan Foods Plc want to change an accounting policy only because the new policy would increase this year's profit and help them meet a bonus target. No IFRS requires the change. Under IAS 8, this change is:","options":["Permitted, as long as it is applied prospectively","Permitted, as long as the reason is disclosed","Not permitted, because it is neither required by IFRS nor shown to give reliable and more relevant information","Treated as a change in accounting estimate"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 8 Accounting Policies, Estimates and Errors","explanation":"A voluntary policy change is allowed only when it makes the statements give reliable and more relevant information, and boosting profit for a bonus is not that. Applying it prospectively (option A) or disclosing the reason (option B) does not cure the lack of a valid ground, and option D is wrong because a choice of policy does not become an estimate just to avoid the rule."}},{"itemId":"61e11f20-adbd-53a8-9eb9-89f55bd342cd","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5c4ebfe6-b555-58ed-aafa-f67ae7a067e5","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I8-013","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A new IFRS requires a change in accounting policy but contains no specific transitional provisions. How should the change be applied?","options":["Retrospectively","Only from the date the standard is first applied, with no adjustment to comparatives","As a correction of a prior period error","Prospectively"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 8 Accounting Policies, Estimates and Errors","explanation":"Without specific transitional provisions, a change in accounting policy is applied retrospectively, as if the new policy had always been applied. Option D (prospective treatment) is the rule for changes in accounting estimates, option B would leave comparatives unadjusted, and option C is wrong because a policy change is not an error."}},{"itemId":"5f2663cc-d4e0-5c23-b489-ba0e2e3ce8c5","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f5a4d7c2-9c74-5c21-b8e0-36c1674d95f2","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I8-014","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Abuja Logistics Ltd decides to classify certain delivery costs as cost of sales instead of administrative expenses. This is a change in accounting policy relating to:","options":["Accounting estimates","Measurement","Recognition","Presentation"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 8 Accounting Policies, Estimates and Errors","explanation":"Changing where costs are classified, such as cost of sales versus administrative expenses, is a change in presentation, and any change in recognition, measurement or presentation is a change in accounting policy. Recognition (option C) is about whether an item is capitalised or written off, measurement (option B) is about cost or valuation, and option A is wrong because this is a policy change, not an estimate."}},{"itemId":"4db258f1-97f0-55b4-89e9-2d22e3bc8351","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"008ebb29-106b-5b76-946d-50dbc9180b18","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I8-015","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A company that has always written off a certain type of expenditure as an expense decides to capitalise it instead. This change concerns which aspect of accounting policy?","options":["Materiality","Presentation","Recognition","Measurement"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 8 Accounting Policies, Estimates and Errors","explanation":"Deciding whether expenditure is capitalised or written off is a recognition question, so a change here is a change in accounting policy. Measurement (option D) is about the basis used, such as cost or valuation, presentation (option B) is about classification, and option A is the test of whether an item matters to users, not one of the three aspects."}},{"itemId":"7bb70fe2-8630-5d4d-a9b7-db1f093dcce5","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1b45280e-0102-5432-8a52-f2e4f40d823d","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I8-016","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Lekki Properties Ltd has measured its investment properties using the cost model. It switches to the fair value model because it believes this gives reliable and more relevant information. This is:","options":["A change in accounting policy affecting presentation","A change in accounting estimate applied prospectively","A change in accounting policy affecting measurement","The correction of a prior period error"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 8 Accounting Policies, Estimates and Errors","explanation":"Moving from the cost model to the fair value model changes the measurement basis, which makes it a change in accounting policy affecting measurement. Option B is wrong because a change in measurement basis is never an estimate change, option A is wrong because nothing is reclassified, and option D is wrong because the old treatment was not a mistake."}},{"itemId":"6220bdf8-ca1c-50cf-8f06-ade5ae05029f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"314bed5d-3f95-5f8b-a9c1-d5db05d6ee53","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I8-017","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Enugu Steel Ltd has never held investment property before. This year it buys its first investment property and adopts the fair value model for it. Under IAS 8, adopting this policy is:","options":["A change in accounting policy that requires a third statement of financial position","A change in accounting policy that must be applied retrospectively","A change in accounting estimate that must be applied prospectively","Not a change in accounting policy, since it applies a policy to a type of transaction that did not occur previously"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 8 Accounting Policies, Estimates and Errors","explanation":"Applying a policy to transactions that did not occur before, or that differ in substance from earlier ones, is not a change in accounting policy; it is simply choosing a suitable policy for a new type of transaction. That is why options B and A, which treat it as a policy change, are wrong, and option C is wrong because no estimate has been revised."}},{"itemId":"e2a331f7-5695-592b-a5dd-95a61c0af111","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a41df13b-49c1-568c-8237-c08d75159a06","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I8-018","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The initial application of a policy to revalue property, plant and equipment under IAS 16 is:","options":["A change in accounting estimate accounted for prospectively under IAS 8","A change in accounting policy accounted for retrospectively under IAS 8","Not a change in accounting policy, since revaluation is a new type of transaction","A change in accounting policy, but accounted for under IAS 16 rather than IAS 8"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 8 Accounting Policies, Estimates and Errors","explanation":"Starting to revalue assets under IAS 16 or IAS 38 is a change in accounting policy, but the standards themselves give the treatment, so it is dealt with as a revaluation under those standards rather than by IAS 8's retrospective rule. Option B applies the wrong standard, option A wrongly calls it an estimate, and option C wrongly says it is not a policy change."}},{"itemId":"e7f7af0b-38d2-5822-8645-6d2e90efa15f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"00926507-4956-52dc-98a2-22d610dbfcf1","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I8-020","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When a change in accounting policy is applied retrospectively, the entity should adjust:","options":["The opening balance of each affected component of equity for the earliest prior period presented, and the other comparative amounts","Only the notes, leaving all amounts in the primary statements unchanged","Only the profit or loss of the current period, with no change to comparatives","The closing balance of retained earnings for the current period only"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 8 Accounting Policies, Estimates and Errors","explanation":"Retrospective application adjusts the opening balance of each affected item of equity for the earliest prior period presented, plus the other comparative amounts, as if the new policy had always applied. Option C describes prospective treatment, and options D and B would leave the comparative figures inconsistent with the new policy."}},{"itemId":"a6be4a09-8a35-51ee-9ad0-92f5532ea781","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1f3892d5-5f59-5fa4-94dd-cb5cba18295d","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I8-021","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Jos Textiles Plc presents comparatives for one previous year. In its financial statements for the year ended 31 December 2025 it applies a change in accounting policy retrospectively. Which statements of financial position must it present?","options":["As at 31 December 2025 and 31 December 2024 only","As at 31 December 2025, 31 December 2024 and 1 January 2024","As at 31 December 2025, 31 December 2024 and 1 January 2025","As at 31 December 2025 and 1 January 2025 only"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 8 Accounting Policies, Estimates and Errors","explanation":"IAS 1 requires an extra statement of financial position at the beginning of the earliest comparative period when a policy is applied retrospectively; with 2024 as the comparative year, that date is 1 January 2024. Option A is what would be shown with no policy change, and options C and D use the start of the current year instead of the start of the earliest comparative period."}},{"itemId":"1ff2c677-3c63-5bc3-b797-d8e5bd93e0a4","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"50798a6a-954c-5d86-9ad2-0ce6e2e15692","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I8-022","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under IAS 8, applying a requirement is impracticable when:","options":["Applying it would take longer than one month to complete","Applying it would reduce the entity's reported profit","Management would prefer not to restate the comparative figures","The entity cannot apply it after making every reasonable effort to do so"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 8 Accounting Policies, Estimates and Errors","explanation":"Impracticable has a strict meaning: the entity has made every reasonable effort and still cannot apply the requirement. A lower profit (option B), extra time (option A) or management's preference (option C) do not make a requirement impracticable."}},{"itemId":"6955417d-4467-56c4-aeac-247f9652f93b","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"006111fa-0c31-5a10-a46d-e27ae364348e","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I8-023","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT one of the situations in which IAS 8 regards retrospective application or restatement for a prior period as impracticable?","options":["It requires assumptions about what management's intent would have been in that period","The effects of the retrospective application are not determinable","It requires significant estimates that cannot be objectively separated from information that came later","The restatement would significantly reduce retained earnings"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 8 Accounting Policies, Estimates and Errors","explanation":"A large reduction in retained earnings is simply the effect of the restatement, not a reason it cannot be done. The three recognised situations are effects that cannot be determined (option B), the need for assumptions about management's past intent (option A) and significant estimates that cannot be objectively separated from hindsight information (option C)."}},{"itemId":"b62e09ba-14bb-5a00-8167-0356caeca314","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0ba95b01-a302-5a52-bcd5-89193c0e1236","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I8-024","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"If retrospective application of a change in accounting policy to all prior periods is impracticable, the entity should:","options":["Abandon the change in policy and continue with the old policy","Apply the new policy retrospectively from the earliest date that is practicable","Apply the new policy only to transactions arising after the end of the current period","Treat the change as a change in accounting estimate"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 8 Accounting Policies, Estimates and Errors","explanation":"Impracticability does not cancel the change; the entity applies the new policy retrospectively from the earliest date that is practicable. Option A wrongly drops the change, option D wrongly reclassifies it as an estimate, and option C goes much further than necessary by ignoring periods where restatement is possible."}},{"itemId":"37f4892a-67f2-5edf-92d2-eeafb2adb5c9","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"911ac192-1dd7-5b46-b266-d565e77d7ccc","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I8-025","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which disclosure is required for a VOLUNTARY change in accounting policy but not for a change required by an IFRS?","options":["The adjustment relating to periods before those presented, to the extent practicable","The nature of the change in accounting policy","The reason why the new accounting policy provides reliable and more relevant information","The amount of the adjustment to each affected item for the current and previous periods"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 8 Accounting Policies, Estimates and Errors","explanation":"A voluntary change is justified only because it gives reliable and more relevant information, so the entity must explain that reason. The nature of the change (option B) and the amounts of the adjustments (options D and A) must be disclosed for both kinds of change."}},{"itemId":"9ef0b06f-c5a0-5b7a-8bbd-4114ad3e936c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c662c193-ce48-54e3-b03e-2c8c60b2c3a1","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I8-026","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which disclosure is required for a change in accounting policy made because of an IFRS, but not for a voluntary change?","options":["A description of any transitional provisions","The nature of the change in accounting policy","An explanation if retrospective application is impracticable","The reason why the new policy provides reliable and more relevant information"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 8 Accounting Policies, Estimates and Errors","explanation":"Transitional provisions come from the new standard, so describing them applies only when the change is required by an IFRS, along with the title of that standard. The nature of the change (option B) and the impracticability explanation (option C) apply to both kinds of change, while the reason for more relevant information (option D) applies only to voluntary changes."}},{"itemId":"77843ce6-7172-5f63-abc4-71f274bfc504","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e1a6b49c-22f5-5afd-816c-d208286a6546","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I8-027","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An accounting estimate is needed for an item in the financial statements when:","options":["The item was recorded incorrectly in a prior period","No IFRS specifically applies to the item","The entity changes the measurement basis of the item","The item cannot be measured with precision and there is some uncertainty about it"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 8 Accounting Policies, Estimates and Errors","explanation":"Estimates are needed where an amount cannot be measured precisely and is uncertain, so it depends partly on management's judgement. Option B is the situation where management develops a policy, option A describes a prior period error, and option C is a change in accounting policy."}},{"itemId":"1f1e5893-76e1-5334-921b-9999ab9bb7aa","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"610616f3-2c06-5686-8981-414718b24d79","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I8-028","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT an example of an item that requires an accounting estimate?","options":["Inventory obsolescence","The decision to measure investment property at fair value rather than cost","The useful lives of non-current assets","The fair value of financial assets or liabilities"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 8 Accounting Policies, Estimates and Errors","explanation":"Choosing fair value instead of cost is a choice of measurement basis, which is an accounting policy, not an estimate. Inventory obsolescence (option A), useful lives (option C) and the fair value of financial assets or liabilities (option D) are all given as items needing management estimates."}},{"itemId":"047599e7-91a8-528f-ac04-2b20f4817d9f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"257df275-8d59-520d-b3c1-e33aa611b084","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I8-029","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Owerri Bottling Ltd changes the depreciation method for its delivery vans from straight line to reducing balance because the vans' benefits are now used up mainly in their early years. How is this accounted for?","options":["As a change in accounting estimate, applied prospectively","As a change in accounting policy, applied prospectively","As a change in accounting policy, applied retrospectively","As a correction of a prior period error, by retrospective restatement"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 8 Accounting Policies, Estimates and Errors","explanation":"The depreciation method reflects the pattern in which an asset's benefits are used up, so choosing between straight line and reducing balance is an accounting estimate, and a change is applied prospectively. Options C and B wrongly treat it as a policy change, and option D is wrong because the old method was a reasonable estimate, not a mistake."}},{"itemId":"58ad1177-b2b0-5c19-b83f-29ced993e7aa","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"24836867-a424-5d5b-ae56-d1547a712be1","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I8-030","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When it is difficult to tell whether a change is a change in accounting policy or a change in accounting estimate, IAS 8 requires the change to be treated as:","options":["A matter for the auditor to decide","A change in accounting policy","A prior period error","A change in accounting estimate"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 8 Accounting Policies, Estimates and Errors","explanation":"Where the two cannot be clearly distinguished, the change is treated as a change in accounting estimate, so it is applied prospectively. Option B reverses the rule, option C is wrong because nothing was done incorrectly, and option A is not how IAS 8 settles the matter."}},{"itemId":"45a522d8-c2fb-5a0e-a771-e2e995f0c6f2","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"152aa7f5-094a-5d5c-9b82-f707b451125f","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I8-031","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A change in the measurement basis applied to an item, such as moving from the cost model to the revaluation model, is:","options":["A prior period error","A change in accounting policy","A change in accounting estimate","Neither a change in policy nor a change in estimate"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 8 Accounting Policies, Estimates and Errors","explanation":"Measurement is one of the three aspects of accounting policy, so changing the measurement basis is a change in accounting policy and not a change in estimate. Option C confuses the basis with the amounts estimated under it, option A wrongly implies the old basis was a mistake, and option D ignores the clear rule."}},{"itemId":"8c8fabb3-8146-5242-9d5c-44b6547612a0","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f8beb64c-e778-5f59-a976-8fca81d71084","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I8-032","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Changes in accounting estimates are not corrections of errors because they:","options":["Always relate to periods before those presented","Result from new information or new developments","Arise from mathematical mistakes in earlier calculations","Are required by a new or revised IFRS"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 8 Accounting Policies, Estimates and Errors","explanation":"A change in estimate reflects a fresh assessment of the present status and expected future benefits or obligations of assets and liabilities, based on new information or developments, so the earlier figure was not wrong. Option D describes a policy change required by a standard, and options A and C point to situations that suggest a prior period error."}},{"itemId":"2e91f912-65cc-51a3-90e0-2e58aafc5076","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b3a5a93e-1e66-54b3-920c-65155b7011d1","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I8-033","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Prospective application of a change in accounting estimate means that the effect of the change is recognised:","options":["As an adjustment to opening retained earnings of the earliest period presented","By restating the comparative figures for all prior periods presented","In the current period and the future periods affected by the change","Only in the notes, with no effect on profit or loss"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 8 Accounting Policies, Estimates and Errors","explanation":"Prospective treatment leaves past figures alone and puts the effect into the period of change and any future periods affected. Options B and A describe retrospective treatment, which is used for policy changes and errors, and option D is wrong because the effect does go through profit or loss."}},{"itemId":"cb5bc2ad-7847-5fe2-8b88-25f6b8d70f9f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c42daa9e-e837-5a1a-b6aa-838a6bd07277","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I8-034","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Kaduna Motors Ltd revises an estimate that affects only the current year, with no effect on later years. Where is the effect of the change recognised?","options":["In profit or loss for the current period and all future periods","In profit or loss for the current period only","By restating the prior year comparative figures","As an adjustment to opening retained earnings"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 8 Accounting Policies, Estimates and Errors","explanation":"When a change in estimate affects only the period in which it is made, its effect goes into profit or loss for that period. Option A applies only when the change also affects future periods, and options D and C are retrospective treatments that are never used for estimate changes."}},{"itemId":"6e252d68-064b-504b-9976-e0fbb3ef3ad5","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6f3842f7-c7a9-51c5-9cd1-3375fbac31ee","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I8-035","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"For a change in accounting estimate, which of the following must be disclosed?","options":["The title of the Standard that required the change","The reason why the new estimate gives reliable and more relevant information","The nature and amount of the change that affects the current period or is expected to affect future periods","Restated comparative figures for every prior period presented"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 8 Accounting Policies, Estimates and Errors","explanation":"IAS 8 requires the nature and amount of a change in estimate that affects the current period or future periods, and if the future effect cannot practicably be estimated, that fact must be stated. Options B and A are disclosures for changes in accounting policy, and option D is wrong because estimate changes are not applied retrospectively."}},{"itemId":"d299b9ba-0817-5b32-8d5d-c7d6a0630cdb","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"fe0ec84b-67bd-578a-bf47-1c2312fdde0f","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I8-037","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Omissions from, and misstatements in, an entity's financial statements for one or more prior periods, arising from failure to use, or misuse of, reliable information that was available when those statements were authorised for issue, are called:","options":["Changes in accounting estimates","Changes in accounting policies","Prior period errors","Non-adjusting events"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 8 Accounting Policies, Estimates and Errors","explanation":"This is the definition of prior period errors: the correct information existed and could reasonably have been obtained and used at the time, but was not used or was misused. Changes in estimates (option A) come from new information, policy changes (option B) are deliberate choices of a different policy, and non-adjusting events (option D) belong to IAS 10."}},{"itemId":"629f591e-7d65-5a90-af77-a3152bf75559","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8c99f9f2-f63f-507f-9e96-6db177a60707","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I8-039","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"While preparing its 2025 financial statements, Aba Leather Ltd discovers that its 2024 depreciation charge was understated because a clerk entered the wrong figure in the calculation. The information needed was available when the 2024 statements were approved. This is:","options":["A change in accounting policy","A prior period error","A change in accounting estimate","A transaction that did not occur previously"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 8 Accounting Policies, Estimates and Errors","explanation":"A mathematical or clerical mistake using information that was available at the time is a prior period error and is corrected retrospectively. It is not a change in estimate (option C) because no new information has emerged, not a policy change (option A) because the policy is unchanged, and option D has nothing to do with this situation."}},{"itemId":"2b0e42db-8b1a-52b7-b989-05810d73e7ea","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"57fd0086-61e8-5ec9-b753-e00e3c13bd1d","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I8-040","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Ilorin Cement Plc had estimated a kiln's useful life at 15 years. This year, new information about wear shows that its total useful life will be only 10 years. The revision is:","options":["A change in accounting estimate, applied prospectively","A prior period error, corrected by retrospective restatement","A change in accounting policy, applied retrospectively","A change in measurement basis, applied retrospectively"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 8 Accounting Policies, Estimates and Errors","explanation":"Useful life is an estimate, and revising it because of new information is a change in accounting estimate, recognised in the current and future periods. Option B is wrong because the original estimate was reasonable when made, and options C and D are wrong because the measurement basis and policy have not changed."}},{"itemId":"a51eb4f4-4f68-560d-a440-caa745dfb046","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a6a021a2-be8d-5e5a-b6bf-c99b063b67a4","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I8-041","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"To the extent that a change in accounting estimate gives rise to changes in assets and liabilities, it is recognised by:","options":["Creating a separate reserve within equity for the change","Disclosing the change only, without changing carrying amounts","Adjusting the carrying amount of the asset or liability at the start of the earliest period presented","Adjusting the carrying amount of the affected asset or liability in the period of change"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 8 Accounting Policies, Estimates and Errors","explanation":"Where an estimate change affects assets or liabilities, their carrying amounts are adjusted in the period in which the change is made. Option C is a retrospective approach, option B would leave the carrying amount out of date, and option A is not a treatment IAS 8 uses."}},{"itemId":"2c039789-0275-5a77-8702-bae9a6a5e2d7","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6cf4923a-3574-53d0-885c-dd511e4f833a","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I8-042","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Ogun Packaging Ltd bought a machine for N12,000,000. It was depreciated straight line over 8 years with nil residual value. At the start of Year 4 (after 3 full years of use), management revises the remaining useful life to 3 years. What is the depreciation charge for Year 4?","options":["N2,500,000","N4,000,000","N1,500,000","N2,000,000"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 8 Accounting Policies, Estimates and Errors","explanation":"The carrying amount at the start of Year 4 is N12,000,000 - (3 x N1,500,000) = N7,500,000, and this is spread over the revised remaining life: N7,500,000 / 3 = N2,500,000. Option C is the old charge, option D wrongly spreads the original cost over the new total life of 6 years, and option B divides cost rather than carrying amount by the remaining life."}},{"itemId":"c24152e9-219c-529a-a9dd-cc9774015ea1","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"89cccf4b-2c10-5dca-a4df-908fa1d8eaca","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I8-043","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Using the same facts (cost N12,000,000, 8-year straight line life, nil residual value, remaining life revised to 3 years at the start of Year 4 after 3 years of use), what is the machine's carrying amount at the end of Year 4?","options":["N4,000,000","N7,500,000","N5,000,000","N6,000,000"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 8 Accounting Policies, Estimates and Errors","explanation":"At the start of Year 4 the carrying amount is N7,500,000, and the revised Year 4 charge is N7,500,000 / 3 = N2,500,000, leaving N5,000,000. Option D uses the old charge of N1,500,000 for Year 4, option A recalculates all four years at N2,000,000 as if the change were retrospective, and option B forgets to charge Year 4 depreciation."}},{"itemId":"acd76d8f-c460-5b60-bc77-456dd1a1d8cc","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4b90a94e-a8cd-5cc5-82d7-f10112d481dd","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I8-044","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Calabar Marine Ltd bought a boat for N30,000,000, with a 10-year useful life and a residual value of N2,000,000, depreciated straight line. At the start of Year 5 (after 4 years), the residual value estimate is revised to N5,600,000; the total useful life is unchanged. What is the depreciation charge for Year 5?","options":["N2,440,000","N2,800,000","N3,133,333","N2,200,000"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 8 Accounting Policies, Estimates and Errors","explanation":"Annual depreciation was (N30,000,000 - N2,000,000) / 10 = N2,800,000, so the carrying amount after 4 years is N18,800,000. Applying the change prospectively, (N18,800,000 - N5,600,000) / 6 remaining years = N2,200,000. Option B is the old charge, option A wrongly restarts from cost over 10 years, and option C ignores the residual value."}},{"itemId":"50d9c4aa-5565-5b1c-bfd4-7aee1be6300a","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0fb5152e-665d-566d-8ee2-dfded69a8c7f","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I8-045","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Warri Energy Ltd bought a generator for N48,000,000 at the start of Year 1, to be depreciated straight line over 12 years with nil residual value. At the start of Year 3, after two full years of use, a review shows that the generator's total useful life will be only 7 years. What is the depreciation charge for Year 3?","options":["N6,857,143","N4,000,000","N10,000,000","N8,000,000"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 8 Accounting Policies, Estimates and Errors","explanation":"The carrying amount at the start of Year 3 is N48,000,000 - (2 x N4,000,000) = N40,000,000, and the remaining life is 7 - 2 = 5 years, giving N40,000,000 / 5 = N8,000,000. Option B is the old charge, option A divides the original cost by the new total life, and option C uses only 4 remaining years instead of 5."}},{"itemId":"37455297-3f47-5f7d-bdee-0ca688b1b787","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4e655791-ce8c-5f38-bfe3-4370a64dd84e","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I8-046","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"For Warri Energy Ltd (generator cost N48,000,000, originally depreciated at N4,000,000 a year; revised charge of N8,000,000 a year from Year 3 after its total life was cut to 7 years), by how much is Year 3 profit lower than it would have been under the old estimate?","options":["N4,000,000","N12,000,000","Nil, because the change is taken to retained earnings","N8,000,000"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 8 Accounting Policies, Estimates and Errors","explanation":"Under prospective application, Year 3 bears the new charge of N8,000,000 instead of N4,000,000, so profit is N4,000,000 lower, and Years 1 and 2 are not reopened. Option D counts the whole new charge rather than the increase, option B adds a catch-up for earlier years as if the change were retrospective, and option C is wrong because estimate changes go through profit or loss, not retained earnings."}},{"itemId":"ca1a549a-d5ef-57e0-814b-2f9ab6a65069","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"80e5aa6e-80ff-56bc-8666-e74c375fbf2d","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I8-047","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Akure Farms Ltd bought a tractor for N18,000,000, depreciated straight line over 6 years with nil residual value. At the start of Year 3, after 2 years of use, new information shows that the tractor's total useful life will be 8 years. What is the depreciation charge for Year 3?","options":["N2,250,000","N1,500,000","N2,000,000","N3,000,000"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 8 Accounting Policies, Estimates and Errors","explanation":"The carrying amount at the start of Year 3 is N18,000,000 - (2 x N3,000,000) = N12,000,000, spread over the remaining 8 - 2 = 6 years, giving N2,000,000. Option D keeps the old charge, option A spreads the original cost over the new total life, and option B divides the carrying amount by the total life instead of the remaining life."}},{"itemId":"172bcba1-6b93-5e9a-904e-919003b03d3e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6d0be435-3109-5be9-bd3a-9f5f9d121944","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I8-048","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Sokoto Haulage Ltd bought a truck for N32,000,000, depreciated straight line over 8 years with nil residual value. At the start of Year 3 it changes to the reducing balance method at 25% a year because this better reflects how the truck's benefits are used up. What is the depreciation charge for Year 3?","options":["N4,500,000","N4,000,000","N8,000,000","N6,000,000"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 8 Accounting Policies, Estimates and Errors","explanation":"A change in depreciation method is a change in estimate, so 25% is applied to the carrying amount at the start of Year 3: N32,000,000 - (2 x N4,000,000) = N24,000,000, and 25% of that is N6,000,000. Option B is the old straight line charge, option C applies 25% to cost, and option A recalculates as if reducing balance had always been used, which is a retrospective approach."}},{"itemId":"a7fbca87-43a1-51be-b738-b6eaf0e0a937","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c8188fe5-2cf0-5399-8b58-bdcf56987fd8","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I8-049","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In 2025, Onitsha Traders Ltd discovers that closing inventory at 31 December 2024 was overstated by N3,000,000 through a counting mistake. Profit for 2024 was originally reported as N20,000,000. Ignoring tax, what is the restated profit for 2024 shown in the comparative figures?","options":["N14,000,000","N17,000,000","N20,000,000","N23,000,000"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 8 Accounting Policies, Estimates and Errors","explanation":"Overstated closing inventory makes cost of sales too low and profit too high, so the 2024 profit is restated to N20,000,000 - N3,000,000 = N17,000,000. Option D adds the error instead of removing it, option C leaves the comparative uncorrected as if the error were dealt with in 2025, and option A deducts the error twice."}},{"itemId":"2ec85b39-8f4e-5bbc-b812-dc5fd21aac38","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4629279a-25de-545a-a026-65d98147ac86","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I8-050","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Yola Hotels Ltd reported retained earnings of N50,000,000 at 31 December 2024. In 2025 it discovers that N4,000,000 of repair costs incurred in 2024 were wrongly capitalised as property, plant and equipment. Ignoring tax and depreciation, what is the restated retained earnings at 1 January 2025?","options":["N46,000,000","N54,000,000","N42,000,000","N50,000,000"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 8 Accounting Policies, Estimates and Errors","explanation":"Repair costs should have been expensed in 2024, so 2024 profit and therefore retained earnings were overstated by N4,000,000; the restated figure is N50,000,000 - N4,000,000 = N46,000,000. Option B adds the amount, option D leaves retained earnings unchanged as if the error went through 2025 profit or loss, and option C deducts it twice."}},{"itemId":"76d10327-36a8-5742-a50c-bcff76065fa2","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"39224a49-7f41-5291-af20-8c6ddbdfed5c","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I8-051","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Ikeja Estates Ltd bought an investment property for N80,000,000 on 1 January 2023 and used the cost model, depreciating it by N2,000,000 a year. In 2025 it changes to the fair value model (a voluntary change applied retrospectively), presenting 2024 comparatives. Fair values were N90,000,000 at 31 December 2023 and N95,000,000 at 31 December 2024. Ignoring tax, what is the adjustment to retained earnings at 1 January 2024?","options":["Increase of N10,000,000","Increase of N19,000,000","Increase of N12,000,000","Increase of N15,000,000"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 8 Accounting Policies, Estimates and Errors","explanation":"Under the cost model the carrying amount at 31 December 2023 was N80,000,000 - N2,000,000 = N78,000,000; under the fair value model it would have been N90,000,000, so opening retained earnings at 1 January 2024 rise by N12,000,000. Option A forgets to add back the 2023 depreciation, while options B and D use the 31 December 2024 fair value, which belongs to the comparative year itself rather than the opening balance."}},{"itemId":"cf36f1f8-f870-53cc-9257-a733fa357b38","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"fc4010f9-ad6f-5cb0-9d25-8baba03480f7","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I8-052","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Using the same facts for Ikeja Estates Ltd (cost model depreciation N2,000,000 a year; fair values N90,000,000 at 31 December 2023 and N95,000,000 at 31 December 2024; change to fair value model applied retrospectively), by how much does the restated 2024 profit differ from the profit originally reported for 2024?","options":["Increase of N3,000,000","Increase of N19,000,000","Increase of N7,000,000","Increase of N5,000,000"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 8 Accounting Policies, Estimates and Errors","explanation":"Under the fair value model 2024 profit includes a fair value gain of N95,000,000 - N90,000,000 = N5,000,000 and no depreciation, so compared with the old N2,000,000 depreciation charge profit rises by N7,000,000. Option D forgets that the depreciation charge disappears, option A deducts depreciation instead of reversing it, and option B lumps the earlier years' effect into 2024 instead of opening retained earnings."}},{"itemId":"f4a43b96-c329-5308-9013-c6237ffc55e3","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a44dc02a-49e4-5460-8d38-0728d3d05724","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I8-053","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Benin Pharma Ltd changes its inventory cost formula from FIFO to weighted average in 2025 and applies the change retrospectively with 2024 comparatives. Inventory was: 31 December 2023 - FIFO N18,000,000, weighted average N16,500,000; 31 December 2024 - FIFO N25,000,000, weighted average N22,000,000. Profit for 2024 under FIFO was N40,000,000. Ignoring tax, what is the restated 2024 profit?","options":["N35,500,000","N38,500,000","N37,000,000","N41,500,000"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 8 Accounting Policies, Estimates and Errors","explanation":"Lower closing inventory at 31 December 2024 (N3,000,000 less) raises cost of sales and cuts profit, while lower opening inventory at 1 January 2024 (N1,500,000 less) reduces cost of sales and adds to profit: N40,000,000 - N3,000,000 + N1,500,000 = N38,500,000. Option C ignores the opening inventory effect, option D reverses the direction of the adjustments, and option A deducts both differences."}},{"itemId":"18a67a4e-0acb-55b3-8a29-b4682dcb80e2","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6598e125-abe4-5cf2-870e-bd602c72787a","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I8-054","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"For Benin Pharma Ltd (inventory at 31 December 2023 - FIFO N18,000,000, weighted average N16,500,000; at 31 December 2024 - FIFO N25,000,000, weighted average N22,000,000; change to weighted average applied retrospectively with 2024 comparatives), what is the adjustment to retained earnings at 1 January 2024? Ignore tax.","options":["Decrease of N1,500,000","Decrease of N3,000,000","Increase of N1,500,000","Decrease of N4,500,000"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 8 Accounting Policies, Estimates and Errors","explanation":"Opening retained earnings at 1 January 2024 reflect inventory at 31 December 2023, which falls by N18,000,000 - N16,500,000 = N1,500,000 under the new formula, so retained earnings decrease by N1,500,000. Option B uses the 2024 difference, which belongs in the comparative year's profit, option D adds both years, and option C has the wrong direction."}},{"itemId":"7465bf59-59f8-5815-8b86-4b9d6cf401df","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"316825fc-fcb9-54a7-acb7-afb1d417de00","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:I8-055","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Minna Mills Ltd discovers in 2025 that depreciation of N1,500,000 on a machine was omitted from its 2024 financial statements by oversight; the amount is material. Profit for 2024 was originally reported as N26,000,000. Ignoring tax, how should the omission be dealt with in the 2025 financial statements?","options":["Restate 2024 profit to N24,500,000; also charge N1,500,000 in 2025 profit or loss","Keep 2024 profit at N26,000,000; charge N1,500,000 in 2025 profit or loss","Restate 2024 profit to N27,500,000; no charge in 2025 profit or loss","Restate 2024 profit to N24,500,000; no charge in 2025 profit or loss"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 8 Accounting Policies, Estimates and Errors","explanation":"An oversight using information available at the time is a prior period error, so the 2024 comparative is restated to N26,000,000 - N1,500,000 = N24,500,000 and the correction does not pass through 2025 profit or loss. Option B wrongly treats the error in the current year, option C adds the omitted charge instead of deducting it, and option A counts the expense twice by also charging it in 2025."}},{"itemId":"eaa295f1-4196-5b86-bdfa-72bfa7d781b6","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3855c08d-c57f-5884-be6a-f4e952fef93b","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:R15-001","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The objective of IFRS 15 is to report useful information about certain features of revenue and cash flows arising from a contract with a customer. Which of the following is NOT one of those features?","options":["Timing","Profitability","Uncertainty","Nature"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"IFRS 15 aims to give users useful information about the nature, amount, timing and uncertainty of revenue and cash flows from contracts with customers. Profitability is not on that list, because the standard is about how and when revenue is reported, not how much profit is made. Options A, C and D are all genuine parts of the objective."}},{"itemId":"1162afe6-85ae-582d-8b36-cf8588cebf14","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3fe2676e-c417-5cb0-81d8-a20f8a93353c","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:R15-002","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"By setting principles for reporting revenue from contracts with customers, IFRS 15 aims to promote which of the following?","options":["Higher reported profits for entities in all industries","Faster collection of trade receivables from customers","Uniform selling prices across competing entities","Consistency and comparability across industries and capital markets"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"IFRS 15 sets one set of principles for revenue so that entities in different industries and capital markets report revenue in a consistent and comparable way. Option A is wrong because the standard does not aim to raise profits; it aims to report them faithfully. Options B and C are business outcomes that an accounting standard does not try to control."}},{"itemId":"004176a9-8f21-5a98-ac60-658dba683987","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ff5dbeb5-8042-5bf6-933c-44bba1eb682c","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:R15-005","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In IFRS 15, a customer is a party that has contracted with an entity to obtain goods or services that are","options":["an output of the entity's ordinary activities in exchange for consideration","any asset of the entity, including its old property, plant and equipment","a contribution from the entity's equity participants","supplied free of charge as part of the entity's social responsibility"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"A customer contracts to obtain goods or services that are an output of the entity's ordinary activities, in exchange for consideration. Option B is wrong because buying an entity's old equipment is not buying its ordinary output. Option C describes owners, not customers, and option D is wrong because a customer gives consideration in exchange."}},{"itemId":"ff2d4073-e634-59d2-801d-77c810fa816a","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4e4b4c6e-b36f-5e8f-b28e-8d9fb5bcc0af","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:R15-006","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Income arising in the course of an entity's ordinary activities is called","options":["Revenue","Equity","Consideration","Performance obligation"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"Revenue is income that arises in the course of an entity's ordinary activities, such as a supermarket selling groceries. Option B, equity, is the owners' residual interest, not a type of income. Option C is what a customer pays or promises in exchange for goods or services, and option D is a promise to transfer a distinct good or service."}},{"itemId":"a1efbda3-f02b-5642-a6af-fdb6d0c5b28f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b01748a2-5cbd-500e-a87e-5a649db86bef","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:R15-007","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Income is defined as increases in economic benefits during the accounting period that increase equity. Which of the following is specifically excluded from income?","options":["Inflows of assets from the sale of goods","Contributions from equity participants","Decreases of liabilities that increase equity","Enhancements of assets that increase equity"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"Income covers inflows or enhancements of assets and decreases of liabilities that increase equity, other than those relating to contributions from equity participants. Money put in by owners increases equity but is not income. Options A, C and D are all forms of income named in the definition."}},{"itemId":"45c252c7-c731-5d6a-8f51-453fb00e504e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ff4be6d2-60c6-58e0-90a7-ddd34da72543","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:R15-008","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Adaeze Foods Ltd, a Lagos supermarket chain, had the following inflows in a year. Which one is revenue under IFRS 15?","options":["Cash paid in by the owners to expand the business","A long-term loan received from a commercial bank","New shares issued to existing shareholders for cash","Sales of groceries to shoppers"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"Revenue is income that arises from an entity's ordinary activities, and selling groceries is exactly what a supermarket does. Options A and C are contributions from equity participants, which are excluded from income altogether. Option B is a loan, which creates a liability rather than an increase in equity."}},{"itemId":"e838fd51-bf89-5da3-a442-545bb25a0eca","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ea34d0ec-321c-55db-9214-ef7a888cc339","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:R15-010","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"CleanPro Ltd contracts to clean a Victoria Island office every day for one year. Each day's cleaning is distinct, substantially the same as other days, and transferred in the same pattern. How is this promise best described under IFRS 15?","options":["No performance obligation until the year ends","A contribution from the customer to the entity's equity","365 separate contracts with the same customer","A single performance obligation made up of a series of distinct services"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"A performance obligation can be a series of distinct goods or services that are substantially the same and have the same pattern of transfer, which fits daily cleaning over a year. Option C is wrong because there is one agreement creating enforceable rights and obligations, not 365 contracts. Option A is wrong because the promise exists from contract inception, and option B confuses a customer with an owner."}},{"itemId":"66d105ca-c9e6-520e-88ed-e31f291e8a08","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"dd1ffed4-dcaa-59a4-b72d-8087a6cde74d","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:R15-011","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"What is the correct order of the five steps in the IFRS 15 revenue model?","options":["Identify the contract; determine the transaction price; identify the performance obligations; recognise revenue; allocate the transaction price","Identify the contract; identify the performance obligations; determine the transaction price; allocate the transaction price; recognise revenue","Identify the performance obligations; identify the contract; allocate the transaction price; determine the transaction price; recognise revenue","Determine the transaction price; identify the contract; identify the performance obligations; allocate the transaction price; recognise revenue"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"The model starts with the contract, then the promises in it, then the price, then splits that price across the promises, and finally recognises revenue as each promise is satisfied. Option C wrongly puts performance obligations before the contract and allocation before the price is known. Option A recognises revenue before the price has been allocated, and option D prices a contract before identifying it."}},{"itemId":"c80df3fb-6c89-5cf8-85e7-149380e73ad3","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f9b01bb3-f48d-5080-a450-9b92952fa95f","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:R15-012","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Step 3 of the IFRS 15 five-step model is to","options":["determine the transaction price","recognise revenue when a performance obligation is satisfied","allocate the transaction price","identify the performance obligations in the contract"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"Step 3 is determining the transaction price, the amount of consideration the entity expects to be entitled to. Option C is Step 4, which comes only after the price is known. Option D is Step 2 and option B is Step 5."}},{"itemId":"1f76efa4-5e31-5c3c-b83c-92d4c48f5e6d","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3b7f8330-6ae1-50ca-9431-d7fcfd93cf6b","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:R15-013","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Distributing the transaction price to the identified performance obligations based on the stand-alone selling price of each distinct good or service is which step of the IFRS 15 model?","options":["Step 5","Step 3","Step 4","Step 2"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"Step 4 is the allocation of the transaction price across the performance obligations using their relative stand-alone selling prices. Option D, Step 2, only identifies the performance obligations. Option B, Step 3, works out the total price before it is shared out, and option A, Step 5, is when revenue is recognised."}},{"itemId":"9cf2b0d1-fd99-553e-97d5-c4126235e879","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b8a94faf-3146-57ef-819b-85c95e34b083","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:R15-014","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Checking whether an agreement has commercial substance and whether its payment terms can be identified is carried out at which step of the IFRS 15 model?","options":["Step 5 - Recognise revenue when a performance obligation is satisfied","Step 2 - Identify the performance obligations","Step 1 - Identify the contract with the customer","Step 4 - Allocate the transaction price"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"Commercial substance and identifiable payment terms are among the criteria that must all be met for a contract to exist, which is the test in Step 1. Option B looks at the distinct promises inside a contract that already exists. Options A and D come later, once a valid contract and its price are known."}},{"itemId":"ca7b0a1e-3043-5e76-b3f1-90a33a1a1afc","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d6dee5e1-ce88-55d3-9eb0-1421da1c74bc","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:R15-015","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following statements about the form of a contract under IFRS 15 is correct?","options":["A contract covers only goods or services explicitly listed in the written agreement","A contract may be written, non-written or implied by the entity's customary business practices","A contract exists only after the customer has paid in full","A contract exists only if it is written and signed by both parties"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"A contract can be written, non-written or implied, and it may include goods or services the customer expects because of the entity's business practices or statements. Option D is wrong because an oral or implied agreement can also create enforceable rights. Option A ignores implied promises, and option C confuses the existence of a contract with payment."}},{"itemId":"053827d6-0cbe-54f6-aa7b-ae392910edcf","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"351fc385-db58-524e-830b-9c027ebf0bcb","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:R15-016","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"PhoneHub Ltd, an Ikeja phone retailer, has always fitted a free screen protector for every customer who buys a phone, although its sales receipt does not mention this. How should the fitting be viewed under IFRS 15?","options":["As outside the contract, because it is not written on the receipt","As a contribution from PhoneHub's equity participants","As income that is not revenue, because it is free","As a promise in the contract, because customers expect it from the entity's customary business practice"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"Contracts are not limited to goods or services explicitly written down; they also include those the customer expects because of the entity's business practices. Option A is wrong for exactly that reason. Option B misuses the equity-participants idea, and option C is wrong because the fitting is part of what the customer pays for when buying the phone."}},{"itemId":"91d3acef-7360-5988-866e-917ab1d682ad","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"cf29350e-9580-587a-a02a-fd013abbd64d","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:R15-017","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT one of the criteria that must be met for a contract with a customer to fall within the scope of IFRS 15?","options":["The contract has commercial substance","The customer has obtained legal title to the asset","The contract has been approved by parties committed to performing their obligations","The payment terms of the contract have been identified"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"Legal title is an indicator, used in Step 5, that control of an asset has passed to the customer; it is not a condition for a contract to exist. Options A, C and D are all genuine Step 1 criteria, together with identifiable rights of each party and probable collection of the consideration."}},{"itemId":"c069378f-8d79-5d21-9b73-1b17a638840a","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"da930000-7fdb-5d1f-9c40-5e8148ce996d","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:R15-018","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is one of the criteria for identifying a contract with a customer under IFRS 15?","options":["The customer simultaneously receives and consumes the benefits","The rights of each party to the contract can be identified","The customer has physical possession of the asset","The stand-alone selling price of each good is observable"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"For a contract to exist, the rights of each party must be identifiable, along with approval, identified payment terms, commercial substance and probable collection. Option A is a criterion for recognising revenue over time in Step 5. Option D relates to allocation in Step 4, and option C is an indicator that control has passed at a point in time."}},{"itemId":"177b3294-c590-59a2-bce4-d3a44a1a6816","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"090a2c76-8b24-5a49-8bba-a7ac1ac29299","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:R15-019","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"If an arrangement with a customer fails any of the five contract criteria in IFRS 15, which of the following is correct?","options":["The arrangement is automatically cancelled and can never be revisited","The entity recognises revenue only for the criteria that were met","An accounting contract does not exist, even though a legal contract may exist","Revenue is recognised immediately because a legal contract exists"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"All five criteria must be met; if any fails, there is no contract for IFRS 15 purposes even if the agreement is legally binding. Option D is wrong because legal enforceability alone is not enough. Option A is wrong because the entity keeps reassessing whether the criteria are later met, and option B is wrong because there is no partial contract."}},{"itemId":"a153b38c-d338-5144-9cc2-a3b88c27fe34","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5594ba67-a44d-504b-b018-fbf062f39422","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:R15-020","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When a contract with a customer does not initially meet the IFRS 15 contract criteria, the entity should","options":["recognise revenue on a straight-line basis over the contract term","derecognise the customer and stop assessing the arrangement","treat all amounts already received as income immediately","continue to assess the contract to determine whether the criteria are subsequently met"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"If the criteria are not met at first, the entity must keep assessing the arrangement to see whether the criteria are met later. Option B is wrong because the assessment does not stop. Option A has no basis since there is no contract yet, and option C is wrong because amounts received are not simply income while the criteria remain unmet."}},{"itemId":"a6e739be-aa9c-5fee-a22c-8e310c9ea8e8","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"36f58e80-bcb0-5fdf-8269-987341354058","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:R15-021","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Okon Plastics Ltd has made goods worth N15,000,000 exclusively for a customer that is undergoing restructuring because of liquidity problems. The customer promises a letter of credit from a bank, but it has not yet been finalised. Which contract criterion is in doubt?","options":["The transaction price excludes amounts collected for third parties","It is probable that the entity will collect the consideration it is entitled to","The contract has commercial substance","The goods are distinct from other goods in the contract"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"A contract exists only if it is probable that the entity will collect the consideration it is entitled to, and a customer with liquidity problems and no finalised letter of credit puts this in doubt. Option C is not the issue, since a genuine sale of goods has commercial substance. Option D is a Step 2 test, and option A concerns measuring the price in Step 3."}},{"itemId":"3a6e047a-2e8a-5a19-a88b-82d1fa44f718","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"aad6464b-f6ce-5384-9d4f-6efa1e6ef323","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:R15-023","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A Lagos supplier receives a non-refundable deposit for 500 machines, but the price per machine has not been agreed because it depends on the cost of a part from another vendor. Which IFRS 15 contract criterion has NOT been met?","options":["The contract has been approved by the parties","The rights of each party can be identified","The payment terms of the contract have been identified","The contract has commercial substance"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"With no agreed price, the payment terms cannot be identified, so the contract criteria are not all met. Option D is not the problem, because selling machines for payment has commercial substance. Options A and B are not in doubt either, since both parties have agreed to the sale and know what each is to give and receive."}},{"itemId":"6b42f611-5fa5-5de6-84a6-d09d928fe845","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"106d3145-2e56-5aa6-aedd-d53ff3b92cbd","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:R15-024","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is a criterion for a good or service to be distinct under IFRS 15?","options":["The customer has accepted the asset","The customer can benefit from it on its own or together with other readily available resources","Its stand-alone selling price is higher than its cost","The entity has a present right to payment for it"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"A good or service is distinct if the customer can benefit from it alone or with readily available resources, and the promise is separately identifiable from other promises in the contract. Options A and D are indicators that control has passed at a point in time, used in Step 5. Option C is about profitability and has nothing to do with being distinct."}},{"itemId":"dbf01708-277b-5f6d-a12d-99507e074fd4","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f78201ce-8191-54ca-af2e-9cd3afcee98a","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:R15-025","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a consideration in deciding whether a promised good or service is distinct?","options":["Whether the promise is separately identifiable from other promises in the contract","Whether the customer has legal title to the asset","Whether it is integrated with or dependent on other goods or services in the contract","Whether the customer can benefit from it on its own or with readily available resources"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"Legal title is an indicator of transfer of control at a point in time, which belongs to Step 5. Options A, C and D are exactly the tests for distinctness: benefit on its own or with readily available resources, separately identifiable, and not integrated with or dependent on other promised items."}},{"itemId":"4f0e153a-3d5b-5fb1-8f76-bfb5e1f315ec","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b27ee73b-6ae7-555c-a0fc-fb8b06573569","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:R15-027","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A software vendor sells a licence with installation. Which fact best shows that the installation service is distinct from the licence?","options":["The installation is priced within the same contract as the licence","The licence is for a standard software product","The vendor issues one invoice for both items","The installation is also carried out by several approved third-party installers"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"If other installers can perform the installation, the customer can benefit from the licence together with readily available resources, so installation is not integrated with or dependent on the licence. Option B relates to the licence, not the service. Options A and C only show the items are sold together, which does not stop them being distinct."}},{"itemId":"86d2cc16-847d-53c8-b9e6-d5e039c73ed9","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5bc4329e-9622-521d-b96e-4c13f940ebbe","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:R15-028","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The transaction price under IFRS 15 is the amount of consideration to which an entity expects to be entitled in exchange for transferring promised goods or services, excluding","options":["any discounts and rebates granted to the customer","amounts that depend on a future event","the cost of the goods or services transferred","amounts collected on behalf of third parties, such as some sales taxes"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"The transaction price excludes amounts collected on behalf of third parties, such as VAT collected for the tax authority. Option A is wrong because discounts and rebates are factored into the price rather than excluded as third-party amounts. Option B describes variable consideration, which is also included in the estimate, and option C is wrong because cost is not part of the price at all."}},{"itemId":"b51b50f2-6f81-5fed-9710-58d50268fd33","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8b1dd6f6-04c9-5e7f-9335-b16d18b7acdb","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:R15-029","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a determinant of the transaction price under IFRS 15?","options":["The entity's customary business practices","The terms of the contract","The entity's cost of producing the goods","Returns, discounts, rebates and other variable consideration"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"The transaction price is what the entity expects to be entitled to from the customer, so production cost does not determine it. Options A, B and D are all determinants: the contract terms, customary business practices, and factors such as returns, bonuses, discounts, rebates and variable consideration."}},{"itemId":"8c54735d-9158-56a3-b960-75130ad96754","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2753a09e-78f8-5b84-92c9-7642c40d6527","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:R15-030","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When an entity's entitlement to consideration depends on the occurrence or non-occurrence of a future event, such as a volume target or performance bonus, the consideration is described as","options":["Stand-alone selling price","A significant financing component","A contribution from equity participants","Variable consideration"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"Consideration that can change depending on whether a future event happens, such as a volume discount or bonus, is variable consideration. Option A is the price at which an item would be sold separately. Option B relates to the time value of money when payment timing gives a significant financing benefit, and option C describes owners' contributions."}},{"itemId":"7ff8d6de-a8f9-562e-b5ad-b23f1e78f914","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"50ca98b2-d307-5787-a3f0-7b0126c42a88","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:R15-031","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under IFRS 15, the transaction price may be adjusted for the effects of the time value of money when","options":["the contract contains more than one performance obligation","the customer pays in a foreign currency","the contract includes a significant financing component","the stand-alone selling price is not observable"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"When a contract includes a significant financing component, the transaction price may be adjusted for the time value of money. Option B is a currency issue, not a financing one. Option A leads to allocation in Step 4, and option D means the stand-alone selling price must be estimated."}},{"itemId":"7a4f8c88-9ab2-5cee-97a7-9f6a7f78cc49","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"da7c4725-8173-5410-8316-6afa7ff96181","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:R15-032","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Where a contract has several performance obligations, IFRS 15 requires the transaction price to be allocated to them by reference to their","options":["order of delivery to the customer","relative costs of production","relative stand-alone selling prices","equal shares, regardless of value"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"The transaction price is allocated in proportion to the stand-alone selling price of each distinct good or service. Option B is wrong because allocation is based on selling prices, not costs. Options A and D ignore the value of each item, so they would not show the consideration the entity expects for each one."}},{"itemId":"17830d45-cc0f-5fb5-a4ac-f4a1800de355","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0ce03ee8-61b9-5fab-bf07-6bfb282b0161","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:R15-033","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"If the stand-alone selling price of a promised good is not observable, the entity should","options":["allocate nothing to that good","allocate the whole transaction price to that good","estimate the stand-alone selling price","treat the good as not distinct"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"When a stand-alone selling price cannot be observed, the entity estimates it and then allocates on the relative basis as usual. Option A would understate revenue for a real performance obligation, and option B ignores the other obligations. Option D is wrong because being distinct depends on the distinct criteria, not on whether a price is observable."}},{"itemId":"ff2890a0-8920-56ec-a46f-4a00c49a14aa","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"baa862a8-168a-5c76-aee7-21657eadc56b","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:R15-034","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A package of goods is sold for less than the total of their stand-alone selling prices, and the discount does not relate to any specific performance obligation. How is the discount treated?","options":["It is allocated to all the performance obligations on a stand-alone selling price basis","It is allocated entirely to the item delivered last","It is recognised as an expense when the package is sold","It is allocated entirely to the most expensive item"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"A discount that does not relate to a specific performance obligation is shared across all of them in proportion to their stand-alone selling prices. Option D and option B load the whole discount onto one item for no reason. Option C is wrong because the discount reduces the transaction price; it is not an expense."}},{"itemId":"485d1efc-2e03-5b6c-a062-dcbef89c3869","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0e7284bb-03b5-50b0-9afd-bcbe006025ca","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:R15-035","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Having the ability to direct the use of, and obtain the remaining benefits from, an asset is the IFRS 15 meaning of","options":["Legal title","Commercial substance","Physical possession","Control"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"Control means the ability to direct the use of an asset and obtain the remaining benefits from it, and revenue is recognised when control passes. Options A and C are only indicators that control may have passed; a customer can have one without full control. Option B is a Step 1 contract criterion."}},{"itemId":"d64cbe21-f85c-50ff-9f49-56a34696ad69","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a71bfa1a-43f2-5d4f-a869-d019ae258216","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:R15-036","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT an indicator that control of an asset has passed to the customer at a point in time?","options":["The customer has significant risks and rewards of ownership","The customer simultaneously receives and consumes the benefits as the entity performs","The customer has accepted the asset","The entity has a present right to payment for the asset"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"Simultaneous receipt and consumption of benefits is a criterion for recognising revenue over time, not an indicator of a point-in-time transfer. Options A, C and D are all point-in-time indicators, along with legal title and transfer of physical possession."}},{"itemId":"d4ed13a1-3738-546b-a19f-505be24dbd02","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"50320068-e813-53a9-b87c-131eff96ffe1","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:R15-037","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following indicates that control of goods has passed to the customer at a point in time?","options":["The customer has legal title to the goods","The entity's performance creates an asset with no alternative use","The contract has commercial substance","The goods can benefit the customer together with readily available resources"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"Legal title is one of the indicators that control has passed at a point in time, with physical possession, a present right to payment, risks and rewards of ownership and customer acceptance. Option C is a Step 1 contract criterion and option D is a Step 2 distinct test. Option B is part of an over-time criterion."}},{"itemId":"d26f5c19-a990-5d7f-901a-f3dc68143770","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ae75a976-6d97-5e0e-8ab4-b5cc7ccd29be","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:R15-038","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under IFRS 15, which pattern of revenue recognition is typical for each type of promise?","options":["Both goods and services are always recognised at a point in time","Both goods and services are always recognised over time","Goods are typically recognised at a point in time; services typically over time","Goods are typically recognised over time; services at a point in time"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"Transfers of goods are typically recognised at a point in time, while promises to transfer services are typically recognised over time, depending on the nature of the obligation. Option D reverses the two. Options A and B are wrong because the timing depends on the nature of each obligation, not a single rule for everything."}},{"itemId":"47c05f96-75f6-5185-9af1-f665c8e76807","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"fe272c0f-69fe-54b9-b410-69b7e0906ee1","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:R15-039","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"GuardSafe Ltd provides security guards for an estate in Lekki for 12 months. The residents benefit from the protection each day as it is provided. On what basis should GuardSafe recognise revenue?","options":["Over time, because the customer has legal title to the service","At a point in time, when the 12-month contract ends","Over time, because the customer simultaneously receives and consumes the benefit as the entity performs","At a point in time, when the contract is signed"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"Security services are consumed as they are provided, which meets the over-time criterion of simultaneous receipt and consumption of benefits. Option B delays revenue even though the service is transferred daily, and option D recognises revenue before any performance. Option A wrongly uses a point-in-time indicator, legal title, to justify over-time recognition."}},{"itemId":"192c5dd2-880e-5012-a098-04143b87791b","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c346d3a7-c01a-505c-a1c7-8645fd84a223","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:R15-040","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"BuildRight Ltd is constructing an office block on land owned and controlled by its customer in Abuja. As each floor is built, it belongs to the customer. Which criterion supports recognising revenue over time?","options":["The contract has been approved by both parties","The customer will have physical possession when the building is completed","The customer will accept the building at the end of the contract","The entity's performance creates or enhances an asset that the customer controls as it is created"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"When the entity's work creates or enhances an asset that the customer already controls, revenue is recognised over time as the work is done. Options B and C are point-in-time indicators that would only apply at completion. Option A is a Step 1 contract criterion and says nothing about timing."}},{"itemId":"963e82ff-94ed-59a0-abb7-2d646d3b753c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7e111143-a5e5-55fe-b8fc-0a3c0f08b8db","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:R15-041","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Ekiti Fabrication Ltd is making a machine built to one customer's unique specification; it could not be sold to anyone else. The contract gives Ekiti Fabrication an enforceable right to payment for work completed to date. How should revenue be recognised?","options":["Over time, because the customer simultaneously consumes the machine","At a point in time, when the contract is signed","Over time, because the asset has no alternative use and there is an enforceable right to payment for work done","At a point in time, only when the machine is delivered"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"An entity recognises revenue over time when its performance creates an asset with no alternative use to it and it has an enforceable right to payment for performance to date, and both conditions are met here. Option D ignores that the over-time criterion is met. Option B recognises revenue before any work, and option A is wrong because a machine is not consumed as it is built."}},{"itemId":"08c2d65b-9b52-5c60-b36c-46c1e2417dd2","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6bd05354-8600-5f8d-8d72-8879f3b0d293","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:R15-042","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A customer buys a generator from a shop in Alaba Market, pays in full, receives the receipt and carries the generator home. When should the shop recognise the revenue?","options":["At the point of sale, when the customer obtains control of the generator","Only when the customer starts using the generator","Over the warranty period, on a straight-line basis","Over the generator's expected useful life"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"The customer has paid, has legal title and has physical possession, so control passes at that point in time and revenue is recognised then. Options C and D wrongly spread revenue for goods that were transferred at once. Option B is wrong because recognition depends on transfer of control, not on when the customer chooses to use the asset."}},{"itemId":"12e1c165-b019-5f55-a9d5-a8d130c631e4","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b80fb686-3f08-55bd-a1a7-d3252e91d211","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:R15-043","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"For a performance obligation satisfied over time, how does an entity determine how much revenue to recognise in each period?","options":["It recognises revenue only when cash is received","It recognises all the revenue when the obligation is fully satisfied","It selects an appropriate measure of progress towards complete satisfaction of the obligation","It recognises all the revenue when the contract is signed"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"For an over-time obligation, revenue is recognised to the extent the obligation has been fulfilled, using an appropriate measure of progress. Option D recognises revenue before any performance and option A ties revenue to cash rather than performance. Option B treats the obligation as if it were satisfied at a point in time."}},{"itemId":"abe72ab8-466d-50e8-b4ad-7342cbd730b5","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5e1773b5-ab79-5c86-9975-5c2de98d1d07","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:R15-044","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Kode Ltd sells a software licence and 12 months of support as a bundle for N1,800,000. Sold separately, the licence sells for N1,500,000 and the support for N500,000. How much of the transaction price is allocated to the licence?","options":["N1,500,000","N1,350,000","N900,000","N1,300,000"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"Total stand-alone selling prices are N2,000,000, so the licence gets 1,500,000/2,000,000 x N1,800,000 = N1,350,000. Option A uses the stand-alone price and ignores the bundle discount, while option D deducts support at full price so that the whole discount falls on the licence. Option C splits the price equally instead of on relative stand-alone selling prices."}},{"itemId":"9350c034-1bb7-598f-ba32-6bc31463ce52","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"86a4a854-b40b-5f52-a848-34bc7dfe89cf","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:R15-045","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A Computer Village retailer sells a laptop and a printer together for N900,000. It normally sells the laptop for N750,000 and the printer for N450,000. How much of the transaction price is allocated to the printer?","options":["N337,500","N562,500","N150,000","N450,000"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"Total stand-alone selling prices are N1,200,000, so the printer gets 450,000/1,200,000 x N900,000 = N337,500. Option D is the printer's full stand-alone price, ignoring the N300,000 package discount. Option C gives the printer only what is left after the laptop's full price, and option B is the laptop's allocation."}},{"itemId":"742e8c74-87d9-5c06-875e-ba3ac334cc1b","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8a19d6df-36ff-594f-b90d-e7ba68d8bcf9","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:R15-046","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A Lekki store sells a phone (stand-alone price N400,000) and earbuds (stand-alone price N100,000) together for N450,000. The discount does not relate to either item specifically. How much of the discount is allocated to the earbuds?","options":["N50,000","N25,000","N0","N10,000"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"The N50,000 discount is shared in proportion to stand-alone prices, so the earbuds take 100,000/500,000 x N50,000 = N10,000, and are allocated N90,000 of the price. Option A puts the whole discount on the earbuds and option C puts none on them. Option B splits the discount equally instead of by relative stand-alone selling prices."}},{"itemId":"61f6ad6f-8637-53b3-8d84-c7c6dc73316e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9de0e6d2-805d-5973-831b-cf3620bbf189","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:R15-048","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In a package where a computer (stand-alone price N500,000) and a printer (stand-alone price N300,000) are sold together for N700,000, what is the total discount to be allocated across the two performance obligations?","options":["N100,000","N200,000","N37,500","N62,500"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"The discount is the total of the stand-alone selling prices less the package price: N800,000 - N700,000 = N100,000, which is then shared on a stand-alone selling price basis. Options D and C are the shares of that discount falling on the computer (N62,500) and the printer (N37,500), not the total. Option B wrongly deducts the printer's price from the computer's."}},{"itemId":"1b6cc5e1-0f9b-579a-9b5c-bb6ab97674d2","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e14dc43a-2323-54e2-8baa-f39c2948d956","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:R15-049","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A bundle contains item X (stand-alone price N600,000) and item Y (stand-alone price N400,000) and is sold for N900,000. What percentage of the transaction price is allocated to item X?","options":["66.67%","40%","60%","50%"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"The allocation ratio is item X's stand-alone price over the total stand-alone prices: 600,000/1,000,000 = 60%, so X receives N540,000. Option A wrongly divides by the transaction price (600,000/900,000). Option B is item Y's ratio, and option D ignores the different stand-alone prices."}},{"itemId":"f57099d3-ddb9-5edc-b791-0c5a7224107c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"529f9fd7-5ed6-5142-be6e-e2a23fef32b3","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:R15-050","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"SolarTech Ltd sells a solar system for N2,400,000, which includes the equipment, installation and one year of maintenance. Stand-alone selling prices are: equipment N2,000,000, installation N500,000 and maintenance N500,000. How much is allocated to installation?","options":["N500,000","N1,600,000","N800,000","N400,000"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"Total stand-alone selling prices are N3,000,000, so installation gets 500,000/3,000,000 x N2,400,000 = N400,000. Option A ignores the N600,000 bundle discount. Option C splits the price equally between three items, and option B is the equipment's allocation."}},{"itemId":"a5ff2d80-8b56-5dac-93ac-2960353595fe","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2e16fc44-3e42-59b3-8ca5-f5f6c9b56f54","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:R15-051","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A software contract is priced at N3,150,000 and covers a licence, installation, updates and technical support. Stand-alone selling prices are: licence N2,000,000, installation N400,000, updates N600,000 and support N1,200,000. How much is allocated to technical support?","options":["N900,000","N1,500,000","N787,500","N1,200,000"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"Total stand-alone selling prices are N4,200,000, so support gets 1,200,000/4,200,000 x N3,150,000 = N900,000. Option D is support's full stand-alone price, ignoring the N1,050,000 discount. Option C splits the price equally across the four items, and option B is the licence's allocation."}},{"itemId":"12e0a3a7-3da1-5fa4-8326-14a91068fb82","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a1569e6f-70c3-5799-8965-07ad7284e5bd","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:R15-052","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In a software contract, N900,000 of the transaction price is allocated to technical support to be provided evenly over five years. How much revenue from technical support is recognised in the first year?","options":["N180,000","N900,000","N0","N75,000"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"Technical support is a service transferred evenly over five years, so revenue is recognised over time: N900,000/5 = N180,000 per year. Option B recognises all of it at once, as if it were a point-in-time sale, and option C defers it all until the end. Option D spreads the amount over 12 months and takes only one month, instead of a full year out of five."}},{"itemId":"8794e767-e5aa-5d66-a142-81594957f752","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"62220022-2677-5561-bf67-c6bf0c57fd66","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:R15-053","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"On 1 October 2025, a telecom company sells a phone plus 12 months of data for N400,000. Stand-alone prices are phone N300,000 and data plan N200,000. The phone is handed over on 1 October; data is provided evenly. How much revenue is recognised for the year ended 31 December 2025?","options":["N240,000","N280,000","N350,000","N400,000"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"The phone gets 300,000/500,000 x N400,000 = N240,000, recognised at once when control passes, and the data plan gets N160,000, recognised over time: 3/12 x N160,000 = N40,000. Total revenue is N280,000. Option C uses stand-alone prices without allocating the bundle discount, option D recognises the whole price immediately, and option A leaves out the three months of data service."}},{"itemId":"43e7c199-425d-5961-be00-3a986469aa26","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ad979610-e13f-5fa8-bd6c-e1e480f2b099","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:R15-054","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A Lagos company invoices a customer N2,150,000, which includes VAT at 7.5% that it collects on behalf of the tax authority. What is the transaction price under IFRS 15?","options":["N1,988,750","N2,000,000","N2,150,000","N2,311,250"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"The transaction price excludes amounts collected on behalf of third parties, such as VAT, so it is N2,150,000/1.075 = N2,000,000. Option C wrongly includes the VAT. Option A deducts 7.5% of the VAT-inclusive amount instead of removing the VAT element, and option D adds VAT a second time."}},{"itemId":"6e14b821-1daa-5de1-b52b-8d00a489a62b","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"661252d7-cc91-5349-9ecb-cbc8bfe147d3","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:R15-055","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Abuja Drinks Ltd sells 10,000 crates to a distributor at N1,000 per crate under a contract that entitles the distributor to a 5% rebate, which Abuja Drinks expects to pay. What is the transaction price?","options":["N500,000","N9,500,000","N10,500,000","N10,000,000"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"Rebates are among the factors that determine the transaction price, so the expected 5% rebate reduces it: N10,000,000 x 95% = N9,500,000. Option D ignores the rebate. Option C adds the rebate instead of deducting it, and option A is only the rebate amount."}},{"itemId":"441c5a89-be3a-5dd7-94af-4d74b9e4c1fa","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d25f1f22-58f7-5ed6-b186-ff102d3527d5","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:R15-056","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Kaduna Grains Ltd sells to a customer at N500 per bag for the year, falling to N400 per bag for all bags if the customer buys more than 2,000 bags in the year. In March the customer buys 900 bags, and Kaduna Grains does not expect the target to be met. In August the customer buys 800 more bags, and Kaduna Grains now expects the target to be met. By how much must revenue already recognised on the March sale be reduced in August?","options":["N170,000","N0","N90,000","N360,000"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"Once the target is expected to be met, all bags are priced at N400, so the March bags were over-recognised by N100 each: 900 x N100 = N90,000. Option D values the March bags at the lower price rather than computing the difference. Option A wrongly includes the August bags, which are recorded at N400 from the start, and option B ignores the change in estimate."}},{"itemId":"6646f80d-d79d-5005-9289-e80b22f2a033","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"518a992b-0338-55e9-aa93-62739e3e2486","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:R15-057","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Kaduna Grains Ltd sells to a customer at N500 per bag for the year, falling to N400 per bag for all bags if the customer buys more than 2,000 bags in the year. In March the customer buys 900 bags, and Kaduna Grains does not expect the target to be met. In August the customer buys 800 more bags, and Kaduna Grains now expects the target to be met. What is the net revenue Kaduna Grains recognises in August, after adjusting for the March sale?","options":["N400,000","N230,000","N410,000","N320,000"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"The August bags are recorded at the new price, 800 x N400 = N320,000, and the March sale is reduced by 900 x N100 = N90,000, giving net revenue of N230,000. Option D forgets the reduction on the March bags and option A still uses the old price. Option C adds the adjustment instead of deducting it."}},{"itemId":"ab19ed30-d292-580e-bf20-786554dad31e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"36dabd9b-446f-501b-ba72-39d7cff01997","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:R15-058","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Kaduna Grains Ltd sells to a customer at N500 per bag for the year, falling to N400 per bag for all bags if the customer buys more than 2,000 bags in the year. In March the customer buys 900 bags, and Kaduna Grains does not expect the target to be met. In August the customer buys 800 more bags, and Kaduna Grains now expects the target to be met. In November the customer buys a further 400 bags. What is Kaduna Grains' total revenue from this customer for the year?","options":["N930,000","N750,000","N1,050,000","N840,000"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"The customer bought 2,100 bags, more than 2,000, so every bag is priced at N400: 2,100 x N400 = N840,000. Option C ignores the volume discount. Option A forgets to reduce the March bags to the lower price, and option B deducts the March adjustment twice."}},{"itemId":"31a9b138-17fe-57e1-ba7e-1686055a705f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"11a63ad5-7346-5c66-a7c0-1b63eb83db61","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:R15-059","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Kano Oils Ltd agrees to sell oil at N1,000 per litre, falling to N800 per litre for all litres if the customer buys more than 2,500 litres in the year. From the start, Kano Oils expects the customer to exceed 2,500 litres. In the first quarter the customer buys 1,200 litres. How much revenue is recognised for the first quarter?","options":["N960,000","N2,000,000","N240,000","N1,200,000"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"Because Kano Oils expects the target to be met from inception, it estimates the price as N800 per litre and recognises 1,200 x N800 = N960,000. Option D uses the full price, which applies only when the target is not expected to be met. Option C is just the discount on the litres sold, and option B recognises revenue on litres not yet delivered."}},{"itemId":"b6b4f8b7-ffb7-56f4-9344-3bf05f54ea45","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6e0fdc80-d5d9-5b94-ad84-5de7af1ccc32","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:R15-060","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Ondo Paints Ltd sells paint at N5,000 per bucket, falling to N4,000 per bucket for all buckets if the customer buys more than 1,000 buckets in the year. Ondo Paints never expects the target to be met, and the customer buys 700 buckets in the year. What revenue is recognised for the year?","options":["N2,800,000","N4,000,000","N700,000","N3,500,000"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"Since the target is not expected to be met and is not met, all 700 buckets are priced at N5,000: 700 x N5,000 = N3,500,000. Option A wrongly applies the volume discount price. Option B recognises revenue on the threshold quantity rather than buckets actually sold, and option C is only the discount amount."}},{"itemId":"6f6aa616-6d96-5abc-bc22-676dd6fcfd4f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"51db34de-308c-54b0-a7e3-c89826154424","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:R15-061","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"On 1 July 2025, Ibadan Cleaners Ltd signs a 24-month cleaning contract for N2,400,000, with the service provided evenly. How much revenue is recognised for the year ended 31 December 2025?","options":["N600,000","N100,000","N1,200,000","N2,400,000"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"Cleaning is a service consumed as it is performed, so revenue is recognised over time: 6/24 x N2,400,000 = N600,000. Option D recognises the whole contract at once. Option C treats half the contract as done after six months, and option B is only one month of service."}},{"itemId":"be006ac7-c141-5ac3-aba2-2ed84539f8dd","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0d2c604c-a97f-561b-a35b-7d3f9022101d","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:R15-062","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Delta Builders Ltd has a contract, satisfied over time, to build a road for N50,000,000. Total expected costs are N40,000,000, and costs incurred to date are N12,000,000. Using costs incurred as the measure of progress, how much revenue is recognised to date?","options":["N12,000,000","N15,000,000","N35,000,000","N10,000,000"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"Progress is 12,000,000/40,000,000 = 30%, so revenue to date is 30% x N50,000,000 = N15,000,000. Option A is just the costs incurred, not revenue. Option D is the expected total profit, and option C is the revenue still to be recognised on the remaining 70% of the work."}},{"itemId":"b547d6cd-10c2-5f52-bc2f-26653b736808","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d8b139d0-59c7-5dda-a958-a4c17ec0ebb0","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:R15-063","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Sokoto Equipment Ltd receives a non-refundable N3,000,000 from a customer, followed later by a further N1,000,000, but the payment terms of the arrangement have still not been identified. What amount of revenue is recognised, and what is the liability?","options":["Revenue N0; liability N4,000,000","Revenue N4,000,000; liability N0","Revenue N0; liability N3,000,000","Revenue N3,000,000; liability N1,000,000"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"Because the payment terms are not identified, no contract exists for IFRS 15 purposes, so both the first payment and any later payments are held as a liability: N3,000,000 + N1,000,000 = N4,000,000. Option D wrongly treats the non-refundable amount as revenue, and option B recognises everything as revenue. Option C forgets that future payments are also held as a liability."}},{"itemId":"23ccc37e-44d2-5f79-a48c-eeb86e1abc19","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a40f8f16-e0a0-575d-b9d8-70a035c9f458","sourceKey":"bank:ACC-CM201:R15-064","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Estimating the effect of an expected volume discount on the amount an entity will receive from a customer is part of which IFRS 15 step?","options":["Determine the transaction price","Identify the contract","Allocate the transaction price","Identify the performance obligations"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IFRS 15 Revenue","explanation":"A volume discount is variable consideration, and estimating it is part of determining the transaction price in Step 3. Option C comes after, when the price is shared out across performance obligations. Options B and D deal with whether a contract exists and what has been promised, not how much will be received."}},{"itemId":"478dea98-f343-5c5e-9a37-2648ea799fb0","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4f2424ee-d4fa-4fd4-a805-7a62bc5df0fc","sourceKey":"quiz:92","parentItemId":null,"revision":2,"payload":{"prompt":"The systematic allocation of the depreciable amount of an asset over its estimated useful life is called","options":["amortisation of a government grant","revaluation","impairment","depreciation"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment","explanation":"Depreciation is the systematic spreading of the depreciable amount over the useful life. Option C is a reduction of carrying amount to recoverable amount, not a systematic allocation. Option B restates an asset to fair value, and option A relates to grants under IAS 20, not to the cost of PPE."}}]$expected_content$::jsonb,
  'financial-accounting-1: cbt_question published payload and identity match source');
set local role authenticated;
select ok((select coalesce(jsonb_agg(jsonb_build_object(
  'itemId', item_id, 'courseId', course_id, 'kind', kind,
  'questionId', question_id, 'sourceKey', source_key,
  'parentItemId', parent_item_id, 'revision', revision,
  'payload', payload) order by source_key collate "C"), '[]'::jsonb)
  from public.read_published_managed_content('40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007')
  where kind = 'theory_question') = $expected_content$[{"itemId":"c9a5d8d9-4efe-54b9-a5fb-b8fb88a7579e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"theory_question","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"theory:1","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"What are the five components of a complete set of financial statements according to IAS 1?","examTip":"List all five components clearly. Don't forget the notes - many students miss this one."}},{"itemId":"1c7a210d-3b08-523c-bcfe-a02ccaf34e4a","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"theory_question","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"theory:10","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Explain the \"Imprest System\" in relation to a Petty Cashbook.","examTip":"Explain the cycle step-by-step with an example amount. State that the float is RESTORED to the fixed amount - this is the key feature."}},{"itemId":"7b757965-e0e0-5855-8986-6e38d676cf94","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"theory_question","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"theory:11","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Differentiate between Capital Expenditure and Revenue Expenditure.","examTip":"Use a clear structure: define each, state the time period (more than one year vs one year), give examples, and explain the impact of wrong classification."}},{"itemId":"9ed907ae-5696-5b5d-876b-91d737bc1789","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"theory_question","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"theory:12","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"What is an Error of Principle? Give an example.","examTip":"Define it, emphasize \"wrong CLASS of account,\" give a clear example with journal entries, and state that trial balance won't catch it."}},{"itemId":"eeffeb74-741b-5268-9148-1976e9af0363","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"theory_question","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"theory:13","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"List three reasons why a cash book balance might not agree with a bank statement balance.","examTip":"List and explain three clearly. State the direction of the difference (cashbook higher/lower). Mention one or two additional reasons."}},{"itemId":"d45c3176-95f4-5168-97c1-d8e77c87d12c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"theory_question","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"theory:14","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Define \"Prime Cost\" in a manufacturing account.","examTip":"State the formula clearly, break down each component with examples, and explain what it EXCLUDES."}},{"itemId":"56f5b8ae-6ebf-5d25-8e34-0f53a68eb0e5","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"theory_question","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"theory:15","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"What is the fundamental difference between Marginal Costing and Absorption Costing?","examTip":"Clearly contrast the two approaches, emphasize the treatment of fixed overheads, and explain the profit impact under different scenarios."}},{"itemId":"41b225cc-5946-5b6b-995f-1df7c419318b","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"theory_question","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"theory:16","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Provide the formulas for calculating the Re-order Level and the Maximum Stock Level.","examTip":"Write both formulas clearly, explain what each represents, and provide a worked example for each."}},{"itemId":"0c831812-5f88-513b-9dfc-973139c4f38e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"theory_question","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"theory:17","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Explain \"Break-Even Point\" and provide its formula in units.","examTip":"Define BEP (zero profit, zero loss), write the formula, show a worked example, and interpret the result."}},{"itemId":"e9b16dc5-3d12-50a6-b6cd-4320b2c1a1be","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"theory_question","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"theory:18","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"What is the \"Margin of Safety\"?","examTip":"Define it, give the formula, provide an example with interpretation, and explain what a high vs low margin means for business risk."}},{"itemId":"7f65e7aa-2de6-5b1d-a081-5fb345fc1f2b","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"theory_question","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"theory:19","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Describe the First-In, First-Out (FIFO) method of inventory valuation.","examTip":"Explain the principle, work through a numerical example step-by-step, and state the impact on profit in rising price scenarios."}},{"itemId":"5f90392c-a10a-569a-8ebb-1241e9e8981c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"theory_question","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"theory:2","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Define \"inventory\" according to IAS 2 and list the three main categories.","examTip":"Start with the full IAS 2 definition, then list the three categories. This shows comprehensive understanding."}},{"itemId":"e636e048-ae9e-5fda-b8c0-173bb1432dc2","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"theory_question","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"theory:20","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"What is \"Labour Turnover\" and how is the Separation Rate measured?","examTip":"Define it, write the formula clearly, provide a worked example, and explain why high turnover is costly for businesses."}},{"itemId":"d4c93605-c101-542c-835e-b9fe4f271618","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"theory_question","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"theory:3","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"How is Net Realizable Value (NRV) calculated under IAS 2?","examTip":"Include the formula AND explain what costs are deducted. Give an example."}},{"itemId":"2f66b8e5-b7ed-5154-92f7-7be966f5e136","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"theory_question","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"theory:4","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"State the five-step model for revenue recognition under IFRS 15.","examTip":"Number each step clearly. Add the core principle about control transfer to show deeper understanding."}},{"itemId":"f82b4aa1-8e36-571b-8786-641a2c249084","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"theory_question","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"theory:5","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Explain the difference between a change in accounting policy and a change in accounting estimate under IAS 8.","examTip":"Give examples for each and clearly state retrospective vs prospective. This distinction is crucial."}},{"itemId":"bd6ccb8d-25ff-5287-8ceb-6e77a5f23f38","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"theory_question","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"theory:6","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under IAS 16, what components make up the initial cost of Property, Plant, and Equipment (PPE)?","examTip":"Structure your answer with three main components. Clearly state what to EXCLUDE to show you understand the boundaries."}},{"itemId":"eb4a5644-323e-573a-ba24-7cfa4145e3a0","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"theory_question","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"theory:7","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"What is the fundamental difference between the Capital Approach and the Income Approach for recognizing government grants related to assets under IAS 20?","examTip":"Clearly label each approach. Explain the balance sheet treatment AND the income statement impact for full marks."}},{"itemId":"46dcfac3-d27e-55db-be01-c1a29c8d519f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"theory_question","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"theory:8","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Define a \"Qualifying Asset\" in the context of IAS 23 (Borrowing Costs).","examTip":"Define it, give the key characteristic (substantial period), provide examples, and explain why it matters for capitalization."}},{"itemId":"4104a77c-9ab1-5705-8fac-c11370a3fc0d","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"theory_question","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"theory:9","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"What happens to borrowing costs incurred during an extended period where active construction of a qualifying asset is halted (e.g., due to a strike) under IAS 23?","examTip":"State that capitalization STOPS, expense the interest, and explain the logic. Mention the exception about brief delays."}}]$expected_content$::jsonb,
  'financial-accounting-1: theory_question published payload and identity match source');
select ok((select coalesce(jsonb_agg(jsonb_build_object(
  'itemId', item_id, 'courseId', course_id, 'kind', kind,
  'questionId', question_id, 'sourceKey', source_key,
  'parentItemId', parent_item_id, 'revision', revision,
  'payload', payload) order by source_key collate "C"), '[]'::jsonb)
  from public.read_published_managed_content('40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007')
  where kind = 'model_answer') = $expected_content$[{"itemId":"965ec205-bbb3-53c8-bb5a-593ab0b3d5b5","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"model_answer","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"theory-answer:1","parentItemId":"c9a5d8d9-4efe-54b9-a5fb-b8fb88a7579e","revision":1,"payload":{"body":"A complete set of financial statements includes:\n\n1. A statement of financial position as at the end of the period\n2. A statement of profit or loss and other comprehensive income for the period\n3. A statement of changes in equity for the period\n4. A statement of cash flows for the period\n5. Notes to the accounts comprising significant accounting policies and other explanatory information"}},{"itemId":"e552f3e4-f695-5574-a9d0-3d8cf60e823f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"model_answer","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"theory-answer:10","parentItemId":"1c7a210d-3b08-523c-bcfe-a02ccaf34e4a","revision":1,"payload":{"body":"The Imprest System is a method of managing petty cash:\n\n**How it works:**\n1. The petty cashier is given a fixed amount of cash at the beginning of a period (e.g., ₦10,000) - this is called the \"float\" or \"imprest amount\"\n\n2. Throughout the period, the petty cashier makes small payments and records them in the petty cashbook\n\n3. At the end of the period (or when the cash runs low), the petty cashier presents receipts and the petty cashbook to the main cashier\n\n4. The main cashier reimburses EXACTLY the amount that was spent (e.g., if ₦7,500 was spent, the petty cashier receives ₦7,500)\n\n5. This restores the petty cash balance back to the original fixed float amount (₦10,000)\n\n**Advantages:**\n- Provides internal control\n- Easy to identify shortages or overages\n- Limits the amount of cash exposure\n- Simple reconciliation"}},{"itemId":"6a9ca9d1-99bb-502c-b414-2de980d59933","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"model_answer","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"theory-answer:11","parentItemId":"7b757965-e0e0-5855-8986-6e38d676cf94","revision":1,"payload":{"body":"**Capital Expenditure:**\n- Money spent to acquire, upgrade, or prepare NON-CURRENT ASSETS for use\n- Provides benefits for MORE THAN ONE YEAR\n- Appears on the Statement of Financial Position (Balance Sheet) as an asset\n- Examples:\n  • Buying a delivery van\n  • Installing a new machine\n  • Legal costs to acquire a building\n  • Major extension to a factory\n\n**Revenue Expenditure:**\n- Money spent on the day-to-day running of the business\n- Benefits are consumed within ONE ACCOUNTING YEAR\n- Charged to the Statement of Profit or Loss (Income Statement) as an expense\n- Examples:\n  • Fuel for the delivery van\n  • Routine repairs and maintenance\n  • Salaries and wages\n  • Electricity and rent\n\n**Why it matters:**\nIncorrect classification distorts profit and asset values. Capitalizing revenue expenses overstates assets and profit. Expensing capital costs understates assets and profit."}},{"itemId":"e2386253-9107-58a7-9929-1b99d52e3272","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"model_answer","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"theory-answer:12","parentItemId":"9ed907ae-5696-5b5d-876b-91d737bc1789","revision":1,"payload":{"body":"**Error of Principle:**\nAn Error of Principle occurs when a transaction is entered into the wrong CLASS or TYPE of account, thereby breaking fundamental accounting rules.\n\n**Key characteristic:**\nThe wrong type of account is used - for example, treating an asset as an expense, or vice versa.\n\n**Example:**\nA business purchases a motor vehicle (a non-current asset) for ₦2,000,000, but the accountant incorrectly debits the Motor Expenses Account (an expense account) instead of the Motor Vehicles Account (an asset account).\n\n**Correct entry should be:**\nDr Motor Vehicles Account ₦2,000,000\nCr Bank/Cash Account ₦2,000,000\n\n**Incorrect entry made:**\nDr Motor Expenses Account ₦2,000,000\nCr Bank/Cash Account ₦2,000,000\n\n**Impact:**\n- Assets are understated\n- Expenses are overstated\n- Profit is understated\n- This error is NOT revealed by the trial balance (both debit and credit totals still match)"}},{"itemId":"b3a2a49d-0141-5b0c-bb59-7c4b4b787a7f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"model_answer","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"theory-answer:13","parentItemId":"eeffeb74-741b-5268-9148-1976e9af0363","revision":1,"payload":{"body":"Three common reasons for differences between the cash book and bank statement:\n\n**1. Unpresented Cheques (Outstanding Cheques):**\n- Cheques that have been drawn (written) by the business and recorded in the cash book\n- But have NOT YET been presented to the bank for payment by the recipient\n- The cash book shows a lower balance, but the bank statement still shows a higher balance\n\n**2. Uncredited Cheques (Deposits in Transit):**\n- Cheques or cash that have been paid into the bank by the business\n- Recorded in the cash book as a receipt\n- But NOT YET cleared or credited by the bank\n- The cash book shows a higher balance, but the bank statement shows a lower balance\n\n**3. Bank Charges, Interest, or Direct Debits:**\n- Items that appear on the bank statement but have NOT YET been recorded in the cash book\n- Examples: bank charges, standing orders, direct debits, interest earned\n- These need to be added to or deducted from the cash book during reconciliation\n\n**Additional reasons:** Dishonored cheques, bank errors, timing differences."}},{"itemId":"922e039a-dd20-563d-83b8-b3afb9f27a4a","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"model_answer","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"theory-answer:14","parentItemId":"d45c3176-95f4-5168-97c1-d8e77c87d12c","revision":1,"payload":{"body":"**Prime Cost** is the aggregate of ALL DIRECT COSTS involved in manufacturing a product.\n\n**Formula:**\nPrime Cost = Direct Materials + Direct Labour + Direct Expenses\n\n**Components:**\n\n1. **Direct Materials:** Raw materials that can be directly traced to the finished product (e.g., wood for furniture, fabric for clothing)\n\n2. **Direct Labour:** Wages of workers directly involved in production (e.g., machine operators, assembly line workers)\n\n3. **Direct Expenses:** Other costs directly attributable to production (e.g., royalties paid per unit, hire of special equipment for a specific job)\n\n**What Prime Cost excludes:**\n- Factory overheads (indirect costs like factory rent, supervisor salaries, depreciation of machinery)\n- Administrative overheads\n- Selling and distribution costs\n\n**Why it matters:**\nPrime Cost is the foundation of product costing. It represents the minimum cost to produce a unit before adding factory overheads."}},{"itemId":"14f56b1c-ca11-59a9-9884-ce37d77629ff","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"model_answer","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"theory-answer:15","parentItemId":"56f5b8ae-6ebf-5d25-8e34-0f53a68eb0e5","revision":1,"payload":{"body":"The fundamental difference lies in how FIXED PRODUCTION OVERHEADS are treated:\n\n**Marginal Costing:**\n- Only VARIABLE costs (direct materials, direct labour, variable overheads) are included in the product cost\n- Fixed production overheads are treated as PERIOD COSTS\n- Fixed costs are written off in full to the Profit or Loss Statement in the period they are incurred\n- Product valuation: Direct Materials + Direct Labour + Variable Overheads\n\n**Absorption Costing:**\n- Both variable costs AND fixed production overheads are included in the product cost\n- Fixed production overheads are ABSORBED into the cost of each unit produced\n- Fixed costs are \"carried forward\" in inventory valuation\n- Product valuation: Direct Materials + Direct Labour + Variable Overheads + Fixed Overheads\n\n**Impact on profit:**\n- When production exceeds sales: Absorption costing shows HIGHER profit (some fixed costs remain in closing inventory)\n- When sales exceed production: Marginal costing shows HIGHER profit\n- When production equals sales: Both methods show the SAME profit"}},{"itemId":"4a0bab6b-867d-5983-8414-ac22bd9c6b40","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"model_answer","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"theory-answer:16","parentItemId":"41b225cc-5946-5b6b-995f-1df7c419318b","revision":1,"payload":{"body":"**Re-order Level Formula:**\nRe-order Level = Maximum Usage per period × Maximum Lead Time\n\n**Explanation:**\n- This is the inventory level at which a new order should be placed\n- Uses maximum values to ensure stock doesn't run out in worst-case scenario\n\n**Example:** If maximum usage is 500 units/day and maximum lead time is 10 days:\nRe-order Level = 500 × 10 = 5,000 units\n\n\n**Maximum Stock Level Formula:**\nMaximum Stock Level = Re-order Level + Economic Order Quantity (EOQ) - (Minimum Usage × Minimum Lead Time)\n\n**Explanation:**\n- This is the highest inventory level the business should hold\n- Prevents overstocking and excessive holding costs\n\n**Example:** If Re-order Level is 5,000, EOQ is 2,000, minimum usage is 300 units/day, and minimum lead time is 5 days:\nMaximum Stock Level = 5,000 + 2,000 - (300 × 5) = 5,500 units"}},{"itemId":"3bba1d5a-1327-5855-8e69-95fb33e7136e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"model_answer","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"theory-answer:17","parentItemId":"0c831812-5f88-513b-9dfc-973139c4f38e","revision":1,"payload":{"body":"**Break-Even Point (BEP):**\nThe break-even point is the level of activity or sales at which TOTAL REVENUE exactly equals TOTAL COSTS, resulting in ZERO PROFIT and ZERO LOSS.\n\n**Formula (in units):**\nBreak-Even Point (units) = Total Fixed Costs ÷ Contribution per Unit\n\nWhere:\nContribution per Unit = Selling Price per Unit - Variable Cost per Unit\n\n**Example:**\n- Fixed Costs = ₦600,000\n- Selling Price per unit = ₦1,500\n- Variable Cost per unit = ₦900\n- Contribution per unit = ₦1,500 - ₦900 = ₦600\n\nBreak-Even Point = ₦600,000 ÷ ₦600 = 1,000 units\n\n**Interpretation:**\nThe business must sell 1,000 units to cover all costs. Sales below 1,000 units result in a loss; sales above 1,000 units generate profit.\n\n**Formula (in sales value):**\nBreak-Even Point (₦) = Total Fixed Costs ÷ Contribution-to-Sales Ratio"}},{"itemId":"d50fdef2-be88-5b64-bd9a-117150e58d9f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"model_answer","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"theory-answer:18","parentItemId":"e9b16dc5-3d12-50a6-b6cd-4320b2c1a1be","revision":1,"payload":{"body":"**Margin of Safety:**\nThe Margin of Safety is the difference between the EXPECTED (or BUDGETED) SALES and the BREAK-EVEN SALES.\n\n**Formula:**\nMargin of Safety = Budgeted Sales - Break-Even Sales\n\nOr expressed as a percentage:\nMargin of Safety (%) = (Margin of Safety ÷ Budgeted Sales) × 100\n\n**What it measures:**\n- How much sales can FALL before the business starts making a loss\n- It's a measure of risk and cushion against adverse conditions\n\n**Example:**\n- Budgeted Sales = 2,000 units\n- Break-Even Sales = 1,000 units\n- Margin of Safety = 2,000 - 1,000 = 1,000 units\n\n**Interpretation:**\nSales can drop by 1,000 units (or 50%) before the business incurs a loss.\n\n**Higher margin = lower risk** (more cushion)\n**Lower margin = higher risk** (operating close to break-even)"}},{"itemId":"4bce501d-934f-59cd-a628-41084f6514e7","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"model_answer","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"theory-answer:19","parentItemId":"7f65e7aa-2de6-5b1d-a081-5fb345fc1f2b","revision":1,"payload":{"body":"**FIFO (First-In, First-Out) Method:**\n\n**Principle:**\nFIFO assumes that the FIRST batches of materials received into the store are the FIRST ones to be issued to production or sold.\n\n**How it works:**\n- Inventory is issued at the cost of the oldest batches first\n- Ending inventory is valued at the prices of the MOST RECENT purchases\n\n**Example:**\nOpening Stock: 100 units @ ₦10 = ₦1,000\nPurchase 1: 200 units @ ₦12 = ₦2,400\nPurchase 2: 150 units @ ₦15 = ₦2,250\n\nIssue: 250 units\n\nUnder FIFO:\n- Issue 100 units @ ₦10 = ₦1,000 (from opening stock)\n- Issue 150 units @ ₦12 = ₦1,800 (from Purchase 1)\n- Total cost of issue = ₦2,800\n\nClosing Stock:\n- 50 units @ ₦12 = ₦600 (remaining from Purchase 1)\n- 150 units @ ₦15 = ₦2,250 (all of Purchase 2)\n- Total closing stock value = ₦2,850\n\n**Impact:**\n- In times of rising prices: Lower cost of sales, higher profit, higher closing stock value\n- Closing stock approximates current market value"}},{"itemId":"2c3c9eb0-3322-5391-977c-6af53e68257c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"model_answer","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"theory-answer:2","parentItemId":"5f90392c-a10a-569a-8ebb-1241e9e8981c","revision":1,"payload":{"body":"Inventories are assets held for sale in the ordinary course of business, in the process of production for such sale, or in the form of materials or supplies to be consumed in the production process or rendering of services.\n\nThe three main categories are:\n1. Raw materials\n2. Work in progress\n3. Finished goods"}},{"itemId":"7c7eea1c-43c8-5091-86fd-f4b7ad9df64c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"model_answer","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"theory-answer:20","parentItemId":"e636e048-ae9e-5fda-b8c0-173bb1432dc2","revision":1,"payload":{"body":"**Labour Turnover:**\nLabour Turnover refers to the rate at which employees LEAVE and ENTER an organization over a specific period.\n\n**Types of Labour Turnover:**\n1. Separation Rate (Leavers)\n2. Replacement Rate\n3. Accession Rate (Joiners)\n\n**Separation Rate Formula:**\nSeparation Rate (%) = (Number of Separations during the period ÷ Average Number of Employees during the period) × 100\n\n**Example:**\n- Number of employees who left during the year = 30\n- Average number of employees = 200\n\nSeparation Rate = (30 ÷ 200) × 100 = 15%\n\n**Interpretation:**\n15% of the workforce left during the year.\n\n**Why it matters:**\n- High turnover increases costs (recruitment, training, lost productivity)\n- Low turnover may indicate good working conditions\n- Very low turnover might suggest lack of career progression opportunities\n\n**Costs of Labour Turnover:**\n- Preventative costs (better conditions, higher pay to retain staff)\n- Replacement costs (recruitment, selection, training)\n- Lost productivity and efficiency"}},{"itemId":"d82404d7-d3df-5e0b-86d6-61bd2e860726","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"model_answer","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"theory-answer:3","parentItemId":"d4c93605-c101-542c-835e-b9fe4f271618","revision":1,"payload":{"body":"Net Realizable Value (NRV) is calculated as:\n\nNRV = Estimated selling price in the ordinary course of business - Estimated costs necessary to make the sale\n\nThe estimated costs to make the sale include marketing costs, advertising costs, and transport/delivery costs to get the product to the customer."}},{"itemId":"38e3aaa1-9ecb-5902-8d77-1dc61d2992e0","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"model_answer","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"theory-answer:4","parentItemId":"2f66b8e5-b7ed-5154-92f7-7be966f5e136","revision":1,"payload":{"body":"The five-step revenue recognition model under IFRS 15 is:\n\nStep 1: Identify the contract with a customer\nStep 2: Identify the performance obligations in the contract\nStep 3: Determine the transaction price\nStep 4: Allocate the transaction price to the performance obligations\nStep 5: Recognize revenue when (or as) the entity satisfies a performance obligation\n\nRevenue is recognized when control of the promised goods or services is transferred to the customer."}},{"itemId":"be5ae6f3-379e-52e3-8bab-3778e9480990","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"model_answer","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"theory-answer:5","parentItemId":"f82b4aa1-8e36-571b-8786-641a2c249084","revision":1,"payload":{"body":"Under IAS 8:\n\n**Change in Accounting Policy:**\n- Example: Switching from the cost model to the fair value model for investment properties\n- Treatment: Applied RETROSPECTIVELY\n- This means you adjust opening balances and past comparative amounts as if the new policy had always been applied\n\n**Change in Accounting Estimate:**\n- Example: Changing an asset's useful life or depreciation method (straight-line to reducing balance)\n- Treatment: Applied PROSPECTIVELY\n- This means it only affects the current and future periods; past periods are not restated\n\nThe key difference is retrospective (restate the past) vs prospective (only affect the future)."}},{"itemId":"a6bb985c-079e-5850-89cf-b80fb10d8a14","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"model_answer","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"theory-answer:6","parentItemId":"bd6ccb8d-25ff-5287-8ceb-6e77a5f23f38","revision":1,"payload":{"body":"The initial cost of PPE under IAS 16 comprises:\n\n1. **Purchase price** - including import duties and non-refundable purchase taxes, less trade discounts and rebates\n\n2. **Directly attributable costs** - any costs directly needed to bring the asset to its working condition and location:\n   - Site preparation\n   - Initial delivery and handling\n   - Installation and assembly\n   - Testing costs\n   - Professional fees (architects, engineers)\n\n3. **Initial estimate** - of dismantling and removing the item and restoring the site on which it is located\n\n**EXCLUDE:** General administrative costs, staff training costs, and regular repairs/maintenance (these are revenue expenditure)."}},{"itemId":"579fbccf-8a22-5ec0-a797-e058a5015869","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"model_answer","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"theory-answer:7","parentItemId":"eb4a5644-323e-573a-ba24-7cfa4145e3a0","revision":1,"payload":{"body":"Under IAS 20, there are two acceptable methods for grants related to assets:\n\n**Capital Approach (Netting Method):**\n- The grant amount is deducted directly from the cost of the non-current asset on the balance sheet\n- This reduces the carrying amount of the asset\n- Result: Lower annual depreciation charge over the asset's useful life\n\n**Income Approach (Deferred Income Method):**\n- The asset is recorded at its full cost (no deduction)\n- The grant is treated as Deferred Income (a liability)\n- The deferred income is systematically released to the profit or loss statement over the asset's useful life\n- Result: Full depreciation charge, but offset by grant income recognized each year\n\nBoth methods ultimately have the same effect on net profit, but they present differently in the financial statements."}},{"itemId":"c89a446d-d7c4-5f6e-a32a-74ce1739a3c8","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"model_answer","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"theory-answer:8","parentItemId":"46dcfac3-d27e-55db-be01-c1a29c8d519f","revision":1,"payload":{"body":"Under IAS 23, a Qualifying Asset is:\n\n**Definition:** An asset that necessarily takes a substantial period of time to get ready for its intended use or sale.\n\n**Key characteristics:**\n- \"Substantial period\" typically means months or years of construction/production\n- Examples: Large buildings, infrastructure projects (bridges, roads), major production facilities, power generation plants\n\n**Why it matters:**\nBorrowing costs (interest, finance charges) directly attributable to the acquisition, construction, or production of a qualifying asset MUST be capitalized as part of the cost of that asset.\n\nFor assets that do NOT qualify (e.g., inventory produced routinely, assets ready for use immediately), borrowing costs must be expensed to profit or loss."}},{"itemId":"13ba1de3-c0e6-52f9-9909-0744346ade31","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"model_answer","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"theory-answer:9","parentItemId":"4104a77c-9ab1-5705-8fac-c11370a3fc0d","revision":1,"payload":{"body":"Under IAS 23:\n\n**Capitalization MUST CEASE** during extended periods in which active development of the qualifying asset is suspended.\n\n**Treatment:**\nThe borrowing costs (interest) incurred during this idle period must be EXPENSED directly to the statement of profit or loss in the period they are incurred.\n\n**When to resume capitalization:**\nCapitalization resumes when active construction or development activities restart.\n\n**Rationale:**\nBorrowing costs can only be capitalized when they are directly related to bringing the asset to its intended condition and use. During idle periods (strikes, delays, disputes), the asset is not actively being developed, so the interest cannot be justified as part of the asset cost.\n\n**Exception:**\nBrief, temporary delays that are a necessary part of the construction process do not stop capitalization."}}]$expected_content$::jsonb,
  'financial-accounting-1: model_answer published payload and identity match source');
