e"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Master Question Bank"}},{"itemId":"a5bfc8ef-811e-521b-9ce3-c4e50735b8d0","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a12e5324-66b7-4e85-805f-09c20e8dd41c","sourceKey":"quiz:374","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Berkeley is","options":["An inductionist","An empiricist","A pragmatist","A rationalist Assessment Compilation by CampusIntell • Page 12"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Master Question Bank"}},{"itemId":"1645ab08-96bc-58b5-9a06-a59dee0bcc45","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d5d6a6fe-c9d1-412f-a2c7-11f3306cf84b","sourceKey":"quiz:375","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"According to ___, science can explain everything","options":["Idealism","Rationalism","Existentialism","Positivism"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Master Question Bank"}},{"itemId":"3f86d1fb-d68b-5d4b-ae13-2bf7242cc5ed","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9fbbf14e-0849-4169-acc3-9d5fcab5c524","sourceKey":"quiz:376","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Materialism is a branch of:","options":["Epistemology","Metaphysics","Logic","Ethics"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"Materialism (the view that matter is the fundamental substance) is a metaphysical position."}},{"itemId":"4be229f9-9ff3-5c47-82de-e08292881e71","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"75abdb9c-ab15-4bd7-b7aa-24fe9fd33ac9","sourceKey":"quiz:377","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Technology covers every aspect of human life:","options":["True","False"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"Under the broad sense of technology (the means by which humans manipulate their environment), it touches virtually every area of human activity."}},{"itemId":"044ebdd9-7a66-51cd-894c-1990c8900a09","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d58d9721-db75-4560-92c4-1aebf80eb080","sourceKey":"quiz:378","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Positivists subscribe to:","options":["Innate ideas","A posteriori knowledge","Mysticism","A priori knowledge only"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"Logical Positivists are strict empiricists — only empirically verifiable (a posteriori) claims are meaningful to them."}},{"itemId":"2e6c55c0-50dd-5373-9928-894441d86211","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6fc67de7-53b8-403b-bfab-3d02c882a657","sourceKey":"quiz:379","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"According to whom are love and strife principles of reality?","options":["Empedocles","Heraclitus","Democritus","Anaxagoras"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"Empedocles proposed love and strife as the two opposing forces governing reality."}},{"itemId":"4e80007b-1668-5b56-9679-e9dc7e5811a0","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"53b6eecf-444f-41c0-bf26-7ecfc02ad5a8","sourceKey":"quiz:38","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Who wrote the famous book, The Prince?","options":["Machiavelli","Julius Caesar","Polus","Thomas Aquinas"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Master Question Bank"}},{"itemId":"dcd4f71c-2290-5a6e-ab23-40c63b02a05b","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"66a7805e-7706-4d79-8496-4fa2fefed091","sourceKey":"quiz:380","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A definition that reports the meaning that the definiendum already has is a:","options":["Lexical definition","Stipulative definition","Precising definition","Theoretical definition"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"A lexical definition reports how a term is already used, unlike a stipulative definition, which assigns new meaning."}},{"itemId":"dea360c8-f19b-51c7-8aa1-0c044926629e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0fef3b69-c1ad-447c-87ca-0452b052bcbd","sourceKey":"quiz:381","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Arguments and Sentences mean the same thing:","options":["True","False"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"An argument is a structured set of propositions (premises + conclusion); a sentence is just a grammatical unit — they are not the same thing."}},{"itemId":"ca25a543-eb19-5c17-8fe9-7f6553ddeb99","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5b5d3830-723a-4f7d-8d3c-0e5ee58da960","sourceKey":"quiz:382","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Knowledge can be differentiated from belief because knowledge entails belief, but belief cannot entail knowledge:","options":["True","False"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"Knowledge is a stronger category than belief — you can believe something false, but you can't 'know' something false. So knowledge implies belief, not the reverse."}},{"itemId":"044bc083-fdb9-56e3-b174-e78ab0957199","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"54eb8efd-5e3c-472b-b9ea-62b3c1aa9dda","sourceKey":"quiz:383","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A proposition with a negation but without a connector is a:","options":["Compound proposition","Simple proposition","Complex proposition","Conditional proposition"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"Without a binary connector (AND, OR, IF-THEN) joining two propositions, a merely negated statement still counts as simple."}},{"itemId":"17fa65b5-9cf4-5186-bf85-3a6f49811fb8","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c08cb0c2-dd55-4573-8e01-c9c521325d95","sourceKey":"quiz:384","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Everything is a product of ideas according to:","options":["Empiricism","Realism","Idealism","Materialism"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"Idealism holds that reality is fundamentally mental/ideal — everything is ultimately a product of mind or idea."}},{"itemId":"b19a877b-f86b-5950-9187-44f539bbbba9","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"67bddf69-82f3-47c8-a4cc-a38c80d97282","sourceKey":"quiz:385","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The ancient Presocratic philosophers mostly philosophised about?","options":["Language structure","The cosmos","Political governance","Morality"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"The Pre-Socratics were primarily concerned with cosmology — explaining the origin and nature of the physical universe."}},{"itemId":"22a09d53-93a5-5452-b535-d15d230c5abd","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3bce9429-3d0b-43fc-b774-be9dcc8c988d","sourceKey":"quiz:386","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A metaphysical specialist treats aspects of being in response to the question, what is reality?","options":["True","False"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"This matches the course's framing of the specialized sciences as regional ontologies (metaphysica specialis), each addressing 'what is reality' within their domain."}},{"itemId":"6c056c45-5ec8-55ca-b80d-146bce221798","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"73997383-7702-4d4a-9da3-81c2c86d9ad5","sourceKey":"quiz:387","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"“We should give him bail because he is the only child of the parent” is an example of:","options":["Argumentum ad baculum","Argumentum ad misericordiam","Argumentum ad populum","Argumentum ad hominem"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"This is an emotional appeal for leniency — an appeal to pity (Argumentum ad Misericordiam)."}},{"itemId":"fd19b0fe-ab38-5b52-8086-c8f5e33f597d","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"fda9ce6b-7c07-49c5-a8ba-0996ecfbab91","sourceKey":"quiz:388","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Morality of human conduct is a discussion in:","options":["Aesthetics","Epistemology","Logic","Ethics"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"Ethics is the branch of philosophy dealing with the morality of human conduct."}},{"itemId":"cdd0d816-de9c-5685-9d2a-ef7d49b5a95d","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4ef37914-6a58-4757-ba77-c35f5d86ac3f","sourceKey":"quiz:389","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"“Buhari is tall while Osinbajo is short” is a:","options":["Conjunction","Conditional","Negation","Disjunction"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"“While” joins two claims that are both asserted as true simultaneously — a conjunction."}},{"itemId":"2450e0fe-b750-5da3-8bee-c6d62761c892","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"79cf44f7-b847-406e-bc8d-012cc68f6ebf","sourceKey":"quiz:39","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Parmenides subscribed to","options":["synthetic a posteriori knowledge","a posteriori knowledge","synthetic a priori knowledge","a priori knowledge"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Master Question Bank"}},{"itemId":"a3b0e1d0-1969-5a8b-b6bd-13ce0fc572bb","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f84f8686-3762-42f0-8d90-70e5e7e9d98a","sourceKey":"quiz:390","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of these is a characteristic of scientific knowledge?","options":["Dogma","Intuition","Superstition","Inquiry"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"Systematic inquiry is a defining feature of scientific knowledge, unlike dogma, superstition, or unaided intuition."}},{"itemId":"2c9982e2-a938-5387-a2a0-4d2d195e3c33","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f53eb32b-05ae-4042-a229-8e68626184b4","sourceKey":"quiz:391","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Philosophers like St. Augustine made a distinction between:","options":["Mind and Body only","Immediate and Mediate awareness","Physical and Chemical awareness","Logical and Illogical thought"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"Augustine distinguished immediate awareness from a deeper, more reflective/spiritual form of awareness."}},{"itemId":"409d7a85-ba17-5591-a883-d65fb30d15cd","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ad84f2c3-2a1d-4e23-b392-5aea1c9391f0","sourceKey":"quiz:392","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"According to whom is there but one truly philosophical problem, and that is suicide?","options":["Albert Camus","Martin Heidegger","Friedrich Nietzsche","Jean-Paul Sartre"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"Camus opens The Myth of Sisyphus by declaring this the one truly serious philosophical question."}},{"itemId":"916e53b4-1818-5edb-830b-c4afe2cea6e0","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"287a4cb3-daa7-4df7-bbf5-ec345b65a3e6","sourceKey":"quiz:393","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The view that evil comes out of ignorance of its consequences was given by:","options":["Plato","Zeno","Aristotle","Socrates"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"Socrates held that wrongdoing stems from ignorance — no one knowingly does evil."}},{"itemId":"1d20fd2c-57d9-5b06-8bde-4f42a96bbe90","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"314a1f2d-3c13-43db-a7ab-aec90d0c6b09","sourceKey":"quiz:394","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of these is not a major function of language according to Copi?","options":["Relative","Expressive","Informative","Directive"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"Copi's classic functions of language are informative, expressive, and directive — “relative” isn't one of them."}},{"itemId":"727d040e-cb96-5817-b7bb-0389a50ffd6b","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4d561348-2eeb-4eaa-9fd8-159001d95bf1","sourceKey":"quiz:395","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of these is not a rule of replacement?","options":["Commutation","Addition","Transposition","De Morgan's Law"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"De Morgan's, Commutation, and Transposition are rules of replacement; Addition is a rule of inference instead."}},{"itemId":"eb74836f-53d6-5667-9ee2-d231e66483cc","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8f3a747e-d8a3-437c-a82a-290edfbcda35","sourceKey":"quiz:396","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The method of dialectics was introduced and employed by:","options":["The Romans","The Egyptians","The Greeks","The Babylonians"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"The dialectical method (question-and-answer reasoning) traces to the Greeks, especially the Socratic method."}},{"itemId":"f5d3ab51-466c-5b37-b877-7259519959d6","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f624da68-f2c7-426a-8666-819298944a68","sourceKey":"quiz:397","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Who developed the Performative theory of truth?","options":["Gottlob Frege","Bertrand Russell","Alfred Tarski","P. F. Strawson"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"P.F. Strawson proposed that asserting 'it is true' performs an act rather than describing a fact."}},{"itemId":"96aa3274-5d16-5850-867e-e0db66256b38","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ae225f6b-85ba-43fc-84b9-26d0e5ed2881","sourceKey":"quiz:398","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Heraclitus belongs to which group?","options":["Scholastic philosophers","Modern philosophers","Pre-Socratic philosophers","Contemporary philosophers"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"Heraclitus is one of the classical Pre-Socratic philosophers."}},{"itemId":"713f805f-df8a-54d8-ad54-ef14d4801359","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b6280a58-023e-4a7d-bee8-5da57e8a7b05","sourceKey":"quiz:399","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Whose conception of philosophy reflects the etymological definition as the love of wisdom?","options":["Marcus Aurelius","Cicero","Seneca","Lucretius"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"Cicero is credited with popularizing the Latin rendering of philosophia as ‘love of wisdom.’"}},{"itemId":"6c3e8c69-885e-559e-804e-6f642d5e7e47","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4d4adead-7608-4724-9a5a-89e61a705cae","sourceKey":"quiz:4","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Berkeley believes in","options":["synthetic a priori knowledge","a posteriori knowledge","a synthetic a posteriori knowledge","a priori knowledge"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Master Question Bank"}},{"itemId":"1ab943ec-1b5a-54d3-b32f-74f1210672c9","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"cbe2a0ac-bf4f-483a-8a38-1048ffdfdf39","sourceKey":"quiz:40","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The philosophical position that reality is located in the sky is","options":["None of the above","Objective idealism","Subjective idealism","Realism"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Master Question Bank"}},{"itemId":"66e3c1e3-50ab-53e4-9977-17458727fa03","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"94822723-191f-4af4-88d1-28d3adce3e0e","sourceKey":"quiz:400","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which fallacy is committed in “People are like cats. You have to know how to scratch them to win their favor”?","options":["Slippery slope","False dichotomy","False analogy","Red herring"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"Comparing people's behavior to cats inappropriately is a False Analogy."}},{"itemId":"ca7b98e9-e8bf-5970-be45-a2eb83e176fb","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"eb1fd944-3cc7-4ac3-a344-834dbcab33c2","sourceKey":"quiz:401","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"__ is a part of a disjunction:","options":["Conjunct","1st disjunct / 2nd disjunct","Antecedent","Consequent"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"In a disjunction P v Q, P and Q are called the first and second disjuncts respectively."}},{"itemId":"e3fc21c4-d008-510f-af41-61b59db431b0","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c108d303-2f1e-4010-b76d-b8016a4d8092","sourceKey":"quiz:402","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of these philosophers predates Zeno of Elea?","options":["Aristotle","Socrates","Parmenides","Plato"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"Zeno of Elea was Parmenides's student, so Parmenides predates him."}},{"itemId":"bfcd2123-6e21-504d-9b32-bc408fe30860","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"594257ad-efb2-42ba-b5e8-e9c996c951ab","sourceKey":"quiz:403","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Neoplatonism was a popular philosophy in the:","options":["Ancient period","Contemporary period","Medieval period","Modern period"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"Neoplatonism (Plotinus) flourished in the late Ancient/Hellenistic era."}},{"itemId":"5cb340ef-ca6e-55d1-9885-d89c5746a822","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"cfafe9ab-d3c6-4249-98eb-60417c844f23","sourceKey":"quiz:404","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Modus Tollens is a:","options":["Formal Fallacy","Rule of Inference","Informal Fallacy","Rule of Replacement"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"Modus Tollens is one of the classical valid rules of inference."}},{"itemId":"05b48500-488f-5877-b372-bea80982f20f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"50699fc6-3ac2-4cc6-810a-843b7ebb7d22","sourceKey":"quiz:405","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The philosophy of science and the science of philosophy are the same thing:","options":["True","False"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"These are distinct — philosophy of science studies science philosophically, while 'science of philosophy' would mean treating philosophy scientifically."}},{"itemId":"c1a9dc05-2c70-5512-85e2-2b6126bacc50","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1899d5b5-eec3-4f8d-be53-84fef8ab69c7","sourceKey":"quiz:406","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Memory is the __ of the mind:","options":["Engine","Filter","Storehouse","Controller"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"Memory is commonly described as the mind's storehouse of past experience and ideas."}},{"itemId":"67346110-3a0f-5365-b8bb-b8f076d6228f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b9b3e1d9-b9c3-41c0-82c0-f19dbf1ab267","sourceKey":"quiz:407","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A valid attempt to train one on reasoning and logical conclusions is done through:","options":["Dialectics","Dogmatism","Sophistry","Rhetoric"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"Dialectics — structured question-and-answer reasoning — trains logical thinking, unlike rhetoric or sophistry, which aim to persuade rather than reason correctly."}},{"itemId":"cb1b5f55-048d-5566-90cf-c04b23c83ce6","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8923cf67-c506-46bc-af38-325443381aa6","sourceKey":"quiz:408","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Areas of human endeavour impacted by science and technology include:","options":["Transportation","Healthcare","Communication","All of the above"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"Science and technology have reshaped healthcare, communication, and transportation alike."}},{"itemId":"3d0bd512-fc7c-5a9c-9e7b-b787ee138c7e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b5e9d905-0406-4374-a7b5-633c5dc385ed","sourceKey":"quiz:409","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Who sees philosophy as a self-reflective activity that cannot take its own nature for granted?","options":["Bertrand Russell","Allen Wood","Immanuel Kant","John Dewey"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"Allen Wood frames philosophy as inherently self-reflective, always questioning its own nature."}},{"itemId":"3c2ed80b-e41a-59c3-ac0d-644569074492","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3b13c0db-1840-4eba-bf24-fb8cff6cc77f","sourceKey":"quiz:41","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Philosophical reflection can be done through","options":["metaphysics","Epistemology","ethics","all of the above"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Master Question Bank"}},{"itemId":"63b4f934-50b9-584a-aea8-42e9007e3f4c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"82c0c863-5cd7-4357-b8b1-5038fc72e9e8","sourceKey":"quiz:410","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Knowledge can be applied to intelligently adjusted behaviour:","options":["True","False"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"Applying knowledge to adjust behavior intelligently is a standard epistemological claim about knowledge's practical function."}},{"itemId":"6c3e4605-b5f1-5f27-9ff9-53f8956237e0","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2a7cb845-a57d-4382-8a35-b2e8a81900f8","sourceKey":"quiz:411","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Discovering conditions that determine events using experimentation and observation is a concern of:","options":["Metaphysics","Philosophy of science","Ethics","Axiology"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"This describes the methodology examined by philosophy of science."}},{"itemId":"f0cc63fe-7101-5e5f-8df2-a6811a5f3044","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c44c9f48-7eeb-4304-90f5-78f15fa57757","sourceKey":"quiz:412","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Major logical symbol names exclude:","options":["Horseshoe","Connective","Wedge","Tilde"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"Tilde, horseshoe, and wedge are specific symbol names; ‘connective’ is the general category term, not a specific symbol."}},{"itemId":"c5843429-e914-58f6-a6f5-14d78adb9bbf","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2b3dc4ac-3b30-469f-8e61-8ad69d8c4bdb","sourceKey":"quiz:413","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"“You cannot win the battle because you are an illiterate” is an:","options":["Argumentum ad ignorationem","Argumentum ad baculum","Argumentum ad populum","Argumentum ad hominem"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"Attacking someone's status/trait (illiteracy) instead of their argument is Argumentum ad Hominem."}},{"itemId":"9761824c-f525-5752-97bf-7e120a29ef3a","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"fc53c28f-b259-4b73-bc3e-a808369b8667","sourceKey":"quiz:414","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Antecedent and consequence are two parts of an:","options":["Conjunction","Disjunction","Negation","Implication"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"In a conditional (implication) P⊃Q, P is the antecedent and Q is the consequent."}},{"itemId":"bf5188b7-abf2-5de3-a847-65ddf6d946b1","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"18850c89-8116-407e-9b65-4f7bc0a4ab0d","sourceKey":"quiz:415","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Realism is a branch of:","options":["Ethics","Epistemology","Metaphysics","Logic"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"Realism (the view that reality exists independent of perception) is a metaphysical position."}},{"itemId":"6f1c4a2c-7ad8-5e69-a9fa-01d61ce25557","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"01fc4e88-ee7e-4c35-8204-5318d54399e5","sourceKey":"quiz:416","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which fallacy is committed in “Either you're with me, or against me”?","options":["False dichotomy","Begging the question","Slippery slope","Straw man"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"This presents only two options when more exist — a False Dichotomy (false dilemma)."}},{"itemId":"829d4f7b-0ed4-5d86-9334-084ee37a7bbb","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"814b4520-82f8-4a67-a768-75a0f399683d","sourceKey":"quiz:417","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Informal fallacies include:","options":["Denying the Antecedent","Validity and Soundness","Relevance and Ambiguity","Affirming the Consequent"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"Informal fallacies split into fallacies of Relevance and fallacies of Ambiguity; the other two options are formal fallacies."}},{"itemId":"1f15b937-73a2-59f3-8a95-925c1f6ab117","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"cfa99d69-46ab-48e4-98eb-700796346e48","sourceKey":"quiz:418","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Who distinguished between intuitive, everyday morality and critical level morality?","options":["R.M. Hare","John Stuart Mill","Jeremy Bentham","Peter Singer"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"The course material credits Peter Singer with this distinction."}},{"itemId":"e2defde9-d262-5871-9cce-d6a16e747908","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6b1073a5-59bf-426f-b412-8d7780408f1a","sourceKey":"quiz:419","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Who worked on finding the true relation between a light source and its lens image?","options":["Ibn al-Haytham (Ibn-al-Hazen)","Al-Kindi","Al-Razi","Ibn Sina"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"Ibn al-Haytham (Alhazen) did foundational work on optics, including light and lens behavior."}},{"itemId":"a4b53d38-3c4a-517d-a12c-df69b69155b3","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"23655b6c-13b7-411c-81a0-f6e0633afc68","sourceKey":"quiz:42","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Plato was a master of","options":["Aristotle","Thales","Socrates","None of the above"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Master Question Bank"}},{"itemId":"229e6c0e-4e22-5fe7-bf27-c60495059a54","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"67144e69-3072-419d-9cec-524c92dd698a","sourceKey":"quiz:420","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In the realm of human conduct, axiology branches off into:","options":["Ethics","Aesthetics","Politics","Logic"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"Of axiology's two branches (Ethics and Aesthetics), Ethics is the one concerned specifically with human conduct."}},{"itemId":"dd8fa459-690c-5f88-875e-8a543d753b15","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7fbfb676-14d4-4d4e-9dd3-2af086871858","sourceKey":"quiz:421","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Through Marxism, workers around the world are mobilized against:","options":["Capitalist oppressors","Democratic rule","Feudal monarchies only","Socialist movements"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"Marxism frames workers as mobilizing against capitalist exploitation."}},{"itemId":"20bafae3-fb5a-5d7a-886c-861f8d51c88e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"57473dcf-7914-4217-adc7-1b6e06a68325","sourceKey":"quiz:422","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Descartes is a:","options":["Rationalist","Skeptic","Materialist","Empiricist"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"Descartes is a foundational Rationalist, grounding knowledge in reason rather than experience."}},{"itemId":"6cb66ecf-73c5-52cc-9444-53dfac3e572c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3d120b6a-9967-47e5-b5c8-503c46333681","sourceKey":"quiz:423","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Thomas Aquinas's cosmological arguments argue for:","options":["The Existence of God","The Eternity of Matter","Rationalist Empiricism","The Falsity of Soul"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"Aquinas's cosmological arguments (from causation and motion) argue for God's existence."}},{"itemId":"e6149823-5672-5b43-8c85-3fa07fce3833","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"507196f4-e036-4870-b2b7-32cb4a70fc7a","sourceKey":"quiz:424","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Whose thinking was associated with the Delphi Oracle “Man, know thyself”?","options":["Plato","Aristotle","Pythagoras","Socrates"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"Socrates is closely associated with this Delphic maxim as central to his philosophy."}},{"itemId":"959de3a8-5233-5853-9906-f5d933442868","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"63b7e2c9-cbdb-4c64-a4dc-a1fda66f8efc","sourceKey":"quiz:425","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Fallacies are employed to:","options":["Strengthen logic","Prove validity","Deceive in arguments","Clarify truth"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"Fallacies are arguments that appear correct but are actually meant (or function) to deceive."}},{"itemId":"564fea74-71a2-551c-9e68-d41856c1c188","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"804a9f51-f084-420c-92db-1004df395421","sourceKey":"quiz:426","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Since the truth of inductive premises does not entail conclusion truth, they are at best:","options":["Sound","Certain","Deductive","Probabilistic"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"Inductive arguments can only ever be probabilistic, never certain, since their conclusions go beyond their premises."}},{"itemId":"5a444b44-238c-5885-9233-f5830fb9b356","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2655270f-ba79-4dac-96d9-7ad0e8586aa1","sourceKey":"quiz:427","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"“John is either a student or a worker” can be symbolized as:","options":["P ∨ Q","∼ P","P → Q","P · Q"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"“Either...or” is a disjunction, symbolized with the wedge."}},{"itemId":"c34e6cf7-83be-5296-88a8-7b551a05460f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"cdbe8b5d-5f6f-4ed3-8248-a88d1b71e8d8","sourceKey":"quiz:428","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"What class of people deny the existence of God?","options":["Deists","Agnostics","Atheists","Theists"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"Atheists, by definition, deny God's existence."}},{"itemId":"5c89b560-654f-56ba-88c2-cac8f04e9a11","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"85b404fa-70c5-4799-a582-7da79656ce1c","sourceKey":"quiz:429","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"St. Aquinas belongs to which group?","options":["Ancient thinkers","Contemporary thinkers","Modern thinkers","Medieval thinkers"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"Thomas Aquinas is a central Medieval philosopher."}},{"itemId":"f2e33143-6693-535e-8f56-cc2f9a6ac97f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"772b7b33-139d-47f7-9439-71284baa10af","sourceKey":"quiz:43","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"_____ started the quest for philosophy","options":["Aristotle","Thales","Socrates","Plato"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Master Question Bank"}},{"itemId":"7a0a9d51-9135-5e73-aa41-7af10f48afc5","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"68e05771-eb4a-49b8-9398-4ba2df1c4d78","sourceKey":"quiz:430","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"All men are mortal, Amusa is a man, therefore Amusa is mortal. This is a:","options":["Analogical argument","Deductive argument","Inductive argument","Fallacious argument"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"This is a classic syllogism — a deductive argument moving from general premises to a certain conclusion."}},{"itemId":"ea86a604-6e6b-52c1-a701-807e931c177c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2904c5d4-55ba-4020-b3d1-a758cb98eef8","sourceKey":"quiz:431","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Who held the view that water is the origin of all things?","options":["Heraclitus","Parmenides","Anaximander","Thales"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"Thales proposed water as the arche, the origin of all things."}},{"itemId":"b90f8c79-9acb-550e-a5ba-b7dafa673ab0","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7dd6143b-4d56-44d6-b52d-4f443027e84b","sourceKey":"quiz:432","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In a fallacious argument:","options":["The conclusion is logically necessary","The argument is sound","Premises do not support the conclusion","Premises guarantee the conclusion"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"A fallacious argument's premises fail to genuinely support its conclusion, despite appearances."}},{"itemId":"4f6bc6cc-f0de-5759-95be-46459f18d14d","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"98b5dbdd-376d-49e5-b14e-2448f644d5c2","sourceKey":"quiz:433","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The theory of natural selection is ascribed to:","options":["Charles Darwin","Jean-Baptiste Lamarck","Gregor Mendel","Louis Pasteur"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"Charles Darwin proposed natural selection in On the Origin of Species."}},{"itemId":"f25e6e6d-ce90-588e-8f05-77b775423f78","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"52c5068b-9abe-4b3e-b00d-11b673a183a9","sourceKey":"quiz:434","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"René Descartes belongs to which group?","options":["Ancient period philosophers","Medieval period philosophers","Modern period philosophers","Contemporary period philosophers"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"Descartes is a foundational Modern period philosopher."}},{"itemId":"2c8e9342-5a06-5486-a297-2929b453aa15","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"20e245ce-77d7-43ca-9747-9038b426ab51","sourceKey":"quiz:435","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Who described technology as knowledge dealing with scientific and industrial methods?","options":["John Dewey","Thomas Edison","Francis Bacon","Procter"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"This specific definition is attributed to Procter in the course's source material on science and technology."}},{"itemId":"c21e9f94-3619-55d0-a959-22a0da7ba6b2","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4faec8e7-a650-4c10-a31c-96b1bc283738","sourceKey":"quiz:436","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Who wrote The Republic?","options":["Socrates","Plato","Aristotle","Xenophon"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"The Republic is one of Plato's major works."}},{"itemId":"a5790a38-1614-5608-a489-699cf856bf91","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ca70b1e8-d344-4e62-a8cb-de040a333966","sourceKey":"quiz:437","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The first condition for the emergence of scientific activities is:","options":["Curiosity","Technology","Government support","Funding"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"Scientific inquiry is described as beginning with curiosity — an initial feeling about a possible explanation."}},{"itemId":"f4d48da2-4530-5a57-b862-15b22b135534","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"eee912e3-8aee-4177-86fd-ab54f5d7db44","sourceKey":"quiz:438","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Moral justification of law is the interest of:","options":["Criminal justice","Legal positivism","Philosophy of law","Sociology of law"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"Philosophy of Law examines the moral justification of legal rules, distinct from simply describing what the law says."}},{"itemId":"b798a549-681b-5319-b3eb-a9095f103627","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d56cc172-b348-4f99-a167-1b7e5b039c8b","sourceKey":"quiz:439","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The question “Why is there something instead of nothing?” is the concern of:","options":["Epistemology","Aesthetics","Logic","Metaphysics"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"This is Heidegger's fundamental metaphysical question."}},{"itemId":"8e0c9632-c58e-5f23-8c74-2ab22e720758","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d346d648-8d51-4710-a3c8-44fa074592ac","sourceKey":"quiz:44","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"_____ considers ultimate reality as fire","options":["Firetoclux","Thales","Heraclitus","Anaximenes"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Master Question Bank"}},{"itemId":"b6d75872-66f7-5e3b-bfc0-5c78fd0904cd","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1ff7b351-2028-4b5b-9dae-3eed806fcd76","sourceKey":"quiz:440","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Who were looking for ultimate reality?","options":["Scholastics","Pre-Socratic philosophers","Logical Positivists","Existentialists"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"The Pre-Socratics were the philosophers searching for the fundamental substance/ultimate reality underlying the cosmos."}},{"itemId":"82777be8-872b-5289-b45c-5bc4e4277cfb","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7d4ad817-de23-4d14-9af4-e736aa9b61f0","sourceKey":"quiz:441","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Hypothetical Syllogism consists of how many premises?","options":["3 premises","4 premises","2 premises","1 premise"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"Hypothetical Syllogism (P⊃Q, Q⊃R, therefore P⊃R) has two premises."}},{"itemId":"48eb6f83-bb14-51e8-9634-3b7ece2d866c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3c4f11e4-aecc-44de-9491-0bcc59736b21","sourceKey":"quiz:442","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of these is not a sentential connective?","options":["Horseshoe","Braces","Wedge","Tilde"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"Tilde, horseshoe, and wedge are genuine connective symbols; braces are just grouping notation."}},{"itemId":"d9c1ebe9-a5a5-5690-9718-78727cf5c25d","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f7a53d80-4ac1-4140-a660-74c41d3ad09b","sourceKey":"quiz:443","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Who was credited with predicting the eclipse of 585 BC?","options":["Pythagoras","Thales","Heraclitus","Anaximenes"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"Thales is famously credited with predicting this solar eclipse."}},{"itemId":"f131bec6-295a-5cec-969e-bb89ee972c75","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b725e38b-d222-4b36-b6c2-93d2d479cc39","sourceKey":"quiz:444","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The essence of man is freedom according to:","options":["Realism","Pragmatism","Existentialism","Idealism"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"Existentialism holds that man's essence lies in his radical freedom to choose."}},{"itemId":"796ef449-d9e6-5937-a6fe-23c652c859dd","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8170296e-9ca9-4e69-b437-348cf1f69872","sourceKey":"quiz:445","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Andronicus of Rhodes was the disciple of:","options":["Plato","Epicurus","Socrates","Aristotle"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"Andronicus of Rhodes headed the Aristotelian (Peripatetic) school and edited Aristotle's works."}},{"itemId":"ed0f335e-ef6f-53b1-a16e-e6df327abd1f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9f29bec3-1917-425d-bef5-66c821d4040e","sourceKey":"quiz:446","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which law asserts that bodies attract with force proportional to mass and inversely to distance squared?","options":["Law of inertia","Kepler's law","Law of conservation of energy","Law of universal gravitation"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"This is Newton's Law of Universal Gravitation."}},{"itemId":"fe07316f-1a8a-5f17-8491-c9f1a49eaae6","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"01331182-55ab-4448-a4af-c7f82d96bdf1","sourceKey":"quiz:447","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Aristotle advocates for:","options":["A priori knowledge","Rational intuition","A posteriori knowledge","Pure skepticism"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"Aristotle is traditionally regarded as an empiricist, favoring knowledge grounded in observation (a posteriori)."}},{"itemId":"ae01ca62-c5a3-5a56-b6ea-f3ba9ae31233","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5c4c38c2-62ec-4188-bbd5-1870847c3d17","sourceKey":"quiz:448","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Rationalism is a branch of:","options":["Axiology","Logic","Epistemology","Metaphysics"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"Rationalism is an epistemological position about how knowledge is acquired (through reason)."}},{"itemId":"6be891f5-2bdf-505e-bf90-18addaae5793","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ad51c354-38f9-4a5a-81cb-e5175988ae7f","sourceKey":"quiz:449","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Who was the first to establish the law of falling bodies?","options":["Galileo Galilei","Nicolaus Copernicus","Johannes Kepler","Isaac Newton"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"Galileo established the law of falling bodies, refuting Aristotle's claims."}},{"itemId":"2ffdd722-79d6-5405-8280-0c543c1571ff","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ba452201-c777-43c9-9bc6-e90773532a8a","sourceKey":"quiz:45","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Philosophers in the medieval period were interested in","options":["political theories and tenets","Relationship between history and language","phenomena of human existence","Using philosophy to justify religious doctrines"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Master Question Bank"}},{"itemId":"719fbebb-55f8-5494-8fbf-e5de0af15601","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2ce1bd12-c950-4ebb-ad3d-a4318b3d96ff","sourceKey":"quiz:450","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"“Nigeria will not disintegrate since Wole Soyinka said so” is an:","options":["Argumentum ad verecundiam","Argumentum ad hominem","Argumentum ad baculum","Argumentum ad populum"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Master Question Bank","explanation":"Citing a respected figure's word as proof is Argumentum ad Verecundiam — appeal to authority."}},{"itemId":"6ebb4b02-2d46-5686-9556-705940547233","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"136fdfd6-37f0-4c9c-afd3-72a725e84c17","sourceKey":"quiz:451","parentItemId":null,"r